/* Pearson Edexcel A level Mathematics (9MA0) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-9MA0', KITS: {"P2":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P2","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebra and functions is the room every other room borrows from: differentiate a cubic and you must factorise it, integrate a rational function and you must split it into partial fractions, find where a line meets a circle and you must handle a quadratic and its discriminant. Papers 1 and 2 test this content directly almost every year — indices, surds, completing the square, the factor theorem, inequalities in set notation, composite and inverse functions, transformations and partial fractions — and Paper 3 hides it inside modelling. Accuracy marks here are lost to careless manipulation far more often than to missing knowledge, so the habit of writing every line down is worth more marks than any single result you can quote.","idea":[{"h":"Indices: one system of rules for every rational power","t":"aᵐ × aⁿ = a^(m + n), aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = a^(m − n) and (aᵐ)ⁿ = a^(mn) hold for every rational m and n, and from them a⁰ = 1, a^(−n) = 1/aⁿ and a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a all follow. The key equivalence is a^(m/n) = ⁿ√(aᵐ) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ, so 8^(2/3) may be read as the cube root of 64 or as the square of the cube root of 8 — take the root first, because the numbers stay small. Examiners set these as 'simplify fully' or 'write as a single power of x', and the method mark is for splitting the expression into a number part and an x part before recombining.","code":"2.1"},{"h":"Surds and the conjugate trick","t":"A surd is an exact irrational root. Simplify by pulling out square factors: √50 = √(25 × 2) = 5√2. The three results you may quote are (√x)² = x, √(xy) = √x √y and (√x + √y)(√x − √y) = x − y. The last one is why rationalising works: multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator and the root disappears from the bottom, because (a − √b)(a + √b) = a² − b. The M mark is for multiplying by the conjugate; the A marks are for the expanded numerator and for the final simplified surd form. 'Exact' in the question means no decimals anywhere in the answer.","code":"2.2"},{"h":"Reading a quadratic three ways","t":"Factorised, (x − p)(x − q) shows the roots; completed square, a(x + b/(2a))² + (c − b²/(4a)), shows the vertex and so the greatest or least value and the range; the discriminant b² − 4ac counts the real roots without finding them — positive gives two distinct roots, zero gives one repeated root, negative gives none. Questions that say 'the line meets the curve at two points', 'has real roots' or 'find the values of k for which…' are discriminant questions in disguise. The same techniques solve a quadratic in a function of x, such as 4^x − 5(2^x) + 4 = 0 or 2sin²θ − sinθ − 1 = 0, once you substitute a single letter for the repeated block.","code":"2.3"},{"h":"One linear, one quadratic: substitute","t":"To solve a pair of equations where one is quadratic, make one variable the subject of the linear equation and substitute it into the quadratic, so you are left with a single quadratic in one unknown. Solve that, then substitute each root back into the linear equation to find the matching value of the other variable — answers must be given in pairs, and a solution is only complete when both variables are stated together. Geometrically you are finding where a line meets a curve, so a repeated root means the line is a tangent and no real roots means the line and curve never meet.","code":"2.4"},{"h":"Inequalities: find the critical values, then read the sketch","t":"For a linear inequality, work as with an equation but never multiply or divide by something that could be negative without reversing the sign. For a quadratic, bring everything to one side, factorise to get the critical values, sketch the parabola and read off the region you want: for an upwards parabola, below the axis is between the roots and above the axis is outside them. Solving px² + qx + r < ax + b is exactly finding the range of x for which the curve is below the line. Give the answer using 'and'/'or' or in set notation, for example {x : x < a} ∪ {x : x > b}, and when shading a region use a dotted line for a strict inequality and a solid line otherwise.","code":"2.5"},{"h":"Polynomials, the factor theorem and cancelling fractions","t":"The factor theorem says that if f(b/a) = 0 then (ax − b) is a factor of f(x). For a cubic, find one root by trying the factors of the constant term, divide by that linear factor (long division or comparing coefficients) and factorise the quadratic that is left. An algebraic fraction simplifies only when numerator and denominator are fully factorised and a whole bracket cancels — never cancel a single term out of a sum. Only division by (ax + b) or (ax − b) is required, and denominators of rational expressions will be linear or quadratic.","code":"2.6"},{"h":"Sketching: shape, intercepts, asymptotes","t":"A sketch needs the correct shape, every intersection with the axes, and any asymptotes drawn and labelled with their equations; B marks are awarded feature by feature. For a polynomial given in factorised form, a repeated factor means the curve touches the axis there, so y = x²(2x − 1)² touches the x-axis at x = 0 and at x = 1/2. y = |ax + b| is a V with its vertex where ax + b = 0, and solving |2x − 1| = x means solving the two branches separately and checking each root lies in the region that branch covers. The curve y = a/(x + b) + c has asymptotes x = −b and y = c. Direct proportion y ∝ x means y = kx, a straight line through the origin with gradient k.","code":"2.7"},{"h":"Functions, domains and inverses","t":"A function maps each element of its domain to exactly one value; the range is the set of values it actually takes. fg means do g first, then f, and fg only exists where g(x) lands inside the domain of f. An inverse exists only for a one-one function, which is often why a domain is restricted; f⁻¹f(x) = ff⁻¹(x) = x, the domain of f⁻¹ is the range of f, and the graph of y = f⁻¹(x) is the reflection of y = f(x) in the line y = x. Find f⁻¹ by writing y = f(x), making x the subject and then swapping the letters.","code":"2.8"},{"h":"Transformations: inside is horizontal, outside is vertical","t":"y = f(x) + a translates the graph by (0, a) and y = af(x) stretches it parallel to the y-axis with scale factor a: both act on the y-coordinate, so they do what the number looks like. y = f(x + a) translates by (−a, 0) and y = f(ax) stretches parallel to the x-axis with scale factor 1/a: both act on the x-coordinate and both are the opposite of what the number looks like. y = |f(x)| reflects in the x-axis every part of the curve below it, while y = f(−x) reflects the whole curve in the y-axis. For a combination, track a named point through the transformations in order and state both the type and the scale factor or vector.","code":"2.9"},{"h":"Partial fractions","t":"A proper fraction whose denominator factorises splits into simpler pieces. For distinct linear factors, (px + q)/((ax + b)(cx + d)) = A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d); for a squared linear factor you need three terms, A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d) + C/(cx + d)². Multiply through by the whole denominator and then either substitute the values of x that make each bracket zero, or compare coefficients — a mixture of the two is usually quickest, since substitution finds the outer constants and comparing x² coefficients finds the middle one. You will need the result again when integrating a rational function and when expanding one binomially, which is why it lives in this room.","code":"2.10"},{"h":"Using functions to model","t":"Modelling questions give a situation and ask you to choose or use a function: a reciprocal for inverse proportion, such as pressure against volume; an exponential for growth and decay; a trigonometric function for anything that repeats, such as the tide or the hours of daylight. Find the constants from the data you are given, use the model to predict, then interpret the answer in context with its units. The last mark is usually for a limitation — the constants were fitted over a small range so extrapolation is unreliable, or a factor such as temperature, friction or air resistance has been ignored — or for saying how the model could be refined.","code":"2.11"}],"examples":[{"title":"Rationalising a denominator into the form a + b√3","setup":"Write (5 + √3)/(2 − √3) in the form a + b√3, where a and b are integers.","steps":["The conjugate of 2 − √3 is 2 + √3, so multiply top and bottom by 2 + √3, which leaves the value unchanged — this is the line the method mark is for.","Numerator: (5 + √3)(2 + √3) = 10 + 5√3 + 2√3 + 3 = 13 + 7√3.","Denominator: (2 − √3)(2 + √3) = 4 − 3, which is 1.","So the expression equals 13 + 7√3, giving a = 13 and b = 7."],"cues":["What do you multiply the top and the bottom by to clear the surd from the denominator?","What does the numerator expand to?","Why does the denominator become a whole number?","What are the values of a and b?"]},{"title":"Using the discriminant to find the values of a constant","setup":"The quadratic equation x² + (k + 3)x + 4k = 0, where k is a constant, has two equal roots. Find the possible values of k.","steps":["Equal roots means the discriminant is zero, and here a = 1, b = k + 3 and c = 4k, so (k + 3)² − 4(1)(4k) = 0.","Expanding the bracket: k² + 6k + 9 − 16k = 0.","Collecting like terms gives a quadratic in k: k² − 10k + 9 = 0.","Factorising: (k − 1)(k − 9) = 0.","So k = 1 or k = 9."],"cues":["What condition on b² − 4ac makes the two roots equal?","What does (k + 3)² come to when expanded?","What quadratic in k is left after collecting the terms?","How does that quadratic factorise?","What are the two possible values of k?"]},{"title":"Factor theorem, then simplifying an algebraic fraction","setup":"f(x) = 2x³ + 3x² − 11x − 6. Show that (x − 2) is a factor of f(x), factorise f(x) completely, and hence simplify f(x)/(x² − 4).","steps":["f(2) = 2(8) + 3(4) − 11(2) − 6 = 16 + 12 − 22 − 6 = 0, so by the factor theorem (x − 2) is a factor.","Dividing gives 2x³ + 3x² − 11x − 6 = (x − 2)(2x² + 7x + 3).","The quadratic factorises as (2x + 1)(x + 3), so f(x) = (x − 2)(2x + 1)(x + 3).","The denominator is a difference of two squares: x² − 4 = (x − 2)(x + 2).","Cancelling the common factor (x − 2) leaves (2x + 1)(x + 3)/(x + 2)."],"cues":["What value of x do you substitute to test whether (x − 2) is a factor, and what must the result be?","What quadratic must (x − 2) multiply to give f(x)?","How does that quadratic factorise?","How does the denominator factorise?","Which bracket cancels, and what is left?"]},{"title":"Range and inverse of a quadratic on a restricted domain","setup":"The function f is defined by f(x) = 2x² − 12x + 23, with domain x ≥ 3. Find the range of f, find f⁻¹(x) and state the domain of f⁻¹.","steps":["Complete the square: 2x² − 12x + 23 = 2(x² − 6x) + 23 = 2((x − 3)² − 9) + 23.","So f(x) = 2(x − 3)² + 5, whose least value is at x = 3, the left-hand end of the domain, so the range is f(x) ≥ 5.","To invert, write y = 2(x − 3)² + 5 and make x the subject: (x − 3)² = (y − 5)/2.","Take the positive square root, because the domain says x ≥ 3: x = 3 + √((y − 5)/2).","So f⁻¹(x) = 3 + √((x − 5)/2), and its domain is the range of f, namely x ≥ 5."],"cues":["Which form shows the least value of the quadratic at a glance?","Where is that least value, and does it lie inside the given domain?","What is the first move in finding an inverse?","Which square root do you keep, and why?","Where does the domain of f⁻¹ come from?"]},{"title":"Partial fractions with a repeated linear factor","setup":"Express (5x² + 17x + 16)/((x + 2)(x + 1)²) in partial fractions, in the form A/(x + 2) + B/(x + 1) + C/(x + 1)², where A, B and C are constants to be found.","steps":["Multiply through by the whole denominator to clear the fractions, giving 5x² + 17x + 16 = A(x + 1)² + B(x + 2)(x + 1) + C(x + 2).","Put x = −2, which kills both B and C: 20 − 34 + 16 = A(−1)², so A = 2.","Put x = −1, which kills both A and B: 5 − 17 + 16 = C(1), so C = 4.","Compare the coefficients of x²: 5 = A + B, so B = 3.","The expression is therefore 2/(x + 2) + 3/(x + 1) + 4/(x + 1)²."],"cues":["What identity do you get once both sides are multiplied by (x + 2)(x + 1)²?","Which value of x leaves only the A term?","Which value of x leaves only the C term?","How can you get B quickly now that A is known?","What is the complete partial fraction form?"]},{"title":"Solving a modulus equation using both branches","setup":"The graphs of y = |2x − 6| and y = x + 1 are drawn on the same axes. Solve the equation |2x − 6| = x + 1, giving your solutions exactly.","steps":["For x ≥ 3 the modulus graph is the line y = 2x − 6, so on that branch the equation is 2x − 6 = x + 1.","That gives x = 7, which does satisfy x ≥ 3, so it is a valid solution.","For x < 3 the modulus graph is the reflected line y = 6 − 2x, so on that branch the equation is 6 − 2x = x + 1.","That gives 5 = 3x, so x = 5/3, and since 5/3 < 3 this root is valid too.","The two solutions are x = 5/3 and x = 7."],"cues":["Which straight line is y = |2x − 6| to the right of its vertex?","What root does that branch give, and is it in the right region?","What is the equation of the other branch of the V?","What root does the second branch give, and does it survive the check?","What are both solutions?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Evaluate 16^(−3/4).","a":"1/8"},{"q":"Write 2x² + 12x + 5 in the form a(x + b)² + c.","a":"2(x + 3)² − 13"},{"q":"For what values of k does x² + kx + 9 = 0 have no real roots?","a":"−6 < k < 6"},{"q":"Express 1/((x + 1)(x + 4)) in partial fractions.","a":"1/(3(x + 1)) − 1/(3(x + 4))"}]},"room":{"facts":["Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = a^(m + n), aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = a^(m − n), (aᵐ)ⁿ = a^(mn), a⁰ = 1, a^(−n) = 1/aⁿ, a^(m/n) = ⁿ√(aᵐ).","Quadratic formula: x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a) for ax² + bx + c = 0.","Discriminant: b² − 4ac > 0 gives two distinct real roots, b² − 4ac = 0 one repeated root, b² − 4ac < 0 no real roots.","Completing the square: ax² + bx + c = a(x + b/(2a))² + (c − b²/(4a)).","Rationalising: (a − √b)(a + √b) = a² − b, so multiply top and bottom by the conjugate of the denominator.","Factor theorem: if f(b/a) = 0 then (ax − b) is a factor of f(x).","Functions: fg(x) = f(g(x)), doing g first; f⁻¹f(x) = ff⁻¹(x) = x; the domain of f⁻¹ is the range of f.","Transformations: y = f(x + a) is a translation of (−a, 0); y = f(x) + a is a translation of (0, a); y = f(ax) is an x-stretch of scale factor 1/a; y = af(x) is a y-stretch of scale factor a."],"questions":[{"q":"Simplify fully (27x⁶)^(2/3).","a":"9x⁴","sol":"A power outside a bracket applies to every factor inside it, so (27x⁶)^(2/3) = 27^(2/3) × (x⁶)^(2/3) (M1). For the number, 27^(2/3) = (³√27)² = 3² = 9. For the algebra, (x⁶)^(2/3) = x^(6 × 2/3) = x⁴ (using (aᵐ)ⁿ = a^(mn)). Hence the expression is 9x⁴ (A1). A single power of x with the wrong coefficient, or 9 with the wrong power, scores the M mark only.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A power on the outside of a bracket acts on everything inside it, the number as well as the x.","Deal with the number part and the x part separately; a power of 2/3 means take the cube root and then square.","(27x⁶)^(2/3) = 27^(2/3) × (x⁶)^(2/3)."],"codes":["2.1"]},{"q":"Write x² − 10x + 32 in the form (x + p)² + q, and hence write down the least value of x² − 10x + 32.","a":"(x − 5)² + 7; least value 7","sol":"p is half the coefficient of x, so p = −5 and (x − 5)² = x² − 10x + 25 (M1). Therefore x² − 10x + 32 = (x − 5)² − 25 + 32 = (x − 5)² + 7 (A1). A square is never negative, so the expression is least when (x − 5)² = 0, that is when x = 5, and its least value is 7 (B1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Any quadratic whose x² coefficient is 1 can be written as one squared bracket plus a number.","Halve the coefficient of x to get the number inside the bracket, then correct the constant for what the bracket has added.","x² − 10x + 32 = (x + p)² − p² + 32, where p is half of −10."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"f(x) = x³ − 4x² + x + 6. Use the factor theorem to show that (x − 3) is a factor of f(x).","a":"f(3) = 27 − 36 + 3 + 6 = 0, so (x − 3) is a factor of f(x)","sol":"(x − 3) = 0 when x = 3, so the factor theorem says to evaluate f(3) (M1). f(3) = 3³ − 4(3²) + 3 + 6 = 27 − 36 + 3 + 6 = 0. Since f(3) = 0, (x − 3) is a factor of f(x) (A1). This is a 'show that', so both the substituted working and the concluding statement must be seen for the accuracy mark (cso).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The factor theorem connects a factor of the form (x − a) with the value of the polynomial at one particular number.","Substitute the value of x that makes the bracket (x − 3) equal to zero, and see what f gives.","Work out f(3) term by term, showing each one."],"codes":["2.6"]},{"q":"f(x) = 4x − 7, x ∈ ℝ. Find f⁻¹(x) and state the value of f⁻¹(5).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 7)/4; f⁻¹(5) = 3","sol":"Write y = 4x − 7, then y + 7 = 4x and x = (y + 7)/4 (M1). Swapping the letters, f⁻¹(x) = (x + 7)/4 (A1). Then f⁻¹(5) = (5 + 7)/4 = 12/4 = 3 (B1). A check is available: f(3) = 4(3) − 7 = 5, as required.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["An inverse undoes what the function does, so the operations are reversed in the opposite order.","Set y equal to the expression and rearrange to make x the subject, then swap the letters.","Rearranging y = 4x − 7 gives y + 7 = 4x."],"codes":["2.8"]},{"q":"A rectangle has area (17 + 7√5) cm² and width (3 + √5) cm. Find the exact perimeter of the rectangle, giving your answer in the form a + b√5 cm, where a and b are integers.","a":"14 + 4√5 cm","sol":"The length is the area divided by the width, so length = (17 + 7√5)/(3 + √5) (M1). Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate 3 − √5 (M1). Numerator: (17 + 7√5)(3 − √5) = 51 − 17√5 + 21√5 − 35 = 16 + 4√5. Denominator: (3 + √5)(3 − √5) = 9 − 5 = 4 (A1). So the length is (16 + 4√5)/4 = 4 + √5 cm (A1). The perimeter is 2(4 + √5) + 2(3 + √5) = 8 + 2√5 + 6 + 2√5 = 14 + 4√5 cm (A1). 'Exact' forbids a decimal, so 22.9 cm loses the final mark.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The width and the area fix the length of a rectangle, and a root underneath a fraction can always be cleared.","Divide the area by the width, remove the surd from the denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the conjugate, then add up the four sides.","The length is (17 + 7√5)/(3 + √5)."],"codes":["2.2"]},{"q":"Solve the equation 4^x − 9(2^x) + 8 = 0.","a":"x = 0 or x = 3","sol":"4^x = (2²)^x = (2^x)², so putting y = 2^x turns the equation into y² − 9y + 8 = 0 (M1). Factorising, (y − 1)(y − 8) = 0, so y = 1 or y = 8 (A1). Converting back (M1): 2^x = 1 gives x = 0, and 2^x = 8 = 2³ gives x = 3. Both values are required for the final mark (A1). Neither value is rejected, since 2^x is positive for all x.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["There is a quadratic hidden inside this equation, written in a function of x rather than in x itself.","Notice how 4^x is related to 2^x, then substitute a single letter for 2^x and solve the quadratic that appears.","Let y = 2^x, so that 4^x = y²."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations y = 2x + 3 and y = x² − 4x + 8.","a":"x = 1, y = 5 and x = 5, y = 13","sol":"Both equations give y, so set the right-hand sides equal: x² − 4x + 8 = 2x + 3 (M1). Rearranging, x² − 6x + 5 = 0 (A1). Factorising, (x − 1)(x − 5) = 0, so x = 1 or x = 5 (M1 A1). Substituting into y = 2x + 3: when x = 1, y = 5; when x = 5, y = 13 (A1). The answers must be paired correctly — unpaired values lose the final mark.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Where the line and the curve meet, the two expressions for y must be equal.","Set the two right-hand sides equal to each other, collect everything on one side and solve the quadratic that results.","x² − 4x + 8 = 2x + 3."],"codes":["2.4"]},{"q":"Solve the inequality x² − 3x + 4 < 2x + 10, giving your answer in set notation.","a":"{x : −1 < x < 6}","sol":"Bring every term to one side: x² − 3x + 4 − 2x − 10 < 0, so x² − 5x − 6 < 0 (M1 A1). Factorising, (x − 6)(x + 1) < 0, so the critical values are x = −1 and x = 6 (M1). The parabola opens upwards, so it lies below the x-axis between the roots, giving −1 < x < 6, written {x : −1 < x < 6} (A1). A sketch or a sign table is the evidence for choosing the inside region; giving the outside region scores the method marks only.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Read it as a picture: you want the values of x for which the curve lies below the line.","Collect every term on one side so a quadratic is compared with zero, then factorise to find the critical values.","x² − 3x + 4 − 2x − 10 < 0."],"codes":["2.5"]},{"q":"Express (8x + 3)/((2x − 1)(x + 3)) in partial fractions.","a":"2/(2x − 1) + 3/(x + 3)","sol":"Write (8x + 3)/((2x − 1)(x + 3)) = A/(2x − 1) + B/(x + 3) and multiply through by (2x − 1)(x + 3): 8x + 3 = A(x + 3) + B(2x − 1) (M1). Put x = −3: −24 + 3 = B(−7), so −21 = −7B and B = 3 (A1). Put x = 1/2: 4 + 3 = A(3.5), so 7 = 3.5A and A = 2 (A1). Hence the expression is 2/(2x − 1) + 3/(x + 3) (A1). A check at x = 0 gives −1 on both sides.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A fraction with two different linear factors underneath comes from adding two simpler fractions, one over each factor.","Write it as A over one factor plus B over the other, multiply through by the denominator and then substitute values of x that make a bracket vanish.","8x + 3 = A(x + 3) + B(2x − 1)."],"codes":["2.10"]},{"q":"The curve C has equation y = 4/(x + 3) − 2. Write down the equations of the two asymptotes of C and find the coordinates of the point where C crosses the x-axis.","a":"x = −3 and y = −2; crosses the x-axis at (−1, 0)","sol":"The denominator is zero when x + 3 = 0, so the vertical asymptote is x = −3 (B1). As |x| grows, 4/(x + 3) tends to 0, so y tends to −2 and the horizontal asymptote is y = −2 (B1). Where C crosses the x-axis, y = 0, so 4/(x + 3) = 2 (M1), giving x + 3 = 2 and x = −1. The point is (−1, 0) (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is the curve y = 4/x after a translation, so think about the values x and y can never take.","The vertical asymptote is where the denominator would be zero; the horizontal one is the value y approaches as x becomes very large.","Solve x + 3 = 0 for one asymptote, and put y = 0 to get 4/(x + 3) = 2 for the crossing point."],"codes":["2.7"]},{"q":"The curve with equation y = f(x) has a maximum point at (−2, 6). Write down the coordinates of the maximum point of the curve with equation (a) y = f(x + 3), (b) y = 2f(x) − 1.","a":"(a) (−5, 6); (b) (−2, 11)","sol":"(a) y = f(x + 3) is a translation of 3 in the negative x-direction, so the x-coordinate becomes −2 − 3 = −5 while the y-coordinate is unchanged: the maximum is at (−5, 6) (M1 A1). (b) y = 2f(x) − 1 is a stretch parallel to the y-axis of scale factor 2 followed by a translation of 1 downwards, so the x-coordinate stays at −2 and the y-coordinate becomes 2 × 6 − 1 = 11: the maximum is at (−2, 11) (M1 A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A change inside the bracket moves the graph sideways; changes outside the function act on the y-coordinate.","For (a) ask what x must be so that the bracket takes the value that gave the maximum before; for (b) apply the vertical operations, in order, to the y-coordinate.","For part (a), solve x + 3 = −2."],"codes":["2.9"]},{"q":"Sketch the graph of y = |4x − 8| and hence solve the inequality |4x − 8| > x + 3, giving your answer in set notation.","a":"{x : x < 1} ∪ {x : x > 11/3}","sol":"y = |4x − 8| is a V with its vertex on the x-axis at (2, 0) and y-intercept 8: it is y = 4x − 8 for x ≥ 2 and y = 8 − 4x for x < 2 (B1). Draw y = x + 3, a line of gradient 1 through (0, 3), on the same axes (B1). The V meets the line where each branch meets it (M1). Right branch: 4x − 8 = x + 3 gives 3x = 11, so x = 11/3, and 11/3 ≥ 2, so this crossing is on that branch (A1). Left branch: 8 − 4x = x + 3 gives 5 = 5x, so x = 1, and 1 < 2, so this crossing is valid too (A1). The sketch shows the V above the line outside the two crossings, so the solution is x < 1 or x > 11/3, that is {x : x < 1} ∪ {x : x > 11/3} (A1). Giving the region between the crossings scores no final mark.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The modulus graph is a V, and the question asks where that V sits above a straight line.","Write the two branches of y = |4x − 8| as separate linear equations, find where each one meets y = x + 3, keep only a crossing that lies on the branch it came from, then read the regions off the sketch.","For x ≥ 2 the branch is y = 4x − 8, so solve 4x − 8 = x + 3 there."],"codes":["2.7","2.5"]},{"q":"The equation x² + (k − 3)x + (3 − 2k) = 0, where k is a constant, has two distinct real roots. Show that k² + 2k − 3 > 0, and hence find the set of possible values of k.","a":"k < −3 or k > 1","sol":"Here a = 1, b = k − 3 and c = 3 − 2k, and two distinct real roots requires b² − 4ac > 0 (M1). So (k − 3)² − 4(3 − 2k) > 0, that is k² − 6k + 9 − 12 + 8k > 0 (M1), which simplifies to k² + 2k − 3 > 0 (A1, cso — the result is printed, so every line of the expansion must be seen). Factorising, (k + 3)(k − 1) > 0, with critical values k = −3 and k = 1 (M1). The parabola in k opens upwards, so the expression is positive outside the roots: k < −3 or k > 1 (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["'Two distinct real roots' is a statement about the discriminant, not about the roots themselves.","Form b² − 4ac with a = 1, b = k − 3 and c = 3 − 2k, expand carefully with the signs, then solve the quadratic inequality in k.","(k − 3)² − 4(3 − 2k) > 0."],"codes":["2.3","2.5"]},{"q":"f(x) = x³ − 7x + 6. Factorise f(x) completely, and hence solve the inequality f(x) < 0.","a":"f(x) = (x − 1)(x − 2)(x + 3); f(x) < 0 for x < −3 or 1 < x < 2","sol":"A root must be among the factors of the constant term 6, so test them: f(1) = 1 − 7 + 6 = 0, so by the factor theorem (x − 1) is a factor (M1 A1). Write the cubic as x³ + 0x² − 7x + 6 and divide by (x − 1), by long division or by comparing coefficients in (x − 1)(x² + ax + b): the quotient is x² + x − 6 (M1 A1). That factorises as (x + 3)(x − 2), so f(x) = (x − 1)(x − 2)(x + 3) (A1). The roots are −3, 1 and 2 and the x³ coefficient is positive, so the curve comes up from the bottom left and crosses the x-axis at each root in turn; it is therefore below the axis for x < −3 and again between x = 1 and x = 2 (M1). Hence f(x) < 0 for x < −3 or 1 < x < 2 (A1). A sketch or a sign table is the evidence for choosing the two regions.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["A cubic with no bracket in sight still has a root hiding among the factors of its constant term.","Find one root by trial, divide f(x) by the factor it gives, factorise the quadratic that is left, then use the shape of a positive cubic through its three roots to decide where the curve lies below the x-axis.","Evaluate f(1), remembering that the cubic is x³ + 0x² − 7x + 6 when you come to divide."],"codes":["2.6","2.7"]},{"q":"The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = √(2x − 6), x ∈ ℝ, x ≥ 3, and g(x) = 3x + 1, x ∈ ℝ. Find fg(x) in its simplest form, state the domain of fg, and hence solve fg(x) = 4.","a":"fg(x) = √(6x − 4), domain x ≥ 2/3; x = 10/3","sol":"fg means do g first, so fg(x) = f(3x + 1) = √(2(3x + 1) − 6) (M1) = √(6x + 2 − 6) = √(6x − 4) (A1). fg only exists where g(x) lands inside the domain of f, so 3x + 1 ≥ 3 (M1), giving x ≥ 2/3: the domain of fg is x ≥ 2/3 (A1). This is the same condition as 6x − 4 ≥ 0, which is what keeps the square root defined. Solving fg(x) = 4: √(6x − 4) = 4, and squaring both sides, using (√x)² = x, gives 6x − 4 = 16 (M1), so 6x = 20 and x = 10/3 (A1). Since 10/3 ≥ 2/3 this value lies in the domain of fg and is accepted; a root outside the domain would have to be rejected (B1).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["fg is built by feeding the whole of g into f, and a function can only be fed values its own domain allows.","Replace every x in f by the expression for g(x) and tidy the surd; then make g(x) satisfy the restriction on the domain of f to get the domain of fg, and square the equation to solve it.","fg(x) = f(3x + 1) = √(2(3x + 1) − 6), and the domain comes from 3x + 1 ≥ 3."],"codes":["2.8","2.2"]},{"q":"The braking distance d metres of a car is modelled as directly proportional to the square of its speed v m s⁻¹. A car travelling at 12 m s⁻¹ has a braking distance of 9 m. Find d when v = 20, and state one limitation of using this model to predict the braking distance at 60 m s⁻¹.","a":"d = 25 m; the constant was fitted from one low-speed measurement, so predicting at 60 m s⁻¹ extrapolates far outside the data and ignores road, tyre and brake conditions","sol":"Direct proportion to v² means d = kv² (M1). Substituting d = 9 and v = 12: 9 = k(144), so k = 1/16 (A1). The model is d = v²/16, so when v = 20, d = 400/16 = 25, that is d = 25 m (M1 A1). Limitation (B1): k was found from a single measurement at 12 m s⁻¹, so using the model at 60 m s⁻¹ extrapolates far beyond the range of the data; the model also takes no account of road surface, tyre condition, load or the state of the brakes, so the predicted distance would be unreliable. Any one clear, contextual limitation earns the mark.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["'Directly proportional to the square of v' can be written as an equation containing one unknown constant.","Write d = kv², use the given pair of values to find k, then use the completed model at the new speed; for the limitation, think about where the data came from.","9 = k × 12²."],"codes":["2.11","2.7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Simplify √75 − √12.","a":"3√3"},{"q":"Write x² + 8x + 3 in the form (x + p)² + q.","a":"(x + 4)² − 13"},{"q":"Solve the inequality x² − x − 12 ≥ 0.","a":"x ≤ −3 or x ≥ 4"},{"q":"f(x) = 5x + 2 and g(x) = x³. Find fg(x).","a":"fg(x) = 5x³ + 2"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The three laws of indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = a^(m + n); aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = a^(m − n); (aᵐ)ⁿ = a^(mn). They hold for all rational m and n.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"Fractional index a^(m/n)","back":"a^(m/n) = ⁿ√(aᵐ) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ — the nth root of the mth power, or the mth power of the nth root.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"a⁰ and a^(−n)","back":"a⁰ = 1 and a^(−n) = 1/aⁿ, for any a ≠ 0.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"The three surd results you may quote","back":"(√x)² = x; √(xy) = √x √y; (√x + √y)(√x − √y) = x − y.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"Rationalising a denominator a − √b","back":"Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate a + √b, since (a − √b)(a + √b) = a² − b, which is rational.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"The quadratic formula and what the discriminant tells you","back":"x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). b² − 4ac > 0: two distinct real roots; = 0: one repeated root; < 0: no real roots.","code":"2.3","open":false},{"front":"Completed-square form of ax² + bx + c","back":"a(x + b/(2a))² + (c − b²/(4a)). The vertex is at x = −b/(2a), and the bracket being zero gives the least value when a > 0.","code":"2.3","open":false},{"front":"Method for one linear and one quadratic simultaneous equation","back":"Substitute the linear equation into the quadratic to get one quadratic in one unknown, solve it, then substitute each root back to find the paired values.","code":"2.4","open":false},{"front":"Set notation for the solution of a quadratic inequality","back":"x < a or x > b is {x : x < a} ∪ {x : x > b}; c < x < d is {x : c < x} ∩ {x : x < d}.","code":"2.5","open":false},{"front":"The factor theorem","back":"If f(b/a) = 0 then (ax − b) is a factor of f(x); in particular f(a) = 0 means (x − a) is a factor.","code":"2.6","open":false},{"front":"Asymptotes of y = a/(x + b) + c","back":"Vertical asymptote x = −b (the denominator is zero); horizontal asymptote y = c (the value y approaches for large |x|).","code":"2.7","open":false},{"front":"Composite function fg(x)","back":"Do g first, then f: fg(x) = f(g(x)). It exists only where g(x) lies in the domain of f.","code":"2.8","open":false},{"front":"Properties of an inverse function","back":"f⁻¹f(x) = ff⁻¹(x) = x; f⁻¹ exists only if f is one-one. The graph of y = f⁻¹(x) is y = f(x) reflected in y = x, and the domain of f⁻¹ is the range of f.","code":"2.8","open":false},{"front":"The four transformations of y = f(x)","back":"f(x) + a: translation (0, a). f(x + a): translation (−a, 0). af(x): y-stretch, scale factor a. f(ax): x-stretch, scale factor 1/a.","code":"2.9","open":false},{"front":"Partial fraction form with a squared linear factor","back":"(px + q)/((ax + b)(cx + d)²) = A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d) + C/(cx + d)².","code":"2.10","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = a^(m + n), aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = a^(m − n), (aᵐ)ⁿ = a^(mn), a⁰ = 1, a^(−n) = 1/aⁿ, a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a, a^(m/n) = ⁿ√(aᵐ) — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Surd results: (√x)² = x, √(xy) = √x √y, (√x + √y)(√x − √y) = x − y, and (a − √b)(a + √b) = a² − b for rationalising — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Quadratic formula: x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a) for ax² + bx + c = 0 — write from memory; the A level Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet does not give it.","Discriminant: b² − 4ac, with > 0 two distinct real roots, = 0 one repeated root, < 0 no real roots — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Completing the square: ax² + bx + c = a(x + b/(2a))² + (c − b²/(4a)), vertex at x = −b/(2a) — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Factor theorem: if f(b/a) = 0 then (ax − b) is a factor of f(x) — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Difference of two squares: x² − a² = (x − a)(x + a), used constantly when cancelling algebraic fractions — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Composite and inverse functions: fg(x) = f(g(x)) (g first); f⁻¹f(x) = ff⁻¹(x) = x; domain of f⁻¹ = range of f; y = f⁻¹(x) is y = f(x) reflected in y = x — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Transformations of y = f(x): f(x) + a is a translation of (0, a); f(x + a) is a translation of (−a, 0); af(x) is a stretch parallel to the y-axis of scale factor a; f(ax) is a stretch parallel to the x-axis of scale factor 1/a — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Modulus graphs: y = |f(x)| reflects in the x-axis any part of y = f(x) below it; y = |ax + b| is a V with vertex where ax + b = 0 — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Asymptotes: y = a/(x + b) + c has asymptotes x = −b and y = c; y = a/x² has asymptotes x = 0 and y = 0 — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Proportion: y ∝ x means y = kx (a line through the origin, gradient k); y ∝ 1/x means y = k/x; y ∝ x² means y = kx² — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Partial fractions: (px + q)/((ax + b)(cx + d)) = A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d), and (px + q)/((ax + b)(cx + d)²) = A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d) + C/(cx + d)² — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","Set notation: {x : x < a} ∪ {x : x > b} for 'x < a or x > b'; {x : c < x} ∩ {x : x < d} for 'c < x and x < d' — write from memory; not in the formulae booklet.","What the booklet gives you for this room: nothing. The Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet provides the arithmetic and geometric series sums, the binomial series, the compound-angle and small-angle results, the trapezium rule, Newton-Raphson and the statistical tables — none of which belong to algebra and functions. Appendix 1 of the specification lists the quadratic formula and the laws of indices (and also the laws of logarithms, the identities cos²A + sin²A ≡ 1, sec²A ≡ 1 + tan²A, cosec²A ≡ 1 + cot²A, sin 2A, cos 2A and tan 2A, and the standard differentiation and integration tables) as results you must remember, so every result above must come from memory."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where the marks go in this room","items":["'Exact' forbids decimals: leave surds, fractions and π as they are, or the final A mark is lost.","In a 'show that' the printed result earns nothing — every line of expansion and simplification down to it must be seen (cso).","Simultaneous equations: state the answers in pairs. Two x-values and two y-values listed separately lose the final mark.","Quadratic inequalities: show the sketch or the sign table. The critical values alone do not prove which region you want.","When a domain is restricted, check whether the vertex of the quadratic is inside it before writing down the range.","Describing a transformation needs the type and the size: 'stretch parallel to the x-axis, scale factor 1/2', not just 'stretch by 1/2'.","Cancel brackets, never terms: (x + 2)(x − 3)/(x + 2) simplifies, but (x + 2)/(x + 5) does not."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:23:03Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-solved all 16 bank questions, the 4 check questions, the 4 exit questions and all 6 worked examples independently in sympy and every key is correct and in the form asked; the eleven faults the first pass raised are all genuinely fixed — the rationalising, cubic-cancelling and quadratic-range questions have been replaced by a rectangle-perimeter surd question, a factorise-then-solve-f(x)<0 cubic and a composite-function question that now asks for the domain of fg, a modulus sketch-and-inequality question (index 11) has been added so 2.7 is tested as well as taught, the three question-shaped card fronts are now named results, the misplaced 'method mark' line in example 1 and the 'cross at two points' giveaway in example 6 are gone, the lesson no longer pre-answers the 4^x question, and the Formulae sheet's booklet summary now matches Appendix 1 exactly (quadratic formula and laws of indices from memory, booklet gives nothing for this room)."}}},"P3":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P3","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Coordinate geometry is where algebra is made to describe a picture, and Pearson examine it on Papers 1 and 2 every year: a straight line question that must end in the form ax + by + c = 0, a circle whose centre and radius are hidden inside an expanded equation, a tangent built from the radius it meets at right angles, and — in Year 2 — a parametric curve you must convert to Cartesian form or use as a model of a motion. Almost every mark here is a method mark, so the gradient you write down, the completed square and the line y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) all score before any answer appears; a correct answer with no working scores very little. A quick sketch catches the sign errors that cost the accuracy marks.","idea":[{"h":"Straight lines: gradient, the two standard forms, parallel and perpendicular","t":"A line is fixed by a gradient and one point on it. Gradient m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁); the line through (x₁, y₁) is y − y₁ = m(x − x₁), rearranged to ax + by + c = 0 with integer a, b, c only when the question asks. Parallel lines satisfy m′ = m, perpendicular lines m′ = −1/m, so their gradients multiply to −1. Straight line models — °C to °F, distance against time at constant speed, a fixed cost plus a rate — are the same algebra with units and a meaning attached, and examiners expect the gradient and the intercept to be interpreted in context.","code":"3.1"},{"h":"Circles: centre and radius, and the three circle properties","t":"(x − a)² + (y − b)² = r² is the circle of centre (a, b) and radius r. Given the expanded form x² + y² + 2fx + 2gy + c = 0, complete the square in x and in y to recover centre (−f, −g) and radius √(f² + g² − c). Three properties drive almost every circle question: the angle in a semicircle is a right angle (so the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is a diameter of its circumcircle); the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord (so the perpendicular bisector of a chord passes through the centre); and the radius is perpendicular to the tangent at the point of contact, which is how every tangent equation is built.","code":"3.2"},{"h":"Parametric equations and conversion to Cartesian form","t":"Parametric equations give x and y separately in terms of a parameter t, so the curve is traced as t runs through its domain. To convert to Cartesian form, eliminate t: make it the subject of the simpler equation and substitute, or — when t appears inside trigonometric functions — use an identity such as cos²t + sin²t = 1, or first rewrite cos 2t in terms of sin t or cos t. x = a + r cos t, y = b + r sin t is the circle centre (a, b) radius r; x = 5t, y = 5/t is xy = 25. Always check what the domain of t does to the curve: a restricted t often gives only part of the Cartesian curve, and the range of x or y must be stated.","code":"3.3"},{"h":"Parametric equations as a model","t":"In a modelling question the parameter is usually time. Constant velocity means each coordinate is linear in t: x = x₀ + pt, y = y₀ + qt, where p and q are the constant rates of change per second and the speed is √(p² + q²). A projectile has x linear in t and y quadratic in t, so eliminating t gives a parabola. The marks go to translating the given information into the parameters (AO3), doing the algebra, and then interpreting the answer in context with units — and, where asked, commenting on the limitations of the model, such as ignoring air resistance or tide.","code":"3.4"}],"examples":[{"title":"A perpendicular line through a given point, answer in the form ax + by + c = 0","setup":"The line l₁ has equation 3x + 4y − 7 = 0. The line l₂ passes through the point P(−2, 5) and is perpendicular to l₁. Find an equation for l₂, giving your answer in the form ax + by + c = 0 where a, b and c are integers.","steps":["Rearrange l₁ into gradient form: 4y = −3x + 7, so y = −(3/4)x + 7/4 and the gradient of l₁ is −3/4.","Perpendicular gradients satisfy m′ = −1/m, so the gradient of l₂ is −1 ÷ (−3/4) = 4/3.","Use y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) with P: y − 5 = (4/3)(x + 2), which is the line that earns the method mark.","Multiply by 3 and collect: 3y − 15 = 4x + 8, so 4x − 3y + 23 = 0."],"cues":["What has to happen to 3x + 4y − 7 = 0 before its gradient can be read off?","Which rule turns the gradient of l₁ into the gradient of l₂?","Which form of a line equation uses a gradient and the point P directly?","How do you reach integer coefficients from a fractional gradient?"]},{"title":"Centre and radius by completing the square, and testing a point","setup":"The circle C has equation x² + y² − 6x + 8y − 11 = 0. Find the centre and the radius of C, and determine whether the point P(8, 1) lies inside C, on C or outside C.","steps":["Group the x terms and the y terms and complete the square: x² − 6x = (x − 3)² − 9 and y² + 8y = (y + 4)² − 16.","Substitute back: (x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 4)² − 16 − 11 = 0, so (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 36.","Compare with (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²: the centre is (3, −4) and the radius is √36 = 6.","Distance from the centre to P: distance² = (8 − 3)² + (1 + 4)² = 25 + 25 = 50, so the distance is √50 ≈ 7.07.","Since √50 > 6, P lies outside C."],"cues":["Which two groups of terms have to be completed as squares?","What happens to the constants left over once each bracket is formed?","Which standard form do you compare with to read the centre and radius?","What distance decides where P sits relative to the circle?","What does that distance, set against the radius, tell you about P?"]},{"title":"The tangent to a circle at a point on its circumference","setup":"The circle C has equation (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 25, and the point A(7, −1) lies on C. Find an equation of the tangent to C at A, giving your answer in the form ax + by + c = 0 where a, b and c are integers.","steps":["Compare with (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²: C has centre (3, −4) and r² = 25, and the radius to A has gradient (−1 + 4)/(7 − 3) = 3/4.","The tangent at A is perpendicular to that radius, so its gradient is −1 ÷ (3/4) = −4/3.","Use y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) at A: y + 1 = −(4/3)(x − 7).","Multiply by 3: 3y + 3 = −4x + 28, so 4x + 3y − 25 = 0."],"cues":["Where does the radius drawn to A start, and what is its gradient?","How is the tangent at A related to that radius?","Which line form uses the gradient just found together with A?","How do you clear the fraction to reach integer coefficients?"]},{"title":"Showing a pair of parametric equations describes a circle","setup":"A curve C has parametric equations x = 2 + 5cos t, y = −4 + 5sin t, for 0 ≤ t < 2π. Show that C is a circle, and state its centre and radius.","steps":["Make the trigonometric functions the subjects: cos t = (x − 2)/5 and sin t = (y + 4)/5.","Use the identity cos²t + sin²t = 1: ((x − 2)/5)² + ((y + 4)/5)² = 1.","Multiply through by 25: (x − 2)² + (y + 4)² = 25.","That is the standard circle form, so C is a circle with centre (2, −4) and radius 5."],"cues":["What links the two equations if you isolate the trigonometric functions?","Which identity connects cos t and sin t as a sum of squares?","How do you clear the denominators from both brackets?","What shape does the resulting equation describe, and with what centre and radius?"]},{"title":"Parametric equations for motion at constant velocity","setup":"An object moves in a straight line with constant velocity. At time t = 0 seconds it is at the point (1, 8) and at t = 5 seconds it is at the point (6, 20), where the coordinates are in metres. Find parametric equations for its position in terms of t, and find its position at t = 8 seconds.","steps":["Constant velocity means each coordinate changes at a fixed rate, so both are linear in t: x = x₀ + pt and y = y₀ + qt.","The starting position fixes the constants: x₀ = 1 and y₀ = 8.","The rates are p = (6 − 1)/5 = 1 and q = (20 − 8)/5 = 2.4, giving x = 1 + t and y = 8 + 2.4t.","Put t = 8 into both: x = 1 + 8 = 9 and y = 8 + 2.4 × 8 = 27.2, so the object is at (9, 27.2) metres."],"cues":["What does constant velocity tell you about how x and y depend on t?","Which piece of information fixes the value of each coordinate when the motion starts?","How do the two given positions give you the rate of change of each coordinate?","How do your equations give the position later in the motion?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The line l has equation 5x − 2y + 3 = 0. What is its gradient?","a":"5/2"},{"q":"State the centre and radius of the circle (x + 2)² + (y − 7)² = 49.","a":"Centre (−2, 7), radius 7"},{"q":"A curve has parametric equations x = 4t, y = 4/t, t ≠ 0. What is its Cartesian equation?","a":"xy = 16 (or y = 16/x)"},{"q":"What is the gradient of any line perpendicular to a line of gradient −2/3?","a":"3/2"}]},"room":{"facts":["Gradient m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁); the line through (x₁, y₁) with gradient m is y − y₁ = m(x − x₁), which rearranges to ax + by + c = 0.","Parallel lines: m′ = m. Perpendicular lines: m′ = −1/m, so mm′ = −1.","Distance between (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²); the midpoint is ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2).","Circle of centre (a, b) and radius r: (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². In the form x² + y² + 2fx + 2gy + c = 0 the centre is (−f, −g) and the radius is √(f² + g² − c).","The angle in a semicircle is a right angle; the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord; the radius at a point of the circumference is perpendicular to the tangent there.","cos²t + sin²t = 1 is the identity that converts x = a + r cos t, y = b + r sin t into (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r².","Constant velocity from (x₀, y₀): x = x₀ + pt, y = y₀ + qt, with speed √(p² + q²)."],"questions":[{"q":"The points A(−1, 4) and B(3, 12) lie on the line l. Find an equation for l, giving your answer in the form y = mx + c.","a":"y = 2x + 6","sol":"Gradient m = (12 − 4)/(3 − (−1)) = 8/4 = 2 (M1 for a correct gradient expression, A1 for m = 2). Using y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) with A(−1, 4): y − 4 = 2(x + 1) (M1). Expanding gives y = 2x + 6 (A1 cao). Check with B: 2 × 3 + 6 = 12, so B lies on l.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A line is pinned down once you know its gradient and one point that lies on it.","Work out the change in y divided by the change in x between A and B, then feed that gradient and either point into the point–gradient form.","Your final line should read y = (gradient)x + (intercept); open by writing m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) with the coordinates of A and B substituted in."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"The line l₁ has equation y = (2/5)x − 3. The line l₂ is perpendicular to l₁ and passes through the point P(4, 1). Find an equation for l₂, giving your answer in the form ax + by + c = 0, where a, b and c are integers.","a":"5x + 2y − 22 = 0","sol":"The gradient of l₁ is 2/5, so the gradient of l₂ is m′ = −1/m = −5/2 (M1 for using m′ = −1/m, A1). Using y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) at P(4, 1): y − 1 = −(5/2)(x − 4) (M1). Multiplying through by 2: 2y − 2 = −5x + 20, so 5x + 2y − 22 = 0 (A1 cao). Check: 5 × 4 + 2 × 1 − 22 = 0.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Perpendicular lines have gradients whose product is −1.","Read the gradient of l₁ straight from its equation, turn it into the perpendicular gradient with m′ = −1/m, then use P in the point–gradient form.","Integer coefficients are asked for, so expect to multiply through by the denominator of the gradient at the end; open with y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) using the coordinates of P."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"The circle C has equation x² + y² − 6x + 8y − 11 = 0. Find the centre and the radius of C.","a":"Centre (3, −4), radius 6","sol":"Complete the square in x and in y: x² − 6x = (x − 3)² − 9 and y² + 8y = (y + 4)² − 16 (M1). So (x − 3)² − 9 + (y + 4)² − 16 − 11 = 0, giving (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 36 (A1). Comparing with (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r², the centre is (3, −4) (A1) and the radius is √36 = 6 (A1). The same answer follows from x² + y² + 2fx + 2gy + c = 0 with f = −3, g = 4, c = −11: centre (−f, −g) = (3, −4) and r = √(9 + 16 + 11) = 6.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["This is the expanded form of (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r², so the two brackets have to be rebuilt.","Complete the square on the x terms and on the y terms separately, then move every constant across to the right-hand side.","The answer is a pair of coordinates and a length; open by writing x² − 6x as a completed square, then do the same for the y terms."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"A circle has centre (−2, 5) and radius 3√2. Find an equation of the circle in the form (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r².","a":"(x + 2)² + (y − 5)² = 18","sol":"With centre (a, b) = (−2, 5) the brackets are (x − (−2))² = (x + 2)² and (y − 5)² (M1). The right-hand side is r² = (3√2)² = 9 × 2 = 18 (B1). Hence (x + 2)² + (y − 5)² = 18 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The standard form of a circle carries the centre inside the brackets and the radius on the right.","Subtract each coordinate of the centre inside its own bracket, watching the sign of the negative coordinate, and square the radius for the right-hand side.","The right-hand side is a single whole number rather than a surd; open by squaring 3√2."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"A curve C has parametric equations x = 5t, y = 5/t, t ≠ 0. Find a Cartesian equation for C.","a":"xy = 25 (equivalently y = 25/x)","sol":"From x = 5t, t = x/5 (M1). Substituting into y = 5/t gives y = 5 ÷ (x/5) = 25/x (M1). Multiplying by x, xy = 25 (A1). Since t ≠ 0, x ≠ 0: C is the hyperbola xy = 25 (every point of the hyperbola is reached, since t = x/5 exists for each non-zero x).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A Cartesian equation contains no t at all.","Make t the subject of whichever equation is easier to rearrange, then substitute it into the other one.","Expect a hyperbola, written either as xy = k or as y = k/x; open by writing t in terms of x from x = 5t."],"codes":["3.3"]},{"q":"The circle C has equation x² + y² − 4x + 6y − 12 = 0 and the point A(5, 1) lies on C. Find an equation of the tangent to C at A, giving your answer in the form ax + by + c = 0, where a, b and c are integers.","a":"3x + 4y − 19 = 0","sol":"Completing the square: (x − 2)² − 4 + (y + 3)² − 9 − 12 = 0, so (x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25, giving centre (2, −3) and radius 5 (M1 A1). The radius from (2, −3) to A(5, 1) has gradient (1 − (−3))/(5 − 2) = 4/3 (M1). The tangent at A is perpendicular to that radius, so its gradient is −1 ÷ (4/3) = −3/4 (M1). Then y − 1 = −(3/4)(x − 5), so 4y − 4 = −3x + 15 and 3x + 4y − 19 = 0 (M1 A1 cao). Check A: 3 × 5 + 4 × 1 − 19 = 0.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["A tangent meets the radius drawn to its point of contact at right angles.","Complete the square to find the centre, find the gradient of the radius joining the centre to A, then turn it into the perpendicular gradient before using the point–gradient form.","Integer coefficients are asked for, so the fraction must be cleared at the end; open by completing the square on x² − 4x and on y² + 6y."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"A straight line model converts a temperature of C degrees Celsius into F degrees Fahrenheit. When F is plotted against C the line passes through (0, 32) and (100, 212). Find F in terms of C, and hence find the temperature at which the two scales give the same number.","a":"F = 1.8C + 32 (that is, F = 9C/5 + 32); the scales agree at −40, since −40 °C = −40 °F","sol":"Gradient = (212 − 32)/(100 − 0) = 180/100 = 1.8 (M1 A1). The point (0, 32) gives the intercept directly, so F = 1.8C + 32 (A1). The scales give the same number when F = C, so C = 1.8C + 32 (M1). Then −0.8C = 32 and C = −40 (A1). So −40 °C is the same temperature as −40 °F; the gradient 1.8 means each 1 °C rise is a 1.8 °F rise.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The two pairs of values are simply two points on a straight line.","Find the gradient from the two points and read the intercept off the point whose C value is zero; for the second part, set F equal to C and solve.","The model should look like F = (gradient)C + (intercept); open by writing the gradient as the change in F over the change in C."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"A curve C has parametric equations x = −3 + 4cos t, y = 1 + 4sin t, 0 ≤ t < 2π. Show that C is a circle, and state its centre and radius.","a":"(x + 3)² + (y − 1)² = 16, a circle with centre (−3, 1) and radius 4","sol":"Rearranging: cos t = (x + 3)/4 and sin t = (y − 1)/4 (M1). Using cos²t + sin²t = 1: ((x + 3)/4)² + ((y − 1)/4)² = 1 (M1). Multiplying by 16: (x + 3)² + (y − 1)² = 16 (A1). This matches (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r², so C is a circle with centre (−3, 1) and radius 4 (A1 cso). As t runs over 0 ≤ t < 2π the whole circle is traced exactly once.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Only one identity links cos t and sin t, and it produces a sum of two squares.","Make cos t and sin t the subjects of the two equations, then substitute both into that identity.","The final line has to match (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²; open by writing cos t in terms of x."],"codes":["3.3","3.2"]},{"q":"The points A(1, 2) and B(7, 10) are the ends of a diameter of the circle C. Find an equation for C in the form (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r².","a":"(x − 4)² + (y − 6)² = 25","sol":"The centre is the midpoint of AB: ((1 + 7)/2, (2 + 10)/2) = (4, 6) (M1 A1). The diameter has length AB = √((7 − 1)² + (10 − 2)²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10 (M1), so the radius is 5 and r² = 25 (A1). Hence C is (x − 4)² + (y − 6)² = 25 (A1). Check A: (1 − 4)² + (2 − 6)² = 9 + 16 = 25.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Every diameter passes through the centre, and the centre sits exactly halfway along it.","Take the midpoint of AB for the centre and half the length of AB for the radius, using the distance formula.","Only r², not r, appears in the final equation; open by writing down the midpoint of A and B."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"Find the coordinates of the points where the line y = x + 1 meets the circle x² + y² = 25.","a":"(−4, −3) and (3, 4)","sol":"Substitute y = x + 1 into the circle: x² + (x + 1)² = 25 (M1). Expanding: 2x² + 2x + 1 = 25, so 2x² + 2x − 24 = 0 and x² + x − 12 = 0 (A1). Factorising: (x + 4)(x − 3) = 0, so x = −4 or x = 3 (M1 A1). Substituting back into y = x + 1 gives y = −3 and y = 4, so the points are (−4, −3) and (3, 4) (A1). Both satisfy x² + y² = 16 + 9 = 25 and 9 + 16 = 25.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["A point of intersection satisfies both equations at the same time.","Replace y in the circle equation by the expression from the line, then solve the quadratic in x that results.","You should end with two pairs of coordinates; open by substituting x + 1 for y in x² + y² = 25."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"A ball is thrown from the origin and its path is modelled by the parametric equations x = 5t, y = 20t − 5t², where x and y are the horizontal and vertical distances in metres and t is the time in seconds, t ≥ 0. Find a Cartesian equation of the path, and find the horizontal distance travelled before the ball returns to the ground (y = 0).","a":"y = 4x − x²/5; the horizontal distance is 20 m","sol":"From x = 5t, t = x/5 (M1). Substituting: y = 20(x/5) − 5(x/5)² = 4x − 5x²/25 = 4x − x²/5 (M1 A1). Setting y = 0: x(4 − x/5) = 0 (M1), so x = 0 at the throw or x = 20 on landing (A1). The ball lands 20 m from the point of release. Checking with the parameter: 20t − 5t² = 5t(4 − t) = 0 gives t = 4 s, and x = 5 × 4 = 20 m.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The path itself makes no mention of time, so t has to go.","Make t the subject of the horizontal equation and substitute it into the vertical one; then set the height to zero and factorise.","The path is a quadratic in x and the distance is a length in metres; open by writing t in terms of x from x = 5t."],"codes":["3.3","3.4"]},{"q":"The triangle ABC has vertices A(−1, 2), B(3, 8) and C(9, 4). Show that angle ABC is a right angle, and hence find an equation of the circle that passes through A, B and C.","a":"(x − 4)² + (y − 3)² = 26","sol":"Gradient AB = (8 − 2)/(3 − (−1)) = 6/4 = 3/2 and gradient BC = (4 − 8)/(9 − 3) = −4/6 = −2/3 (M1 A1). Their product is (3/2) × (−2/3) = −1, so AB is perpendicular to BC and angle ABC = 90° (A1 cso). The angle in a semicircle is a right angle, so AC must be a diameter of the circle through A, B and C (B1). Centre = midpoint of AC = ((−1 + 9)/2, (2 + 4)/2) = (4, 3) (M1 A1). r² = (4 − (−1))² + (3 − 2)² = 25 + 1 = 26 (M1). Hence the circumcircle is (x − 4)² + (y − 3)² = 26 (A1). Check B: (3 − 4)² + (8 − 3)² = 1 + 25 = 26.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Two sides of the triangle meet at B, so compare their directions.","Show that the gradients of AB and BC multiply to −1, then use the circle property that tells you which side of a right-angled triangle must be a diameter.","The final answer is a circle in completed-square form; open with the gradient of AB from the coordinates of A and B."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"A curve C has parametric equations x = 2t + 1, y = t² − 3, for −2 ≤ t ≤ 3. Find a Cartesian equation for C in the form y = f(x), and state the domain of f.","a":"y = (x − 1)²/4 − 3, with domain −3 ≤ x ≤ 7","sol":"From x = 2t + 1, t = (x − 1)/2 (M1). Substituting: y = ((x − 1)/2)² − 3 = (x − 1)²/4 − 3 (M1 A1). For the domain, x = 2t + 1 increases with t, so t = −2 gives x = 2(−2) + 1 = −3 and t = 3 gives x = 2(3) + 1 = 7 (M1). Hence the domain is −3 ≤ x ≤ 7 (A1). C is therefore the part of the parabola with vertex (1, −3) lying between those two x values.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The restriction on t does not vanish when t does — it becomes a restriction on x.","Rearrange the linear equation for t and substitute into the equation for y, then put each end value of t into the equation for x.","The answer is a quadratic in x together with an inequality in x; open by making t the subject of x = 2t + 1."],"codes":["3.3"]},{"q":"A boat moves with constant velocity across a harbour. With distances measured in metres from an origin at the harbour entrance and t measured in seconds, the boat is at (2, 3) when t = 0 and at (10, 9) when t = 4. Find parametric equations for the position of the boat at time t, find its speed, and find the value of t at which the boat crosses the line y = x.","a":"x = 2 + 2t, y = 3 + 1.5t; speed 2.5 m/s; t = 2 s","sol":"Constant velocity makes each coordinate linear in t, so x = 2 + pt and y = 3 + qt (M1). Using the position at t = 4: p = (10 − 2)/4 = 2 and q = (9 − 3)/4 = 1.5 (M1 A1), giving x = 2 + 2t and y = 3 + 1.5t (A1). Speed = √(p² + q²) = √(2² + 1.5²) = √6.25 = 2.5 m/s (M1 A1). On y = x: 3 + 1.5t = 2 + 2t (M1), so 1 = 0.5t and t = 2 (A1). At that moment the boat is at (6, 6), which does lie on y = x.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Constant velocity means each coordinate changes by the same amount every second.","Write each coordinate as its starting value plus a constant rate multiplied by t, find the two rates from the second position, then combine the rates using Pythagoras for the speed.","Expect two linear equations in t, a speed in m/s and a single time in seconds; open by writing x = 2 + pt and y = 3 + qt."],"codes":["3.4","3.1"]},{"q":"The line with equation y = 2x + k is a tangent to the circle x² + y² = 20. Find the two possible values of k.","a":"k = 10 and k = −10","sol":"Substituting y = 2x + k into x² + y² = 20: x² + (2x + k)² = 20 (M1), so 5x² + 4kx + k² − 20 = 0 (A1). A tangent touches the circle exactly once, so this quadratic has a repeated root and b² − 4ac = 0 (M1): (4k)² − 4 × 5 × (k² − 20) = 0 (A1). That is 16k² − 20k² + 400 = 0, so 4k² = 400 and k² = 100 (M1), giving k = 10 or k = −10 (A1). Equivalently, the perpendicular distance from the origin to the line must equal √20.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point, so the two equations have exactly one common solution.","Substitute the line into the circle to obtain a quadratic in x, then apply the condition on its discriminant that forces a repeated root.","You are looking for two values of k; open by replacing y in x² + y² = 20 with 2x + k."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"A curve C has parametric equations x = 3 + 2sin t, y = 4cos 2t, 0 ≤ t < 2π. Show that the Cartesian equation of C can be written as y = a + b(x − 3)², stating the values of the constants a and b, and state the range of possible values of x.","a":"y = 4 − 2(x − 3)², so a = 4 and b = −2, with 1 ≤ x ≤ 5","sol":"From x = 3 + 2sin t, sin t = (x − 3)/2 (M1). Using the double angle formula cos 2t = 1 − 2sin²t: y = 4(1 − 2sin²t) (M1). Substituting: y = 4(1 − 2((x − 3)/2)²) = 4(1 − (x − 3)²/2) = 4 − 2(x − 3)² (A1), so a = 4 and b = −2 (A1 cso). Since −1 ≤ sin t ≤ 1, x = 3 + 2sin t lies between 3 − 2 = 1 and 3 + 2 = 5 (M1), so 1 ≤ x ≤ 5 (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The two equations involve t differently, so one of them must be rewritten before they can be linked.","Express cos 2t in terms of sin t with a double angle formula, make sin t the subject of the x equation and substitute; for the last part, think about the largest and smallest values sin t can take.","The answer is a quadratic in (x − 3) together with an inequality in x; open by making sin t the subject of x = 3 + 2sin t."],"codes":["3.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the gradient of any line perpendicular to the line 3x − y + 5 = 0.","a":"−1/3"},{"q":"State the centre and radius of the circle (x − 7)² + (y + 2)² = 9.","a":"Centre (7, −2), radius 3"},{"q":"A curve has parametric equations x = 4cos t, y = 4sin t. Write down its Cartesian equation.","a":"x² + y² = 16"},{"q":"An object moves with constant velocity from (0, 5) at t = 0 to (12, 11) at t = 3. Write down parametric equations for its position at time t.","a":"x = 4t, y = 5 + 2t"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Gradient, distance and midpoint for the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂)","back":"Gradient m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁); distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²); midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2).","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"Equation of the line with gradient m through the point (x₁, y₁)","back":"y − y₁ = m(x − x₁), rearranged to ax + by + c = 0 with integer coefficients when the question asks for that form.","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"Gradient conditions for two straight lines to be parallel or perpendicular","back":"Parallel: m′ = m, equal gradients. Perpendicular: m′ = −1/m, so mm′ = −1; the exception is a horizontal line (m = 0) with a vertical line, whose gradient is undefined.","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"Name one context in which a straight line model is used","back":"Converting degrees Celsius to degrees Fahrenheit, F = 1.8C + 32; distance against time at constant speed; a fixed charge plus a constant rate per unit, such as a delivery cost.","code":"3.1","open":true},{"front":"Equation of the circle with centre (a, b) and radius r","back":"(x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². The right-hand side is r², not r.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"Centre and radius of x² + y² + 2fx + 2gy + c = 0","back":"Centre (−f, −g) and radius √(f² + g² − c), obtained by completing the square in x and in y.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"The angle in a semicircle","back":"It is a right angle: if AB is a diameter and P lies on the circle, angle APB = 90°. So the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is a diameter of its circumcircle.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord","back":"It bisects the chord, so it passes through the chord's midpoint; equivalently, the perpendicular bisector of any chord passes through the centre.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"How the radius and the tangent of a circle are related at the point of contact","back":"They are perpendicular, so the tangent's gradient is −1 divided by the gradient of the radius drawn to that point.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"How to find the circumcircle of a triangle with given vertices","back":"Find the centre where the perpendicular bisectors of two sides meet, or, if one angle is 90°, take the midpoint of the hypotenuse; the radius is the distance from that centre to a vertex.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"Identity used to convert x = a + r cos t, y = b + r sin t into Cartesian form","back":"cos²t + sin²t = 1, which gives (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r²: a circle of centre (a, b) and radius r.","code":"3.3","open":false},{"front":"How to convert parametric equations into Cartesian form","back":"Eliminate the parameter: make t the subject of the simpler equation and substitute, or use a trigonometric identity. Then state any restriction on x or y that the domain of t imposes.","code":"3.3","open":false},{"front":"Effect of a restricted domain of t on a curve given parametrically","back":"Only that arc is drawn: x ranges between its least and greatest values there, from the t-interval ends or a turning value inside (x = 3 + 2 sin t, 0 ≤ t < 2π: 1 ≤ x ≤ 5).","code":"3.3","open":false},{"front":"Parametric form of a projectile modelled with constant horizontal speed","back":"x is linear in t (x = ut) and y is quadratic in t (y = vt − kt²). Eliminating t gives a parabola; y = 0 at launch and at landing, and the greatest height is at the vertex.","code":"3.4","open":false},{"front":"Parametric equations of an object moving with constant velocity from (x₀, y₀)","back":"x = x₀ + pt, y = y₀ + qt, where p and q are the constant rates of change of x and y; the speed is √(p² + q²).","code":"3.4","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Gradient: m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) — not in the booklet, learn it.","Straight line through (x₁, y₁) with gradient m: y − y₁ = m(x − x₁); general form ax + by + c = 0 — not in the booklet, learn it.","Parallel lines: m′ = m. Perpendicular lines: m′ = −1/m (so mm′ = −1) — not in the booklet, learn it.","Distance between two points: √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²) — not in the booklet, learn it.","Midpoint of two points: ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2) — not in the booklet, learn it.","Circle, centre (a, b), radius r: (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r² — not in the booklet, learn it.","Expanded circle x² + y² + 2fx + 2gy + c = 0: centre (−f, −g), radius √(f² + g² − c) — not in the booklet; you are expected to reach it by completing the square.","Circle properties: the angle in a semicircle is a right angle; the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord; the radius at a point of the circumference is perpendicular to the tangent there — not in the booklet, learn all three.","Discriminant test for a line and a circle: b² − 4ac > 0 two intersections, = 0 tangent, < 0 no intersection — not in the booklet, learn it.","Pythagorean identity for parametric elimination: cos²t + sin²t = 1 — not in the booklet, learn it.","Double angle formulae, used when a parameter appears as 2t: sin 2t = 2 sin t cos t and cos 2t = cos²t − sin²t = 1 − 2sin²t = 2cos²t − 1 — not in the booklet, learn it.","Constant velocity model: x = x₀ + pt, y = y₀ + qt, speed √(p² + q²) — not in the booklet, learn it.","In short: the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet gives you nothing for coordinate geometry, so every result on this sheet must be written from memory."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where the marks go in coordinate geometry","items":["Write the gradient as a fraction before simplifying: the method mark is for (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) with the coordinates substituted, even if the arithmetic then slips.","Subtracting a negative coordinate is the commonest error: in y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) with x₁ = −2 the bracket is (x + 2).","'Giving your answer in the form ax + by + c = 0 where a, b and c are integers' means the fraction must be cleared; leaving y − 5 = (4/3)(x + 2) loses the final accuracy mark.","When completing the square, the constants taken out of both brackets and the constant already in the equation all move to the right-hand side together; forgetting one of them gives the wrong r².","A radius is a length, so take the positive square root: r = √(f² + g² − c), never ± .","For a tangent, always find the gradient of the radius first and then apply m′ = −1/m; quoting the tangent gradient with no radius gradient shown scores no method mark.","In a parametric question, the domain of t is examinable: state the resulting range of x (or y) whenever the parameter is restricted.","In a modelling question finish with a sentence in context and the unit — '2.5 m/s' or 'the ball lands 20 m from the thrower' — because the interpretation mark is awarded for the words, not the number."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:18:58.781273Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Independently re-solved all 16 bank questions, the 4 lesson checks, the 4 exit questions and all 5 worked examples with a CAS: every answer key, every sol line and every mark tariff (M/A/B counts match m in all 16) is correct, and all six problems from the first examination are genuinely fixed — questions[3] now totals 3 (M1 B1 A1), the formulae sheet carries sin 2t / cos 2t as its own 'not in the booklet, learn it' line with the false 'derivable from the booklet' parenthetical gone, examples[1] step 4 reads distance^2 = ... = 50, the deck is rebalanced to 4/6/3/2 with new 3.3 domain and 3.4 projectile cards, and questions[12] is retiered to d2 (tiers now 5/7/4); the one substantive residue is that the new 3.3 domain card states an endpoint rule that fails on the kit's own question 15."}}},"P4":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P4","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Sequences and series appear on Paper 1 and Paper 2 every year, usually as a whole question worth six to twelve marks, and they are one of the few pure topics where a short AO3 modelling context (savings schemes, salaries, loan repayments) is almost guaranteed. The room is formula-heavy, but the exam rewards two things above all: knowing which formulae the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet gives you and which you must write from memory, and showing the method line by line, because a correct answer straight off a calculator earns almost nothing. The binomial expansion sits here too, first for positive integer powers and then for any rational power, where a stated range of validity is worth a mark on its own and is the mark most often thrown away.","idea":[{"h":"Binomial expansion: Pascal's triangle first, then any rational power","t":"For a positive integer n, (a + bx)ⁿ terminates: the coefficients come from row n of Pascal's triangle, or from ⁿCᵣ = n!/(r!(n − r)!), and the general term is ⁿCᵣ aⁿ⁻ʳ (bx)ʳ. The commonest error is raising x but forgetting to raise b as well. For any rational n the expansion (1 + x)ⁿ = 1 + nx + n(n − 1)/2! x² + … is infinite, and valid only for |x| < 1, whenever n is not a non-negative integer. If the bracket does not begin with 1, factorise first: (a + bx)ⁿ = aⁿ(1 + bx/a)ⁿ, valid for |bx/a| < 1. You may be asked to comment on the range of validity, to use the expansion to approximate a root or a reciprocal, or to expand a rational function after splitting it into partial fractions.","code":"4.1"},{"h":"Sequences from a formula or from a recurrence relation","t":"A sequence can be given by a formula for the nth term, such as uₙ = 1/(3n + 1), or by a relation of the form uₙ₊₁ = f(uₙ) together with a first term, such as u₁ = 5, uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 3. With a recurrence relation you must generate the terms one at a time; there is no shortcut to u₄. Three descriptions are examined: increasing, meaning uₙ₊₁ > uₙ for all n; decreasing, meaning uₙ₊₁ < uₙ for all n; and periodic of order k, meaning uₙ₊ₖ = uₙ for all n. To prove a sequence is increasing or decreasing, form uₙ₊₁ − uₙ and show the sign is the same for every n — a couple of numerical terms is not a proof and scores no accuracy mark.","code":"4.2"},{"h":"Sigma notation: a compact way to write a sum","t":"Σ from r = 1 to n of f(r) means add f(1), f(2), …, f(n). Constants come outside the Σ and sums split across addition, so Σ(5r − 2) = 5Σr − 2Σ1. You are expected to know Σ from r = 1 to n of 1 = n, and the sum of the first n natural numbers, Σ from r = 1 to n of r = ½n(n + 1), which is just the arithmetic sum with a = d = 1. When the lower limit is not 1, subtract: Σ from r = k to n equals Σ from r = 1 to n minus Σ from r = 1 to k − 1. Counting terms is a frequent slip — from r = 11 to r = 30 there are twenty terms, not nineteen and not thirty.","code":"4.3"},{"h":"Arithmetic sequences and series","t":"An arithmetic sequence adds the same common difference d each time, so uₙ = a + (n − 1)d. The sum of the first n terms is Sₙ = ½n[2a + (n − 1)d] = ½n(a + l), where l is the last term: it is the average of the first and last terms multiplied by the number of terms. The booklet gives you Sₙ but not uₙ, and the specification says the proof of the sum formula must be known — write the sum forwards, write it backwards underneath, add the two lines to get n identical pairs each equal to 2a + (n − 1)d, then halve. Typical exam questions give you two terms, or a term and a sum, and ask you to solve simultaneously for a and d.","code":"4.4"},{"h":"Geometric sequences, series and the sum to infinity","t":"A geometric sequence multiplies by the same common ratio r each time, so uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹ and Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r), which is more convenient written as a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) when r > 1. The booklet gives you Sₙ and S∞ but not uₙ, and the proof of the sum formula must be known: write Sₙ out, subtract rSₙ, and everything in the middle cancels. When |r| < 1 the term rⁿ tends to zero, so the sum settles to S∞ = a/(1 − r); always check the condition before using it, because a question with r = 1.2 is testing whether you notice that no sum to infinity exists. If a question gives the sum and asks for the number of terms, rearrange the sum formula to rⁿ = … before you take logs, because logging Sₙ = k as it stands gets you nowhere — and remember that dividing an inequality by ln r reverses it when 0 < r < 1 (ln r is then negative).","code":"4.5"},{"h":"Modelling with sequences and series","t":"The AO3 part of this topic dresses the same two sequences in context. Paying in a fixed extra amount each year, or a salary rising by a fixed number of pounds, is arithmetic; paying in a fixed percentage more each year, or a salary rising by a fixed percentage, is geometric with r = 1 + p/100. Read carefully whether the question wants a single year's amount (a term of the sequence) or the total paid in over several years (a sum of the series), and whether the first payment counts as year one. Answers should be rounded sensibly and given with the unit, and you may be asked to comment on the limitations of the model — for example that a percentage rise is unlikely to continue unchanged for thirty years.","code":"4.6"}],"examples":[{"title":"Expanding a bracket raised to a positive integer power","setup":"Expand (2 + 3x)⁵ in ascending powers of x, up to and including the term in x³. Give each coefficient as a whole number.","steps":["Row five of Pascal's triangle gives the coefficients 1, 5, 10, 10, 5, 1, so the general term is ⁵Cᵣ × 2⁵⁻ʳ × (3x)ʳ.","Taking r = 0 and r = 1: 1 × 2⁵ = 32, and 5 × 2⁴ × 3x = 5 × 16 × 3x = 240x.","Taking r = 2: 10 × 2³ × (3x)² = 10 × 8 × 9x² = 720x².","Taking r = 3: 10 × 2² × (3x)³ = 10 × 4 × 27x³ = 1080x³.","So the expansion begins 32 + 240x + 720x² + 1080x³ in ascending powers of x."],"cues":["Which row of Pascal's triangle do you need, and what does a general term look like?","What are the first two terms, remembering that the 2 is raised to a power as well?","What does the term in x² come to?","What does the term in x³ come to?","How do you write the four terms in ascending powers of x?"]},{"title":"Binomial expansion with a fractional power, and its range of validity","setup":"Expand (1 + 4x)^(1/2) in ascending powers of x, up to and including the term in x³, giving each coefficient as an integer, and state the range of values of x for which the expansion is valid.","steps":["Put n = 1/2 and y = 4x, so the series is 1 + ny + n(n − 1)/2! y² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! y³ + …","The coefficients are n(n − 1)/2! = (1/2)(−1/2)/2 = −1/8 and n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! = (1/2)(−1/2)(−3/2)/6 = 1/16.","So (1 + y)^(1/2) = 1 + y/2 − y²/8 + y³/16.","Substitute y = 4x: 1 + (4x)/2 − (16x²)/8 + (64x³)/16.","Simplifying gives 1 + 2x − 2x² + 4x³, valid for |4x| < 1, that is |x| < 1/4."],"cues":["What substitution turns this into the standard (1 + y)ⁿ form?","What are the coefficients of y² and y³ when n = 1/2?","What is the expansion written in terms of y?","What happens to each term when you put y = 4x back in?","What condition must |y| satisfy, and what does that say about x?"]},{"title":"Proving the sum formula for an arithmetic series","setup":"The first term of an arithmetic series is a and the common difference is d. Prove that the sum of the first n terms is Sₙ = ½n[2a + (n − 1)d].","steps":["Write the sum out forwards: Sₙ = a + (a + d) + (a + 2d) + … + (a + (n − 1)d).","Write the same sum out backwards underneath: Sₙ = (a + (n − 1)d) + (a + (n − 2)d) + … + a.","Add the two lines term by term: each of the n pairs comes to 2a + (n − 1)d, so 2Sₙ = n[2a + (n − 1)d].","Dividing by 2 gives Sₙ = ½n[2a + (n − 1)d], as required."],"cues":["How would you write the sum of the first n terms in full?","In what other order could exactly the same sum be written?","What happens if you add the two lines together, pair by pair?","How do you finish from the line beginning 2Sₙ?"]},{"title":"Using logs to find how many terms a geometric series needs","setup":"A geometric series has first term 5 and common ratio 1.2. Find the smallest value of n for which the sum of the first n terms is greater than 1000.","steps":["Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) = 5(1.2ⁿ − 1)/(1.2 − 1) = 25(1.2ⁿ − 1).","The condition becomes 25(1.2ⁿ − 1) > 1000, so 1.2ⁿ − 1 > 40 and 1.2ⁿ > 41.","Taking logs of both sides, and noting ln 1.2 > 0 so the inequality does not reverse: n ln 1.2 > ln 41.","n > ln 41 ÷ ln 1.2 = 20.4 to three significant figures, and n must be a whole number of terms.","The smallest value is n = 21; checking, the sum of twenty terms is 933 and the sum of twenty-one terms is 1125, both to the nearest whole number."],"cues":["Which sum formula fits here, and what does it simplify to?","What inequality does the condition give once the formula is tidied?","How do you get n down from the exponent?","What value does n have at the boundary?","Which whole number of terms is the first to pass the target?"]},{"title":"A sum in sigma notation, proved and then used between new limits","setup":"Show that Σ from r = 1 to n of (4r + 3) = n(2n + 5). Hence find the value of Σ from r = 11 to 30 of (4r + 3).","steps":["Split the sum and take the constants outside: Σ(4r + 3) = 4Σr + 3Σ1, where Σ from 1 to n of r = ½n(n + 1) and Σ from 1 to n of 1 = n.","So the sum is 4 × ½n(n + 1) + 3n = 2n(n + 1) + 3n = 2n² + 5n = n(2n + 5), as required.","For the limits r = 11 to 30, use Σ from 1 to 30 minus Σ from 1 to 10, because the terms from r = 1 to 10 must be taken away.","Σ from 1 to 30 is 30(60 + 5) = 1950, and Σ from 1 to 10 is 10(20 + 5) = 250.","Hence Σ from r = 11 to 30 of (4r + 3) = 1950 − 250 = 1700."],"cues":["How can this sum be broken into two standard sums?","What do the two standard results give when combined and factorised?","How do you build a sum starting at r = 11 out of sums starting at r = 1?","What two values does the formula you proved give?","What is the value of the required sum?"]},{"title":"Modelling a savings scheme with a percentage increase","setup":"Leon pays £1500 into a savings scheme on 1 January 2026, and on 1 January of each following year he pays in 4% more than he paid the year before. Find the total amount he has paid into the scheme once he has made 12 payments, giving your answer to the nearest pound.","steps":["Each payment is 1.04 times the one before, so the payments form a geometric series with a = 1500 and r = 1.04.","Twelve payments means n = 12, and the total is S₁₂ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) = 1500(1.04¹² − 1)/(1.04 − 1).","1.04¹² = 1.601032…, so S₁₂ = 1500 × 0.601032…/0.04.","S₁₂ = 1500 × 15.02580… = 22538.70…","To the nearest pound, the total paid in is £22539."],"cues":["What kind of series is this, and what are its first term and common ratio?","How many terms are being added, and which formula gives that total?","What does the power of the common ratio come to?","What is the total before any rounding?","How should the final answer be presented?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A geometric series has a = 3 and r = 1.5. Does it have a sum to infinity?","a":"No: |r| = 1.5 is not less than 1, so the series does not converge and S∞ does not exist."},{"q":"What is the value of ⁷C₃?","a":"35"},{"q":"A sequence is given by u₁ = 4 and uₙ₊₁ = 1/uₙ. What is u₅?","a":"4 — the sequence is periodic of order 2, so every odd-numbered term is 4."},{"q":"For which values of x is the binomial expansion of (1 + 5x)⁻¹ valid?","a":"|x| < 1/5"}]},"room":{"facts":["Arithmetic sequence: uₙ = a + (n − 1)d; Sₙ = ½n[2a + (n − 1)d] = ½n(a + l).","Geometric sequence: uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹; Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) for r ≠ 1.","Sum to infinity: S∞ = a/(1 − r), which exists only when |r| < 1.","Σ from r = 1 to n of r = ½n(n + 1), and Σ from r = 1 to n of 1 = n.","Binomial for positive integer n: (a + b)ⁿ = aⁿ + ⁿC₁aⁿ⁻¹b + ⁿC₂aⁿ⁻²b² + … + bⁿ, with ⁿCᵣ = n!/(r!(n − r)!).","Binomial for any rational n: (1 + x)ⁿ = 1 + nx + n(n − 1)/2! x² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! x³ + …, valid for |x| < 1; for (a + bx)ⁿ factorise to aⁿ(1 + bx/a)ⁿ, valid for |bx/a| < 1."],"questions":[{"q":"An arithmetic sequence has first term 3 and common difference 4. Find u₂₀ and S₂₀, the sum of the first 20 terms.","a":"u₂₀ = 79 and S₂₀ = 820","sol":"u₂₀ = a + (n − 1)d = 3 + 19 × 4 (M1 for a + (n − 1)d with a = 3, d = 4 and n = 20) = 79 (A1). S₂₀ = ½ × 20 × (a + l) = ½ × 20 × (3 + 79) = 10 × 82 = 820 (A1; ½ × 20 × (2 × 3 + 19 × 4) = 820 scores the same).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A sequence that goes up by the same amount each time is built entirely from its first term and that constant step.","Use the nth term formula for the first part, then either form of the arithmetic sum formula for the second.","u₂₀ = 3 + 19 × 4."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"For the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, …, find the 8th term and the sum of the first 8 terms.","a":"u₈ = 4374 and S₈ = 6560","sol":"r = 6 ÷ 2 = 3 (B1). u₈ = ar⁷ = 2 × 3⁷ = 2 × 2187 (M1) = 4374 (A1). S₈ = a(r⁸ − 1)/(r − 1) = 2(6561 − 1)/2 = 6560 (A1).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Divide any term by the one before it to see what the sequence is being multiplied by each time.","Use uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹ for the term, then the finite geometric sum formula, written with r − 1 on the bottom because r > 1.","r = 6 ÷ 2, so u₈ = 2 × r⁷."],"codes":["4.5"]},{"q":"Find the sum to infinity of the geometric series 12 + 3 + 3/4 + …","a":"16","sol":"r = 3 ÷ 12 = 1/4, and |r| < 1 so the sum to infinity exists (B1). S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 12/(1 − 1/4) (M1) = 12 ÷ (3/4) = 16 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Only one quantity decides whether an infinite geometric series settles down to a finite value.","Find the common ratio, check the condition on its modulus, then use the sum to infinity formula.","r = 3 ÷ 12, so S∞ = 12/(1 − r)."],"codes":["4.5"]},{"q":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 5 and uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 3 for n ≥ 1. Find u₂, u₃ and u₄.","a":"u₂ = 7, u₃ = 11 and u₄ = 19","sol":"u₂ = 2 × 5 − 3 = 7 (M1 for correct substitution into the relation). u₃ = 2 × 7 − 3 = 11, and u₄ = 2 × 11 − 3 = 19 (A1 for all three values correct).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This sequence is defined by how each term is made from the one immediately before it, so you cannot jump straight to the fourth term.","Substitute the term you have into the relation to get the next one, and repeat.","u₂ = 2 × 5 − 3."],"codes":["4.2"]},{"q":"Find the coefficient of x² in the binomial expansion of (2 + x)⁶.","a":"240","sol":"The general term is ⁶Cᵣ × 2⁶⁻ʳ × xʳ, so the term in x² is the one with r = 2: ⁶C₂ × 2⁴ × x² (M1). ⁶C₂ = 15 and 2⁴ = 16, so the coefficient is 15 × 16 = 240 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only one term of the expansion contains x², so the whole expansion is not needed.","Write down the general term ⁿCᵣ aⁿ⁻ʳ bʳ and choose the value of r that produces x².","The term required is ⁶C₂ × 2⁴ × x²."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"Expand (3 − 2x)⁴ in ascending powers of x, simplifying each coefficient.","a":"81 − 216x + 216x² − 96x³ + 16x⁴","sol":"Row four of Pascal's triangle is 1, 4, 6, 4, 1, so (3 − 2x)⁴ = 3⁴ + 4 × 3³(−2x) + 6 × 3²(−2x)² + 4 × 3(−2x)³ + (−2x)⁴ (M1 for the correct structure, A1 for correct coefficients and powers of 3). = 81 + 4 × 27 × (−2x) + 6 × 9 × 4x² + 4 × 3 × (−8x³) + 16x⁴ (M1). = 81 − 216x + 216x² − 96x³ + 16x⁴ (A1 cao).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The power is a positive whole number, so the expansion stops after a fixed number of terms and Pascal's triangle supplies the coefficients.","Use (a + b)⁴ with a = 3 and b = −2x, raising both parts of each bracket to the correct power and watching the alternating signs.","The first two terms are 3⁴ + 4 × 3³ × (−2x)."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"Find Σ from r = 1 to n of (5r − 2), giving your answer as a single simplified expression in n.","a":"n(5n + 1)/2","sol":"Σ(5r − 2) = 5Σr − 2Σ1 (M1). Using Σ from 1 to n of r = ½n(n + 1) and Σ from 1 to n of 1 = n: 5 × ½n(n + 1) − 2n (A1). = (5n² + 5n − 4n)/2 = (5n² + n)/2 = n(5n + 1)/2 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A sum like this breaks into two sums whose values you are expected to know.","Take each constant outside its Σ, then quote the standard results for Σr and for Σ1.","Σ(5r − 2) = 5Σr − 2Σ1."],"codes":["4.3","4.4"]},{"q":"The 5th term of an arithmetic series is 17 and the 12th term is 45. Find the first term, the common difference and the sum of the first 30 terms.","a":"a = 1, d = 4 and S₃₀ = 1770","sol":"a + 4d = 17 and a + 11d = 45 (M1 for both equations correct). Subtracting: 7d = 28, so d = 4 (A1). a = 17 − 4 × 4 = 1 (A1). S₃₀ = ½ × 30 × (2 × 1 + 29 × 4) (M1) = 15 × 118 = 1770 (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two facts about two different terms give two pieces of information about the same two unknowns.","Write each given term in the form a + (n − 1)d, solve the pair simultaneously, then substitute into the sum formula.","a + 4d = 17 and a + 11d = 45."],"codes":["4.4"]},{"q":"A convergent geometric series has first term 8 and sum to infinity 10. Find the common ratio and the 4th term of the series.","a":"r = 1/5 and u₄ = 8/125","sol":"S∞ = a/(1 − r) gives 8/(1 − r) = 10, so 8 = 10(1 − r) (M1). 1 − r = 0.8, so r = 0.2 = 1/5 (A1); |r| < 1, consistent with the series converging. u₄ = ar³ = 8 × (1/5)³ (M1) = 8/125 = 0.064 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The sum to infinity ties the first term and the common ratio together in a single equation.","Substitute the two given values into the sum to infinity formula, solve for r, then use the nth term formula.","8/(1 − r) = 10."],"codes":["4.5"]},{"q":"A sequence is given by u₁ = 3 and uₙ₊₁ = 1/uₙ for n ≥ 1. State the order of this periodic sequence, and find Σ from r = 1 to 20 of uᵣ.","a":"Order 2, and the sum is 100/3","sol":"u₁ = 3, u₂ = 1/3, u₃ = 3, u₄ = 1/3, … so uₙ₊₂ = uₙ for all n and the sequence is periodic of order 2 (B1). The first 20 terms consist of 10 complete pairs, each pair summing to 3 + 1/3 = 10/3 (M1). Σ from r = 1 to 20 of uᵣ = 10 × 10/3 (A1) = 100/3 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Write out the first few terms and look for the point at which the list begins to repeat itself.","Find the length of the repeating block, work out what one block sums to, then count how many complete blocks make up 20 terms.","u₁ = 3 and u₂ = 1/3."],"codes":["4.2","4.3"]},{"q":"Find the first three terms in ascending powers of x of the binomial expansion of (4 + x)^(1/2), and state the range of values of x for which the expansion is valid.","a":"2 + x/4 − x²/64, valid for |x| < 4","sol":"Factorise the bracket so that it starts with 1: (4 + x)^(1/2) = 4^(1/2)(1 + x/4)^(1/2) = 2(1 + x/4)^(1/2) (M1). With n = 1/2 and y = x/4, (1 + y)^(1/2) = 1 + ½y + [(½)(−½)/2!]y² + … = 1 + ½(x/4) − (1/8)(x²/16) (M1 for the structure, A1 for the coefficients) = 1 + x/8 − x²/128. Multiplying by 2: 2 + x/4 − x²/64 (A1). The expansion is valid for |x/4| < 1, that is |x| < 4 (B1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The standard series only applies to a bracket that begins with 1, so the bracket has to be adjusted before you can expand it.","Take a factor of 4 to the power one half outside, expand the remaining bracket with the general binomial series, then multiply back through.","(4 + x)^(1/2) = 2(1 + x/4)^(1/2)."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"Show that the sequence defined by uₙ = 1/(3n + 1), for n ≥ 1, is a decreasing sequence.","a":"uₙ₊₁ − uₙ = −3/[(3n + 1)(3n + 4)] < 0 for all n ≥ 1, so uₙ₊₁ < uₙ and the sequence is decreasing","sol":"uₙ₊₁ = 1/(3(n + 1) + 1) = 1/(3n + 4) (B1). uₙ₊₁ − uₙ = 1/(3n + 4) − 1/(3n + 1) = [(3n + 1) − (3n + 4)]/[(3n + 4)(3n + 1)] (M1) = −3/[(3n + 4)(3n + 1)]. For every integer n ≥ 1 both brackets are positive, so the whole fraction is negative (A1). Hence uₙ₊₁ < uₙ for all n ≥ 1, which is exactly what it means for the sequence to be decreasing (cso). Testing two or three numerical terms is not accepted as a proof.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Decreasing has a precise meaning here: every term must be smaller than the one before it, for every value of n, not just the first few.","Write down uₙ₊₁, subtract uₙ from it, combine into a single fraction and show the sign of that fraction cannot change.","uₙ₊₁ = 1/(3(n + 1) + 1)."],"codes":["4.2"]},{"q":"(a) Expand (1 − 3x)⁻² in ascending powers of x, up to and including the term in x³, and state the range of values of x for which the expansion is valid. (b) Hence, by substituting x = 0.01, find an approximation to 10000/9409, giving your answer to 5 decimal places.","a":"(a) 1 + 6x + 27x² + 108x³, valid for |x| < 1/3; (b) 1.06281","sol":"(a) Take n = −2 and y = −3x in (1 + y)ⁿ = 1 + ny + n(n − 1)/2! y² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! y³ + … (M1 for the correct structure). The coefficients are n = −2, n(n − 1)/2! = (−2)(−3)/2 = 3 and n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! = (−2)(−3)(−4)/6 = −4 (A1). So (1 − 3x)⁻² = 1 + (−2)(−3x) + 3(−3x)² + (−4)(−3x)³ = 1 + 6x + 27x² + 108x³ (A1). Valid for |−3x| < 1, that is |x| < 1/3 (B1). (b) When x = 0.01, 1 − 3x = 0.97, so (1 − 3x)⁻² = 0.97⁻² = 1/0.9409 = 10000/9409 (M1). Substituting into the expansion: 1 + 6(0.01) + 27(0.01)² + 108(0.01)³ = 1 + 0.06 + 0.0027 + 0.000108 = 1.062808, so 10000/9409 ≈ 1.06281 to 5 decimal places (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The power is negative, so the expansion never terminates and only represents the function when x is small enough.","Use the general series with y = −3x for part (a); for part (b) notice what 1 − 3x becomes at the given value of x and square its reciprocal.","With n = −2 and y = −3x, the term in x is (−2) × (−3x)."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The 2nd, 4th and 7th terms of an arithmetic sequence with first term a and common difference d, where d ≠ 0, are also the first three terms of a geometric sequence. Show that a = 3d, and find the common ratio of the geometric sequence.","a":"a = 3d and r = 3/2","sol":"The three terms are a + d, a + 3d and a + 6d (B1). In a geometric sequence consecutive ratios are equal, so (a + 3d)/(a + d) = (a + 6d)/(a + 3d), giving (a + 3d)² = (a + d)(a + 6d) (M1). Expanding both sides: a² + 6ad + 9d² = a² + 7ad + 6d² (M1). Hence 3d² = ad, so d(3d − a) = 0, and since d ≠ 0 we have a = 3d (A1 cso). The three terms are then 4d, 6d and 9d (M1), so the common ratio is r = 6d ÷ 4d = 3/2 (A1); the check 9d ÷ 6d = 3/2 confirms it.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Write the three terms using the arithmetic rule first; the geometric condition then says something about how those three expressions are related.","In a geometric sequence the second term divided by the first equals the third divided by the second, which gives one equation connecting a and d.","The three terms are a + d, a + 3d and a + 6d."],"codes":["4.4","4.5"]},{"q":"Kate and Jon both start work on 1 January 2026. Kate's annual salary in 2026 is £24000 and it rises by £900 on 1 January of each following year. Jon's annual salary in 2026 is £22000 and it rises by 4% on 1 January of each following year. Find the first calendar year in which Jon's annual salary is greater than Kate's.","a":"2038","sol":"In the year 2026 + k, Kate's salary is 24000 + 900k, an arithmetic sequence (M1), and Jon's salary is 22000 × 1.04ᵏ, a geometric sequence with r = 1.04 (M1). Jon overtakes Kate when 22000 × 1.04ᵏ > 24000 + 900k (A1 for the correct inequality). This cannot be solved algebraically, so test whole values of k: at k = 11, Jon has 22000 × 1.539454 = £33868 while Kate has 24000 + 9900 = £33900, so Jon is still behind (M1); at k = 12, Jon has 22000 × 1.601032 = £35223 while Kate has 24000 + 10800 = £34800, so Jon is ahead (A1). The first such year is therefore 2026 + 12 = 2038 (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["One salary grows by adding a fixed amount and the other by multiplying by a fixed factor, so the two are different kinds of sequence.","Write each salary as a term of its own sequence after k years, set up the inequality between them, and test whole-number values of k.","After k years Kate's salary is 24000 + 900k."],"codes":["4.6","4.4","4.5"]},{"q":"Prove that the sum of the first n terms of a geometric series with first term a and common ratio r, where r ≠ 1, is Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r).","a":"Sₙ − rSₙ = a − arⁿ, so (1 − r)Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ) and hence Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r)","sol":"Write the sum out in full: Sₙ = a + ar + ar² + … + arⁿ⁻¹ (B1). Multiply every term by r: rSₙ = ar + ar² + ar³ + … + arⁿ (M1). Subtract the second line from the first; every term from ar to arⁿ⁻¹ appears in both lines and cancels, leaving Sₙ − rSₙ = a − arⁿ (M1). Factorising both sides gives (1 − r)Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ), and since r ≠ 1 we may divide by 1 − r, giving Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) (A1 cso).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Every term of a geometric series is r times the term before it, so multiplying the whole sum by r produces almost the same list of terms.","Write the sum out in full, write r times the sum underneath it lined up, and subtract one line from the other.","Sₙ = a + ar + ar² + … + arⁿ⁻¹."],"codes":["4.5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Find the 10th term of the arithmetic sequence 5, 8, 11, …","a":"32"},{"q":"Find the sum to infinity of the geometric series 18 + 6 + 2 + …","a":"27"},{"q":"Write down the coefficient of x³ in the binomial expansion of (1 + x)⁷.","a":"35"},{"q":"Evaluate Σ from r = 1 to 12 of (2r − 1).","a":"144"}]},"cards":[{"front":"nth term of an arithmetic sequence","back":"uₙ = a + (n − 1)d, where a is the first term and d is the common difference. Not given in the formulae booklet.","code":"4.4","open":false},{"front":"Sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series","back":"Sₙ = ½n[2a + (n − 1)d] = ½n(a + l), where l is the last term. Given in the formulae booklet, but its proof must be known.","code":"4.4","open":false},{"front":"Sum of the first n natural numbers","back":"Σ from r = 1 to n of r = ½n(n + 1). It is the arithmetic sum with a = 1 and d = 1. Not given in the booklet.","code":"4.4","open":false},{"front":"nth term of a geometric sequence","back":"uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹, where a is the first term and r is the common ratio. Not given in the formulae booklet.","code":"4.5","open":false},{"front":"Sum of the first n terms of a geometric series","back":"Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1), for r ≠ 1. Given in the formulae booklet, but its proof must be known.","code":"4.5","open":false},{"front":"Sum to infinity of a geometric series, and when it exists","back":"S∞ = a/(1 − r), and it exists only when |r| < 1, because then rⁿ → 0 as n → ∞.","code":"4.5","open":false},{"front":"Binomial expansion for a positive integer n","back":"(a + b)ⁿ = aⁿ + ⁿC₁aⁿ⁻¹b + ⁿC₂aⁿ⁻²b² + … + bⁿ, where ⁿCᵣ = n!/(r!(n − r)!). Given in the booklet; it terminates after n + 1 terms.","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"Binomial series for any rational n","back":"(1 + x)ⁿ = 1 + nx + n(n − 1)/2! x² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/3! x³ + …, valid for |x| < 1. Given in the booklet; the series is infinite (n not a non-negative integer).","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"Expanding (a + bx)ⁿ for rational n, and its range of validity","back":"Factorise first: (a + bx)ⁿ = aⁿ(1 + bx/a)ⁿ. The expansion is valid for |bx/a| < 1, that is |x| < |a/b|.","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"Σ from r = 1 to n of 1","back":"Σ from r = 1 to n of 1 = n: adding the number 1 to itself n times. Knowledge of this result is expected.","code":"4.3","open":false},{"front":"Summing between limits that do not start at 1","back":"Σ from r = k to n equals Σ from r = 1 to n minus Σ from r = 1 to k − 1. The number of terms is n − k + 1.","code":"4.3","open":false},{"front":"Increasing and decreasing sequences","back":"Increasing: uₙ₊₁ > uₙ for all n. Decreasing: uₙ₊₁ < uₙ for all n. Prove it by finding the sign of uₙ₊₁ − uₙ.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"Periodic sequence of order k","back":"A sequence with uₙ₊ₖ = uₙ for all n, where k is the smallest such positive integer. The terms repeat in blocks of k.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a periodic sequence, and state its order","back":"u₁ = 3, uₙ₊₁ = 1/uₙ gives 3, 1/3, 3, … of order 2; u₁ = 0.2, uₙ₊₁ = 1 − uₙ gives 0.2, 0.8, 0.2, … of order 2; uₙ = sin(90n)° gives 1, 0, −1, 0, … of order 4.","code":"4.2","open":true},{"front":"Arithmetic or geometric: fixed amount added versus fixed percentage rise","back":"A fixed amount added each period is arithmetic, with that amount as d. A fixed percentage rise of p% each period is geometric, with r = 1 + p/100.","code":"4.6","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Arithmetic sequence, nth term: uₙ = a + (n − 1)d — NOT in the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet, so learn it.","Arithmetic series, sum to n terms: Sₙ = ½n[2a + (n − 1)d] = ½n(a + l), where l is the last term — GIVEN in the booklet, but the specification says the proof must be known.","Sum of the first n natural numbers: Σ from r = 1 to n of r = ½n(n + 1) — NOT in the booklet; it is the arithmetic sum with a = d = 1.","Σ from r = 1 to n of 1 = n — NOT in the booklet; knowledge of this result is expected.","Summing between limits: Σ from r = k to n of f(r) = Σ from r = 1 to n of f(r) − Σ from r = 1 to k − 1 of f(r), and the number of terms is n − k + 1 — NOT in the booklet.","Geometric sequence, nth term: uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹ — NOT in the booklet, so learn it.","Geometric series, sum to n terms: Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1), r ≠ 1 — GIVEN in the booklet, but the specification says the proof must be known.","Geometric series, sum to infinity: S∞ = a/(1 − r), valid only when |r| < 1 — GIVEN in the booklet, with the |r| < 1 condition; always state that the condition holds before using it.","Binomial expansion for positive integer n: (a + b)ⁿ = aⁿ + ⁿC₁aⁿ⁻¹b + ⁿC₂aⁿ⁻²b² + … + bⁿ, with ⁿCᵣ = n!/(r!(n − r)!) — GIVEN in the booklet.","Binomial series for any real n: (1 + x)ⁿ = 1 + nx + n(n − 1)/(1 × 2) x² + n(n − 1)(n − 2)/(1 × 2 × 3) x³ + …, valid for |x| < 1 — GIVEN in the booklet.","Range of validity after factorising: (a + bx)ⁿ = aⁿ(1 + bx/a)ⁿ is valid for |bx/a| < 1 — NOT in the booklet; this is the mark most often dropped.","Finding n with logarithms: from arⁿ⁻¹ = k, take logs of both sides to give (n − 1) ln r = ln(k/a); from Sₙ = k you must rearrange first, to rⁿ = 1 − k(1 − r)/a, and then take logs to give n ln r = ln(1 − k(1 − r)/a); if you divide an inequality by ln r and ln r < 0, reverse the inequality sign — NOT in the booklet.","Definitions to write from memory: increasing means uₙ₊₁ > uₙ for all n; decreasing means uₙ₊₁ < uₙ for all n; periodic of order k means uₙ₊ₖ = uₙ for all n."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where the marks go in this room","items":["State the range of validity whenever you expand with a rational or negative power — it is a separate B mark and is dropped more often than any other mark in this topic.","When expanding (a + bx)ⁿ for positive integer n, raise b as well as x: the term in x² of (2 + 3x)⁵ is ⁵C₂ × 2³ × (3x)², not ⁵C₂ × 2³ × x².","Check |r| < 1 and say so before using S∞ = a/(1 − r); a question with |r| ≥ 1 is testing whether you notice that the sum to infinity does not exist.","A recurrence relation must be iterated term by term; writing down u₄ with no intermediate terms shown earns no method mark.","To prove a sequence is increasing or decreasing, work with uₙ₊₁ − uₙ in general — listing a few numerical terms is not a proof and scores nothing.","In a modelling question, decide whether the answer is a single term (one year's payment or salary) or a sum (the total over several years), and say whether the first year counts as n = 1 or k = 0.","Round only at the end, give the unit, and keep the unrounded value on your calculator — a premature round can lose the final accuracy mark.","The proofs of both sum formulae are examinable: forwards-and-backwards addition for the arithmetic series, and subtracting rSₙ from Sₙ for the geometric series."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:20:55Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Third independent examination: all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket questions and the 4 lesson checks were re-solved from the question text alone and every answer, form and accuracy matches (u₂₀ = 79/S₂₀ = 820; u₈ = 4374/S₈ = 6560; S∞ = 16; 7, 11, 19; 240; 81 − 216x + 216x² − 96x³ + 16x⁴; n(5n + 1)/2; a = 1, d = 4, S₃₀ = 1770; r = 1/5, u₄ = 8/125; order 2 and 100/3; 2 + x/4 − x²/64 with |x| < 4; the decreasing proof; 1 + 6x + 27x² + 108x³, |x| < 1/3 and 1.06281; a = 3d, r = 3/2; 2038; and the geometric sum proof), the six worked examples are arithmetically exact (1.04¹² gives £22538.70 → £22539; ln41/ln1.2 = 20.37 so n = 21 with S₂₀ = 933 and S₂₁ = 1125) and correctly faded, and every booklet claim matches Appendix 1, which lists only uₙ = a + (n − 1)d and uₙ = arⁿ⁻¹ for this topic. All three faults from the previous verdict are repaired: idea 4.1 and card 7 now restrict the infinite rational-n series to n not a non-negative integer, idea 4.5 now reads 0 < r < 1 for the reversed inequality, and question 15 (the geometric sum proof) is re-tiered d = 2; no new errors were introduced by the fixes, so the kit is publishable as it stands."}}},"P5":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P5","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Trigonometry is the largest recurring strand of Papers 1 and 2, and it comes back inside calculus, vectors and mechanics, so a weakness here costs marks twice. Examiners set the same traps every year: a calculator left in degree mode for a radians question, an interval not converted before a multiple angle is solved, a division by cos x that quietly deletes solutions, a rejected root left unmentioned, and a decimal written where the question said exact. This room drills the definitions, the identities, the exact values and the habit of finding every solution in the interval, so that those marks stop leaking.","idea":[{"h":"Triangles, the unit circle and radian measure","t":"Sine, cosine and tangent are defined for every angle by a point on the unit circle: cos θ is the x coordinate and sin θ the y coordinate, which is why they keep working beyond 90°. In any triangle use a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A and area = ½ab sin C, and watch the ambiguous case, where the sine rule also allows the obtuse partner 180° − θ. A radian is the angle that subtends an arc equal to the radius, so π radians = 180°, and only with θ in radians do s = rθ and A = ½r²θ hold.","code":"5.1"},{"h":"Small angle approximations","t":"When θ is small and measured in radians, sin θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2 and tan θ ≈ θ. Questions almost always feed in a multiple of θ, so replace the whole argument: cos 3x ≈ 1 − (3x)²/2 = 1 − 9x²/2. Substitute first, tidy the algebra, then cancel the powers of x to reach a number such as −9/8. The three approximations are printed in the formulae booklet, but they are only true in radians, so check the units before you start.","code":"5.2"},{"h":"Graphs, symmetry, periodicity and exact values","t":"y = sin x and y = cos x have period 360° (2π) and range −1 ≤ y ≤ 1; y = tan x has period 180° (π) and vertical asymptotes at x = 90° + 180n°. The symmetries sin(180° − x) = sin x, cos(360° − x) = cos x and tan(180° + x) = tan x are what produce the extra solutions of an equation. You must know the exact values of sin and cos at 0, π/6, π/4, π/3, π/2 and π and of tan at 0, π/6, π/4, π/3 and π, and be able to sketch transformations such as y = cos(x + 30°) or y = tan 2x.","code":"5.3"},{"h":"Reciprocal and inverse trigonometric functions","t":"sec x = 1/cos x, cosec x = 1/sin x and cot x = 1/tan x = cos x/sin x. Each is undefined wherever its partner is zero, which is where its graph has asymptotes, and because |cos x| ≤ 1 we always have |sec x| ≥ 1 and |cosec x| ≥ 1 — a value such as sec x = −1/2 must be rejected in writing. The inverse functions need restricted domains: arcsin has domain −1 ≤ x ≤ 1 and range −π/2 ≤ y ≤ π/2, arccos the same domain with range 0 ≤ y ≤ π, and arctan all real x with range −π/2 < y < π/2.","code":"5.4"},{"h":"The Pythagorean identities","t":"Everything is built on tan θ = sin θ/cos θ and sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. Divide that identity by cos²θ to get 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ, and by sin²θ to get 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ. Their job in an equation is to leave a single trigonometric function so the equation becomes a quadratic, and the method mark is awarded for the correct replacement, not for the final solving. None of the three is printed in the formulae booklet, so learn them and the direction of each division.","code":"5.5"},{"h":"Compound angles, double angles and the harmonic form","t":"The booklet gives sin(A ± B), cos(A ± B) and tan(A ± B). Setting B = A turns them into sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A = 2cos²A − 1 = 1 − 2sin²A and tan 2A = 2 tan A/(1 − tan²A); choose the version of cos 2A that matches the other terms in the question. To write a cos θ + b sin θ as R cos(θ − α), expand the target form and compare coefficients: R cos α = a and R sin α = b, so R = √(a² + b²) and tan α = b/a. The maximum of the expression is then R and the minimum is −R.","code":"5.6"},{"h":"Solving equations over a given interval","t":"Deal with the argument first. For sin(2x + 30°) with 0° ≤ x < 360°, solve for u = 2x + 30° over 30° ≤ u < 750°, find every u, then convert back; halving the interval at the end is where solutions are lost. Take the principal value from the calculator, use the symmetry rules or a CAST diagram for its partner in the cycle, then add or subtract whole periods until you leave the interval. Never divide an equation by sin x or cos x — factorise instead, or a whole family of roots disappears.","code":"5.7"},{"h":"Proving trigonometric identities","t":"A proof is not an equation to be solved: work on one side only, usually the more complicated one, and transform it line by line until it is identical to the other side, then write a closing statement. The standard moves are writing everything in sin and cos, taking a common denominator, replacing sin²θ + cos²θ by 1, using a double angle formula, or spotting a difference of two squares such as cos²x − sin²x. Every mark sits in the justified steps, because the final line is printed in the question.","code":"5.8"},{"h":"Trigonometric models in context","t":"Tide depth, the height of a point on a rotating wheel, daylight hours and alternating current are all modelled by y = a + b sin(kt + c) or its cosine version. Here a is the mean value, |b| is the amplitude so the greatest and least values are a + |b| and a − |b|, and the period is 360°/k in degrees or 2π/k in radians. Typical questions ask for the value at a given time, the first time a value is reached, how long a condition lasts, or the greatest value, and then for a comment on the limitations of the model.","code":"5.9"}],"examples":[{"title":"The area of a segment","setup":"A sector OAB of a circle has centre O, radius 12 cm and angle AOB = 0.6 radians. The chord AB cuts a segment off the sector. Find the area of the segment, in cm², to 3 significant figures.","steps":["The sector area is given by ½r²θ with θ in radians, which here is ½ × 12² × 0.6 = 43.2 cm².","Triangle OAB has two sides of 12 cm with the given angle between them, so its area is ½ × 12² × sin 0.6 = 72 sin 0.6.","In radian mode sin 0.6 = 0.564642, so the triangle has area 40.654 cm².","Segment = sector − triangle = 43.2 − 40.654 = 2.546, so the segment has area 2.55 cm² to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["Which formula gives a sector's area when the angle is in radians?","What shape must be taken away to leave the segment, and which formula fits it?","Is the calculator in radian mode, and what is sin 0.6?","How do the two areas combine to give the segment?"]},{"title":"A small angle approximation","setup":"Given that θ is small and measured in radians, find an approximation, in its simplest form, for the expression (1 − cos 4θ)/(θ sin 3θ).","steps":["Apply cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2 to the whole argument: cos 4θ ≈ 1 − (4θ)²/2 = 1 − 8θ².","The numerator becomes 1 − (1 − 8θ²) = 8θ².","Apply sin x ≈ x to the whole argument: sin 3θ ≈ 3θ, so the denominator is θ × 3θ = 3θ².","The quotient is 8θ² ÷ 3θ² = 8/3."],"cues":["Which approximation suits a cosine, and what plays the part of the angle?","What is left of the numerator once that is substituted?","Which approximation handles the sine, and what does the denominator become?","What survives when the squared terms cancel?"]},{"title":"A quadratic in cos x","setup":"Solve 5 sin²x + 3 cos x = 5 for 0 ≤ x < 360°, giving your answers to 1 decimal place where they are not exact.","steps":["Remove the sine with sin²x = 1 − cos²x: 5(1 − cos²x) + 3 cos x = 5.","Expanding and tidying gives 5 − 5cos²x + 3 cos x − 5 = 0, that is 5cos²x − 3 cos x = 0.","Factorise rather than divide by cos x: cos x(5 cos x − 3) = 0, so cos x = 0 or cos x = 3/5.","cos x = 0 gives x = 90° and x = 270°.","cos x = 3/5 has principal value 53.1301°, and cosine is also positive in the fourth quadrant, so the other value is 360° − 53.1301° = 306.8699°.","The solutions are x = 90°, 270°, 53.1° and 306.9°, to 1 decimal place where needed."],"cues":["The equation mixes sin²x with cos x — which identity clears the sine?","What quadratic in cos x does the expansion leave?","Every term here has a factor of cos x — what is the safe next move, and what would dividing both sides by cos x cost?","Which angles in the interval have cosine zero?","Where else is cosine positive, and what is the related angle?","What is the full set of solutions?"]},{"title":"Harmonic form, then solve","setup":"Express 2 sin θ + 5 cos θ in the form R sin(θ + α), where R > 0 and 0° < α < 90°, and hence solve 2 sin θ + 5 cos θ = 3 for 0° ≤ θ < 360°, giving each answer to 1 decimal place.","steps":["Expanding the target form, R sin(θ + α) = R cos α sin θ + R sin α cos θ, so R cos α = 2 and R sin α = 5.","Squaring and adding gives R = √(2² + 5²) = √29, and dividing gives tan α = 5/2, so α = 68.1986° and the expression is √29 sin(θ + 68.1986°).","The equation becomes √29 sin(θ + 68.1986°) = 3, so sin(θ + 68.1986°) = 3/√29 = 0.55709.","Put u = θ + 68.1986°; as θ runs over 0° ≤ θ < 360°, u runs over 68.1986° ≤ u < 428.1986°.","arcsin 0.55709 = 33.8545°, so in that interval u = 180° − 33.8545° = 146.1455° or u = 360° + 33.8545° = 393.8545°.","Subtracting 68.1986° from each gives θ = 77.9° or θ = 325.7°, to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["What does R sin(θ + α) look like expanded, and which coefficients must match?","How do those two equations give R and α?","What does the equation become once the new form is used?","What interval does the whole angle θ + α run over?","Which angles in that interval have that sine?","How do you get from u back to θ?"]},{"title":"Proving an identity","setup":"Prove that (sin 2x)/(1 + cos 2x) ≡ tan x for all values of x for which both sides are defined.","steps":["Take the left-hand side and use sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x together with the version cos 2x = 2cos²x − 1.","The denominator becomes 1 + (2cos²x − 1) = 2cos²x.","The left-hand side is therefore (2 sin x cos x)/(2cos²x).","Cancelling the common factor 2 cos x leaves sin x/cos x.","sin x/cos x = tan x, which is the right-hand side, so the identity is proved."],"cues":["Which double angle forms will make the denominator collapse?","What does the denominator simplify to with that choice?","What does the whole fraction look like now?","Which factor cancels top and bottom?","How should the proof be finished off?"]},{"title":"A tide model","setup":"The depth of water, D metres, at a jetty t hours after midnight is modelled by D = 7 + 2.5 cos(πt/6) for 0 ≤ t < 24. Find the times before noon at which the model gives a depth of 5.75 m.","steps":["Substituting the required depth gives 7 + 2.5 cos(πt/6) = 5.75, so 2.5 cos(πt/6) = −1.25 and cos(πt/6) = −0.5.","Put u = πt/6; as t runs from 0 to 24, u runs from 0 to 4π, and the morning is the first half of that.","In one cycle, cos u = −0.5 at u = 2π/3 and u = 4π/3.","Converting back, πt/6 = 2π/3 gives t = 4 and πt/6 = 4π/3 gives t = 8.","Both times are before noon, so the model gives a depth of 5.75 m at 4 a.m. and at 8 a.m."],"cues":["What equation comes from putting the required depth into the model?","What range does the angle inside the cosine cover over the day?","Where in one cycle does a cosine take that value?","What times t do those angles correspond to?","Which of these times answer the question as asked?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the period of y = tan 2x, in radians?","a":"π/2"},{"q":"Convert 2π/9 radians into degrees.","a":"40°"},{"q":"Which identity rewrites 1 + cot²θ as a single function?","a":"1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ"},{"q":"What is R when 9 sin θ − 40 cos θ is written as R sin(θ − α) with R > 0?","a":"41"},{"q":"For small θ in radians, what does 1 − cos θ become?","a":"θ²/2"}]},"room":{"facts":["π radians = 180°; to convert degrees to radians multiply by π/180, and radians to degrees multiply by 180/π.","For a sector with θ in radians: arc length s = rθ and area A = ½r²θ.","Sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C; cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A; area of a triangle = ½ab sin C.","sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, sec²θ = 1 + tan²θ and cosec²θ = 1 + cot²θ, with tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.","sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A and cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A = 2cos²A − 1 = 1 − 2sin²A.","a cos θ + b sin θ = R cos(θ − α) with R = √(a² + b²) > 0 and tan α = b/a; the maximum is R and the minimum is −R.","For small θ in radians: sin θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2 and tan θ ≈ θ.","Exact values: sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2, tan 30° = 1/√3, sin 45° = cos 45° = √2/2, tan 45° = 1, sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 60° = √3."],"questions":[{"q":"A sector of a circle has radius 9 cm and the angle at the centre is 0.4 radians. Find the area of the sector.","a":"16.2 cm²","sol":"The angle is already in radians, so A = ½r²θ applies directly: A = ½ × 9² × 0.4 (M1 for the correct formula with r = 9 and θ = 0.4). A = ½ × 81 × 0.4 = 16.2, so the area is 16.2 cm² (A1, the unit is needed).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The angle is already in radians, so nothing needs converting before a sector formula is used.","Sector area uses the square of the radius and the angle at the centre, not the arc length.","The first line is the sector area formula: one half of the radius squared multiplied by the angle in radians."],"codes":["5.1"]},{"q":"Write down the exact value of sin(2π/3) − cos(π/3).","a":"(√3 − 1)/2","sol":"2π/3 = π − π/3 lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive, so sin(2π/3) = sin(π/3) = √3/2, and cos(π/3) = 1/2 (B1 for both exact values). Subtracting over the common denominator 2, √3/2 − 1/2 = (√3 − 1)/2 (B1; the question says exact, so a decimal such as 0.366 loses the mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["2π/3 is in the second quadrant — which first-quadrant angle does it mirror?","Use the symmetry sin(π − x) = sin x, then recall the exact surd values at π/3.","Replace sin(2π/3) by sin(π/3) and write both terms over the denominator 2."],"codes":["5.3"]},{"q":"Solve sin(x + 70°) = 0.5 for 0 < x < 360°.","a":"x = 80° and x = 320°","sol":"Work with the whole angle. Let u = x + 70°; since 0 < x < 360°, the interval for u is 70° < u < 430° (M1). sin u = 0.5 has principal value 30°, so in this interval u = 180° − 30° = 150° and u = 360° + 30° = 390° (M1). Subtracting 70° from each, x = 80° and x = 320° (A1 cao, both needed and no extras inside the interval).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The angle inside the bracket is the thing to solve for first.","Shift the interval to find the values x + 70° can take, then find every angle in that range whose sine is 0.5.","Let u = x + 70°, so that 70° < u < 430°."],"codes":["5.7","5.3"]},{"q":"Given that θ is small and measured in radians, use the small angle approximations to find the value that (θ tan 2θ)/(1 − cos θ) approaches.","a":"4","sol":"tan 2θ ≈ 2θ, so the numerator θ tan 2θ ≈ 2θ² (M1). cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2, so the denominator 1 − cos θ ≈ 1 − (1 − θ²/2) = θ²/2 (M1). The quotient is therefore approximately 2θ² ÷ (θ²/2) = 4 (A1 cao).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Replace every trigonometric term by its small angle approximation before doing any algebra.","The tangent approximation applies to the whole argument, and the cosine approximation leaves the denominator in θ².","Write the numerator as θ × 2θ."],"codes":["5.2"]},{"q":"Solve cot x = √3 for 0° ≤ x < 360°.","a":"x = 30° and x = 210°","sol":"cot x = 1/tan x, so the equation is tan x = 1/√3 (M1). The principal value is x = 30° (A1). Tangent has period 180°, so the only other solution in the interval is x = 30° + 180° = 210° (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Cotangent is the reciprocal of tangent, so rewrite the equation in terms of tan x.","Find the first-quadrant angle with that tangent, then use the period of the tangent graph for the rest.","The equation becomes tan x = 1/√3."],"codes":["5.4","5.7"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, AB = 7 cm, AC = 9 cm and angle BAC = 1.1 radians. Find the length of BC, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"8.54 cm","sol":"Two sides and the included angle are known, so use the cosine rule with the calculator in radian mode: BC² = 7² + 9² − 2 × 7 × 9 × cos 1.1 (M1 for a correct statement and substitution). BC² = 49 + 81 − 126 × 0.453596 = 130 − 57.153 = 72.847 (A1). BC = √72.847 = 8.5350, so BC = 8.54 cm (A1 awrt 8.54).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two sides and the angle between them are known — which rule gives the third side?","Use a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, and make sure the calculator is set to radians.","Write BC² = 7² + 9² − 2 × 7 × 9 × cos 1.1."],"codes":["5.1"]},{"q":"Solve 3 + 5 cos 2x = 1 for −180° < x < 180°, giving your answers to 1 decimal place.","a":"x = 56.8°, 123.2°, −56.8° and −123.2°","sol":"Rearranging, 5 cos 2x = −2, so cos 2x = −0.4 (M1). Let u = 2x; since −180° < x < 180°, the interval is −360° < u < 360° (M1). cos u = −0.4 has principal value u = 113.578°, and cosine is even and has period 360°, so u = 113.578°, −113.578°, 360° − 113.578° = 246.422° and −246.422° (A1 for two correct values of u, A1 for all four). Halving each, x = 56.8°, −56.8°, 123.2° and −123.2° to 1 decimal place (A1 cao).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Make cos 2x the subject before you look at the interval.","Double the interval to get the range for 2x, find every angle in it with that cosine, then halve.","The equation reduces to cos 2x = −0.4 with −360° < 2x < 360°."],"codes":["5.7"]},{"q":"Solve 6 cos²x + sin x − 5 = 0 for 0 ≤ x < 360°, giving your answers to 1 decimal place where they are not exact.","a":"x = 30°, 150°, 199.5° and 340.5°","sol":"Replace cos²x by 1 − sin²x: 6(1 − sin²x) + sin x − 5 = 0 (M1). Expanding and tidying gives 6sin²x − sin x − 1 = 0 (A1). Factorising, (3 sin x + 1)(2 sin x − 1) = 0, so sin x = −1/3 or sin x = 1/2 (M1 A1). From sin x = 1/2, x = 30° and x = 180° − 30° = 150° (A1). From sin x = −1/3 the related acute angle is 19.471°, and sine is negative in the third and fourth quadrants, so x = 180° + 19.471° = 199.5° and x = 360° − 19.471° = 340.5° (A1).","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["A quadratic needs a single trigonometric function — which identity removes cos²x?","Use sin²x + cos²x = 1 to get a quadratic in sin x, then factorise it and solve each bracket over the whole interval.","Substitute cos²x = 1 − sin²x into the equation."],"codes":["5.5","5.7"]},{"q":"Express 7 cos θ − 24 sin θ in the form R cos(θ + α), where R > 0 and 0 < α < 90°, giving α to 1 decimal place.","a":"25 cos(θ + 73.7°)","sol":"Expanding the required form, R cos(θ + α) = R cos α cos θ − R sin α sin θ, so comparing coefficients R cos α = 7 and R sin α = 24 (M1). Squaring and adding, R² = 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625, so R = 25 (A1). Dividing, tan α = 24/7, so α = 73.7398° = 73.7° to 1 decimal place (M1 A1). Hence 7 cos θ − 24 sin θ = 25 cos(θ + 73.7°).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Expanding the required form and comparing coefficients is the whole method.","Match the cos θ terms and the sin θ terms to get two equations in R and α, then use R² = a² + b² and a ratio for tan α.","Write R cos θ cos α − R sin θ sin α ≡ 7 cos θ − 24 sin θ."],"codes":["5.6"]},{"q":"Prove that (cos 2x)/(cos x + sin x) ≡ cos x − sin x for all x for which cos x + sin x ≠ 0.","a":"cos 2x = (cos x − sin x)(cos x + sin x), so the fraction cancels to cos x − sin x","sol":"Work on the left-hand side. Use the version cos 2x = cos²x − sin²x (M1). This is a difference of two squares, so cos²x − sin²x = (cos x − sin x)(cos x + sin x) (M1 A1). Hence the left-hand side is (cos x − sin x)(cos x + sin x)/(cos x + sin x), and since cos x + sin x ≠ 0 that common factor cancels to leave cos x − sin x, which is the right-hand side, so the identity is proved (A1 cso).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Only the numerator needs changing — which version of cos 2x has two squared terms?","Choose cos 2x = cos²x − sin²x and look at the factors that form produces.","Replace cos 2x in the numerator by cos²x − sin²x."],"codes":["5.8","5.6"]},{"q":"Given that sin A = 8/17, where A is obtuse, and cos B = 3/5, where B is acute, find the exact value of cos(A − B).","a":"−13/85","sol":"A is obtuse, so cos A is negative: cos A = −√(1 − (8/17)²) = −√(225/289) = −15/17 (M1 A1). B is acute, so sin B is positive: sin B = √(1 − (3/5)²) = 4/5 (B1). Then cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B (M1) = (−15/17)(3/5) + (8/17)(4/5) = −45/85 + 32/85 = −13/85 (A1 cao, exact).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The expansion needs all four of sin A, cos A, sin B and cos B.","Use sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for the missing ratios and take the sign from the quadrant each angle lies in.","Because A is obtuse, cos A must be taken as negative."],"codes":["5.6","5.5"]},{"q":"Solve cosec²θ − 3 cot θ − 5 = 0 for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 180°, giving your answers to 1 decimal place where they are not exact.","a":"θ = 14.0° and θ = 135°","sol":"Use cosec²θ = 1 + cot²θ: 1 + cot²θ − 3 cot θ − 5 = 0, so cot²θ − 3 cot θ − 4 = 0 (M1 A1). Factorising, (cot θ − 4)(cot θ + 1) = 0, so cot θ = 4 or cot θ = −1 (M1). cot θ = 4 gives tan θ = 1/4 and θ = 14.036° = 14.0° (A1). cot θ = −1 gives tan θ = −1; tangent is negative in the second quadrant, so θ = 180° − 45° = 135° (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["There is a quadratic hiding here once every term is written in one function.","The identity 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ turns this into a quadratic in cot θ, and each root then gives a value of tan θ.","Replace cosec²θ by 1 + cot²θ."],"codes":["5.5","5.4","5.7"]},{"q":"The height of a passenger on a big wheel is modelled by H = 13 − 12 cos(πt/5), where H is the height in metres above the ground and t is the time in minutes after boarding. Find, to 1 decimal place, the length of time during the first revolution for which the passenger is more than 19 m above the ground.","a":"3.3 minutes (exactly 10/3 minutes)","sol":"The period is 2π ÷ (π/5) = 10, so one revolution is 0 ≤ t ≤ 10 (B1). H > 19 gives 13 − 12 cos(πt/5) > 19, so −12 cos(πt/5) > 6 and cos(πt/5) < −0.5 (M1 A1). With u = πt/5, as t runs from 0 to 10 u runs from 0 to 2π, and cos u < −0.5 for 2π/3 < u < 4π/3 (M1). Converting back, πt/5 = 2π/3 gives t = 10/3 and πt/5 = 4π/3 gives t = 20/3 (A1). The passenger is above 19 m for 20/3 − 10/3 = 10/3 minutes = 3.3 minutes to 1 decimal place (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Turn the words into an inequality in cos(πt/5) before thinking about time.","Solve the boundary equation for the two times when the height is exactly 19 m and subtract; the period tells you the length of one revolution.","Rearranging H > 19 gives cos(πt/5) < −0.5."],"codes":["5.9","5.7","5.1"]},{"q":"Show that the equation 2 tan²x − 3 sec x = 0 can be written as 2 sec²x − 3 sec x − 2 = 0, and hence solve 2 tan²x − 3 sec x = 0 for 0 ≤ x < 360°.","a":"x = 60° and x = 300°","sol":"From sec²x = 1 + tan²x we get tan²x = sec²x − 1 (M1). Substituting, 2(sec²x − 1) − 3 sec x = 2 sec²x − 3 sec x − 2, and setting this equal to zero gives the printed form (A1 cso, every line must be seen). Factorising, (2 sec x + 1)(sec x − 2) = 0, so sec x = −1/2 or sec x = 2 (M1 A1). sec x = −1/2 gives cos x = −2, which is impossible since −1 ≤ cos x ≤ 1, so it is rejected (B1). sec x = 2 gives cos x = 1/2, so x = 60° and x = 360° − 60° = 300° (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The printed form contains no tangent, so one identity does the whole first part.","Replace tan²x using sec²x = 1 + tan²x, then treat the result as a quadratic in sec x and check whether each root is possible.","Write tan²x = sec²x − 1 and substitute it in."],"codes":["5.5","5.4","5.7"]},{"q":"A sector of a circle of radius r cm has an angle of θ radians at the centre. The perimeter of the sector is 20 cm and its area is 21 cm². Given that θ < 1, find the value of r and the value of θ.","a":"r = 7 cm and θ = 6/7 radians","sol":"The perimeter is two radii plus the arc, so 2r + rθ = 20 (B1). The area gives ½r²θ = 21, so rθ = 42/r (M1). Substituting into the perimeter equation, 2r + 42/r = 20, so 2r² − 20r + 42 = 0 and r² − 10r + 21 = 0 (M1 A1). Factorising, (r − 3)(r − 7) = 0, so r = 3 or r = 7 (A1). If r = 3 then θ = 42/9 = 14/3, which is not less than 1, so this root is rejected; if r = 7 then θ = 42/49 = 6/7 < 1 (M1). Hence r = 7 cm and θ = 6/7 radians (A1). Check: 2(7) + 7(6/7) = 20 and ½(7²)(6/7) = 21.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["The perimeter of a sector is two radii plus the arc, and the two facts give two equations in r and θ.","Eliminate θ between 2r + rθ = 20 and ½r²θ = 21 to leave a quadratic in r, then use the condition on θ to choose the right root.","The area gives rθ = 42/r."],"codes":["5.1"]},{"q":"Given that θ is small and measured in radians, show that cos(θ − π/3) ≈ a + bθ + cθ², and state the exact values of the constants a, b and c.","a":"a = 1/2, b = √3/2 and c = −1/4","sol":"Expand with the compound angle formula: cos(θ − π/3) = cos θ cos(π/3) + sin θ sin(π/3) (M1). The exact values give cos(θ − π/3) = ½ cos θ + (√3/2) sin θ (A1). Now apply the small angle approximations cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2 and sin θ ≈ θ (M1): cos(θ − π/3) ≈ ½(1 − θ²/2) + (√3/2)θ = ½ + (√3/2)θ − θ²/4 (A1). Comparing with a + bθ + cθ² gives a = 1/2, b = √3/2 and c = −1/4 (A1 cao, exact values required).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Two tools are needed here: one to split the bracket and one to deal with the small angle.","Expand cos(A − B) first, put in the exact values for π/3, and only then approximate cos θ and sin θ.","Start from cos(θ − π/3) = cos θ cos(π/3) + sin θ sin(π/3)."],"codes":["5.6","5.2","5.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Solve tan x = −1 for 0° ≤ x < 360°.","a":"x = 135° and x = 315°"},{"q":"Given that cot θ = 3/4, find the exact value of cosec²θ.","a":"25/16"},{"q":"Given that x is small and in radians, write down an approximation for cos 2x.","a":"1 − 2x²"},{"q":"Express 5 sin θ + 12 cos θ in the form R sin(θ + α), where R > 0 and 0 < α < 90°, giving α to 1 decimal place.","a":"13 sin(θ + 67.4°)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Definition of a radian","back":"The angle subtended at the centre of a circle by an arc equal in length to the radius. π radians = 180°, so one radian is about 57.3°.","code":"5.1","open":false},{"front":"Arc length and sector area","back":"s = rθ and A = ½r²θ, with θ in radians. Neither is printed in the formulae booklet.","code":"5.1","open":false},{"front":"Sine rule, cosine rule and the area of a triangle","back":"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C; a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A; area = ½ab sin C. With the sine rule, 180° − θ may also fit.","code":"5.1","open":false},{"front":"The small angle approximations","back":"For θ in radians: sin θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2, tan θ ≈ θ. All three are printed in the formulae booklet.","code":"5.2","open":false},{"front":"Exact values at π/6, π/4 and π/3","back":"sin: 1/2, √2/2, √3/2. cos: √3/2, √2/2, 1/2. tan: 1/√3, 1, √3.","code":"5.3","open":false},{"front":"Periods, ranges and asymptotes of the three graphs","back":"y = sin x and y = cos x: period 360° or 2π, range −1 ≤ y ≤ 1. y = tan x: period 180° or π, asymptotes at x = 90° + 180n°.","code":"5.3","open":false},{"front":"Definitions of sec, cosec and cot","back":"sec x = 1/cos x, cosec x = 1/sin x, cot x = 1/tan x = cos x/sin x. Each is undefined where its denominator is zero, and |sec x| ≥ 1, |cosec x| ≥ 1.","code":"5.4","open":false},{"front":"Domains and ranges of arcsin, arccos and arctan","back":"arcsin and arccos have domain −1 ≤ x ≤ 1, with ranges −π/2 ≤ y ≤ π/2 and 0 ≤ y ≤ π. arctan has domain all real x and range −π/2 < y < π/2.","code":"5.4","open":false},{"front":"The three Pythagorean identities","back":"sin²θ + cos²θ = 1; sec²θ = 1 + tan²θ; cosec²θ = 1 + cot²θ. Divide the first by cos²θ and by sin²θ to reach the others.","code":"5.5","open":false},{"front":"Compound angle formulae","back":"sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B; cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B. These and tan(A ± B) are in the formulae booklet.","code":"5.6","open":false},{"front":"Double angle formulae","back":"sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A; cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A = 2cos²A − 1 = 1 − 2sin²A; tan 2A = 2 tan A/(1 − tan²A).","code":"5.6","open":false},{"front":"Harmonic form of a cos θ + b sin θ","back":"a cos θ + b sin θ = R cos(θ − α) with R = √(a² + b²) > 0 and tan α = b/a for acute α. Maximum R, minimum −R.","code":"5.6","open":false},{"front":"Finding every solution in a given interval","back":"Convert the interval to match the whole argument, then use sin(180° − x) = sin x, cos(360° − x) = cos x and tan(180° + x) = tan x, adding whole periods before converting back.","code":"5.7","open":false},{"front":"How a proof of an identity must be laid out","back":"Work on one side only, name the identity used on each line, finish with a line identical to the other side and a closing statement. Never solve it like an equation.","code":"5.8","open":false},{"front":"Reading the model y = a + b sin(kt)","back":"a is the mean value, |b| the amplitude, the greatest and least values are a + |b| and a − |b|, and the period is 360°/k in degrees or 2π/k in radians.","code":"5.9","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Degrees and radians: π radians = 180°; degrees to radians multiply by π/180, radians to degrees multiply by 180/π — not in the booklet.","Arc length of a sector: s = rθ, with θ in radians — not in the booklet.","Area of a sector: A = ½r²θ, with θ in radians — not in the booklet.","Sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C — not in the booklet.","Cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A — not in the booklet.","Area of a triangle: area = ½ab sin C — not in the booklet.","tan θ = sin θ/cos θ — not in the booklet.","Reciprocal functions: sec θ = 1/cos θ, cosec θ = 1/sin θ, cot θ = 1/tan θ = cos θ/sin θ — not in the booklet.","sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 — not in the booklet.","sec²θ = 1 + tan²θ — not in the booklet.","cosec²θ = 1 + cot²θ — not in the booklet.","sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B — given in the booklet.","cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B — given in the booklet.","tan(A ± B) = (tan A ± tan B)/(1 ∓ tan A tan B) — given in the booklet.","sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A — not in the booklet; put B = A in sin(A + B).","cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A = 2cos²A − 1 = 1 − 2sin²A — not in the booklet.","tan 2A = 2 tan A/(1 − tan²A) — not in the booklet.","Harmonic form: a cos θ + b sin θ = R cos(θ − α), R = √(a² + b²), tan α = b/a — not in the booklet.","Small angle approximations (θ in radians): sin θ ≈ θ, cos θ ≈ 1 − θ²/2, tan θ ≈ θ — given in the booklet.","Exact values: sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2, tan 30° = 1/√3; sin 45° = cos 45° = √2/2, tan 45° = 1; sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 60° = √3 — not in the booklet.","Symmetries: sin(180° − x) = sin x, cos(360° − x) = cos x, tan(180° + x) = tan x — not in the booklet."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where the marks are lost","items":["Radian mode: a sector, small angle or modelling question in radians worked in degree mode loses every accuracy mark.","Dividing an equation by sin x or cos x deletes a whole family of solutions — factorise instead.","Change the interval before solving a multiple or shifted angle, and convert back only at the end.","Exact means exact: leave 5π/6, √3/2 or 6/7 — a rounded decimal loses the final A mark.","In a show that question the printed answer scores nothing; the marks are in the lines that reach it.","Reject impossible roots in writing, for example sec x = −1/2 giving cos x = −2, which no angle satisfies.","Give the unit (cm, cm², minutes) and round once, at the end, to the accuracy the question asks for."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:21:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"I re-solved all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit tickets, the 5 lesson checks and all 6 worked examples independently in node before reading a single answer key, and every one matches: 16.2 cm², (√3−1)/2, 80°/320°, 4, 30°/210°, BC² = 72.84689 → 8.535 → 8.54 cm, cos 2x = −0.4 → ±56.8°/±123.2°, 6sin²x − sin x − 1 = 0 → 30°/150°/199.5°/340.5°, 25 cos(θ + 73.7°), the difference-of-squares proof, −13/85, cot²θ − 3cot θ − 4 = 0 → 14.0°/135°, 10/3 = 3.3 minutes, 2sec²x − 3sec x − 2 = 0 → 60°/300° with sec x = −1/2 rejected, r = 7 with θ = 6/7, and a = ½, b = √3/2, c = −¼; the worked examples check out too (segment 43.2 − 40.654 = 2.55 cm², 8/3, 53.1301°/306.8699°, √29 with α = 68.1986° → 77.9°/325.7°, the tan x proof, 4 a.m. and 8 a.m.), and every mark tally in a sol adds to its m field. The five faults the first examiner listed are all repaired: questions[5].sol now reads √72.847 = 8.5350 (correct to 4 d.p.), questions[0].hints[2] gives the shape of the sector formula with no substituted values, exit[1] is now a cold reciprocal/identity question (cot θ = 3/4 → cosec²θ = 25/16, a genuine 5.4/5.5 strand the bank had not opened with) instead of a repeat of questions[0], examples[2].cues[2] no longer names factorising, and the Formulae sheet now carries sec/cosec/cot as item 7 marked 'not in the booklet'. All nine idea codes 5.1–5.9 appear in order with nothing beyond the specification (no tan(½θ)), no hint states an answer, the cards are all facts rather than questions, and the Formulae sheet's booklet claims match Appendix 1 exactly — the sine and cosine rules, ½ab sin C, s = rθ, A = ½r²θ, the three Pythagorean identities, the exact values and sin 2A/cos 2A/tan 2A are all correctly marked 'not in the booklet' while the compound-angle formulae and the small-angle approximations are correctly marked as given; publish it, with the two cosmetic items above as the next pass."}}},"P6":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P6","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Exponentials and logarithms appear on every pure paper, and they are the gateway to nearly all of the modelling in this qualification. A logarithm is the only tool that releases an unknown from an index, so a^x = b, e^(ax + b) = p and ln(ax + b) = q all depend on it. The number e matters because the gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx), which is exactly the proportional-rate condition that makes an exponential the right model for populations, radioactive decay, drug concentration and continuous compound interest. Examiners also use logarithms in reverse, turning y = axⁿ or y = kbˣ into a straight line so that constants can be read off a graph, and they then ask you to interpret the constants or criticise the model.","idea":[{"h":"Graphs of aˣ, eˣ and y = e^(ax + b) + c","t":"Every curve y = aˣ with a > 0 and a ≠ 1 passes through (0, 1) and has the x-axis as an asymptote. For a > 1 the curve increases from left to right; for 0 < a < 1 it decreases — that shape difference is what examiners ask you to describe. y = eˣ is the member of the family with e ≈ 2.718. For y = e^(ax + b) + c the added c lifts the whole curve, so the horizontal asymptote is y = c and the y-intercept is (0, e^b + c); a negative a turns the curve the other way round. Sketch marks are awarded feature by feature, so label the intercept, write the equation of the asymptote and show the correct direction.","code":"6.1"},{"h":"The gradient of e^(kx), and why a proportional rate means an exponential","t":"Differentiating e^(kx) gives k e^(kx): the gradient at every point is k times the value of the function itself. Read the other way round, if a quantity changes at a rate proportional to how much is there, so that dy/dx = ky, then y = Ae^(kx) is the function that behaves in exactly that way. That is the reason an exponential model is chosen in the first place, and questions often want it said in a sentence: the rate of growth is proportional to the size of the population, so an exponential model is appropriate.","code":"6.2"},{"h":"Logarithms as inverses, and solving equations with e and ln","t":"log_a x is defined as the inverse of aˣ: log_a x = y means exactly a^y = x, for a > 0, a ≠ 1 and x > 0. ln x means log_e x, so ln(eˣ) = x and e^(ln x) = x — each undoes the other. That is how e^(ax + b) = p is solved, by taking ln of both sides to reach ax + b = ln p, and how ln(ax + b) = q is solved, by raising e to each side to reach ax + b = e^q. Because logarithms of zero and of negative numbers do not exist, every solution must be checked back in the original equation and any root that makes an argument non-positive rejected in writing.","code":"6.3"},{"h":"The laws of logarithms","t":"The three laws mirror the index laws: log_a x + log_a y = log_a(xy), log_a x − log_a y = log_a(x/y) and k log_a x = log_a xᵏ, together with log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0. The power law also holds for negative and fractional k, so −log_a x = log_a(1/x) and ½ log_a x = log_a √x, which is how roots and reciprocals are brought into a single logarithm. In an equation, combine everything into one logarithm first and then undo it: the method mark sits on the line where the logarithms become one, and the last accuracy mark usually depends on rejecting the root that makes a logarithm undefined.","code":"6.4"},{"h":"Solving aˣ = b by taking logarithms","t":"When the unknown sits in the index, take logarithms of both sides and use the power law to bring it down: aˣ = b gives x log a = log b, so x = log b ÷ log a. Any base works, so ln is usually quickest on a calculator, and you may instead quote the change of base formula log_a b = (log b) ÷ (log a). When the index is a linear expression, as in 2^(3x − 1) = 3, treat the whole bracket as the subject first: (3x − 1) log 2 = log 3, then solve the linear equation that is left. The method mark is for taking logarithms correctly, not for the final decimal, so an unsupported calculator answer scores almost nothing.","code":"6.5"},{"h":"Reading constants off a logarithmic graph","t":"If y = axⁿ, taking logarithms gives log y = n log x + log a, so a plot of log y against log x is a straight line of gradient n and intercept log a. If y = kbˣ, taking logarithms gives log y = (log b)x + log k, so you plot log y against x instead, giving gradient log b and intercept log k. The decision to make first is which of the two plots the question has drawn, because that tells you which model is being tested. Convert back at the end with a = 10^(intercept), or b = 10^(gradient), when base 10 has been used; marks are lost by quoting the intercept itself as a, or the gradient itself as b.","code":"6.6"},{"h":"Exponential growth and decay in modelling","t":"A model N = Ae^(kt) has A as the initial value, because e⁰ = 1, with k > 0 for growth and k < 0 for decay. Substitute the pairs of values given to form equations for A and k, then take logarithms to release k from the index. Doubling time and half-life are both (ln 2)/|k| and do not depend on A. You will usually be asked afterwards to interpret a constant in context, or to comment on the model for large t: unrestricted exponential growth predicts unlimited numbers, which limited food, space or money make impossible, so a refined model with an upper ceiling is more realistic.","code":"6.7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Solving 3^(2x − 1) = 40 by taking logarithms","setup":"Solve the equation 3^(2x − 1) = 40, giving the value of x to 3 significant figures.","steps":["Take logarithms of both sides and use the power law to bring the index down: (2x − 1) log 3 = log 40.","Divide by log 3 to make the bracket the subject: 2x − 1 = 1.60206 ÷ 0.477121 = 3.35776.","Add 1 to both sides: 2x = 4.35776.","Halve: x = 2.17888, which is x = 2.18 to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["The x sits in the index — what operation brings it down to the front?","How do you get the whole bracket on its own?","What is the next move towards leaving x by itself?","What single operation is left before you round?"]},{"title":"Solving log₄(3x + 2) − log₄(x − 1) = 1","setup":"Solve the equation log₄(3x + 2) − log₄(x − 1) = 1, and check that the solution you obtain is valid.","steps":["Two logarithms to the same base joined by a minus sign make one logarithm of a quotient: log₄((3x + 2)/(x − 1)) = 1.","Write the statement in index form so the logarithm disappears: (3x + 2)/(x − 1) = 4.","Multiply both sides by (x − 1): 3x + 2 = 4(x − 1) = 4x − 4.","Collect the terms: 2 + 4 = 4x − 3x, so x = 6.","Test the arguments: x − 1 = 5 and 3x + 2 = 20 are both positive, so both logarithms exist and the solution is valid."],"cues":["What can be done with two logarithms to the same base subtracted from one another?","How do you remove a single logarithm from an equation?","What is the first algebraic move now that the logarithms have gone?","How do you finish the linear equation?","Why is it not safe to stop at the value of x?"]},{"title":"Interpreting and using a cooling model","setup":"A cup of coffee stands in a room. Its temperature T °C, t minutes after it is poured, is modelled by T = 22 + 58e^(−0.04t). Write down the temperature the coffee cools towards, find the temperature of the coffee after 15 minutes to 1 decimal place, and find how long, to the nearest minute, the coffee takes to cool to 50 °C.","steps":["As t increases without limit, e^(−0.04t) tends to 0, so T tends to 22: the coffee cools towards the room at 22 °C.","After 15 minutes: T = 22 + 58e^(−0.6) = 22 + 58 × 0.548812 = 22 + 31.831 = 53.8 °C to 1 decimal place.","For the last part set T = 50: 58e^(−0.04t) = 50 − 22 = 28, so e^(−0.04t) = 28 ÷ 58 = 0.482759.","Take natural logarithms: −0.04t = ln 0.482759 = −0.728239, so t = 18.206, which is 18 minutes to the nearest minute."],"cues":["What happens to the exponential term as time goes on, and what value does T approach?","Which number goes in place of t for the second part?","How do you get the exponential term by itself when T is known?","What operation releases t from the index?"]},{"title":"Finding a and n from a log–log graph","setup":"The variables x and y are thought to satisfy y = axⁿ, where a and n are constants. When log₁₀ y is plotted against log₁₀ x the points lie on a straight line through (0.5, 1.4) and (2.0, 3.8). Find the value of n, and the value of a to 3 significant figures.","steps":["Take logarithms of the model: log₁₀ y = log₁₀ a + n log₁₀ x, which is the equation of a straight line with gradient n and intercept log₁₀ a.","The gradient of the line joining the two given points is n = (3.8 − 1.4) ÷ (2.0 − 0.5) = 2.4 ÷ 1.5 = 1.6.","Put the point (0.5, 1.4) into log₁₀ y = log₁₀ a + 1.6 log₁₀ x: 1.4 = log₁₀ a + 1.6 × 0.5, so log₁₀ a = 1.4 − 0.8 = 0.6.","Undo the logarithm: a = 10^0.6 = 3.98107, so a = 3.98 to 3 significant figures and the relationship is y = 3.98x^1.6."],"cues":["What must you do to y = axⁿ before it can look like the equation of a straight line?","Which feature of the line is n, and how is it found from two points?","How do you pin down the intercept once the gradient is known?","The intercept is not a itself — what has to happen to it?"]},{"title":"Finding the constants in a growth model and judging it","setup":"A colony of bees is modelled by B = B₀e^(kt), where B is the number of bees t weeks after the colony is set up, and B₀ and k are constants. The colony starts with 2000 bees and after 6 weeks there are 5000 bees. Find B₀, find k to 3 significant figures, use the model to predict the number of bees after 20 weeks to the nearest hundred, and give one reason why the model is unlikely to hold for large t.","steps":["At the start the exponential factor is e⁰ = 1, so B equals the constant in front of it and B₀ = 2000.","Substitute t = 6 and B = 5000: 5000 = 2000e^(6k), so e^(6k) = 5000 ÷ 2000 = 2.5.","Take natural logarithms: 6k = ln 2.5 = 0.916291, so k = 0.152715, which is 0.153 to 3 significant figures.","Predict with the unrounded value of k: B = 2000e^(0.152715 × 20) = 2000e^(3.05430) = 42413, which is 42400 bees to the nearest hundred.","For large t the model gives unlimited growth, but food, space and disease limit any real colony, so the model will overestimate the number of bees and one with a ceiling would be better."],"cues":["What does the start do to the exponential factor, and which constant is left behind?","Which pair of values can you substitute to leave an equation containing only k?","How do you release k from the index?","Which value of t goes in now, and which value of k should you use?","What does this model say happens eventually, and can a real colony do that?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Solve 4ˣ = 32, giving an exact answer.","a":"x = 5/2"},{"q":"Write ln 12 − ln 4 as a single logarithm.","a":"ln 3"},{"q":"A quantity decays according to Ae^(−kt) and halves every 7 years. Find k to 3 significant figures.","a":"k = (ln 2)/7 = 0.0990"},{"q":"State the equation of the horizontal asymptote of y = e^(3x − 2) − 4.","a":"y = −4"}]},"room":{"facts":["log_a x = y means exactly a^y = x, for a > 0, a ≠ 1 and x > 0.","log_a x + log_a y = log_a(xy); log_a x − log_a y = log_a(x/y); k log_a x = log_a xᵏ.","log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0; a^(log_a x) = x and log_a(aˣ) = x.","ln x is log_e x, the inverse of eˣ: ln(eˣ) = x and e^(ln x) = x.","The gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx), so dy/dx = ky has solution y = Ae^(kx).","aˣ = b gives x = (log b) ÷ (log a); y = axⁿ gives log y = n log x + log a; y = kbˣ gives log y = x log b + log k.","In N = Ae^(kt), A is the value at t = 0, and the doubling time or half-life is (ln 2)/|k|."],"questions":[{"q":"Solve 2ˣ = 20, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"x = 4.32","sol":"Take logarithms of both sides and use the power law to bring the index down: x log 2 = log 20 (M1). So x = (log 20) ÷ (log 2) = 1.30103 ÷ 0.301030 = 4.32193, which is x = 4.32 to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The unknown is stuck in the index, so you need the operation that brings an index down to the front.","Take logarithms of both sides, then use k log a = log aᵏ in reverse to free the x, and divide.","Write x log 2 = log 20, then make x the subject."],"codes":["6.5"]},{"q":"Express 3 log_a x + ½ log_a y − 2 log_a z as a single logarithm.","a":"log_a (x³√y ÷ z²)","sol":"Use the power law on each term: 3 log_a x = log_a x³, ½ log_a y = log_a √y and 2 log_a z = log_a z² (M1). Add the first two with the sum law: log_a x³ + log_a √y = log_a (x³√y) (M1). Subtract the third with the difference law: log_a (x³√y) − log_a z² = log_a (x³√y ÷ z²) (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A number written in front of a logarithm does not belong there — one of the laws moves it.","Turn every coefficient into a power first, then use the sum law on the two terms being added and the difference law on the one being subtracted.","Begin with 3 log_a x = log_a x³ and ½ log_a y = log_a √y."],"codes":["6.4"]},{"q":"Solve e^(2x + 1) = 5, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"x = 0.305","sol":"ln and e are inverses, so taking natural logarithms of both sides gives 2x + 1 = ln 5 (M1). ln 5 = 1.60944, so 2x = 0.609438 (M1). Dividing by 2, x = 0.304719, which is x = 0.305 to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["ln is the inverse of e, so a single operation will remove the exponential completely.","Take ln of both sides so that the whole index becomes the subject, then solve the linear equation that is left.","Write 2x + 1 = ln 5."],"codes":["6.3"]},{"q":"The curve C has equation y = e^(2x + 1) + 3. Write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote of C, and find the exact coordinates of the point where C crosses the y-axis.","a":"Asymptote y = 3; y-intercept (0, e + 3)","sol":"As x → −∞, e^(2x + 1) → 0, so y → 3 and the horizontal asymptote is y = 3 (B1). At x = 0, y = e^(2 × 0 + 1) + 3 = e¹ + 3 (M1), so C crosses the y-axis at (0, e + 3): the question asks for exact coordinates, so leave e + 3 as it is (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what the exponential part does when x becomes large and negative, and separately what happens on the y-axis.","The asymptote comes from the constant that has been added; the intercept comes from putting x = 0 into the equation.","For the intercept, substitute x = 0 into the equation of C and leave the result in terms of e."],"codes":["6.1"]},{"q":"Given that y = 5e^(3x), write down dy/dx and find the value of dy/dx when x = 0.","a":"dy/dx = 15e^(3x), and dy/dx = 15 when x = 0","sol":"The gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx), so differentiating 5e^(3x) gives 5 × 3e^(3x) = 15e^(3x) (B1). At x = 0, e⁰ = 1, so dy/dx = 15 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Differentiating an exponential leaves the exponential itself unchanged — only a multiplier appears in front.","Use the standard result for the gradient of e^(kx), keeping the constant 5 as a multiplier throughout, then substitute.","Start from dy/dx = 5 × d/dx(e^(3x)), then put x = 0 and use e⁰ = 1."],"codes":["6.2"]},{"q":"Solve 2^(3x − 1) = 3, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"x = 0.862","sol":"Take logarithms of both sides and bring the index down: (3x − 1) log 2 = log 3 (M1). Divide by log 2: 3x − 1 = (log 3) ÷ (log 2) = 1.58496 (M1). So 3x = 2.58496 and x = 0.861654, which is x = 0.862 to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The whole index is a linear expression, so treat it as a single bracket to begin with.","Take logarithms of both sides and use the power law, then make that bracket the subject before solving for x.","Write (3x − 1) log 2 = log 3."],"codes":["6.5"]},{"q":"Solve log₂ x + log₂ (x − 2) = 3.","a":"x = 4","sol":"Combine the left-hand side with the sum law: log₂(x(x − 2)) = 3 (M1). Write it in index form: x(x − 2) = 2³ = 8 (M1). So x² − 2x − 8 = 0, which factorises as (x − 4)(x + 2) = 0, giving x = 4 or x = −2 (A1). x = −2 would require log₂(−2), which does not exist, so that root is rejected and x = 4 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two logarithms to the same base added together can be written as one.","Combine into a single logarithm, rewrite log₂(something) = 3 in index form, then solve the quadratic that appears.","Write log₂(x(x − 2)) = 3, and plan to test each root in the original equation."],"codes":["6.3","6.4"]},{"q":"Solve e^(2x) − 5e^x + 4 = 0, giving your answers in exact form.","a":"x = 0 and x = 2 ln 2","sol":"Since e^(2x) = (e^x)², the substitution u = e^x turns the equation into u² − 5u + 4 = 0 (M1). Factorising, (u − 1)(u − 4) = 0, so u = 1 or u = 4 (A1). e^x = 1 gives x = ln 1 = 0 (A1). e^x = 4 gives x = ln 4 = 2 ln 2 (A1). Both values of u are positive, so neither root is rejected.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the two powers: one is the square of the other, so this is a familiar type of equation in disguise.","Substitute u = e^x to get a quadratic in u, solve it, then return to x by taking logarithms of each value of u.","Write u = e^x, so that u² − 5u + 4 = 0."],"codes":["6.3"]},{"q":"The curve C has equation y = 3e^(−x) − 6. State the equation of the horizontal asymptote of C, and find the exact coordinates of the two points where C crosses the coordinate axes.","a":"Asymptote y = −6; crossings (0, −3) and (−ln 2, 0)","sol":"As x → ∞, 3e^(−x) → 0, so y → −6 and the horizontal asymptote is y = −6 (B1). At x = 0, y = 3 − 6 = −3, so C meets the y-axis at (0, −3) (B1). Setting y = 0 gives 3e^(−x) = 6, so e^(−x) = 2 (M1). Taking natural logarithms, −x = ln 2, so x = −ln 2 and C meets the x-axis at (−ln 2, 0) (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Each feature comes from a different substitution: one from large x, one from x = 0 and one from y = 0.","For the asymptote see what the exponential term tends to; for the intercepts put x = 0, then set y = 0 and use logarithms to solve for x.","For the crossing on the x-axis, start from 3e^(−x) − 6 = 0."],"codes":["6.1","6.3"]},{"q":"The variables x and y satisfy y = axⁿ, where a and n are constants. A plot of log₁₀ y against log₁₀ x gives the straight line log₁₀ y = 0.9 + 1.5 log₁₀ x. Find the value of n, and the value of a to 3 significant figures.","a":"n = 1.5 and a = 7.94","sol":"Take logarithms of the model: log₁₀ y = log₁₀ a + n log₁₀ x (M1). Comparing this with log₁₀ y = 0.9 + 1.5 log₁₀ x, the coefficient of log₁₀ x gives n = 1.5 (A1). The constant term gives log₁₀ a = 0.9, so a = 10^0.9 = 7.94328, which is a = 7.94 to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Take logarithms of the model first, so that it has the same shape as the equation of the printed line.","Compare log₁₀ y = log₁₀ a + n log₁₀ x with the line given: one constant is the gradient, the other is the intercept, and one of them still needs undoing.","Write log₁₀ y = log₁₀ a + n log₁₀ x and match the two coefficients."],"codes":["6.6"]},{"q":"The mass m grams of a radioactive sample t years after it is made is modelled by m = 250e^(−kt), where k is a positive constant. After 8 years the mass has fallen to 180 g. Find the value of k to 3 significant figures, and hence find the half-life of the sample to 3 significant figures.","a":"k = 0.0411 and the half-life is 16.9 years","sol":"Substitute t = 8 and m = 180: 180 = 250e^(−8k), so e^(−8k) = 180 ÷ 250 = 0.72 (M1). Take natural logarithms: −8k = ln 0.72 = −0.328504 (M1), so k = 0.0410630, which is k = 0.0411 to 3 significant figures (A1). The half-life T satisfies e^(−kT) = 0.5, so kT = ln 2 (M1) and T = 0.693147 ÷ 0.0410630 = 16.8801, which is 16.9 years to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Put the pair of values you are given into the model, then tidy it until only the exponential is left on one side.","Divide to isolate e^(−8k) and take natural logarithms to find k; for the second part use the fact that the half-life is the time for any starting mass to fall to half of itself.","Write 180 = 250e^(−8k)."],"codes":["6.7"]},{"q":"(a) Show that the equation log₂(x + 5) = 4 − log₂ x can be written as x² + 5x − 16 = 0. (b) Hence solve log₂(x + 5) = 4 − log₂ x, giving your answer in exact form.","a":"x = (−5 + √89)/2","sol":"(a) Add log₂ x to both sides so that all the logarithms are together: log₂(x + 5) + log₂ x = 4 (M1). Use the sum law: log₂(x(x + 5)) = 4 (M1). Write it in index form: x(x + 5) = 2⁴ = 16 (M1). Expanding, x² + 5x = 16, so x² + 5x − 16 = 0 as required (A1 cso). (b) The quadratic does not factorise, so use the formula: x = (−5 ± √(5² + 4 × 16))/2 = (−5 ± √89)/2 (M1). √89 = 9.43398, so the roots are x = 2.21699 and x = −7.21699 (A1). The negative root would require log₂ of a negative number, so it is rejected and x = (−5 + √89)/2 (A1).","m":7,"d":2,"hints":["In part (a) every logarithm has to be on the same side of the equation before any law can be used.","Collect the logarithms, combine them into one with the sum law and write the result in index form; in part (b) apply the quadratic formula to the printed equation and then test the roots.","Start part (a) from log₂(x + 5) + log₂ x = 4."],"codes":["6.3","6.4"]},{"q":"The variables x and y are believed to satisfy y = kbˣ, where k and b are constants. When log₁₀ y is plotted against x the points lie on a straight line passing through (2, 1.10) and (6, 2.30). Find the value of b and the value of k, each to 3 significant figures.","a":"b = 2.00 and k = 3.16","sol":"Take logarithms of the model: log₁₀ y = log₁₀ k + x log₁₀ b, a straight line of gradient log₁₀ b and intercept log₁₀ k (M1). The gradient is (2.30 − 1.10) ÷ (6 − 2) = 1.20 ÷ 4 = 0.300, so log₁₀ b = 0.300 and b = 10^0.300 = 1.99526, which is b = 2.00 to 3 significant figures (M1 A1). Using the point (2, 1.10): 1.10 = log₁₀ k + 0.300 × 2, so log₁₀ k = 1.10 − 0.600 = 0.500 (M1). Hence k = 10^0.5 = 3.16228, which is k = 3.16 to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Only one of the two standard plots straightens y = kbˣ — look carefully at what has been plotted on each axis here.","Take logarithms of the model, match the gradient and the intercept to log b and log k, and get the gradient from the two points given.","Write log₁₀ y = log₁₀ k + x log₁₀ b."],"codes":["6.6"]},{"q":"The number of bacteria N in a culture t hours after the start is modelled by N = 400e^(0.15t). State the number of bacteria at the start, find to 3 significant figures the time at which N reaches 5000, and explain why the model is not appropriate for large values of t.","a":"400 bacteria; t = 16.8 hours; the model grows without limit while food and space are finite, so the real number must level off","sol":"At the start t = 0 and e⁰ = 1, so N = 400 (B1). For N = 5000: 400e^(0.15t) = 5000, so e^(0.15t) = 5000 ÷ 400 = 12.5 (M1). Take natural logarithms: 0.15t = ln 12.5 = 2.52573 (M1), so t = 16.8382, which is t = 16.8 hours to 3 significant figures (A1). For large t the model predicts growth without any limit (B1), whereas nutrients and space are finite, so a real culture must level off; a model with an upper ceiling would fit better (B1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["'At the start' fixes one value of t; for the middle part the exponential must be alone before logarithms can help.","Substitute t = 0 for the initial number, divide to isolate e^(0.15t) and take natural logarithms for the time, then think about what an increasing exponential does as t keeps growing.","Write 400e^(0.15t) = 5000 and divide both sides by 400."],"codes":["6.7"]},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations log₂ x + log₂ y = 5 and log₂ x − log₂ y = 1.","a":"x = 8 and y = 4","sol":"Apply the sum law to the first equation: log₂(xy) = 5, so xy = 2⁵ = 32 (M1 A1). Apply the difference law to the second: log₂(x/y) = 1, so x/y = 2 and therefore x = 2y (M1 A1). Substituting into xy = 32 gives 2y² = 32, so y² = 16 and y = 4, the negative root being rejected because log₂ y must exist (A1). Then x = 2 × 4 = 8 (A1). Check: log₂ 8 + log₂ 4 = 3 + 2 = 5 and log₂ 8 − log₂ 4 = 3 − 2 = 1.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Each equation collapses to a single logarithm, and a single logarithm can be removed altogether.","Turn both equations into ordinary equations in x and y with no logarithms, then solve those simultaneously by substitution.","Write log₂(xy) = 5 and log₂(x/y) = 1."],"codes":["6.3","6.4"]},{"q":"Given that a > 0, b > 0, a ≠ 1, b ≠ 1 and log_a b = c, where c ≠ 0, prove that log_b a = 1/c.","a":"log_a b = c gives a^c = b, so c log_b a = log_b b = 1 and therefore log_b a = 1/c","sol":"Use the definition of a logarithm to write the assumption in index form: log_a b = c means a^c = b (M1). Take logarithms to base b of both sides: log_b(a^c) = log_b b (M1). By the power law the left-hand side is c log_b a, and log_b b = 1, so c log_b a = 1 (A1). Since c ≠ 0 the equation may be divided by c, giving log_b a = 1/c, which is what was required (A1 cso).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A logarithm statement and an index statement say the same thing — move to whichever is easier to work with.","Rewrite the given statement in index form, then take logarithms of both sides to the other base and use the power law on the result.","Begin with a^c = b."],"codes":["6.3","6.4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"State the equation of the horizontal asymptote of y = e^(4x) − 7.","a":"y = −7"},{"q":"Write log₅ 40 − log₅ 8 as a single logarithm and evaluate it.","a":"log₅ 5 = 1"},{"q":"Solve 3ˣ = 15, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"x = 2.46"},{"q":"A population is modelled by P = 800e^(0.05t), where t is in years. State the initial population and find the doubling time to 3 significant figures.","a":"800; doubling time 13.9 years"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Definition of log_a x","back":"log_a x = y means exactly a^y = x, for a > 0, a ≠ 1 and x > 0. It answers the question: a to what power gives x?","code":"6.3","open":false},{"front":"The three laws of logarithms","back":"log_a x + log_a y = log_a(xy); log_a x − log_a y = log_a(x/y); k log_a x = log_a xᵏ, including k = −1 and k = −½.","code":"6.4","open":false},{"front":"The values of log_a a and log_a 1","back":"log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0, because a¹ = a and a⁰ = 1. From the definition it also follows that a^(log_a x) = x.","code":"6.4","open":false},{"front":"How ln x and eˣ are related","back":"ln x means log_e x. It is the inverse function of eˣ: ln(eˣ) = x for every x, and e^(ln x) = x for x > 0.","code":"6.3","open":false},{"front":"The gradient of e^(kx)","back":"The gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx): differentiating multiplies by k and leaves the exponential itself unchanged.","code":"6.2","open":false},{"front":"Why a proportional rate of change means an exponential model","back":"If dy/dx is proportional to y, so dy/dx = ky, then y = Ae^(kx). A rate of change proportional to the amount present is exactly what an exponential describes.","code":"6.2","open":false},{"front":"Shape of y = aˣ for a > 1 and for 0 < a < 1","back":"For a > 1 the curve rises from left to right; for 0 < a < 1 it falls. Both pass through (0, 1) and have the x-axis, y = 0, as an asymptote.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"Features of y = e^(ax + b) + c","back":"The horizontal asymptote is y = c, the y-intercept is (0, e^b + c), and the curve rises for a > 0 and falls for a < 0.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"Method for solving aˣ = b","back":"Take logarithms of both sides: x log a = log b, so x = (log b) ÷ (log a). Any base may be used, and ln is usually quickest.","code":"6.5","open":false},{"front":"The change of base formula","back":"log_a x = (log_b x) ÷ (log_b a) for any valid base b; in particular log_a x = (ln x) ÷ (ln a). It is printed in the formulae booklet.","code":"6.5","open":false},{"front":"Straight-line form of y = axⁿ","back":"log y = n log x + log a. Plotting log y against log x gives a straight line of gradient n and intercept log a.","code":"6.6","open":false},{"front":"Straight-line form of y = kbˣ","back":"log y = (log b)x + log k. Plotting log y against x gives a straight line of gradient log b and intercept log k.","code":"6.6","open":false},{"front":"What 'initial' means in an exponential model","back":"Initial means the value when t = 0. In N = Ae^(kt) the initial value is A, because e⁰ = 1.","code":"6.7","open":false},{"front":"Doubling time and half-life for Ae^(kt)","back":"For growth with k > 0 the doubling time is (ln 2)/k; for decay Ae^(−kt) the half-life is (ln 2)/k. Neither depends on A.","code":"6.7","open":false},{"front":"State one limitation of an unrestricted exponential growth model.","back":"It predicts growth without any limit, so it overestimates once resources run out; it ignores limiting factors such as food, space or disease; it assumes the proportional growth rate never changes.","code":"6.7","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Laws of indices: aˣaʸ = a^(x + y), aˣ ÷ aʸ = a^(x − y) and (aˣ)ʸ = a^(xy) — not in the booklet, learn it.","Definition: log_a x = y ⟺ a^y = x, for a > 0, a ≠ 1 and x > 0 — not in the booklet, learn it.","log_a x + log_a y = log_a(xy) — not in the booklet, learn it.","log_a x − log_a y = log_a(x/y) — not in the booklet, learn it.","k log_a x = log_a xᵏ, including k = −1 and k = −½ — not in the booklet, learn it.","log_a a = 1 and log_a 1 = 0 — not in the booklet, learn it.","ln x = log_e x, with ln(eˣ) = x and e^(ln x) = x — not in the booklet, learn it.","The gradient of e^(kx) is k e^(kx) — not in the booklet's differentiation list, learn it.","Solving aˣ = b: x log a = log b, so x = (log b) ÷ (log a) — not in the booklet, learn it.","Change of base: log_a x = (log_b x) ÷ (log_b a) — given in the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet, under Logarithms and exponentials.","e^(x ln a) = aˣ — given in the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet, under Logarithms and exponentials.","Straight-line form of y = axⁿ: log y = n log x + log a, gradient n and intercept log a — not in the booklet, learn it.","Straight-line form of y = kbˣ: log y = x log b + log k, gradient log b and intercept log k — not in the booklet, learn it.","Exponential model N = Ae^(kt): A is the value at t = 0, and the doubling time or half-life is (ln 2)/|k| — not in the booklet, learn it.","Quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ (2a), needed whenever a logarithmic equation reduces to a quadratic — assumed from GCSE and not in the booklet."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where the marks go","items":["'Show that' prints the answer, so nothing is earned for writing it down: every line from the given equation to the printed result must be seen, and the last line must match exactly (cso).","A correct decimal with no logarithm line scores no method mark — write x log 2 = log 20 before reaching for the calculator.","Logarithms of zero and of negative numbers do not exist, so a root that makes any argument non-positive must be rejected in writing; the final accuracy mark usually depends on it.","'Exact' means leaving the answer as ln 2, 2 ln 2, e + 3 or (−5 + √89)/2; a decimal there loses the final A mark.","Round only at the very end: putting a 3-significant-figure value of k back into a model can shift the next answer, so keep the unrounded value stored in the calculator.","On a logarithmic graph, say which quantity is the gradient and which is the intercept — for log y against log x the gradient is n, not a, and the intercept is log a, not a."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:19:49.094540Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.94,"notes":"Independently re-solved all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples: every key, rounding and form is correct (4.32, 0.305, 0.862, (0, e+3), x = 4, 2 ln 2, (−5+√89)/2, n = 1.5 with a = 7.94, b = 2.00 with k = 3.16, k = 0.0411 with half-life 16.9 y, t = 16.8 h, x = 8 and y = 4, and 2.18 / x = 6 / 53.8 °C and 18 min / a = 3.98 with n = 1.6 / k = 0.153 with 42400 bees), every Appendix 1 memory claim on the formulae sheet is right and change of base and e^(x ln a) are correctly attributed to the booklet, so only two presentational nits remain."}}},"P8":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P8","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Integration is the second largest topic on Papers 1 and 2, and it is the engine of Paper 3 modelling. It answers the two questions differentiation cannot: what is the total when you only know the rate, and what is the area under a curve. Examiners test it in three layers — routine reversal of the power rule and the standard results, then the choice of technique (substitution, parts, partial fractions) for an unfamiliar integrand, then separable differential equations set in a real context where you must also interpret the solution and criticise the model. Marks are lost far more often for a missing constant of integration, for unchanged limits after a substitution, or for integrating the wrong way round on an area question, than for not knowing a standard integral.","idea":[{"h":"Integration reverses differentiation, so + c is compulsory","code":"8.1","t":"The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus says integration and differentiation are inverse processes: if dy/dx = f(x) then y = ∫ f(x) dx, and differentiating ∫ f(x) dx gives f(x) back. That is your free check — differentiate every answer you write. Because any constant differentiates to zero, an indefinite integral is a whole family of curves y = F(x) + c, not one curve, so + c must be there; a definite integral is a number and never carries + c. Given f'(x) and one point on the curve, substitute the point to pin down c and give the single curve y = f(x). Omitting + c costs the final A mark every time."},{"h":"The power rule backwards, and the standard integrals","code":"8.2","t":"∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + c for every n except n = −1, where ∫ (1/x) dx = ln|x| + c. Every term must be written as a power of x first: (x + 2)²/√x has to be expanded to x^(3/2) + 4x^(1/2) + 4x^(−1/2) before you can touch it. The standard results are ∫ e^(kx) dx = (1/k)e^(kx) + c, ∫ sin kx dx = −(1/k)cos kx + c, ∫ cos kx dx = (1/k)sin kx + c and ∫ sec² kx dx = (1/k)tan kx + c — notice the 1/k, which is the reverse chain rule. Squared trig functions are not standard: use cos 2A = 1 − 2sin² A = 2cos² A − 1 for sin² x and cos² 3x, and sec² x = 1 + tan² x for tan² x."},{"h":"Definite integrals, and areas under and between curves","code":"8.3","t":"∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a): integrate, substitute the top limit, subtract the value at the bottom limit, and show that subtraction line because it carries a mark. The area between a curve and a line, or between two curves, is ∫ (upper − lower) dx, with limits found by solving the two equations simultaneously — always test a point between the limits to see which graph really is on top. A region below the x-axis gives a negative integral, so split at each crossing point and add the magnitudes. For a curve given parametrically, area = ∫ y (dx/dt) dt with limits in t."},{"h":"Why a definite integral is an area: the limit of a sum","code":"8.4","t":"Divide the region under y = f(x) between x = a and x = b into thin strips of width δx. Each strip is very nearly a rectangle of height f(x), so its area is about f(x) δx and the whole region is about Σ(x = a to b) f(x) δx. As the strips get thinner the approximation improves without limit, and ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = lim(δx→0) Σ(x = a to b) f(x) δx. Examiners ask you to translate in both directions: a limit of a sum written in Σ notation must be rewritten as an integral with the right limits, and the elongated S should be read as a continuous sum."},{"h":"Substitution and by parts: the chain and product rules reversed","code":"8.5","t":"Substitution reverses the chain rule. Put u equal to the awkward inner function, replace the integrand, replace dx using du/dx, and for a definite integral replace both limits with values of u so you never substitute back. The special case worth knowing on sight is ∫ f'(x)/f(x) dx = ln|f(x)| + c, which turns tan x into −ln|cos x| + c. Integration by parts reverses the product rule: ∫ u (dv/dx) dx = uv − ∫ v (du/dx) dx. Choose u to be the factor that gets simpler when differentiated, normally the polynomial — except when ln x appears, where u = ln x always, which is how ∫ ln x dx = x ln x − x + c is found. Parts may be needed twice."},{"h":"Partial fractions turn algebraic fractions into logarithms","code":"8.6","t":"A proper fraction with linear factors in the denominator splits as A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d); find A and B by substituting the values of x that make each bracket zero, then integrate each piece to (A/a)ln|ax + b| + (B/c)ln|cx + d| + c, remembering the 1/a and 1/c that the reverse chain rule demands. Log laws then combine the answer into a single logarithm when asked. Not every rational integrand needs splitting: x/(x² + 5) is the ∫ f'(x)/f(x) pattern up to a factor, and 2/(2x − 1)⁴ is a power, integrated as 2(2x − 1)^(−4) by the reverse chain rule."},{"h":"Separating the variables in a first order differential equation","code":"8.7","t":"dy/dx = f(x)g(y) is separable: factorise first if you must (dy/dx = xy + 2x is dy/dx = x(y + 2)), write (1/g(y)) dy = f(x) dx, integrate each side with respect to its own variable, and include one constant of integration on the right. A given condition then fixes that constant and gives the particular solution, which you should rearrange to y = ... if the question asks for y in terms of x. Writing the constant as ln A instead of c often makes the rearrangement far cleaner. Different values of c give the family of solution curves, which you may be asked to sketch."},{"h":"Reading the solution back into the real problem","code":"8.8","t":"The last marks on a modelling question are for saying what the answer means. Put the value back into the context with its units, and look at the behaviour for large values of the variable: divide through by the dominant exponential to find the limit the model predicts. Then judge it. Unlimited exponential growth is unrealistic because food, space or money run out; a temperature model that predicts cooling below room temperature is wrong; a solution may only be valid over the range of the data collected. Links to kinematics are common, where v = ∫ a dt and s = ∫ v dt."}],"examples":[{"title":"Finding the curve from its gradient function","setup":"A curve C has gradient function dy/dx = (x + 2)²/√x for x > 0, and C passes through the point (1, 0). Find an equation for C in the form y = f(x).","steps":["Expand and write every term as a power of x: (x + 2)²/√x = (x² + 4x + 4)x^(−1/2) = x^(3/2) + 4x^(1/2) + 4x^(−1/2).","Integrate term by term, which is where the M mark falls: y = (2/5)x^(5/2) + (8/3)x^(3/2) + 8x^(1/2) + c.","C passes through (1, 0), so 0 = 2/5 + 8/3 + 8 + c.","Over a denominator of 15: 2/5 + 8/3 + 8 = 6/15 + 40/15 + 120/15 = 166/15, so c = −166/15.","y = (2/5)x^(5/2) + (8/3)x^(3/2) + 8√x − 166/15."],"cues":["Why can this expression not be integrated as it stands?","What does the power rule do to each of those three terms?","What is the point (1, 0) there for?","Which common denominator combines those three numbers?","What still has to be tidied before you write y = f(x)?"]},{"title":"Area of the region between a curve and a line","setup":"The curve C has equation y = 6x − x² and the line l has equation y = 2x. C and l enclose a finite region R. Find the exact area of R.","steps":["Solve 6x − x² = 2x for the limits: x² − 4x = 0, x(x − 4) = 0, so x = 0 and x = 4.","Test x = 2: the curve gives 8 and the line gives 4, so the curve is the upper boundary, and area = ∫₀⁴ ((6x − x²) − 2x) dx = ∫₀⁴ (4x − x²) dx.","Integrate, which earns the M mark: [2x² − x³/3]₀⁴.","At the top limit: 2(16) − 64/3 = 32 − 64/3; at the bottom limit the bracket is zero.","Area = 96/3 − 64/3 = 32/3."],"cues":["What has to be known before any integrating can begin?","Between the limits, which graph is on top, and how would you check?","What is the integral of 4x − x²?","What do you now do with the two limits?","How must the area be written when the question says exact?"]},{"title":"A definite integral by substitution","setup":"Use the substitution u = 2x + 1 to find the exact value of ∫₀⁴ x√(2x + 1) dx.","steps":["From u = 2x + 1, du/dx = 2 so dx = du/2, and x = (u − 1)/2; the limits become x = 0 → u = 1 and x = 4 → u = 9.","Replace the integrand, dx and both limits, which is the M mark: ∫₀⁴ x√(2x + 1) dx = ∫₁⁹ ((u − 1)/2)u^(1/2)(du/2) = (1/4)∫₁⁹ (u^(3/2) − u^(1/2)) du.","Integrate: (1/4)[(2/5)u^(5/2) − (2/3)u^(3/2)]₁⁹.","Top limit: (2/5)(243) − (2/3)(27) = 486/5 − 18 = 396/5. Bottom limit: 2/5 − 2/3 = −4/15.","(1/4)(396/5 + 4/15) = (1/4)(1192/15) = 298/15."],"cues":["Which three things must change when the variable changes?","Once every x has gone, what is left to integrate?","Which rule now applies to each power of u?","What are 9^(5/2) and 9^(3/2)?","How are the two bracket values combined?"]},{"title":"Integrating with partial fractions","setup":"Express 1/((x + 1)(2x − 3)) in partial fractions, and hence find ∫ 1/((x + 1)(2x − 3)) dx for x > 3/2, giving the answer as a single logarithm.","steps":["Set 1/((x + 1)(2x − 3)) equal to A/(x + 1) + B/(2x − 3) and multiply through by the denominator: 1 = A(2x − 3) + B(x + 1).","Substituting x = −1 gives 1 = −5A, so A = −1/5; substituting x = 3/2 gives 1 = (5/2)B, so B = 2/5.","Integrate each fraction, which carries the M mark: ∫ (−1/5)/(x + 1) dx + ∫ (2/5)/(2x − 3) dx = −(1/5)ln(x + 1) + (1/5)ln(2x − 3) + c.","Combine the logarithms: (1/5)ln((2x − 3)/(x + 1)) + c."],"cues":["What form must the two partial fractions take?","Which values of x make one bracket vanish at a time?","What extra factor does the 2x − 3 fraction need?","Which log law turns a difference into one logarithm?"]},{"title":"A differential equation modelling cooling","setup":"The temperature excess θ °C of a mug of coffee above room temperature, t minutes after it is poured, is modelled by dθ/dt = −kθ, where k is a positive constant. When t = 0, θ = 60, and when t = 5, θ = 48. Find θ in terms of t.","steps":["Separate the variables and integrate, which is the M mark: ∫ (1/θ) dθ = ∫ −k dt, so ln θ = −kt + c.","Undo the logarithm: θ = e^(−kt + c) = Ae^(−kt), writing A for e^c.","The first reading gives A = 60, so θ = 60e^(−kt).","The second reading gives 48 = 60e^(−5k), so e^(−5k) = 4/5 and k = (1/5)ln(5/4).","θ = 60e^(−(t/5)ln(5/4)), which is more neatly written θ = 60(4/5)^(t/5)."],"cues":["Which variable belongs on which side of the equation?","How do you get θ on its own from ln θ?","What does the reading at t = 0 tell you?","What does the second reading tell you?","How can that exponential be tidied into a power?"]},{"title":"Using a double angle identity to integrate sin²","setup":"Find ∫ 4 sin² 3x dx, using the identity cos 2A = 1 − 2 sin² A. Give your answer in terms of x.","steps":["Replace A by 3x in cos 2A = 1 − 2 sin² A and rearrange: sin² 3x = (1 − cos 6x)/2.","So the integrand becomes 4 sin² 3x = 2(1 − cos 6x) = 2 − 2 cos 6x.","Integrate term by term for the M mark: ∫ 2 dx = 2x, and ∫ 2 cos 6x dx = (2/6) sin 6x.","∫ 4 sin² 3x dx = 2x − (1/3) sin 6x + c."],"cues":["What must A be for the identity to produce sin² 3x?","What does the factor of 4 in front do to that?","What is the integral of cos 6x?","What must never be missing from an indefinite integral?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Find ∫ (2x + 1)⁴ dx.","a":"(2x + 1)⁵/10 + c"},{"q":"Evaluate ∫₁ᵉ (1/x) dx.","a":"1"},{"q":"Separate the variables in dy/dx = 3y.","a":"∫ (1/y) dy = ∫ 3 dx"},{"q":"Which technique does ∫ x cos x dx need, and what should u be?","a":"Integration by parts, with u = x"}]},"room":{"facts":["∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + c for n ≠ −1, and ∫ (1/x) dx = ln|x| + c.","∫ e^(kx) dx = (1/k)e^(kx) + c, ∫ sin kx dx = −(1/k)cos kx + c, ∫ cos kx dx = (1/k)sin kx + c, ∫ sec² kx dx = (1/k)tan kx + c.","∫ f'(x)/f(x) dx = ln|f(x)| + c, which gives ∫ tan x dx = −ln|cos x| + c = ln|sec x| + c.","Integration by parts: ∫ u (dv/dx) dx = uv − ∫ v (du/dx) dx, with u the factor that simplifies when differentiated, and u = ln x whenever ln x appears.","Area between two curves = ∫ (upper − lower) dx, limits from the points of intersection; for a parametric curve, area = ∫ y (dx/dt) dt.","∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = lim(δx→0) Σ(x = a to b) f(x) δx, and ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a) with no constant of integration.","A separable equation dy/dx = f(x)g(y) is solved by ∫ (1/g(y)) dy = ∫ f(x) dx, with one constant found from the given condition.","cos 2A = 1 − 2sin² A = 2cos² A − 1 and sec² x = 1 + tan² x are what make sin² x, cos² 3x and tan² x integrable."],"questions":[{"q":"Find ∫ (6x² − 4x + 1) dx.","a":"2x³ − 2x² + x + c","sol":"Raise each power by one and divide by the new power: 6x²  →  6x³/3 = 2x³, −4x  →  −4x²/2 = −2x², 1  →  x (M1 for a correct power on any term). Answer 2x³ − 2x² + x (A1), and + c (B1) because any constant differentiates to zero.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Integration undoes the power rule, so think about what happens to the index.","Deal with each term separately, using ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1).","The first term becomes a multiple of x³, and the expression is not a definite integral."],"codes":["8.1","8.2"]},{"q":"Find ∫ (½x² − 3x^(−1/2)) dx.","a":"x³/6 − 6√x + c","sol":"½x²  →  (½)x³/3 = x³/6 (M1 A1). −3x^(−1/2)  →  −3x^(1/2)/(1/2) = −6x^(1/2) = −6√x (A1). Add the constant: x³/6 − 6√x + c. Check by differentiating: x²/2 − 3x^(−1/2), which is the integrand.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The rule ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) works for negative and fractional n as well.","Add one to the index −1/2 and then divide by the new index.","Dividing by a fraction multiplies, so the second term will not keep its coefficient of 3."],"codes":["8.2"]},{"q":"Find ∫ (e^(5x) + 1/(2x) + sec² 2x) dx.","a":"(1/5)e^(5x) + ½ln|x| + ½tan 2x + c","sol":"Each is a standard integral divided by the coefficient of x: ∫ e^(5x) dx = (1/5)e^(5x) (M1 A1); ∫ 1/(2x) dx = ½∫ (1/x) dx = ½ln|x| (A1); ∫ sec² 2x dx = ½tan 2x (A1). Add + c. The modulus signs in ln|x| are needed because x may be negative.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["All three are standard results from the reverse chain rule.","Integrate each as if the x had coefficient 1, then divide by the coefficient of x.","Take the constant ½ outside the middle term before integrating."],"codes":["8.2"]},{"q":"Evaluate ∫₁⁴ √x dx.","a":"14/3","sol":"Write √x as x^(1/2), so ∫ x^(1/2) dx = (2/3)x^(3/2) (M1 A1). Evaluate: (2/3)(4)^(3/2) − (2/3)(1)^(3/2) = (2/3)(8) − (2/3)(1) = 16/3 − 2/3 (dM1). Value 14/3 (A1). No + c, because a definite integral is a number.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A root has to be written as a power before the power rule can be used.","Integrate first, then substitute the two limits into the result.","4^(3/2) means the cube of the square root of 4."],"codes":["8.3"]},{"q":"Write lim(δx→0) Σ(x = 1 to 3) 3x² δx as a definite integral, and hence evaluate it.","a":"∫₁³ 3x² dx = 26","sol":"The limit of a sum of strips f(x) δx between x = 1 and x = 3 is the definite integral with those limits: ∫₁³ 3x² dx (B1). Integrating and substituting the limits gives 27 − 1 (M1), so the value is 26 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The Σ becomes the elongated S, and δx becomes dx.","The smallest and largest values of x in the sum are the limits of the integral.","Once written as an integral it is an ordinary power-rule question."],"codes":["8.4"]},{"q":"A curve has gradient function dy/dx = 3x² − 2 and passes through the point (1, 5). Find an equation for the curve in the form y = f(x).","a":"y = x³ − 2x + 6","sol":"Integrate the gradient function: y = x³ − 2x + c (M1 for integrating, A1 for both terms). Substitute x = 1, y = 5: 5 = 1 − 2 + c (M1, dependent on the + c being there), so c = 6 (A1). Hence y = x³ − 2x + 6.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The gradient function is the derivative, so you need the reverse process.","Integrate first and keep the arbitrary constant, then make the point work for you.","Without the constant of integration there is nothing for the point to determine."],"codes":["8.1","8.2"]},{"q":"Find ∫ ln x dx.","a":"x ln x − x + c","sol":"Use parts with u = ln x and dv/dx = 1, so du/dx = 1/x and v = x (M1 for this choice). Then ∫ ln x dx = x ln x − ∫ x(1/x) dx (A1) = x ln x − ∫ 1 dx (M1) = x ln x − x + c (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["There appears to be only one factor here, but a second one can always be invented.","Integration by parts needs a u and a dv/dx; ln x cannot be the part you integrate.","Think of the integrand as ln x multiplied by 1, and let the formula do the rest."],"codes":["8.5"]},{"q":"Find ∫ x e^(2x) dx.","a":"½x e^(2x) − ¼e^(2x) + c","sol":"By parts with u = x (it simplifies when differentiated) and dv/dx = e^(2x), so du/dx = 1 and v = ½e^(2x) (M1). ∫ x e^(2x) dx = ½x e^(2x) − ∫ ½e^(2x) dx (A1) = ½x e^(2x) − ¼e^(2x) + c (M1 A1). It may be written ¼e^(2x)(2x − 1) + c.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A product of a polynomial and an exponential is the signature of one particular technique.","Choose as u the factor that becomes simpler when it is differentiated.","Integrating e^(2x) brings in a factor of ½."],"codes":["8.5"]},{"q":"Find ∫ (5x + 1)/((x − 1)(x + 2)) dx.","a":"2ln|x − 1| + 3ln|x + 2| + c","sol":"Let (5x + 1)/((x − 1)(x + 2)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 2), so 5x + 1 = A(x + 2) + B(x − 1) (M1). x = 1 gives 6 = 3A, A = 2 (A1); x = −2 gives −9 = −3B, B = 3 (A1). Then ∫ 2/(x − 1) dx + ∫ 3/(x + 2) dx = 2ln|x − 1| + 3ln|x + 2| + c (M1 A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The denominator is already factorised, which is a strong hint about the first move.","Split the fraction into two simpler ones, then integrate each.","Once split, each piece has a constant over a linear bracket, so each one becomes a logarithm."],"codes":["8.6"]},{"q":"Solve the differential equation dy/dx = xy + 2x, given that y = 3 when x = 0. Give your answer in the form y = f(x).","a":"y = 5e^(x²/2) − 2","sol":"Factorise: dy/dx = x(y + 2) (B1). Separate and integrate: ∫ 1/(y + 2) dy = ∫ x dx, so ln|y + 2| = x²/2 + c (M1 A1). Substitute x = 0, y = 3: ln 5 = c (M1). So ln((y + 2)/5) = x²/2, giving y + 2 = 5e^(x²/2) (dM1) and y = 5e^(x²/2) − 2 (A1).","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The right-hand side is not yet a product of a function of x and a function of y.","Take out the common factor of x, then separate the variables and integrate both sides.","Writing the constant as ln A rather than c makes the rearrangement much tidier."],"codes":["8.7"]},{"q":"Find the exact area of the finite region bounded by the curve y = x² − 5x + 6 and the curve y = 4 − x².","a":"9/8","sol":"Intersections: x² − 5x + 6 = 4 − x² gives 2x² − 5x + 2 = 0, (2x − 1)(x − 2) = 0, x = ½ and x = 2 (M1 A1). At x = 1, 4 − x² = 3 and x² − 5x + 6 = 2, so y = 4 − x² is the upper curve. Area = ∫(½ to 2) ((4 − x²) − (x² − 5x + 6)) dx = ∫(½ to 2) (−2x² + 5x − 2) dx (M1). Integrating gives [−2x³/3 + 5x²/2 − 2x] (A1). At x = 2: −16/3 + 10 − 4 = 2/3. At x = ½: −1/12 + 5/8 − 1 = −11/24 (dM1). Area = 2/3 + 11/24 = 16/24 + 11/24 = 27/24 = 9/8 (A1).","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two curves bound a region only between the points where they cross.","Solve the equations simultaneously for the limits, then integrate upper minus lower.","Start from 2x² − 5x + 2 = 0, the equation for the points of intersection."],"codes":["8.3"]},{"q":"Show that ∫₀^(π/4) tan² x dx = 1 − π/4.","a":"1 − π/4 (shown)","sol":"Use the identity sec² x = 1 + tan² x, so tan² x = sec² x − 1 (M1 — tan² x is not a standard integral). Then ∫ tan² x dx = tan x − x (A1). Evaluate between 0 and π/4: (tan(π/4) − π/4) − (tan 0 − 0) (dM1) = (1 − π/4) − 0 = 1 − π/4 (A1 cso). Every line must be seen, since the answer is printed.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["tan² x is not in the list of standard integrals, so it must be rewritten.","One of the Pythagorean identities links tan² x to a function you can integrate.","Every line must be shown, and tan(π/4) = 1 does the rest at the top limit."],"codes":["8.2","8.3"]},{"q":"The finite region R is bounded by the curve y = x e^x, the x-axis and the line x = 2. Find the exact area of R.","a":"e² + 1","sol":"The curve meets the x-axis where x e^x = 0, i.e. x = 0, so area = ∫₀² x e^x dx (B1). By parts with u = x, dv/dx = e^x, so v = e^x: ∫ x e^x dx = x e^x − ∫ e^x dx (M1 A1) = x e^x − e^x (A1). Evaluate: (2e² − e²) − (0 − e⁰) (dM1) = e² + 1 (A1). Exact is asked, so leave it in terms of e.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The second limit is not given, so find where the curve meets the x-axis.","The integrand is a product of a polynomial and an exponential, so one technique applies.","Remember that e⁰ = 1 at the lower limit, and that exact means leaving e in the answer."],"codes":["8.5","8.3"]},{"q":"The curve C has parametric equations x = t², y = 2t, t ≥ 0. The finite region R is bounded by C, the x-axis and the line x = 4. Using ∫ y (dx/dt) dt, find the exact area of R.","a":"32/3","sol":"dx/dt = 2t (B1). The limits in t: x = 0 gives t = 0 and x = 4 gives t = 2 (B1). Area = ∫₀² (2t)(2t) dt = ∫₀² 4t² dt (M1). Integrating gives [4t³/3]₀² (A1), so 32/3 − 0 (dM1) = 32/3 (A1). (Check in Cartesian form: y = 2√x, and ∫₀⁴ 2√x dx = 32/3.)","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The variable of integration has to become t, so both limits must change too.","Differentiate x with respect to t, then multiply by y before integrating.","x = 4 corresponds to one value of t because t is not negative."],"codes":["8.3"]},{"q":"The number of fish, P thousand, in a lake t years after stocking is modelled by dP/dt = 0.1P(5 − P), with P = 1 when t = 0. Find P in terms of t.","a":"P = 5e^(t/2)/(4 + e^(t/2))","sol":"Separate: ∫ 1/(P(5 − P)) dP = ∫ 0.1 dt (M1). Partial fractions: 1/(P(5 − P)) = (1/5)(1/P) + (1/5)(1/(5 − P)) (M1 A1). Integrate: (1/5)ln P − (1/5)ln(5 − P) = 0.1t + c, so ln(P/(5 − P)) = t/2 + k (A1). Use P = 1, t = 0: ln(1/4) = k (M1). So P/(5 − P) = (1/4)e^(t/2) (dM1), giving 4P = (5 − P)e^(t/2) and P(4 + e^(t/2)) = 5e^(t/2) (A1), so P = 5e^(t/2)/(4 + e^(t/2)) (A1).","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The right-hand side is a function of t times a function of P, so the variables come apart.","The P side is an algebraic fraction with two linear factors in the denominator.","Both logarithms have the same coefficient, so they combine into one before you undo them."],"codes":["8.6","8.7"]},{"q":"A mug of tea is left to stand in a room kept at 21 °C. The temperature of the tea, θ °C, t minutes after it is poured is modelled by dθ/dt = −0.04(θ − 21), with θ = 86 when t = 0. Solve the differential equation to find θ in terms of t, state with a reason the temperature the model predicts for the tea in the long term, and give one limitation of the model.","a":"θ = 21 + 65e^(−0.04t), so θ → 21 °C as t becomes large","sol":"Separate the variables: ∫ 1/(θ − 21) dθ = ∫ −0.04 dt (M1). Integrate: ln|θ − 21| = −0.04t + c (A1). Use θ = 86 when t = 0: ln 65 = c (M1). So ln((θ − 21)/65) = −0.04t, giving θ − 21 = 65e^(−0.04t) and θ = 21 + 65e^(−0.04t) (A1). As t becomes large e^(−0.04t) → 0 (M1), so θ → 21: the model predicts the tea cools towards 21 °C, the temperature of the room, but never quite reaches it (A1). One acceptable limitation (B1): the model assumes the room stays at exactly 21 °C and that nothing else disturbs the tea, so for large t it predicts a temperature that is never actually attained; accept also that it ignores stirring, a lid or the tea being drunk, or that it is only fitted to readings taken over the first few minutes and cannot be trusted far beyond them.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["The right-hand side is a constant times a function of θ alone, so the two variables come apart.","Integrating the θ side gives a logarithm; the temperature at t = 0 fixes the constant, and the long-term value comes from what the exponential does as t grows.","Start from ∫ 1/(θ − 21) dθ = ∫ −0.04 dt."],"codes":["8.7","8.8"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Find ∫ (4x³ − 1/x) dx.","a":"x⁴ − ln|x| + c"},{"q":"Evaluate ∫₀² 3x² dx.","a":"8"},{"q":"Find ∫ 2x/(x² + 1) dx.","a":"ln(x² + 1) + c"},{"q":"Solve dy/dx = 2xy², given y = 1 when x = 0.","a":"y = 1/(1 − x²)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The integral of xⁿ, and the exception at n = −1","back":"xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + c: add one to the index and divide by the new index. The rule breaks at n = −1, where ∫ (1/x) dx = ln|x| + c instead.","code":"8.2","open":false},{"front":"The constant of integration in an indefinite integral","back":"Every constant differentiates to zero, so infinitely many curves share one gradient function: the answer is the family y = F(x) + c, not a single curve.","code":"8.1","open":false},{"front":"Differentiation as the check on an integral","back":"Differentiate it: the two processes are inverse, so differentiating your answer must return the integrand.","code":"8.1","open":false},{"front":"The limitation of an exponential growth model for large t","back":"It predicts growth without limit, while food, space or money run out in reality, so the solution is only trustworthy over the range of t the data covers.","code":"8.8","open":false},{"front":"The integral of e^(kx)","back":"(1/k)e^(kx) + c.","code":"8.2","open":false},{"front":"The integrals of sin kx, cos kx and sec² kx","back":"−(1/k)cos kx + c; (1/k)sin kx + c; (1/k)tan kx + c.","code":"8.2","open":false},{"front":"The identities that make sin² x, cos² 3x and tan² x integrable","back":"cos 2A = 1 − 2sin² A = 2cos² A − 1, giving sin² x = (1 − cos 2x)/2 and cos² 3x = (1 + cos 6x)/2; and tan² x = sec² x − 1.","code":"8.2","open":false},{"front":"The integral of f'(x)/f(x)","back":"ln|f(x)| + c. So ∫ tan x dx = −ln|cos x| + c, which is ln|sec x| + c.","code":"8.5","open":false},{"front":"The integration by parts formula, and how to choose u","back":"∫ u (dv/dx) dx = uv − ∫ v (du/dx) dx. Take u to be the factor that gets simpler when differentiated, usually the polynomial; but u = ln x whenever ln x appears.","code":"8.5","open":false},{"front":"The integral of ln x","back":"x ln x − x + c, from parts with u = ln x and dv/dx = 1.","code":"8.5","open":false},{"front":"The effect of a substitution on a definite integral","back":"The integrand, dx (using du/dx) and both limits, which become values of u. Then there is no need to substitute back at the end.","code":"8.5","open":false},{"front":"The area between two curves","back":"Solve the equations simultaneously for the limits, check which graph is upper, then integrate (upper − lower) between those limits. The result is positive.","code":"8.3","open":false},{"front":"∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx written as a limit of a sum","back":"∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = lim(δx→0) Σ(x = a to b) f(x) δx: strips of width δx and height f(x), summed as the width tends to zero.","code":"8.4","open":false},{"front":"The integral of 1/((ax + b)(cx + d)) by partial fractions","back":"Split into A/(ax + b) + B/(cx + d), find A and B, then integrate to (A/a)ln|ax + b| + (B/c)ln|cx + d| + c.","code":"8.6","open":false},{"front":"The three steps for solving dy/dx = f(x)g(y)","back":"Write (1/g(y)) dy = f(x) dx; integrate both sides with one constant; use the given condition to find the constant and rearrange for y.","code":"8.7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Memorise: ∫ xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + c, n ≠ −1.","Memorise: ∫ (1/x) dx = ln|x| + c.","Memorise: ∫ e^(kx) dx = (1/k)e^(kx) + c.","Memorise: ∫ sin kx dx = −(1/k)cos kx + c.","Memorise: ∫ cos kx dx = (1/k)sin kx + c.","Memorise: ∫ f'(x)/f(x) dx = ln|f(x)| + c, and the reverse chain rule ∫ f'(x)[f(x)]ⁿ dx = [f(x)]ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + c, n ≠ −1.","Memorise: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a), where F'(x) = f(x); no constant of integration in a definite integral.","Memorise: ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = lim(δx→0) Σ(x = a to b) f(x) δx.","Memorise: area between curves = ∫ₐᵇ (y_upper − y_lower) dx; for a parametric curve, area = ∫ y (dx/dt) dt with limits in t.","Memorise: dy/dx = f(x)g(y) is solved by ∫ (1/g(y)) dy = ∫ f(x) dx + c.","Memorise: cos 2A = 1 − 2sin² A = 2cos² A − 1 and sec² x = 1 + tan² x, the identities that turn sin² x, cos² 3x and tan² x into integrable forms.","Given in the booklet (Calculus section, the Integration table): the trigonometric integrals listed there — sec² kx, tan kx, cot kx, cosec kx and sec kx — so ∫ sec² kx dx = (1/k)tan kx + c and ∫ tan x dx = ln|sec x| + c can be looked up. Recognising tan x as the ∫ f'(x)/f(x) pattern is quicker, and learn ∫ sec² kx dx anyway: it is needed at speed.","Given in the booklet (Calculus section): integration by parts, ∫ u (dv/dx) dx = uv − ∫ v (du/dx) dx. The choice of u is not given, so learn it: the factor that simplifies when differentiated, and u = ln x whenever ln x appears.","Memorise: for a region lying below the x-axis ∫ₐᵇ y dx is negative, so its area is |∫ₐᵇ y dx|; where a curve crosses the axis between a and b, split the integral at the crossing and add the magnitudes."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Choosing the technique","items":["A sum of powers of x, or a standard function of kx: integrate straight off, dividing by k. Expand brackets and split fractions over a single term first.","A product where one factor is (almost) the derivative of the inside of the other: reverse chain rule or substitution, e.g. ∫ x e^(x²) dx or ∫ 2x(x² + 1)³ dx.","A fraction whose numerator is (a multiple of) the derivative of the denominator: ∫ f'(x)/f(x) dx = ln|f(x)| + c, e.g. ∫ x/(x² + 5) dx = ½ln(x² + 5) + c.","A product of two unrelated functions, such as a polynomial times e^(kx), sin kx or ln x: integration by parts, possibly twice.","A proper algebraic fraction with two or more linear factors in the denominator: partial fractions, then logarithms.","A single bracket raised to a power, such as 2/(2x − 1)⁴: no partial fractions needed — write it as a negative power and use the reverse chain rule.","sin², cos² or tan²: use a double angle identity or sec² x = 1 + tan² x before integrating anything.","dy/dx equals something in x times something in y: separate the variables, integrate both sides, then fit the constant to the given condition.","If the question names the substitution, you must use it — no method marks are available for any other route.","Finally, differentiate your answer. It takes ten seconds and catches a missing 1/k, a lost factor of 2 or a forgotten + c."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:24:49Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Re-solved independently in sympy: all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit tickets, the 4 lesson checks and all 6 worked examples — every answer key, every line of every sol and every M/A/B tally (which sums exactly to each m) is correct, including 14/3, 26, 9/8, 1 − π/4, e² + 1, 32/3, 298/15, −166/15, y = 5e^(x²/2) − 2, P = 5e^(t/2)/(4 + e^(t/2)) and θ = 21 + 65e^(−0.04t) with limit 21; ideas 8.1–8.8 are covered in order with nothing beyond the specification (partial fractions stay linear, parts stops short of reduction formulae, no trapezium rule), all 15 cards are now named results rather than questions and every back is right, and the Formulae sheet's booklet claims match Appendix 1 exactly — xⁿ, 1/x, e^(kx), sin kx, cos kx, f′(x) + g′(x), f′(g(x))g′(x), area = ∫y dx, sec²A = 1 + tan²A and cos 2A are correctly marked 'Memorise', while sec² kx, tan kx, cot kx, cosec kx, sec kx and integration by parts are correctly credited to the booklet's Integration table; the five faults from the first examination are all genuinely gone, leaving only two third-hint wordings and one duplicated cooling context."}}},"P9":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P9","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Most equations you meet outside a textbook cannot be solved exactly, and most integrals cannot be done by the rules of Pure Year 2 either. This room is how the specification expects you to get an answer anyway: locate a root by a change of sign, close in on it with an iteration or with Newton-Raphson, and estimate an area with the trapezium rule. On Papers 1, 2 and especially 3 these questions are short, heavily structured and generous with marks, but they are lost on wording rather than on arithmetic: the accuracy mark for a root needs the two values, the change of sign and the word continuous, and an over-estimate claim needs the shape of the curve as its reason. Calculator fluency matters here more than anywhere else in Pure, because every mark after the first depends on carrying full accuracy from one iterate to the next.","idea":[{"code":"9.1","h":"Locating a root by a change of sign, and when the test lets you down","t":"If f is continuous on the interval [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, then f(x) = 0 has at least one root between a and b. In the exam you must evaluate both ends, show both values, say there is a change of sign, say f is continuous on that interval, and then conclude that a root lies between the two values: the accuracy mark is cso, so a missing word costs it. The specification names two ways the test fails. If the interval is too large it may contain an even number of roots, so the signs at the ends match and the change of sign never appears even though roots are there. If f is not continuous, a sign change can be produced by a vertical asymptote rather than by a root, as with f(x) = 1/(x - 3) across x = 3. A third case is worth carrying as a further caution, although the specification does not name it: at a repeated root the curve touches the axis without crossing, so there is no sign change there either."},{"code":"9.2","h":"Simple iteration xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ), staircases and cobwebs","t":"Rearrange the equation into the form x = f(x), pick a starting value x₀, and feed each output back in as the next input. The specification asks you to understand what happens next in geometrical terms, so the diagram is the thing to learn. Draw y = x and y = f(x) on the same axes; the root α is where they cross. Start at x₀ on the x-axis, go vertically to the curve, horizontally to the line, and repeat. When the curve rises across the line the iterates creep towards α from one side and the path is a staircase; when the curve falls across the line they land alternately either side of α and the path is a cobweb. The same picture shows whether the iteration works, and the deciding feature is how steep y = f(x) is where it crosses compared with the line y = x itself. If the curve is the shallower of the two there, its gradient near α lying between -1 and 1, each vertical-then-horizontal step lands nearer the crossing point than the one before and the staircase or cobweb closes in on α. If the curve is steeper than the line, every step lands further away and the diagram marches or spirals outwards, which is why two rearrangements of the same equation can behave completely differently: one closes in on the root, the other runs away from it. (That comparison of steepness can be written algebraically as |f′(α)| < 1, which is true and worth knowing, but it is extension beyond this specification; what is asked of you is the diagram and the reasoning read off it.) Keep the full calculator value between steps and round only the numbers you write down, or the last figure of a 4 or 5 decimal place answer will be wrong."},{"code":"9.3","h":"Newton-Raphson, and why it can go wrong","t":"The Newton-Raphson iteration xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ) is given in the formulae booklet, but you must differentiate f yourself, so this question is always partly a differentiation question. Geometrically, x₁ is the point where the tangent to y = f(x) at (x₀, f(x₀)) crosses the x-axis, and each step slides down the next tangent, which is why it converges so much faster than a simple iteration when it works. It fails exactly where that geometry fails. If f′(xₙ) = 0 the tangent is horizontal, never meets the x-axis and the formula divides by zero, so a starting value at a stationary point is fatal. If f′(xₙ) is merely small the tangent is nearly horizontal and the next value is thrown a long way off, possibly past a turning point and towards a different root or away from every root. If f is not defined at or near xₙ the method cannot start at all."},{"code":"9.4","h":"The trapezium rule, and whether your estimate is too big or too small","t":"Split [a, b] into n strips of equal width h = (b - a)/n, so that there are n + 1 ordinates y₀, y₁, …, yₙ, and estimate the area by replacing the curve on each strip with a straight chord: ∫ₐᵇ y dx ≈ ½h{(y₀ + yₙ) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + … + yₙ₋₁)}. The rule is in the formulae booklet, so the marks sit in getting h right, listing the right number of ordinates, doubling only the inner ones and keeping enough figures. The second part of the question almost always asks whether the estimate is an over-estimate or an under-estimate, and the reason must be the shape of the curve, not the size of the answer: where the curve is convex, d²y/dx² > 0, each chord lies above the curve and the trapezia hold extra area, so the estimate is too big; where it is concave, d²y/dx² < 0, each chord lies below the curve and the estimate is too small. Increasing n narrows the gap, and a second estimate from the other side gives limits the true value must lie between."},{"code":"9.5","h":"Using these methods on a problem set in context","t":"Paper 3 dresses all of this in a context: a profit that falls to zero, a temperature that reaches a given value, an area of ground under a modelled edge, a distance found from measured speeds. The mathematics is unchanged, but three extra things are marked. First, you must form the equation or the integral yourself from the model before any numerical work starts. Second, you must work to the accuracy the question demands, and say what the number means in context with its unit, for example that the company breaks even about 8.2 months after launch rather than writing t = 8.215 and stopping. Third, you may be asked to comment on the model or on the accuracy of the numerical solution: more strips or more iterations improve the estimate, readings taken every five seconds may miss what happens between them, and a model fitted over a short interval should not be trusted far outside it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Showing a root lies in an interval","setup":"f(x) = x³ - 2x - 5. Show that the equation f(x) = 0 has a root α between x = 2 and x = 3, and make clear the property of f that the argument depends on.","steps":["f(2) = 8 - 4 - 5 = -1, so f(2) < 0.","f(3) = 27 - 6 - 5 = 16, so f(3) > 0.","f is a cubic polynomial, so it is continuous on the interval.","There is a change of sign of f(x) over the interval and f is continuous there, so f(x) = 0 has a root α with 2 < α < 3."],"cues":["Which value of f would you work out first, and what would its sign tell you?","What happens to f at the other end of the interval?","What must be true of f before a sign change can prove anything?","How should the conclusion be worded so that nothing is left unsaid?"]},{"title":"An iteration and the diagram that goes with it","setup":"The equation x³ - 2x - 5 = 0 has a root α near x = 2. The iteration xₙ₊₁ = ∛(2xₙ + 5) is used with x₀ = 2. Work out x₁, x₂ and x₃, each to 4 decimal places, and say whether the diagram showing this convergence is a staircase or a cobweb.","steps":["x₁ = ∛(2 × 2 + 5) = ∛9 = 2.0801 to 4 decimal places, keeping 2.080084 in the calculator.","x₂ = ∛(2 × 2.080084 + 5) = ∛9.160168 = 2.0924, keeping 2.092351.","x₃ = ∛(2 × 2.092351 + 5) = ∛9.184702 = 2.0942.","Here f(x) = ∛(2x + 5) has f′(x) = (2/3)(2x + 5)^(-2/3), which is positive near α, so the iterates climb towards α from one side and the diagram is a staircase."],"cues":["What goes into the right-hand side of the formula on the very first step?","Which value becomes the new input, the rounded one or the one on the display?","What is the next input, and roughly how much is the answer still moving?","What decides between a staircase and a cobweb, and what is its sign here?"]},{"title":"Two steps of Newton-Raphson","setup":"f(x) = x³ - 2x - 5 has a root α close to x = 2. Using the Newton-Raphson method with x₀ = 2, find x₂, giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","steps":["Differentiating, f′(x) = 3x² - 2.","f(2) = -1 and f′(2) = 3(4) - 2 = 10, so x₁ = 2 - (-1)/10 = 2.1.","f(2.1) = 9.261 - 4.2 - 5 = 0.061 and f′(2.1) = 3(4.41) - 2 = 11.23.","x₂ = 2.1 - 0.061/11.23 = 2.094568… = 2.0946 to 4 decimal places."],"cues":["What does the formula need that the question has not given you?","What are the two values at x₀, and what do they make x₁?","Which two quantities must be recomputed before the second step?","How are those two quantities combined to move from x₁ to x₂?"]},{"title":"The trapezium rule, and which side of the truth it falls","setup":"Estimate ∫₀¹ √(2x + 1) dx by the trapezium rule with four strips, using the values of √(2x + 1) at x = 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1. Give the estimate to 4 decimal places, and say with a reason whether it is an over-estimate or an under-estimate.","steps":["h = (1 - 0)/4 = 0.25, and the five ordinates are y₀ = √1 = 1, y₁ = √1.5 = 1.224745, y₂ = √2 = 1.414214, y₃ = √2.5 = 1.581139, y₄ = √3 = 1.732051.","The inner ordinates add to y₁ + y₂ + y₃ = 1.224745 + 1.414214 + 1.581139 = 4.220097.","Estimate = (0.25/2)[1 + 2(4.220097) + 1.732051] = 0.125 × 11.172245 = 1.3965 to 4 decimal places.","For y = (2x + 1)^(1/2), d²y/dx² = -(2x + 1)^(-3/2) < 0, so the curve is concave, every chord lies below it and 1.3965 is an under-estimate."],"cues":["How wide is each strip, and how many ordinates does that mean?","Which of those ordinates get doubled in the formula?","What multiplies the bracket once the doubling is done?","What would you have to work out about the curve to settle over or under?"]},{"title":"Distance from measured speeds","setup":"A cyclist's speed is measured every 5 seconds from rest. At t = 0, 5, 10, 15 and 20 seconds the speed v is 0, 8.2, 13.4, 16.1 and 17.0 m s⁻¹. Use the trapezium rule with all five readings to estimate the distance travelled in the first 20 seconds, and say whether the estimate is too large or too small.","steps":["The distance is the area under the speed-time graph, so the trapezium rule applies with four strips of width h = 5.","The inner readings add to 8.2 + 13.4 + 16.1 = 37.7.","Distance ≈ (5/2)[0 + 2(37.7) + 17.0] = 2.5 × 92.4 = 231 m.","The gaps between readings shrink (8.2, then 5.2, then 2.7, then 0.9), so the graph is concave, the chords lie below it and 231 m is an under-estimate."],"cues":["What does an area under this particular graph represent, and how wide is a strip?","Which readings are doubled by the rule, and what do they come to?","What is the whole bracket multiplied by to finish the estimate?","What do the differences between successive readings say about the shape?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A continuous function f has f(1) < 0 and f(2) > 0. What may you conclude, and what may you not conclude?","a":"At least one root of f(x) = 0 lies between 1 and 2; you may not conclude that there is exactly one root"},{"q":"How many ordinates does the trapezium rule need for 5 strips, and which of them are doubled?","a":"6 ordinates; the 4 inner ones are doubled, the first and last are not"},{"q":"Why does Newton-Raphson break down if x₀ is at a stationary point of f?","a":"f′(x₀) = 0, so the formula divides by zero; the tangent there is horizontal and never crosses the x-axis"},{"q":"Near a root α the curve y = f(x) falls as it crosses the line y = x, and is shallower there than the line. Does the construction between the curve and the line close in on α, and is the diagram a staircase or a cobweb?","a":"Yes, it closes in on α, because the curve is shallower than the line so each step lands nearer the crossing point; the diagram is a cobweb, because the curve falls across the line so the iterates land alternately either side of α"}]},"room":{"facts":["Change of sign: if f is continuous on [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, then f(x) = 0 has at least one root in (a, b).","Simple iteration: xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) finds a root of x = f(x); on the diagram of y = x and y = f(x) the steps close in on α when the curve is shallower there than the line, its gradient near α lying between -1 and 1, and move away when the curve is the steeper of the two.","f′(α) > 0 gives a staircase diagram, f′(α) < 0 gives a cobweb diagram.","Newton-Raphson: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ); it fails when f′(xₙ) = 0 or is small.","Trapezium rule: ∫ₐᵇ y dx ≈ ½h{(y₀ + yₙ) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + … + yₙ₋₁)}, where h = (b - a)/n and there are n + 1 ordinates.","A convex curve (d²y/dx² > 0) gives an over-estimate; a concave curve (d²y/dx² < 0) gives an under-estimate."],"questions":[{"q":"f(x) = x³ - 2x - 5. Show that the equation f(x) = 0 has a root between x = 2 and x = 3.","a":"f(2) = -1 and f(3) = 16; there is a change of sign and f is continuous, so a root lies between x = 2 and x = 3","sol":"f(2) = 8 - 4 - 5 = -1 (M1 for attempting f at both ends of the interval). f(3) = 27 - 6 - 5 = 16. f(2) < 0 and f(3) > 0, and f is a polynomial so it is continuous on [2, 3]; there is a change of sign, so f(x) = 0 has a root between x = 2 and x = 3 (A1 cso: both values correct and a conclusion that names the change of sign, the continuity and the interval).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The test for a root in an interval looks only at what f does at the two ends.","Substitute each end value into f and compare what you get.","A full conclusion has three ingredients: the two values, the sign change, and something about f itself."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"Use the trapezium rule with two strips to estimate ∫₀¹ √(2x + 1) dx, giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"1.3901","sol":"h = (1 - 0)/2 = 0.5, so the ordinates are at x = 0, 0.5, 1: y₀ = √1 = 1, y₁ = √2 = 1.414214, y₂ = √3 = 1.732051 (M1 for correct h and the three ordinates). Estimate = (0.5/2)[1 + 2(1.414214) + 1.732051] (M1 for correct use of the rule with the inner ordinate doubled) = 0.25 × 5.560479 = 1.3901 (A1 awrt 1.3901).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Two strips across an interval of width 1 fixes how wide each strip is.","Work out the height of the curve at each strip boundary before using the rule.","Set the working out as h/2 times a bracket in which the inner height is doubled."],"codes":["9.4"]},{"q":"f(x) = x² - 7. Apply the Newton-Raphson method once with x₀ = 3, giving x₁ as an exact fraction.","a":"x₁ = 8/3","sol":"f′(x) = 2x (B1). f(3) = 9 - 7 = 2 and f′(3) = 6, so x₁ = 3 - 2/6 (M1 for correct substitution into xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ)) = 3 - 1/3 = 8/3 (A1, exact fraction; a decimal such as 2.667 loses this mark).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The formula needs something about f that the question has not given you.","Work out the value of f and of its derivative at the starting value, then substitute both.","The word exact means the answer stays as a fraction, not a decimal."],"codes":["9.3"]},{"q":"The iteration xₙ₊₁ = ∛(2xₙ + 5) is used with x₀ = 2. Find x₁, x₂ and x₃, each to 4 decimal places.","a":"x₁ = 2.0801, x₂ = 2.0924, x₃ = 2.0942","sol":"x₁ = ∛(2 × 2 + 5) = ∛9 = 2.080084… = 2.0801 (M1 for a correct first substitution, A1 for x₁). x₂ = ∛(2 × 2.080084 + 5) = ∛9.160168 = 2.092351… = 2.0924. x₃ = ∛(2 × 2.092351 + 5) = ∛9.184702 = 2.094217… = 2.0942 (A1 for x₂ and x₃ both correct, awrt). The full display value must be carried forward at each step; rounding to 4 decimal places before the next substitution can change the final figure.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Each answer becomes the input for the next line.","Put the starting value into the right-hand side, then repeat with whatever comes out.","Use the answer key on the calculator so that the full value, not the rounded one, is fed back in."],"codes":["9.2"]},{"q":"Explain why the Newton-Raphson method cannot be used to find a root of f(x) = 0 starting from a value x₀ at which f′(x₀) = 0.","a":"f′(x₀) = 0 makes x₁ = x₀ - f(x₀)/f′(x₀) undefined, because the tangent at x₀ is horizontal and never crosses the x-axis","sol":"The iteration is xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ), so with f′(x₀) = 0 the fraction f(x₀)/f′(x₀) requires division by zero and x₁ does not exist (B1). Geometrically, x₁ is where the tangent to y = f(x) at (x₀, f(x₀)) meets the x-axis; when f′(x₀) = 0 that tangent is horizontal, so it is parallel to the x-axis and never meets it (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Write the iteration down and look at what sits underneath the fraction.","Say what happens to the calculation, then say what the tangent at that point looks like.","Two separate points are wanted: one algebraic, one about the tangent line."],"codes":["9.3"]},{"q":"Use the trapezium rule with four strips to estimate ∫₀¹ √(2x + 1) dx, using the values of √(2x + 1) at x = 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1. Give your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"1.3965","sol":"h = (1 - 0)/4 = 0.25 (B1). The five ordinates are y₀ = √1 = 1, y₁ = √1.5 = 1.224745, y₂ = √2 = 1.414214, y₃ = √2.5 = 1.581139, y₄ = √3 = 1.732051 (M1 for all five correct to at least 4 decimal places). Inner sum y₁ + y₂ + y₃ = 4.220097. Estimate = (0.25/2)[1 + 2(4.220097) + 1.732051] (M1 for the correct structure, ends not doubled) = 0.125 × 11.172245 = 1.3965 (A1 awrt 1.3965).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Four strips means more boundaries than strips, so count the heights you need.","List every ordinate to at least six figures before combining them.","The bracket contains the two end heights once each and everything else twice."],"codes":["9.4"]},{"q":"State, with a reason based on the shape of the curve, whether the trapezium rule estimate of ∫₀¹ √(2x + 1) dx is an over-estimate or an under-estimate.","a":"An under-estimate, because y = √(2x + 1) is concave (d²y/dx² < 0) so each chord lies below the curve","sol":"y = (2x + 1)^(1/2) gives dy/dx = (2x + 1)^(-1/2) and d²y/dx² = -(2x + 1)^(-3/2) (M1 for a correct second derivative or for a clear sketch showing the chord below the curve). On 0 ≤ x ≤ 1, 2x + 1 > 0 so d²y/dx² < 0 and the curve is concave; each trapezium has its top chord below the curve, so area is missed and the estimate is an under-estimate (A1 for the conclusion with the concavity as the reason). Note the true value is (1/3)(3√3 - 1) = 1.398717…, above the estimate 1.3965.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The reason must come from the curve, not from comparing with an exact answer.","Sketch one strip and ask whether the straight top lies above or below the arc.","Differentiating twice settles which way the curve bends."],"codes":["9.4"]},{"q":"f(x) = x³ - 2x - 5. Using the Newton-Raphson method with x₀ = 2, find x₂, giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"x₂ = 2.0946","sol":"f′(x) = 3x² - 2 (B1). f(2) = -1, f′(2) = 10, so x₁ = 2 - (-1)/10 = 2.1 (M1 for correct use of the formula, A1 for x₁ = 2.1). f(2.1) = 9.261 - 4.2 - 5 = 0.061 and f′(2.1) = 3(4.41) - 2 = 11.23, so x₂ = 2.1 - 0.061/11.23 = 2.094568… = 2.0946 (A1 awrt 2.0946).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This is as much a differentiation question as a numerical one.","One pass of the formula gives x₁; then everything must be recalculated at x₁.","Keep the unrounded x₁ in the calculator when you evaluate f and f′ at it."],"codes":["9.3"]},{"q":"f(x) = (x - 1)/(x - 3). Given that f(2) = -1 and f(4) = 3, explain why it would be wrong to conclude that f(x) = 0 has a root between x = 2 and x = 4.","a":"f is not continuous on [2, 4]: it has a vertical asymptote at x = 3, and the sign change is caused by the asymptote, not by a root (the only root is x = 1)","sol":"The change of sign test requires f to be continuous on the whole interval (B1 for stating the condition). Here the denominator x - 3 is zero at x = 3, which lies inside [2, 4], so f is undefined there and the curve has a vertical asymptote: f → -∞ as x → 3⁻ and f → +∞ as x → 3⁺ (M1 for identifying the discontinuity at x = 3 as the cause). The sign therefore changes across the asymptote rather than across a root; in fact f(x) = 0 only when x - 1 = 0, that is x = 1, which is not in the interval (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One of the conditions the sign change test relies on is not met here.","Look at where this particular function is undefined, and whether that point is inside the interval.","Then solve f(x) = 0 directly to see where the only root really is."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"The iteration xₙ₊₁ = ∛(2xₙ + 5) converges to the root α ≈ 2.0946. State, with a reason, whether the diagram illustrating this convergence is a staircase or a cobweb.","a":"A staircase, because f(x) = ∛(2x + 5) has f′(x) = (2/3)(2x + 5)^(-2/3) > 0 near α","sol":"For f(x) = (2x + 5)^(1/3), f′(x) = (2/3)(2x + 5)^(-2/3), which is positive for all 2x + 5 > 0 and in particular near α (M1). Because f′(α) > 0 the iterates approach α from one side without alternating, so the path between y = x and y = f(x) is a staircase (A1). This matches the values 2, 2.0801, 2.0924, 2.0942, which increase towards α.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The two diagrams are told apart by one feature of the function near the root.","Differentiate the iteration function and look at its sign there.","You can check your answer against whether the iterates alternate or march one way."],"codes":["9.2"]},{"q":"f(x) = x³ - 2x - 5 has a root α. By considering f(2.0945) and f(2.0955), show that α = 2.095 correct to 3 decimal places.","a":"f(2.0945) = -0.000575 and f(2.0955) = 0.010592, so 2.0945 < α < 2.0955 and α = 2.095 to 3 d.p.","sol":"f(2.0945) = 9.188425 - 4.189 - 5 = -0.000575 (awrt -0.0006) and f(2.0955) = 9.201592 - 4.191 - 5 = 0.010592 (awrt 0.0106) (M1 for attempting f at both ends, A1 for both values correct). There is a change of sign and f is continuous, so 2.0945 < α < 2.0955; every number in that interval rounds to 2.095, so α = 2.095 to 3 decimal places (A1 cso).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two numbers you are given are the ends of the interval that rounds to the stated value.","Evaluate f at each of them and compare the signs.","The conclusion must say why the whole interval rounds the same way."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"The table gives values of y at equally spaced values of x: at x = 1, 1.5, 2, 2.5 and 3 the values of y are 2.0, 2.6, 3.1, 3.4 and 3.5. Use the trapezium rule with all five values to estimate ∫₁³ y dx.","a":"5.925","sol":"There are 5 ordinates, so 4 strips, and h = (3 - 1)/4 = 0.5 (B1). Estimate = (0.5/2)[(2.0 + 3.5) + 2(2.6 + 3.1 + 3.4)] (M1 for correct use of the rule with only the inner values doubled) = 0.25[5.5 + 2(9.1)] = 0.25 × 23.7 = 5.925 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Count the readings first, because that tells you how many strips there are.","The strip width comes from the ends of the interval and the number of strips.","Add the first and last readings once and the rest twice inside the bracket."],"codes":["9.4"]},{"q":"The equation x³ + 4x - 10 = 0 has one root α, where α = 1.5568 to 4 decimal places. It can be rearranged either as x = (10 - x³)/4 or as x = ∛(10 - 4x). Sketched with the line y = x, the curve y = (10 - x³)/4 has gradient about -1.8 where it crosses the line and the curve y = ∛(10 - 4x) has gradient about -0.55 where it crosses. Using the construction that goes vertically to the curve and horizontally to the line, state the shape of each diagram, say which of the two iterations closes in on α and which moves away from it, and give the geometrical reason for the difference.","a":"Both diagrams are cobwebs; xₙ₊₁ = ∛(10 - 4xₙ) closes in on α and xₙ₊₁ = (10 - xₙ³)/4 moves away, because y = ∛(10 - 4x) is shallower than the line y = x at the crossing so each step lands nearer α, while y = (10 - x³)/4 is steeper than the line so each step lands further away","sol":"Each curve falls as it crosses y = x, so the construction lands alternately on either side of α and both diagrams are cobwebs (B1). Compare each curve with the line itself, which has gradient 1: y = ∛(10 - 4x) is the shallower of the two at the crossing, so the horizontal step back to the line is shorter than the vertical step that produced it, every new point is nearer the crossing point than the last and the cobweb spirals inwards, so xₙ₊₁ = ∛(10 - 4xₙ) closes in on α (M1 for comparing the steepness of curve and line, A1 for the conclusion). The curve y = (10 - x³)/4 is steeper than the line there, so each step overshoots by more than the last, the cobweb spirals outwards and xₙ₊₁ = (10 - xₙ³)/4 moves away from α (A1). From x₀ = 1.5 the first iteration gives 1.5874, 1.5397, 1.5661, … closing in on 1.5568, while the second gives 1.6563, 1.3642, 1.8653, 0.8774, … running away.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Both curves are falling where they cross the line, and that alone settles the shape of both diagrams.","The line y = x has gradient 1, so compare the steepness of each curve at the crossing with the steepness of the line.","Follow one step of the construction on a sketch: go vertically to the curve, then horizontally back to the line, and ask whether the point you land on is nearer the crossing point than the one you started from."],"codes":["9.2"]},{"q":"The curved edge of a flower bed is modelled by y = √(1 + x³) for 0 ≤ x ≤ 2, where x and y are distances in metres; the other three edges are the x-axis, the y-axis and the line x = 2. Use the trapezium rule with four strips to estimate the area of the flower bed in m² to 3 decimal places, and state with a reason whether this over-estimates or under-estimates the area.","a":"3.283 m², an over-estimate because y = √(1 + x³) is convex (d²y/dx² > 0) on 0 < x ≤ 2","sol":"Area = ∫₀² √(1 + x³) dx (B1 for setting up the integral). h = (2 - 0)/4 = 0.5 and the ordinates are y₀ = √1 = 1, y₁ = √1.125 = 1.060660, y₂ = √2 = 1.414214, y₃ = √4.375 = 2.091650, y₄ = √9 = 3 (M1 for h and all five ordinates). Estimate = (0.5/2)[(1 + 3) + 2(1.060660 + 1.414214 + 2.091650)] (M1 correct structure) = 0.25[4 + 2(4.566524)] = 0.25 × 13.133048 = 3.283 m² to 3 decimal places (A1 awrt 3.283). For the shape: y = (1 + x³)^(1/2) gives dy/dx = (3x²/2)(1 + x³)^(-1/2) and d²y/dx² = (1 + x³)^(-3/2)(3x + 0.75x⁴) (M1), which is positive for 0 < x ≤ 2, so the curve is convex, every chord lies above it and the trapezia include area that is not part of the bed: the estimate is an over-estimate (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Turn the description of the bed into an integral before doing any arithmetic.","Find the strip width, list the five heights, then apply the rule and keep the unit.","For the last part, decide which way the curve bends and say where the chords lie."],"codes":["9.4","9.5"]},{"q":"A company's monthly profit, £P thousand, t months after launch, is modelled by P = 3t + 2 - e^(0.4t) for t > 0. Show that P = 0 has a root between t = 8 and t = 9, then apply the Newton-Raphson method once with t₀ = 8 to find t₁ to 3 decimal places and interpret the result in context.","a":"P(8) = 1.467 > 0 and P(9) = -7.598 < 0, so a root lies between 8 and 9; t₁ = 8.215, so the model predicts the profit falls to zero about 8.2 months after launch","sol":"Let P(t) = 3t + 2 - e^(0.4t). P(8) = 26 - e^(3.2) = 26 - 24.53253 = 1.46747 > 0 and P(9) = 29 - e^(3.6) = 29 - 36.59823 = -7.59823 < 0 (M1 for both values). P is continuous for t > 0 and there is a change of sign, so P = 0 has a root between t = 8 and t = 9 (A1 cso). P′(t) = 3 - 0.4e^(0.4t) (M1 for differentiating the exponential correctly), so P′(8) = 3 - 0.4(24.53253) = -6.81301. Then t₁ = 8 - 1.46747/(-6.81301) (M1 for correct substitution into tₙ₊₁ = tₙ - P(tₙ)/P′(tₙ)) = 8 + 0.215392 = 8.215 to 3 decimal places (A1 awrt 8.215). In context: the model predicts that the company's monthly profit falls to zero about 8.2 months after launch, and is negative after that (A1 for an interpretation in months with the meaning of P = 0 stated).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The first part is the ordinary interval test, applied to the profit function itself.","For the second part you need the derivative of the model before the iteration formula is any use.","Finish with a sentence about months and profit, not just a number."],"codes":["9.1","9.3","9.5"]},{"q":"f(x) = x³ - 6x² + 11x - 6. Show that f(1.5) and f(3.5) have the same sign, and explain why this does not show that f(x) = 0 has no root between x = 1.5 and x = 3.5.","a":"f(1.5) = 0.375 and f(3.5) = 1.875 are both positive, but f(x) = (x - 1)(x - 2)(x - 3) has two roots, x = 2 and x = 3, in the interval, and an even number of roots leaves the end signs unchanged","sol":"f(1.5) = 3.375 - 13.5 + 16.5 - 6 = 0.375 > 0 and f(3.5) = 42.875 - 73.5 + 38.5 - 6 = 1.875 > 0, so both are positive (M1 for both, A1 for the values). Factorising, f(1) = 1 - 6 + 11 - 6 = 0 so (x - 1) is a factor and f(x) = (x - 1)(x² - 5x + 6) = (x - 1)(x - 2)(x - 3) (M1 for the factorisation). The roots x = 2 and x = 3 both lie in (1.5, 3.5): the curve crosses the axis twice, so the sign returns to positive and no change of sign is seen at the ends (A1). A change of sign proves a root is present, but the absence of one proves nothing when the interval may contain an even number of roots.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work out both values first; the explanation depends on what lies between them.","Try to factorise the cubic by spotting one root from the integer factors of 6.","Count how many roots sit inside the interval and think about what an even number does to the end signs."],"codes":["9.1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"f is continuous on [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs. What does this prove, and what does it not prove?","a":"It proves f(x) = 0 has at least one root between a and b; it does not prove there is exactly one"},{"q":"Rearrange x³ = 2x + 5 into the form x = f(x) using a cube root, and write down the iterative formula it gives.","a":"x = ∛(2x + 5), so xₙ₊₁ = ∛(2xₙ + 5)"},{"q":"Write down the Newton-Raphson formula and the condition on f′ that makes it fail.","a":"xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ); it fails when f′(xₙ) = 0 (or is very small)"},{"q":"A speed is recorded at t = 0, 5, 10, 15 and 20 seconds. Using the trapezium rule on all five readings, what is h, and what does the estimate represent?","a":"h = 5; it estimates the distance travelled between t = 0 and t = 20 seconds"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The change of sign test for a root of f(x) = 0","back":"If f is continuous on [a, b] and f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, then f(x) = 0 has at least one root between a and b.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"Name one way a change of sign test can fail","back":"The interval contains an even number of roots, so the end signs match; f is discontinuous and the sign change comes from a vertical asymptote, not a root; the root is repeated, so the curve touches the axis without crossing it.","code":"9.1","open":true},{"front":"The wording needed for full marks on a change of sign question","back":"Give both values of f, say there is a change of sign, say f is continuous on the interval, then conclude that a root lies between the two x-values.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"A simple iteration of the form xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ)","back":"Rearrange the equation into x = f(x), choose a starting value x₀, then substitute repeatedly, each output becoming the next input. Any limit reached is a root of x = f(x).","code":"9.2","open":false},{"front":"Convergence and failure of xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) in geometrical terms","back":"The staircase or cobweb closes in on α when y = f(x) is shallower than y = x where they cross, its gradient near α between -1 and 1; a steeper curve makes the steps move away.","code":"9.2","open":false},{"front":"Staircase and cobweb diagrams","back":"Drawn between y = x and y = f(x). When f′(α) > 0 the iterates approach α from one side: a staircase. When f′(α) < 0 they alternate either side of α: a cobweb.","code":"9.2","open":false},{"front":"The Newton-Raphson formula","back":"xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ). It is given in the formulae booklet, but f′ must be worked out by you.","code":"9.3","open":false},{"front":"Newton-Raphson in geometrical terms","back":"x₁ is where the tangent to y = f(x) at (x₀, f(x₀)) crosses the x-axis, and each step repeats this, so the iterates run down successive tangents to the root.","code":"9.3","open":false},{"front":"Why Newton-Raphson fails near a point of small gradient","back":"f′(xₙ) = 0 makes the formula undefined and the tangent horizontal; a small f′(xₙ) gives a nearly horizontal tangent that meets the axis far away, so the next value is thrown off, possibly towards another root.","code":"9.3","open":false},{"front":"The trapezium rule","back":"∫ₐᵇ y dx ≈ ½h{(y₀ + yₙ) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + … + yₙ₋₁)}, where h = (b - a)/n. It is given in the formulae booklet.","code":"9.4","open":false},{"front":"Strips and ordinates in the trapezium rule","back":"n strips need n + 1 equally spaced ordinates of width h = (b - a)/n. Only the first and last ordinates are not doubled.","code":"9.4","open":false},{"front":"The trapezium rule on a convex curve","back":"Where d²y/dx² > 0 the curve is convex, every chord lies above it, and the trapezia include extra area: the estimate is an over-estimate.","code":"9.4","open":false},{"front":"The trapezium rule on a concave curve","back":"Where d²y/dx² < 0 the curve is concave, every chord lies below it, and area is missed: the estimate is an under-estimate.","code":"9.4","open":false},{"front":"Limits that the true value of an integral must lie between","back":"A trapezium estimate and an estimate that errs the other way bracket the true value, so it lies between them. Doubling the number of strips narrows the bracket.","code":"9.4","open":false},{"front":"Reasons a numerical method is used rather than an exact one","back":"The equation has no solution in closed form, such as x = cos x; the integral has no elementary antiderivative, such as ∫√(1 + x³) dx; the values are experimental readings, so there is no formula to integrate at all.","code":"9.5","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Change of sign test: f continuous on [a, b] and f(a)f(b) < 0 ⇒ f(x) = 0 has at least one root in (a, b). NOT in the booklet — learn the result and the exact wording.","Simple iteration: to solve x = f(x), use xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) from a starting value x₀. NOT in the booklet.","Convergence of xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) in geometrical terms: the iteration converges to α when the curve y = f(x) is shallower than the line y = x near α (gradient between −1 and 1) and diverges when it is steeper; the specification asks for this explanation from a staircase or cobweb diagram. NOT in the booklet.","Diagram type: a curve rising near α gives a staircase, a curve falling near α gives a cobweb. NOT in the booklet.","The Newton-Raphson iteration for solving f(x) = 0: xₙ₊₁ = xₙ - f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ). GIVEN in the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet under Numerical Methods — but you must differentiate f yourself.","The trapezium rule: ∫ₐᵇ y dx ≈ ½h{(y₀ + yₙ) + 2(y₁ + y₂ + … + yₙ₋₁)}, where h = (b - a)/n. GIVEN in the booklet under Numerical Methods.","Ordinates: n strips need n + 1 ordinates at x = a, a + h, a + 2h, …, b. NOT in the booklet.","Over- or under-estimate: convex curve (d²y/dx² > 0) ⇒ over-estimate; concave curve (d²y/dx² < 0) ⇒ under-estimate. NOT in the booklet.","Newton-Raphson failure: f′(xₙ) = 0 (horizontal tangent, division by zero) or f′(xₙ) small (tangent nearly horizontal, next value thrown far away). NOT in the booklet.","Differentiation you will need with these formulae, all NOT in the booklet: d/dx (ax + b)^n = an(ax + b)^(n-1), d/dx e^(kx) = k e^(kx), d/dx ln x = 1/x."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"When numerical methods fail, and what to write","items":["Interval too wide: an even number of roots inside leaves the end signs the same, so no change of sign appears even though roots are there. Write: 'f(a) and f(b) have the same sign, but there may be an even number of roots in the interval.'","Discontinuity: a sign change across a vertical asymptote is not a root. Check the denominator, and write: 'f is not continuous on [a, b] because there is an asymptote at x = …, so the sign change does not prove a root.'","Repeated root: the curve touches the x-axis without crossing, so f does not change sign even though f(x) = 0 there.","Iteration diverges: the curve y = f(x) is steeper than y = x near the root for that rearrangement, so the staircase or cobweb moves away from α. Write: \"the iteration diverges because the gradient of y = f(x) near α is greater than 1 in size\" and, if asked, try a different rearrangement of the same equation.","Newton-Raphson at a stationary point: f′(xₙ) = 0, so the formula is undefined and the tangent is horizontal, never meeting the x-axis.","Newton-Raphson near a stationary point: f′(xₙ) is small, the tangent is nearly horizontal and xₙ₊₁ lands a long way off, so the method may converge to a different root or diverge.","Newton-Raphson outside the domain: an iterate can land where f is undefined, for example a negative value inside a logarithm or a square root, and the method then stops.","Accuracy: keep the full calculator value between iterations and round only what you write; state the answer to the accuracy the question demands, and in a context question add the unit and a sentence of interpretation."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:19:53.982989Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"I re-solved all 16 questions and the 4 exit items independently in node and every answer key, every solution line and every mark tally is right (trapezium estimates 1.3901, 1.3965, 5.925, 3.283 and 231 m; Newton-Raphson 8/3, 2.0946 and t₁ = 8.215; iterates 2.0801, 2.0924, 2.0942; f(2.0945) = -0.000575, f(2.0955) = 0.010592; root of x³ + 4x - 10 at 1.5568 with rearrangement gradients -1.818 and -0.550; over/under claims confirmed against the true integrals 1.398717 and 3.241309), the Formulae sheet's booklet claims match Appendix 1 (Newton-Raphson and the trapezium rule given, the change of sign test, iteration, ordinate count, over/under rule and the derivatives of (ax + b)ⁿ, e^(kx) and ln x from memory), and 9.2 is now taught geometrically from the staircase and cobweb construction with |f′(α)| < 1 named once as extension — the only thing left to tidy is the wording that lets a rising curve imply convergence."}}},"P10":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"P10","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Vectors are the language Edexcel uses whenever direction matters. In Papers 1 and 2 they turn up as pure geometry: proving three points are collinear, finding the missing vertex of a parallelogram, splitting a line in a given ratio. In Paper 3 every mechanics question — displacement, velocity, resultant force — is written in i, j, k form, so the algebra you meet here is reused there for the rest of the course. The work is short and self-contained and the marks are cheap, which is why the losses are nearly always careless: subtracting position vectors the wrong way round, forgetting that AB = b − a, dropping a zero component out of a column vector, or writing a rounded decimal when the question says exact.","idea":[{"h":"Column vectors and i, j, k form","t":"A two-dimensional vector can be written as a column with a top and a bottom entry, or as ai + bj; in three dimensions the third entry goes with k, giving ai + bj + ck. The two notations mean exactly the same thing, and Edexcel will set a question in one and accept or demand the other, so practise reading a column straight off as components and writing components straight back as a column. Always keep the order i, j, k, and keep a zero component visible in a column instead of quietly dropping it.","code":"10.1"},{"h":"Magnitude, direction and unit vectors","t":"The magnitude |a| is Pythagoras on the components: |ai + bj| = √(a² + b²) and |ai + bj + ck| = √(a² + b² + c²). Leave it as a surd whenever the question says exact. In two dimensions the direction comes from tan θ = b/a, with a sketch to fix the quadrant, and the conversion back is r cos θ i + r sin θ j for magnitude r at angle θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis. A unit vector has magnitude 1, and the unit vector in the direction of a is a divided by |a|; check it by squaring the components and adding, which must give 1.","code":"10.2"},{"h":"Adding, scaling and testing for parallel","t":"Add and subtract vectors component by component, and multiply by a scalar by multiplying every component. On a diagram the triangle law puts the tail of the second vector at the tip of the first, so the sum runs from the start of the journey to its finish, while the parallelogram law draws both vectors from the same point and takes the diagonal. Multiplying by a scalar k stretches a vector by |k| and reverses it when k is negative, so two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other — the test every question that says show that ... is parallel to ... is looking for.","code":"10.3"},{"h":"Position vectors and distance","t":"The position vector of a point A is OA = a, the directed line segment from the fixed origin. The vector between two points is always finish minus start: AB = OB − OA = b − a, and this single line drives nearly every vector geometry question on the paper. The distance AB is then |b − a|, so d² = (x₁ − x₂)² + (y₁ − y₂)² in two dimensions and d² = (x₁ − x₂)² + (y₁ − y₂)² + (z₁ − z₂)² in three. The midpoint of AB has position vector (1/2)(a + b). Reversing b − a only flips the signs, but it still costs the accuracy mark.","code":"10.4"},{"h":"Vector geometry and vectors in context","t":"Harder questions ask you to travel round a diagram in vectors. To reach the fourth corner D of a parallelogram ABCD use AD = BC, which gives d = a + c − b. To reach the point P dividing AB in the ratio m : n, walk there: OP = a + (m/(m + n))(b − a). To show three points are collinear, show that one of AB and BC is a scalar multiple of the other and say that they share a common point. In context the same algebra carries units: a resultant force is the sum of the force vectors, a displacement after time t is t times the velocity vector, and the final line must be interpreted with its units and to the accuracy asked for.","code":"10.5"}],"examples":[{"title":"Moving between column form and i, j, k form","setup":"The vector p is the column vector whose entries are 5, −1 and 2, taken in the i, j and k directions, and q = −3i + 4j + 6k. Write p in i, j, k form, and find p + 2q, giving your answer as a column vector.","steps":["Attaching each entry of p to its unit vector in order gives p = 5i − j + 2k.","Doubling q component by component: 2q = −6i + 8j + 12k.","Adding the i, j and k parts separately: p + 2q = (5 − 6)i + (−1 + 8)j + (2 + 12)k.","So p + 2q = −i + 7j + 14k, that is the column vector with entries −1, 7 and 14."],"cues":["Which unit vector does each entry of p belong to?","What does the scalar 2 do to each component of q?","How do you combine p and 2q?","How is that written back as a column?"]},{"title":"Magnitude, unit vector and direction of a two-dimensional vector","setup":"The vector a = 5i − 12j. Find |a|, find the unit vector in the direction of a, and find the acute angle that a makes with the positive x-axis, giving it to one decimal place.","steps":["|a| = √(5² + (−12)²) = √(25 + 144) = √169 = 13.","The unit vector is the vector divided by its own magnitude: (5i − 12j)/13.","So the unit vector in the direction of a is (5/13)i − (12/13)j.","For the angle, tan θ = 12/5, so θ = 67.4° to one decimal place."],"cues":["Which rule turns two components into a length?","What must you divide by to make the length 1?","What are the two components once that division is done?","Which trigonometric ratio links the two components to the angle?"]},{"title":"Journeys on a triangle and a test for parallel","setup":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. The point M is the midpoint of OA, and the point N lies on OB with ON = (1/3)OB. Express AB and MN in terms of a and b, and determine whether MN is parallel to AB.","steps":["Travelling from A to B through O gives AB = AO + OB = −a + b = b − a.","Since OM = (1/2)a and ON = (1/3)b, MN = ON − OM = (1/3)b − (1/2)a.","If MN were parallel to AB there would be one number k with MN = k(b − a).","The b part forces k = 1/3 and the a part forces k = 1/2, and these disagree, so MN is not parallel to AB."],"cues":["Which two journeys take you from A to B through O?","How do you write the journey from M to N through O?","What must be true of two parallel vectors?","What value of k does each part demand?"]},{"title":"Position vectors, the vector between two points and the distance","setup":"The points A and B have position vectors a = 2i − j + 4k and b = 6i + 2j − 8k relative to a fixed origin O. Find the vector AB and the exact distance AB.","steps":["AB = b − a = (6 − 2)i + (2 − (−1))j + (−8 − 4)k.","Tidying the components gives AB = 4i + 3j − 12k.","The distance is the magnitude: |AB| = √(4² + 3² + (−12)²) = √(16 + 9 + 144).","So |AB| = √169 = 13, and the distance AB is 13."],"cues":["Which subtraction gives the vector from A to B?","What are the three components once tidied?","Which rule turns those components into a length?","What does that square root simplify to?"]},{"title":"The fourth corner of a parallelogram and a diagonal","setup":"In the parallelogram ABCD the vertices A, B and C have position vectors a = 3i + j, b = 7i + 4j and c = 5i + 9j relative to the origin O. Find the position vector of D, and find the exact length of the diagonal AC.","steps":["Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal vectors, so AD = BC and therefore d = a + (c − b).","c − b = (5 − 7)i + (9 − 4)j = −2i + 5j.","d = (3i + j) + (−2i + 5j) = i + 6j.","AC = c − a = (5 − 3)i + (9 − 1)j = 2i + 8j.","|AC| = √(2² + 8²) = √68 = 2√17."],"cues":["Which pair of sides of ABCD are equal vectors?","What is the vector journey from B to C?","Where does that journey land when it starts at A?","Which subtraction gives the diagonal from A to C?","How do those components become an exact length?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The points A and B have position vectors a = i + 2j + 3k and b = 4i + 2j + 3k. What is AB?","a":"AB = b − a = 3i"},{"q":"Is 2i + 4j − 6k parallel to −i − 2j + 3k?","a":"Yes: 2i + 4j − 6k = −2(−i − 2j + 3k)"},{"q":"Find |−3i + 4j + 12k|.","a":"13"},{"q":"Write the vector of magnitude 8 at 30° anticlockwise from the positive x-axis in the form ai + bj, exactly.","a":"4√3 i + 4j"}]},"room":{"facts":["|ai + bj| = √(a² + b²) and |ai + bj + ck| = √(a² + b² + c²).","For points A and B with position vectors a and b, AB = b − a.","The unit vector in the direction of a is a divided by |a|.","Two vectors are parallel when one is a scalar multiple of the other.","A vector of magnitude r at angle θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis is r cos θ i + r sin θ j.","The point P dividing AB in the ratio m : n has position vector OP = a + (m/(m + n))(b − a); the midpoint is (1/2)(a + b)."],"questions":[{"q":"The vector u is the column vector whose entries are −2, 7 and 3 in the i, j and k directions. Write u in i, j, k form, and write 4i − 5k as a column vector.","a":"u = −2i + 7j + 3k; 4i − 5k is the column vector with entries 4, 0 and −5","sol":"Attach each entry of the column to its unit vector in order: u = −2i + 7j + 3k (B1). Going the other way, 4i − 5k has no j term, so the middle entry is 0 and the column vector has entries 4, 0 and −5 in the i, j and k directions (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The three entries of a column vector belong, from top to bottom, to three different unit vectors.","Read the entries in the order i, j, k, then reverse the process for the second vector.","For the second vector, decide what the missing j component must be before you build the column."],"codes":["10.1"]},{"q":"Given that a = 7i − 24j, find |a| and hence find the unit vector in the direction of a.","a":"|a| = 25; the unit vector is (7/25)i − (24/25)j","sol":"|a| = √(7² + (−24)²) (M1) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25 (A1). The unit vector in the direction of a is a/|a| = (7i − 24j)/25 = (7/25)i − (24/25)j (A1). Check: (7/25)² + (24/25)² = 625/625 = 1.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A unit vector points the same way as a but has length 1.","Find the length of a by Pythagoras on its components, then scale a down by that length.","Square both components and add them before you take any square root."],"codes":["10.2"]},{"q":"The vectors a = 3i − j + 2k and b = −i + 4j + 5k. Find 2a − b.","a":"7i − 6j − k","sol":"2a = 6i − 2j + 4k (M1). Subtracting b component by component: 2a − b = (6 − (−1))i + (−2 − 4)j + (4 − 5)k = 7i − 6j − k (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A scalar multiplies every component, and vectors combine one component at a time.","Double a first, then subtract b term by term.","Write the three i, j and k calculations out separately, watching the signs on b."],"codes":["10.3"]},{"q":"The points P and Q have position vectors p = 4i + 3j and q = −2i + 11j relative to an origin O. Find the vector PQ and the distance PQ.","a":"PQ = −6i + 8j; PQ = 10","sol":"PQ = q − p (M1) = (−2 − 4)i + (11 − 3)j = −6i + 8j (A1). Distance PQ = |PQ| = √((−6)² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The vector from one point to another is finish minus start.","Subtract the position vectors component by component, then take the magnitude of what is left.","Get the two components of PQ down on paper before you square anything."],"codes":["10.4"]},{"q":"Find the value of the constant t for which the vector 3i + tj − 6k is parallel to the vector −i + 2j + 2k.","a":"t = −6","sol":"Parallel vectors are scalar multiples, so 3i + tj − 6k = λ(−i + 2j + 2k) for some λ (M1). Comparing i components: 3 = −λ, so λ = −3 (A1). The k components agree, since 2λ = −6. Comparing j components: t = 2λ = −6 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Two vectors point the same way only when one is a multiple of the other.","Introduce a scalar λ, write one vector as λ times the other and compare components.","Use the i components, which contain no unknown other than λ, to pin λ down first."],"codes":["10.3"]},{"q":"The vector c = 2i + λj, where λ is a positive constant, has magnitude 2√10. Find the value of λ and the unit vector in the direction of c.","a":"λ = 6; the unit vector is (1/√10)i + (3/√10)j","sol":"|c|² = 2² + λ² and (2√10)² = 40, so 4 + λ² = 40 (M1). Hence λ² = 36 and, as λ > 0, λ = 6 (A1). Then c = 2i + 6j and the unit vector is c/|c| = (2i + 6j)/(2√10) (M1) = (1/√10)i + (3/√10)j, which may also be written (√10/10)i + (3√10/10)j (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The magnitude is fixed, so the unknown component is trapped by Pythagoras.","Square both sides of the magnitude statement, solve for λ, then divide c by its magnitude.","Square the given magnitude first, so that you are working with whole numbers."],"codes":["10.2"]},{"q":"The points A, B and C have position vectors a = i + 2j − k, b = 4i + 8j − 3k and c = 10i + 20j − 7k. Show that A, B and C are collinear, and find the ratio AB : BC.","a":"BC = 2AB and B is common to both, so A, B and C are collinear; AB : BC = 1 : 2","sol":"AB = b − a = 3i + 6j − 2k (M1 A1). BC = c − b = 6i + 12j − 4k (A1). Since BC = 2(3i + 6j − 2k) = 2AB, the vectors AB and BC are parallel, and they share the point B, so A, B and C lie on the same straight line (A1 cso). The magnitudes are in the same ratio as the scalar, so AB : BC = 1 : 2 (B1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Points on one straight line give vectors along that line that all point the same way.","Work out AB and BC, test whether one is a multiple of the other, and say why sharing a point matters.","Both vectors come from finish minus start, so subtract the position vectors in pairs."],"codes":["10.3","10.4"]},{"q":"OABC is a parallelogram, where O is the origin, A has position vector 5i − 2j and C has position vector i + 6j. Find the position vector of B and the exact length of the diagonal OB.","a":"OB = 6i + 4j; |OB| = 2√13","sol":"In parallelogram OABC, OA and CB are opposite sides, so CB = OA and OB = OC + CB = c + a (M1). Hence OB = (i + 6j) + (5i − 2j) = 6i + 4j (A1). Then |OB| = √(6² + 4²) = √(36 + 16) = √52 (M1) = 2√13 (A1, exact).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["In a parallelogram drawn from the origin, the diagonal from O is the sum of the two sides meeting at O.","Add the two position vectors to reach B, then take the magnitude of the result.","Name the two sides that meet at O before you add anything, and keep the surd unsimplified to decimals."],"codes":["10.5","10.2"]},{"q":"A vector v has magnitude 26 and makes an angle of 60° with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise. Find v in the form ai + bj, giving a and b exactly.","a":"v = 13i + 13√3 j","sol":"For magnitude r at angle θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis, v = r cos θ i + r sin θ j (M1). So v = 26 cos 60° i + 26 sin 60° j (M1) = 26(1/2)i + 26(√3/2)j = 13i + 13√3 j (A1, exact).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is the conversion from magnitude and direction back to components.","Resolve the vector along and perpendicular to the x-axis using cosine and sine of the angle.","Use the exact surd values of cos 60° and sin 60° rather than a decimal from the calculator."],"codes":["10.2"]},{"q":"The points A and B have coordinates (3, −1, 2) and (k, 5, −4), where k is a constant, and the distance AB is 11. Find the two possible values of k.","a":"k = 10 or k = −4","sol":"AB² = (k − 3)² + (5 − (−1))² + (−4 − 2)² (M1) = (k − 3)² + 36 + 36. Setting this equal to 11² = 121 gives (k − 3)² = 49 (A1). So k − 3 = 7 or k − 3 = −7 (M1), giving k = 10 or k = −4 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The distance formula in three dimensions gives one equation in k.","Write AB² as the sum of the three squared differences and set it equal to the square of the distance.","Square the given distance first, and remember a squared bracket has two square roots."],"codes":["10.4"]},{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. The point P lies on AB with AP : PB = 3 : 2. Express OP in terms of a and b, giving your answer in its simplest form.","a":"OP = (2a + 3b)/5","sol":"AB = b − a (B1). Since AP : PB = 3 : 2, AP = (3/5)AB = (3/5)(b − a) (M1). Then OP = OA + AP = a + (3/5)(b − a) (M1) = (1 − 3/5)a + (3/5)b = (2/5)a + (3/5)b, that is OP = (2a + 3b)/5 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The ratio tells you what fraction of the whole journey from A to B the point P is along.","Travel to P in two stages: from O to A, then part of the way along AB.","Write down the vector AB in terms of a and b before you take any fraction of it."],"codes":["10.3","10.5"]},{"q":"Three forces F₁ = (4i − 3j) N, F₂ = (−i + 7j) N and F₃ = (pi + qj) N act on a particle, and the resultant of the three forces is (5i + j) N. Find the values of p and q, and find the magnitude of F₃ to 3 significant figures.","a":"p = 2, q = −3 and |F₃| = √13 = 3.61 N (3 s.f.)","sol":"The resultant is the sum of the forces, so F₁ + F₂ + F₃ = 5i + j (M1). Comparing i components: 4 − 1 + p = 5, so p = 2 (A1). Comparing j components: −3 + 7 + q = 1, so q = −3 (A1). Then F₃ = 2i − 3j and |F₃| = √(2² + (−3)²) = √13 (M1) = 3.61 N to 3 significant figures (A1). The unit is newtons because F₃ is a force.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["A resultant force is nothing more than a vector sum.","Add the three force vectors, set the sum equal to the resultant and compare the i and j parts separately.","Collect the known i components on one side before you bring in p, and remember the answer needs its unit."],"codes":["10.3","10.5","10.2"]},{"q":"The vector w = (t + 1)i + (t − 5)j, where t is a constant. Given that |w| = 5√2, find the possible values of t.","a":"t = 6 or t = −2","sol":"|w|² = (t + 1)² + (t − 5)² and (5√2)² = 50, so (t + 1)² + (t − 5)² = 50 (M1). Expanding: t² + 2t + 1 + t² − 10t + 25 = 50 (M1), so 2t² − 8t + 26 = 50 and 2t² − 8t − 24 = 0. Dividing by 2: t² − 4t − 12 = 0 (A1). Factorising: (t − 6)(t + 2) = 0 (M1), so t = 6 or t = −2 (A1). Check: t = 6 gives 7i + j with magnitude √50; t = −2 gives −i − 7j with magnitude √50.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The magnitude condition becomes an equation in t once both sides are squared.","Square the given magnitude, write the sum of the two squared components equal to it and expand.","Expect a quadratic in t, so collect every term on one side and simplify before solving."],"codes":["10.2"]},{"q":"A boat leaves a harbour O at time t = 0 and moves with constant velocity (6i + 8j) km per hour, where i and j are unit vectors due east and due north. A lighthouse L has position vector (21i + 2j) km relative to O. Find the position vector of the boat 2.5 hours after it leaves O, and find the distance of the boat from the lighthouse at that time, to 3 significant figures.","a":"Position vector 15i + 20j km; distance 6√10 km = 19.0 km (3 s.f.)","sol":"With constant velocity, displacement = velocity × time, so the position vector after 2.5 hours is 2.5(6i + 8j) (M1) = 15i + 20j km (A1). The vector from the boat to the lighthouse is (21i + 2j) − (15i + 20j) (M1) = 6i − 18j (A1). Its magnitude is √(6² + (−18)²) = √(36 + 324) = √360 = 6√10 (M1), which is 19.0 km to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Constant velocity means the displacement from O grows in proportion to the time.","Find where the boat is first, then use finish minus start to get the vector joining it to the lighthouse.","Multiply the velocity vector by the time before you compare anything with the lighthouse, and keep the units in your final line."],"codes":["10.4","10.5","10.2"]},{"q":"In the quadrilateral OABC, OA = a, OB = b and OC = c. The points P, Q, R and S are the midpoints of OA, AB, BC and CO respectively. Prove that PQRS is a parallelogram.","a":"PQ = SR = (1/2)b, so PQ and SR are equal and parallel, hence PQRS is a parallelogram","sol":"Position vectors of the midpoints: p = (1/2)a, q = (1/2)(a + b), r = (1/2)(b + c), s = (1/2)c (M1 A1). Then PQ = q − p = (1/2)(a + b) − (1/2)a = (1/2)b (M1 A1). Also SR = r − s = (1/2)(b + c) − (1/2)c = (1/2)b (A1). Since PQ = SR, the sides PQ and SR are parallel and of equal length, and PQRS is therefore a parallelogram (A1 cso).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["A quadrilateral is a parallelogram when one pair of opposite sides are equal as vectors.","Write the position vector of each midpoint in terms of a, b and c, then form two opposite sides.","The midpoint of a segment has position vector the average of the two ends, so start by writing all four of them down."],"codes":["10.3","10.4","10.5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"The points A(1, 2, 2) and B(4, 6, 14) have position vectors a and b relative to the origin O. Write AB in i, j, k form and state the distance AB.","a":"AB = 3i + 4j + 12k and AB = 13"},{"q":"Find the unit vector in the direction of 3i − 4j.","a":"(3/5)i − (4/5)j, that is 0.6i − 0.8j"},{"q":"Find the value of s for which 4i + sj is parallel to 2i − 3j.","a":"s = −6"},{"q":"ABCD is a parallelogram in which A, B and C have position vectors i + j, 4i + 2j and 6i + 5j. Find the position vector of D.","a":"3i + 4j"}]},"cards":[{"front":"What is the difference between a vector and a scalar?","back":"A vector has both magnitude and direction; a scalar has magnitude only.","code":"10.1","open":false},{"front":"How is the column vector with entries a, b and c written in unit vector form?","back":"ai + bj + ck. The entries are the components in the i, j and k directions, read from top to bottom.","code":"10.1","open":false},{"front":"|ai + bj| = ?","back":"√(a² + b²)","code":"10.2","open":false},{"front":"|ai + bj + ck| = ?","back":"√(a² + b² + c²)","code":"10.2","open":false},{"front":"What is a unit vector, and how do you obtain the unit vector in the direction of a?","back":"A vector of magnitude 1. In the direction of a it is a divided by |a|, so divide every component of a by |a|.","code":"10.2","open":false},{"front":"How do you find the angle that ai + bj makes with the positive x-axis?","back":"The acute angle satisfies tan θ = |b|/|a|; sketch the vector to decide which quadrant it lies in and adjust the angle accordingly.","code":"10.2","open":false},{"front":"A vector has magnitude r and direction θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis. What are its components?","back":"r cos θ i + r sin θ j","code":"10.2","open":false},{"front":"What is the triangle law of vector addition?","back":"Place the tail of b at the tip of a; then a + b runs from the tail of a to the tip of b. On a triangle, AB + BC = AC.","code":"10.3","open":false},{"front":"What is the parallelogram law of vector addition?","back":"Draw a and b from the same point; a + b is the diagonal of the parallelogram they form from that point.","code":"10.3","open":false},{"front":"When are two vectors parallel?","back":"When one is a scalar multiple of the other: a is parallel to b if a = kb for some non-zero number k.","code":"10.3","open":false},{"front":"What does multiplying a vector by the scalar k do to it?","back":"It multiplies the magnitude by |k| and keeps the direction the same; if k is negative the direction is reversed.","code":"10.3","open":false},{"front":"For points A and B with position vectors a and b, AB = ?","back":"AB = OB − OA = b − a, the position vector of the finish minus the position vector of the start.","code":"10.4","open":false},{"front":"What is the distance between the points (x₁, y₁, z₁) and (x₂, y₂, z₂)?","back":"d = √((x₁ − x₂)² + (y₁ − y₂)² + (z₁ − z₂)²)","code":"10.4","open":false},{"front":"What is the position vector of the midpoint of AB, and of the point dividing AB in the ratio m : n?","back":"The midpoint is (1/2)(a + b); the point dividing AB in the ratio m : n is a + (m/(m + n))(b − a).","code":"10.4","open":false},{"front":"Give one way of showing that a quadrilateral ABCD is a parallelogram using vectors.","back":"Show AB = DC as vectors; show AD = BC as vectors; show that the diagonals AC and BD have the same midpoint.","code":"10.5","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Magnitude in two dimensions: |ai + bj| = √(a² + b²) — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Magnitude in three dimensions: |ai + bj + ck| = √(a² + b² + c²) — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Unit vector in the direction of a: a divided by |a| — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Components from magnitude and direction: magnitude r at angle θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis gives r cos θ i + r sin θ j — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Vector between two points: AB = OB − OA = b − a for position vectors a and b — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Distance between two points in two dimensions: d² = (x₁ − x₂)² + (y₁ − y₂)² — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Distance between two points in three dimensions: d² = (x₁ − x₂)² + (y₁ − y₂)² + (z₁ − z₂)² — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Parallel test: a is parallel to b exactly when a = kb for some k ≠ 0 — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Midpoint of AB: (1/2)(a + b) — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Point dividing AB in the ratio m : n: a + (m/(m + n))(b − a) — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","Fourth vertex of parallelogram ABCD: d = a + c − b, from AD = BC — not in the formulae booklet, learn it.","The Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet has no vectors section for A level Mathematics, so every result above must be written from memory; the booklet does give the compound-angle identities, the small-angle approximations and the cone and sphere mensuration results, but Pythagoras' theorem, the sine rule, the cosine rule, area = (1/2)ab sin C, cos²A + sin²A = 1 and the sector results s = rθ and A = (1/2)r²θ are in Appendix 1 and must be memorised too."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Exam notes for this room","items":["There is no scalar (dot) product and no vector equation of a line in A level Mathematics — both belong to Further Mathematics. If a question seems to ask for the angle between two vectors, use trigonometry on a triangle of magnitudes.","AB = b − a, not a − b. Reversing it gives a vector of the same length in the opposite direction and loses the accuracy mark.","Keep a zero component in a column vector rather than writing a two-entry column for a three-dimensional vector.","Exact means surd form: leave √52 as 2√13 and leave 13√3 alone. A rounded decimal loses the final A mark.","To prove points collinear you must do two things: show one displacement is a scalar multiple of the other, and state that they share a common point.","In a mechanics context give the unit (N, m, m s⁻¹, km) with the final answer and round only at the very end."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:15:26Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-solved independently and checked numerically: all 15 bank questions, 4 exit tickets, 4 lesson checks and 5 worked examples are correct and unambiguous, the five spec codes 10.1-10.5 are covered in order with nothing outside the A level restrictions (the notes correctly warn that the scalar product and the vector equation of a line are Further Maths), and the formulae sheet's booklet claims now match Appendix 1 exactly - Appendix 1's only vectors entry is |xi + yj + zk| = sqrt(x^2+y^2+z^2), so marking every vector result 'not in the formulae booklet, learn it' is right, as is the closing item naming the compound-angle identities, small-angle approximations and cone/sphere mensuration as booklet content while Pythagoras, the sine and cosine rules, (1/2)ab sin C, cos^2 A + sin^2 A = 1 and s = r(theta), A = (1/2)r^2(theta) are memory items; publish as it is."}}},"S1":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"S1","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every statistics question you will ever meet begins with data that came from somewhere, and Paper 3 opens by testing whether you know where. These are the cheapest marks in the whole Statistics and Mechanics paper, but they are marked on precision of wording: the examiner wants the mechanism of a sampling method, not a vague gesture at it, and a criticism written about the context in the question rather than a stock phrase. Learn the five techniques, the words population, census, sample and sampling frame, and the stratified and systematic calculations, and you bank three to six marks in under five minutes.","idea":[{"h":"Population, census and sample","t":"The population is every member of the group you are studying — all 184 days of summer 2015 at Heathrow, all the light bulbs a firm makes this week, all the adults in a city. A census collects data from every single member of that population; a sample collects data from only part of it. A census is complete and accurate, but it is costly, slow, and simply impossible when the test destroys what it tests. A sample is cheap and quick, but it may not represent the population, and two different samples can lead to two different conclusions. From a sample you make an informal inference: the sample value is an estimate of the population value, never the population value itself.","code":"1.1"},{"h":"The five techniques and the mechanism of each","t":"Five methods are named in the specification and each is marked on its mechanism. Simple random sampling: number every member of the sampling frame, generate random numbers, ignore repeats, take the members those numbers identify — every possible sample of that size is equally likely. Systematic sampling: work out k = population size ÷ sample size, choose a random start from 1 to k, then take every kth member. Stratified sampling: split the population into strata, take (stratum size ÷ population size) × sample size from each, and choose within each stratum by simple random sampling. Quota sampling: an interviewer questions people until a fixed number from each group is filled — no frame, no randomness. Opportunity (convenience) sampling: take whoever is available at the time.","code":"1.1 Sampling techniques"},{"h":"Choosing and criticising a method in context","t":"A select-or-critique question gives you a real situation — a weather station, a factory list, a street interview — and wants three things: the name of the technique, a reason tied to that situation, and the consequence. Ask first whether a sampling frame exists: without one, only quota and opportunity sampling are available. Then ask who or what could never be selected, because that is where bias lives: shoppers in full-time work at 10 am, fish smaller than the mesh, the July days that a May-only sample never reaches. Finish by remembering that the estimate a sample gives is one of many possible estimates, so a conclusion drawn from one sample might not be repeated by another.","code":"1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques"}],"examples":[{"title":"Working out a stratified sample","setup":"A sixth form has 750 students: 420 study a science, 210 study humanities and 120 study arts. A stratified sample of 50 students is to be taken. Work out how many students should be chosen from each of the three groups, and say how the students within each group should be selected.","steps":["Sampling fraction = 50 ÷ 750 = 1/15.","Science: 420 × 1/15 = 28.","Humanities: 210 × 1/15 = 14.","Arts: 120 × 1/15 = 8.","Check 28 + 14 + 8 = 50; then number the students in each group and use random numbers to choose 28 science, 14 humanities and 8 arts students."],"cues":["What fraction of the whole sixth form is being sampled?","How do you turn that fraction into a number for the science group?","How many does the same fraction give for the humanities group?","Which group has still to be worked out?","What check confirms the three numbers, and how are the individuals then picked?"]},{"title":"Describing a systematic sample, and where it goes wrong","setup":"A hospital has a list of 2400 patients, numbered 1 to 2400. A systematic sample of 60 patients is required. Describe fully how the sample should be taken, and state one situation in which this method would give a biased sample.","steps":["Sampling interval k = 2400 ÷ 60 = 40.","Choose the starting patient at random from the numbers 1 to 40; suppose the random number generator gives 17.","Take patient 17, then every 40th patient after that: 17, 57, 97, and so on up to 2377.","The sample is biased if the list repeats with a period of 40 — say the list is arranged ward by ward with 40 beds in each ward — because then every patient chosen lies in the same bed position in a ward, and the beds nearest the nurses' station hold the most seriously ill patients."],"cues":["How many patients are there for each one you will select?","Where must the very first patient come from, and how is that one picked?","Once you have a start, how do you find the rest of the sample?","What kind of pattern in the list would wreck this sample?"]},{"title":"Criticising a sample from the large data set","setup":"Tom is investigating the daily mean temperature at Leuchars over the three months June, July and August in 2015. The large data set gives a reading for each of these 92 days, and its records show that at Leuchars the daily mean temperature climbs through the summer to a peak in July and August. Tom uses the readings for the first 10 days of June, finds their mean, and states that this is the mean daily mean temperature at Leuchars for the three months. Name the sampling technique Tom has used, criticise it, and describe a better method.","steps":["Tom simply took the days that were convenient to him, so this is opportunity (convenience) sampling.","Criticism: the sample leaves out July and August altogether, and the large data set shows those are the two warmest of the three months, so the mean of ten early-June days is likely to be too low for the period Tom is describing.","Criticism: a sample of 10 of the 92 days is very small, so another sample of the same size could easily give a very different mean.","Better method: number the days 1 to 92, generate random numbers in that range and use the days they identify — a simple random sample, in which every possible sample of that size is equally likely."],"cues":["What decided which days ended up in Tom's sample?","Which of the three months does Tom's sample miss, and what does the large data set say about them?","How many of the 92 days did Tom actually use?","How could every day of the three months be given the same chance of selection?"]},{"title":"Deciding between a census and a sample","setup":"A firm makes 5000 fireworks each week. To test a firework the firm lights it, which destroys it. The quality manager wants to know the proportion of fireworks that light within 3 seconds. Explain whether the firm should carry out a census or take a sample, and give one advantage and one disadvantage of the method you choose.","steps":["A census would mean lighting all 5000 fireworks, which would leave the firm with nothing to sell, so a census is impossible here.","The firm must therefore take a sample: light a small number of fireworks and record how many of them light within the required time.","Advantage: only the fireworks in the sample are destroyed, so the test is cheap and quick and the rest of the week's production can still be sold.","Disadvantage: the sample may not represent the week's production, so the proportion found is only an estimate, and another sample could give a different value."],"cues":["What would a census mean here, given what the test does to a firework?","So what must the firm do instead, and what does it record?","What does the firm gain by testing only some of them?","What is the price of not testing them all?"]},{"title":"Working a stratified sample backwards","setup":"A company has offices in four cities. The London office has 252 staff, the Leeds office has 144 staff and the Cardiff office has 96 staff; the number of staff in the Glasgow office is not known. A stratified sample of 50 staff is taken from across the company, and 8 of those sampled work in the Cardiff office. Find the number of staff in the Glasgow office.","steps":["The sampling fraction is the same in every stratum, so from Cardiff it is 8 ÷ 96 = 1/12.","The whole sample is that same fraction of the whole company, so the company has 50 × 12 = 600 staff.","The three known offices hold 252 + 144 + 96 = 492 staff.","Glasgow therefore has 600 − 492 = 108 staff."],"cues":["What is the same for every office in a stratified sample?","How does that fraction give you the size of the whole company?","What do the three offices you know about add up to?","How do you get Glasgow from those two totals?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What is the difference between a population and a sample?","a":"The population is every member of the group being studied; a sample is only some of them."},{"q":"A list of 1500 names is to be sampled systematically with a sample of 50. What is k, and where does the starting number come from?","a":"k = 30; the start is a random number from 1 to 30."},{"q":"Which two of the five techniques need no sampling frame?","a":"Quota sampling and opportunity (convenience) sampling."},{"q":"Why must a stratified sample use simple random sampling inside each stratum?","a":"So that every member of a stratum has an equal chance of selection; otherwise bias can enter even though the proportions are correct."}]},"room":{"facts":["Sampling fraction = sample size ÷ population size, and in a stratified sample it is the same in every stratum.","Number taken from a stratum = (stratum size ÷ population size) × total sample size.","Systematic sampling: k = population size ÷ sample size; choose a random start from 1 to k, then take every kth member.","In a simple random sample of size n, every possible sample of size n is equally likely and every member of the population is equally likely to be chosen.","Quota and opportunity sampling need no sampling frame; simple random, systematic and stratified sampling all need one.","A census uses every member of the population; a sample uses only part of it, so different samples can lead to different conclusions."],"questions":[{"q":"A school has 1200 students, of whom 480 are in Year 12. A stratified sample of 150 students is to be taken. Work out how many Year 12 students should be in the sample.","a":"60","sol":"Every stratum is sampled in the same proportion as the population. Sampling fraction = 150 ÷ 1200 = 1/8 (M1). Year 12: 480 × 1/8 = 60 (A1). So 60 Year 12 students are in the sample.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a stratified sample every group is sampled in the same proportion as it appears in the population.","Find what fraction of the whole school is being sampled, then apply that fraction to Year 12 only.","The answer is a whole number of students, so a tidy division should come out exactly."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"A company has a list of all 640 of its employees. Describe how a simple random sample of 25 employees could be taken.","a":"Number the employees 1 to 640, generate 25 different random numbers in that range, and take the employees with those numbers","sol":"Use the list of all employees as the sampling frame and number the employees 1 to 640 (B1). Generate random numbers between 1 and 640 using a calculator, a random number table or a computer (B1). Ignore any repeats until 25 different numbers have been obtained, and select the employees with those numbers (B1). Every possible sample of 25 employees is then equally likely.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The complete list of employees is the sampling frame, and it is what makes this method possible at all.","Two things are needed: a label for each person, and a source of randomness to pick the labels.","Your answer is a description in words with three moves in it: label, generate, select."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"The large data set records the daily mean temperature at Heathrow for each of the 184 days of the summer of 2015. Steve wants a systematic sample of 23 of these days. Find the sampling interval k that he should use, and state the numbers from which the starting day must be chosen at random.","a":"k = 8, with the starting day chosen at random from 1 to 8","sol":"k = population size ÷ sample size = 184 ÷ 23 (M1) = 8 (A1). The first day is chosen at random from the numbers 1 to 8, and then every 8th day after it is taken (B1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Systematic sampling works through the list taking members at a fixed interval.","The interval is the size of the population divided by the size of the sample.","Two things are wanted: a whole number for k, and a range of possible starting days rather than one named day."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"Name the sampling technique in which the population is split into groups and an interviewer questions people until a set number from each group has been reached, with no random selection and no sampling frame.","a":"Quota sampling","sol":"The defining features are: the population is divided into groups; a set number (the quota) must be filled from each group; the interviewer decides whom to approach, so the selection is not random; and no sampling frame is needed. This is quota sampling (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Only two of the five named techniques can be carried out without a sampling frame.","Check each of those two against every feature the question lists: groups, a set number to reach from each, no randomness, no sampling frame.","One word in front of the word 'sampling' is all that is required."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"A firm tests how long its light bulbs last by leaving each one switched on until it fails. Explain why the firm must use a sample rather than a census.","a":"The test destroys each bulb tested, so a census would destroy the whole stock and leave nothing to sell","sol":"A census means collecting data from every member of the population, which here would mean testing every bulb the firm makes (B1). The test destroys the bulb, so after a census there would be no bulbs left to sell; a census is therefore impossible and a sample must be used (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A census takes data from every single member of the population.","Think about what this particular test does to each bulb it is applied to.","Your answer should say what the firm would have left at the end of a census."],"codes":["1.1"]},{"q":"A factory employs 1800 people: 1080 work the day shift and 720 work the night shift. A stratified sample of 75 workers is to be taken. Find the number of workers to be taken from each shift, and describe how the workers within each shift should be chosen.","a":"45 day-shift workers and 30 night-shift workers, chosen within each shift by simple random sampling","sol":"Sampling fraction = 75 ÷ 1800 = 1/24 (M1). Day shift: 1080 × 1/24 = 45 (A1). Night shift: 720 × 1/24 = 30 (A1); check 45 + 30 = 75. Within each shift, number the workers and use random numbers to select the required number from that shift — a simple random sample from each stratum (B1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Each shift is a stratum, and a stratum must appear in the sample in the same proportion as in the workforce.","Find the fraction of the workforce that is being sampled, then apply it to each shift separately.","You need two numbers that add to 75, plus a sentence saying how individuals inside a shift are picked."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"A list of 960 factory workers is arranged in teams of 8: within each team the supervisor is listed first, followed by the 7 operatives of that team. Adam takes a systematic sample of 120 workers from this list. Explain why his sample will be biased.","a":"k = 8 matches the repeating block of 8, so every worker selected sits in the same position in a team — all supervisors or all operatives","sol":"k = 960 ÷ 120 = 8 (M1). The list repeats with a period of 8, and the sample takes every 8th worker from a fixed random start, so every worker selected occupies the same position within a team (A1). The sample therefore consists entirely of supervisors, or entirely of operatives, and cannot represent the whole workforce (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Work out the sampling interval before you think about the bias at all.","Compare that interval with the length of the repeating block in the list.","Your answer must finish by saying what every selected worker has in common."],"codes":["1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"A researcher stands outside a supermarket at 10 am on a Tuesday and questions the first 30 people who leave, in order to find out what all of the supermarket's customers think of its opening hours. Name the sampling technique used, and give one reason why the sample may not represent all of the supermarket's customers.","a":"Opportunity (convenience) sampling; only customers who shop at 10 am on a weekday can be chosen, so those in full-time work are excluded","sol":"The researcher questions whoever happens to be available at that place and time, so this is opportunity, or convenience, sampling (B1). Only customers who are leaving the shop at 10 am on a weekday have any chance of being chosen, so customers in full-time work, or those who shop at weekends or in the evening, can never be included; the sample is biased and may not represent all of the supermarket's customers (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what decided which customers were able to be in the sample at all.","Think about which groups of customers could never be outside that shop at that time on that day.","Two things are wanted: the name of the method, and one reason written about this supermarket rather than in general."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques","1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques"]},{"q":"A market research company must find the views of 200 adults living in a city, but no list of the city's adult population exists. Explain why quota sampling is a suitable technique here, and give one disadvantage of using it.","a":"It needs no sampling frame, since interviewers simply fill a set number from each group; but the interviewer chooses whom to approach, so the sample can be biased","sol":"Simple random, systematic and stratified sampling all need a sampling frame, and no list of the city's adults exists, so none of them can be used (B1). Quota sampling needs no frame: the population is split into groups, for example by age and sex, and interviewers question people in the street until each quota is filled (B1). Disadvantage: the interviewer decides whom to approach, so the selection is not random and the sample can be biased; non-response also cannot be recorded (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["What do the three random techniques all require that this city does not have?","Think about what an interviewer standing in a street actually does, and what they need to know before starting.","Your answer needs one reason the method suits this context and one weakness of the method itself."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques","1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques"]},{"q":"Two students each take a simple random sample of 30 days from the 184 days of the summer of 2015 at the same weather station, and each calculates the mean daily rainfall of their own sample. They obtain different values. Explain why this happens, and state what it shows about a conclusion drawn from a single sample.","a":"The two samples contain different days, so they give different estimates; a conclusion drawn from one sample may not be repeated by another sample","sol":"Each student's simple random sample is a different set of 30 days out of the 184, and daily rainfall varies a great deal from day to day, so the two sample means are almost certain to differ (B1). This shows that a sample gives only an estimate of the population value, and that different samples can lead to different conclusions about the population, so a conclusion drawn from a single sample must be treated with caution (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Nothing has gone wrong here — both students carried out the method correctly.","Ask what is actually different about the two sets of 30 days they ended up with.","The second half of your answer is a statement about conclusions, not a statement about rainfall."],"codes":["1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","1.1"]},{"q":"A college teaches students at three sites. Site A has 500 students and Site B has 325 students. Site C has n students. A stratified sample of 80 students is taken across the whole college, and 14 of the 80 come from Site C. Find the value of n.","a":"n = 175","sol":"The sampling fraction is the same in every stratum. For Site C it is 14/n, and for the whole college it is 80/(500 + 325 + n) = 80/(825 + n) (M1). Setting them equal: 14/n = 80/(825 + n), so 14(825 + n) = 80n (M1). Expanding: 11550 + 14n = 80n, so 66n = 11550 (A1) and n = 175 (A1). Check: the college has 1000 students, the sampling fraction is 0.08, and 175 × 0.08 = 14.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The sampling fraction is identical for every site — that is what makes the sample stratified.","Write the fraction for Site C using n, write the fraction for the whole college using n, and set the two equal.","The equation you form is linear in n, so expect a single three-figure whole number."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"Rebecca is comparing the mean daily mean temperature at Heathrow in the summer of 1987 with that in the summer of 2015. The large data set gives a reading for each of the 184 days from 1 May to 31 October in each of those summers. Rebecca proposes to use the readings for the first 30 days of each summer. Explain why this is a poor choice of sample, and describe a better method she could use.","a":"The first 30 days are all in May, so July and August are not represented and the means are biased low; instead take a simple random sample of 30 of the 184 days in each summer using random numbers","sol":"The 184 days run from 1 May to 31 October, so the first 30 days of each summer all lie in May (B1). The sample therefore misses July and August, which knowledge of the large data set tells us are the warmest months at Heathrow, while May is one of the coolest; inferring the direction from that knowledge, the sample is biased and the mean it gives will be too low for the summer as a whole (B1). A better method: number the days of each summer 1 to 184 and generate 30 different random numbers in that range, taking the days those numbers identify (B1). This is a simple random sample, in which every possible sample of that size is equally likely, and the same procedure is used for each summer so that the two means can fairly be compared (B1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work out which part of the summer the first 30 days of the record actually cover.","Compare that with the months covered by the other 154 days of each summer.","Your improved method must give every one of the 184 days the same chance of being chosen, and must be applied to both summers."],"codes":["1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"A biologist wants to estimate the mean length of the fish in a lake. She catches fish using a net with a large mesh, measures every fish she catches, and finds the mean of her measurements. Name the sampling technique she has used, and explain why her estimate of the mean length of the fish in the lake is likely to be too large.","a":"Opportunity (convenience) sampling; fish smaller than the mesh pass through and are never measured, so only the longer fish are in the sample","sol":"She measures whichever fish happen to be caught in the net, so this is opportunity, or convenience, sampling (B1). Fish shorter than the mesh size swim through the net and therefore have no chance of appearing in the sample (B1). The sample consists only of the longer fish and is biased, so its mean is greater than the mean length of all the fish in the lake (B1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Ask which fish in the lake could never end up in her net, whatever she does.","A sample is biased when part of the population has no chance at all of being selected.","Your answer must name a technique and then link the missing fish to the size of the mean she calculates."],"codes":["1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","1.1"]},{"q":"A sports club has 90 members, of whom 54 are men and 36 are women. A simple random sample of 3 members is chosen, without replacement. Find the probability that all 3 members chosen are women, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.0608","sol":"In a simple random sample every member is equally likely to be chosen, so P(first chosen is a woman) = 36/90 (B1). Because members are not replaced, P(second is a woman, given the first is) = 35/89 and P(third is a woman, given the first two are) = 34/88 (M1). P(all three are women) = (36/90) × (35/89) × (34/88) = 42840/704880 (M1) = 0.06077... = 0.0608 to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In a simple random sample every member of the club is equally likely to be the one chosen.","The members are taken one at a time and are not put back, so both numbers in the fraction change after each pick.","Multiply three fractions together and only round at the very end, to 3 significant figures."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]},{"q":"In a company, 45% of the employees work in Production, 35% work in Sales and the rest work in Admin. A stratified sample of employees is taken, and it contains 16 employees from Admin. Find the number of employees from Production in the sample.","a":"36","sol":"Admin makes up 100% − 45% − 35% = 20% of the employees (B1). A stratified sample keeps each stratum in the same proportion, so Admin makes up 20% of the sample: 0.20 × (sample size) = 16 (M1), giving sample size = 16 ÷ 0.20 = 80 (A1). Production is 45% of the sample: 0.45 × 80 = 36 (A1). Check: 36 + 0.35 × 80 + 16 = 36 + 28 + 16 = 80.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The percentages in a stratified sample are the same as the percentages in the population.","Admin's percentage is whatever is left over; use it together with the 16 to find the size of the whole sample first.","Two steps are needed, and the answer to the second one is a whole number of employees."],"codes":["1.1 Sampling techniques"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Data is collected from every single member of a population. What is this called?","a":"A census"},{"q":"A population of 900 is to be sampled systematically with a sample of 45. Find the sampling interval k.","a":"k = 20"},{"q":"A club has 320 men and 180 women. A stratified sample of 50 members is taken. How many women are in the sample?","a":"18"},{"q":"Two different simple random samples from the same population give different means. What does this show about a conclusion drawn from one sample?","a":"A different sample could give a different conclusion, so the conclusion from a single sample may not hold for the population"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Population (in statistics)","back":"Every member of the whole group of people or items that is being studied.","code":"1.1","open":false},{"front":"Census","back":"A collection of data from every single member of the population.","code":"1.1","open":false},{"front":"Sample","back":"A selection of some members of the population, used to make an informal inference about the whole population.","code":"1.1","open":false},{"front":"Sampling frame","back":"A list identifying every member of the population, usually numbered, from which the sample is actually drawn.","code":"1.1","open":false},{"front":"Sampling unit","back":"One individual member of the population that can be sampled.","code":"1.1","open":false},{"front":"Simple random sampling: the definition","back":"A sample chosen so that every possible sample of that size is equally likely: number the sampling frame, generate random numbers, ignore repeats, take the members identified.","code":"1.1 Sampling techniques","open":false},{"front":"Systematic sampling: the mechanism","back":"Number the sampling frame, work out k = population size ÷ sample size, choose a random start from 1 to k, then take every kth member.","code":"1.1 Sampling techniques","open":false},{"front":"Stratified sampling: the mechanism","back":"Split the population into strata; take (stratum size ÷ population size) × sample size from each; choose the members inside each stratum by simple random sampling.","code":"1.1 Sampling techniques","open":false},{"front":"Quota sampling: the mechanism","back":"The population is split into groups and an interviewer questions people until a fixed number from each group is filled. No sampling frame and no randomness.","code":"1.1 Sampling techniques","open":false},{"front":"Opportunity (convenience) sampling: the mechanism","back":"Take whoever is available at the time and willing to take part, until the sample is big enough. No sampling frame is needed.","code":"1.1 Sampling techniques","open":false},{"front":"Census: advantages and disadvantages","back":"Advantages: it is completely accurate; every member of the population is counted. Disadvantages: it is expensive; it is slow; it is impossible when the test destroys what it tests.","code":"1.1","open":true},{"front":"Opportunity sampling: disadvantages","back":"It is unlikely to represent the population; it depends entirely on who happens to be available; two researchers can obtain completely different samples.","code":"1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","open":true},{"front":"Stratified sampling: disadvantages","back":"The population must divide into clearly defined strata; the size of every stratum must be known in advance; a sampling frame is still needed within each stratum.","code":"1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","open":true},{"front":"Why two samples from the same population can disagree","back":"Each sample contains different members, so the estimate varies from sample to sample; a conclusion drawn from one sample may not be repeated by another.","code":"1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","open":false},{"front":"What a criticism of a sampling method must contain for full marks","back":"The name of the technique, a reason tied to the context given (bias, representativeness, cost, need for a sampling frame), and the consequence for the conclusion.","code":"1.1 Select or critique sampling techniques","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Sampling fraction = sample size ÷ population size. Not in the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet — write it from memory.","Stratified sample: number taken from a stratum = (stratum size ÷ population size) × total sample size. Not in the booklet.","Stratified sample worked backwards: population size = (stratum size × total sample size) ÷ number taken from that stratum. Not in the booklet.","Systematic sample: interval k = population size ÷ sample size, with the first member chosen at random from 1 to k and every kth member taken after it. Not in the booklet.","Simple random sample of size n: every possible sample of size n is equally likely, and every member of the population is equally likely to be chosen. A definition rather than a formula, and not in the booklet.","The Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet gives no formula or definition for any sampling technique: every result above must be written from memory. The nearest it comes is its table of Random Numbers, a tool for making a selection rather than a formula."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Wording the mark scheme rewards","items":["Describing simple random sampling: say 'number the sampling frame 1 to N', 'generate random numbers', 'ignore repeats', 'select the members with those numbers'. A bare 'pick them at random' scores nothing.","Describing systematic sampling: the random start is a marking point. 'Take every kth member' without 'from a random start between 1 and k' loses a mark.","Describing stratified sampling: the proportions are only half of it — say that the members within each stratum are then chosen by simple random sampling.","Criticising a method: answer in the context of the question. Name the technique, say who or what can never be selected, and say what that does to the estimate.","Advantages and disadvantages of a census: complete and accurate, but costly, slow and impossible when testing destroys the item.","Always say that a sample gives an estimate of the population value, and that another sample could give a different value and so a different conclusion."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:18:24.381444Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Publishable: every one of the 15 bank answers, the 4 exit answers and all 5 worked examples re-solve exactly (including 184 = 1 May to 31 Oct, k = 8 for 23 days, n = 175 for the reverse-stratified college, 36/90 x 35/89 x 34/88 = 0.0608 to 3 s.f. and Glasgow = 108), every M/A/B tally matches its `m`, and the Formulae sheet's claim that the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet supplies nothing for sampling is confirmed against Appendix 1, whose Statistics section lists only the mean and the standard Normal variable; all eleven faults from the first examination are genuinely gone - the Leuchars example now restricts Tom to the 92 days of June, July and August and states in the setup that Leuchars peaks in July and August, so 'too low' follows from the case; the ward-sister slip is replaced by a ward-by-ward list with 40 beds per ward; questions[11].sol now says outright that the direction is inferred from large data set knowledge; the three imperative card fronts are named topics; questions[3].hints[1], examples[3].cues[0] and examples[2].cues[1]-[2] no longer reveal or mismatch their steps; examples[0] is split into five one-move steps with singular cues; and questions[10] and [14] have dropped to d = 2, leaving a sensible 5/7/3 tier spread."}}},"S2":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"S2","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every Paper 3 statistics section opens with data: a histogram, a box plot, a cumulative frequency curve, a scatter diagram or a block of summary statistics from the large data set. This room is where the routine marks live — frequency density, Sₓₓ, linear interpolation, coding, the outlier rule — and where the AO2 and AO3 marks live too, because Edexcel asks you to say what the number means for the runners, the rainfall or the rents. A correct calculation with no interpretation loses the final mark just as surely as a wrong one, and an answer written with no working scores nothing at all, however good your calculator is.","idea":[{"code":"2.1","h":"Diagrams for one variable: area is frequency","t":"A histogram uses area, not height, to show frequency, so the vertical axis is frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. When the axis is unlabelled, find the scaling constant k from a class whose frequency you are told (frequency = k × area), then use it on any other bar; to estimate a frequency over part of a class, take that fraction of the class and assume the values are spread evenly. A cumulative frequency diagram is read across from n/2 for the median and from n/4 and 3n/4 for the quartiles, and a box plot shows the lowest value that is not an outlier, Q₁, the median, Q₃ and the highest value that is not an outlier, with outliers marked separately as crosses. Comparing two box plots earns marks only when you compare both a measure of location (medians) and a measure of spread (IQR or range) and say what it means in context. Skew is judged by comparing Q₂ − Q₁ with Q₃ − Q₂."},{"code":"2.2","h":"Scatter diagrams, correlation and regression lines","t":"Bivariate data are plotted with the explanatory (independent) variable on the horizontal axis and the response (dependent) variable on the vertical axis. Describe correlation with two words — strength (strong, weak, zero) and direction (positive, negative) — and then say it again in the language of the two variables, because that is where the second mark sits. Correlation never proves causation: a third variable may drive both, or the association may be coincidence. The regression line y = a + bx is used only to predict inside the range of the explanatory variable (interpolation); outside it (extrapolation) the linear pattern has no evidence behind it. Watch for scatter diagrams that contain two distinct sections of the population, where one line for all the points describes neither group. For 9MA0 you are not asked to calculate a or b, but you are asked to interpret them, and to use logarithms to straighten a curved relationship: y = axⁿ gives log y = log a + n log x, and y = kbˣ gives log y = log k + x log b."},{"code":"2.3","h":"Averages, spread, Sₓₓ, interpolation and coding","t":"Mean, median and mode locate the data; range, interquartile range, interpercentile range, variance and standard deviation measure how spread out it is. From summary statistics use Sₓₓ = Σx² − (Σx)²/n and then σ = √(Sₓₓ/n); showing the substitution into Sₓₓ is the method mark, so write it out even when your calculator gives σ directly. For grouped data the median and percentiles are estimated by linear interpolation: go a fraction of the way into the class that contains the value you want, assuming the class is evenly filled. Coding y = (x − a)/b makes awkward numbers manageable: reverse it with x̄ = bȳ + a, and remember that subtracting a leaves the spread untouched while dividing by b divides the standard deviation by b, so the standard deviation of x is b times that of y. If a value is added to or removed from a data set, adjust Σx and Σx² first and then rebuild the mean and Sₓₓ from the new totals."},{"code":"2.4","h":"Outliers, cleaning data and critiquing a diagram","t":"An outlier is a value the question defines for you: usually below Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR or above Q₃ + 1.5 × IQR, or more than 2 or 3 standard deviations from the mean. Never invent a rule — read the one you are given, work out both boundaries, compare, and then state clearly which values are outliers. Cleaning data means deciding what to do next: a value that is a recording or transcription error should be removed and the summary statistics recalculated, while a genuine extreme observation is normally kept and commented on. Missing data are usually excluded from the calculation, and the reduced n must then be used. You may also be asked to critique a presentation — for example that a histogram with unequal classes drawn with frequency on the vertical axis is misleading, that a mean is distorted by an outlier where the median is not, or that a box plot hides how many values lie at each end. Answers score for naming the feature of the data that causes the problem, not for a general opinion."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a frequency off a histogram with no vertical scale","setup":"A histogram is drawn for the times, t minutes, taken by 80 runners to finish a race. On the histogram the bar for the class 20 ≤ t < 25 is 2 cm wide and 4.5 cm tall, and 18 runners finished in that class. The bar for the class 25 ≤ t < 35 is 4 cm wide and 6 cm tall. Estimate how many runners took between 25 and 32 minutes.","steps":["Area = 2 × 4.5 = 9 cm², and that area stands for a frequency of 18.","Frequency = k × area, so k = 18 ÷ 9 = 2 runners per cm².","Area of the 25 ≤ t < 35 bar = 4 × 6 = 24 cm², so its frequency = 2 × 24 = 48.","The part 25 ≤ t < 32 covers 7 of the 10 minutes of that class, so the estimated frequency is (7/10) × 48 = 33.6.","So about 34 runners took between 25 and 32 minutes."],"cues":["Which bar do you know a frequency for, and what area does it cover?","How many runners does one square centimetre stand for?","What is the area of the second bar, and so how many runners are in it?","What fraction of that class is being asked about?","What is the estimate, to the nearest runner?"]},{"title":"Mean and standard deviation from Σx and Σx²","setup":"A biologist weighs 20 seeds taken from one plant. For the masses, x grams, she finds Σx = 140.8 and Σx² = 1088.6. Find the mean mass and the standard deviation of the masses, each to 3 significant figures.","steps":["x̄ = 140.8 ÷ 20 = 7.04.","Sₓₓ = Σx² − (Σx)²/n = 1088.6 − 140.8²/20 = 1088.6 − 991.232.","Sₓₓ = 97.368.","Variance = Sₓₓ/n = 97.368 ÷ 20 = 4.8684.","σ = √4.8684 = 2.2064…, so the mean mass is 7.04 g and the standard deviation is 2.21 g (3 s.f.)."],"cues":["What is the mean mass?","Which form of Sₓₓ uses only the totals you are given?","What does that subtraction come to?","How do you turn Sₓₓ into the variance?","What are the mean and the standard deviation, each to 3 significant figures?"]},{"title":"A percentile from grouped data by linear interpolation","setup":"The lengths, t minutes, of 60 phone calls to a helpline are summarised as follows: 0 ≤ t < 5, frequency 12; 5 ≤ t < 10, frequency 18; 10 ≤ t < 20, frequency 21; 20 ≤ t < 40, frequency 9. Use linear interpolation to estimate the 65th percentile of the call lengths, to 3 significant figures.","steps":["The cumulative frequencies are 12, 30, 51, 60.","The 65th percentile sits at 0.65 × 60 = 39, which falls in the class 10 ≤ t < 20 (its cumulative frequency runs from 30 up to 51).","Fraction of the way into that class = (39 − 30)/21 = 9/21.","Add that fraction of the class width: 10 + (9/21) × 10 = 10 + 4.2857…","So the 65th percentile is 14.3 minutes (3 s.f.)."],"cues":["What are the running totals of the frequencies?","Which value are you looking for, and which class contains it?","How far into that class does the value lie, as a fraction?","How much of the class width does that fraction give?","What is the estimate to 3 significant figures?"]},{"title":"Straightening an exponential model with logarithms","setup":"A colony of bacteria is modelled by N = k bᵗ, where N is the number of bacteria in thousands, t hours after the start. Readings are taken for 1 ≤ t ≤ 8, and when log₁₀ N is plotted against t the points lie close to the line log₁₀ N = 0.6 + 0.2t. Find k and b, each to 3 significant figures, and use the model to estimate the number of bacteria when t = 5.","steps":["Taking logs of both sides of N = k bᵗ gives log₁₀ N = log₁₀ k + t log₁₀ b.","Comparing with the fitted line: log₁₀ k = 0.6 and log₁₀ b = 0.2.","k = 10^0.6 = 3.98 and b = 10^0.2 = 1.58 (3 s.f.).","t = 5 lies inside the range of the readings, so this prediction is interpolation and is reasonable.","At t = 5: log₁₀ N = 0.6 + 0.2 × 5 = 1.6.","N = 10^1.6 = 39.8, so the model predicts about 39 800 bacteria."],"cues":["What happens to N = k bᵗ if you take logarithms of both sides?","Which part of the straight line matches log₁₀ k, and which matches log₁₀ b?","How do you undo a base-10 logarithm?","Is the value of t you are asked about inside the data or outside it?","What is log₁₀ N at that value of t?","What is N, and how many bacteria is that?"]},{"title":"Testing the extremes of a box plot against the 1.5 × IQR rule","setup":"A grower measures the heights, in cm, of 15 seedlings. The five-number summary is: lowest 4, Q₁ = 11, median 14, Q₃ = 17, highest 38. An outlier is defined as any value more than 1.5 × IQR below Q₁ or more than 1.5 × IQR above Q₃. Decide whether the lowest and the highest values are outliers, and say how the box plot should show them.","steps":["IQR = 17 − 11 = 6.","1.5 × IQR = 1.5 × 6 = 9.","Lower boundary = 11 − 9 = 2; upper boundary = 17 + 9 = 26.","4 > 2, so the lowest value is not an outlier, but 38 > 26, so the highest one is.","So 38 cm is the only outlier: it is marked with a cross, and the upper whisker is drawn back to the tallest seedling that is not an outlier."],"cues":["What is the interquartile range?","What is one and a half times that?","Where do the two boundaries sit on the height scale?","Does either extreme value lie beyond a boundary?","How should the box plot be drawn as a result?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A histogram class of width 8 has frequency 24. What is the frequency density?","a":"3"},{"q":"For n = 10 values, Σx = 50 and Σx² = 300. Find Sₓₓ.","a":"50"},{"q":"A regression line y = 14 + 3.2x is fitted to data covering 5 ≤ x ≤ 25. Predict y when x = 10, and say whether the prediction can be trusted.","a":"y = 46; yes — x = 10 is inside the range of the data, so it is interpolation"},{"q":"The coded data y = (x − 20)/5 have mean 3 and standard deviation 2. Find the mean and standard deviation of x.","a":"Mean 35, standard deviation 10"}]},"room":{"facts":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width, and in a histogram frequency = k × area of the bar.","Sₓₓ = Σ(x − x̄)² = Σx² − (Σx)²/n, and standard deviation σ = √(Sₓₓ/n).","Linear interpolation: median = L + ((n/2 − F)/f) × w; for the kth percentile replace n/2 by kn/100.","Coding y = (x − a)/b gives x̄ = bȳ + a, and the standard deviation of x is b times the standard deviation of y.","Outlier rule (when the question specifies it): below Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR or above Q₃ + 1.5 × IQR.","Reducing to linear form: y = axⁿ gives log y = log a + n log x; y = kbˣ gives log y = log k + x log b.","For n discrete values, Q₁ is at position n/4 and Q₃ at 3n/4: if the position is a whole number take the midpoint of that value and the next, otherwise round the position up."],"questions":[{"q":"In a histogram showing the masses, m grams, of a sample of apples, the class 80 ≤ m < 100 has frequency 46. Find the frequency density of this class.","a":"2.3","sol":"Class width = 100 − 80 = 20 g. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width = 46 ÷ 20 (M1) = 2.3 apples per gram (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The vertical axis of a histogram is not frequency.","You need the width of the class before you can do anything.","Write down 46 ÷ (100 − 80)."],"codes":["2.1"]},{"q":"The times, in seconds, of 8 sprints are 11, 12, 12, 13, 14, 15, 15, 20. Find the median and the interquartile range.","a":"Median 13.5 s, IQR 3 s","sol":"n = 8, so the median is the mean of the 4th and 5th values: (13 + 14)/2 = 13.5 s (B1). n/4 = 2, a whole number, so Q₁ is the mean of the 2nd and 3rd values: (12 + 12)/2 = 12. 3n/4 = 6, a whole number, so Q₃ is the mean of the 6th and 7th values: (15 + 15)/2 = 15 (M1 for both quartiles). IQR = Q₃ − Q₁ = 15 − 12 = 3 s (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The data are already in order, so you are counting positions.","With n = 8, positions n/4 and 3n/4 are both whole numbers — that changes what you do.","The median is at position 4.5, so average the 4th and 5th values."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"For a set of 12 values, Σx = 96 and Σx² = 900. Find Sₓₓ and hence the standard deviation, giving the standard deviation to 3 significant figures.","a":"Sₓₓ = 132, σ = 3.32 (3 s.f.)","sol":"Sₓₓ = Σx² − (Σx)²/n = 900 − 96²/12 (M1) = 900 − 768 = 132 (A1). σ = √(Sₓₓ/n) = √(132/12) = √11 = 3.3166… = 3.32 to 3 s.f. (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["You are given exactly the two totals the Sₓₓ formula needs.","Find Sₓₓ first, then divide by n before taking the square root.","Sₓₓ = 900 − 96²/12."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"On a scatter diagram of daily ice cream sales, in £, against daily maximum temperature, in °C, the points lie close to a straight line that rises from left to right. Describe the correlation and say what it means for the two variables.","a":"Strong positive correlation: days with a higher maximum temperature tend to have higher ice cream sales","sol":"The points lie close to a straight line, so the correlation is strong, and the line rises from left to right, so it is positive (B1). In context: days with a higher maximum temperature tend to have higher ice cream sales (B1). Note that this association alone does not show that the temperature causes the extra sales.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two words are wanted: one about how tight the points are, one about the direction.","Then the second mark is for saying it again using the names of the variables.","Look first at how tightly the points cluster, then at whether the line rises or falls."],"codes":["2.2"]},{"q":"A data set has Q₁ = 24 and Q₃ = 36. An outlier is defined as any value more than 1.5 × IQR below Q₁ or more than 1.5 × IQR above Q₃. Find the two outlier boundaries.","a":"6 and 54","sol":"IQR = Q₃ − Q₁ = 36 − 24 = 12 (B1). 1.5 × IQR = 1.5 × 12 = 18. Lower boundary = 24 − 18 = 6 (M1). Upper boundary = 36 + 18 = 54 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["You need the interquartile range before you can use the rule.","Work out 1.5 × IQR once, then use it twice.","IQR = 36 − 24."],"codes":["2.4"]},{"q":"A histogram is drawn for the volumes, v litres, of 200 parcels. The bar for the class 5 ≤ v < 10 is 1 cm wide and 4 cm tall, and this class contains 32 parcels. The bar for the class 10 ≤ v < 25 is 3 cm wide and 5 cm tall. Estimate the number of parcels with a volume between 10 and 20 litres.","a":"80 parcels","sol":"Area of the 5 ≤ v < 10 bar = 1 × 4 = 4 cm², representing 32 parcels, so frequency = k × area with k = 32 ÷ 4 = 8 parcels per cm² (M1 A1). Area of the 10 ≤ v < 25 bar = 3 × 5 = 15 cm², so the frequency of that class = 8 × 15 = 120 (M1). The interval 10 ≤ v < 20 is 10 of the 15 litres of that class, so, assuming an even spread, the estimate is (10/15) × 120 = 80 parcels (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["In a histogram it is area, not height, that carries the frequency.","Use the class whose frequency you know to find how many parcels one square centimetre represents.","Area of the first bar = 1 × 4 = 4 cm²."],"codes":["2.1"]},{"q":"The masses, w grams, of 80 eggs are grouped: 40 ≤ w < 50, frequency 8; 50 ≤ w < 55, frequency 20; 55 ≤ w < 60, frequency 30; 60 ≤ w < 70, frequency 22. Use linear interpolation to estimate the median mass.","a":"57 g","sol":"Cumulative frequencies: 8, 28, 58, 80. The median is the 40th value (n/2 = 40), which lies in the class 55 ≤ w < 60 because the cumulative frequency runs from 28 to 58 across it (M1). Median = 55 + ((40 − 28)/30) × 5 (M1) = 55 + (12/30) × 5 = 55 + 2 = 57 g (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Build the running totals of the frequencies first.","Find which class the 40th value falls in, then go a fraction of the way into it.","Cumulative frequencies are 8, 28, 58, 80."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"A variable x is coded using y = (x − 30)/4. For the 25 coded values, Σy = 60 and Σy² = 400. Find the mean and the standard deviation of x.","a":"Mean 39.6, standard deviation 12.8","sol":"ȳ = Σy/n = 60/25 = 2.4 (B1). Reversing the coding, x = 4y + 30, so x̄ = 4 × 2.4 + 30 = 39.6 (M1 A1). Syy = Σy² − (Σy)²/n = 400 − 60²/25 = 400 − 144 = 256, so the standard deviation of y = √(256/25) = 3.2 (M1). Subtracting 30 does not change the spread and dividing by 4 divides it by 4, so the standard deviation of x = 4 × 3.2 = 12.8 (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Work entirely in y first, then undo the coding at the end.","The mean must be un-coded with both the ×4 and the +30; the spread only feels the ×4.","ȳ = 60/25."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"The regression line of yield y, in kg, on fertiliser x, in g per m², for a set of plots is y = 4.2 + 0.35x. The data were collected for 10 ≤ x ≤ 50. Interpret the value 0.35, and explain why the equation should not be used to estimate the yield when x = 90.","a":"Each extra 1 g per m² of fertiliser is associated with 0.35 kg more yield; x = 90 lies outside 10 ≤ x ≤ 50, so using the line there is extrapolation and unreliable","sol":"The gradient of a regression line is the change in the response variable for a one-unit increase in the explanatory variable, so each extra 1 g per m² of fertiliser is associated with an extra 0.35 kg of yield (B1 for the rate, B1 for putting it in context). The data only cover 10 ≤ x ≤ 50, and x = 90 lies well outside that range, so a prediction there is extrapolation: there is no evidence that the linear relationship continues that far (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A gradient is always a rate — so many units of one thing per unit of the other.","Give the rate with its units, then compare 90 with the values of x the line was built from.","A gradient is a rate: read the units off the two axes."],"codes":["2.2"]},{"q":"The heights, in cm, of 11 plants are 12, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20, 22, 23, 25, 27, 46. An outlier is a value more than 1.5 × IQR above Q₃ or below Q₁. Show that 46 is an outlier, and find the effect on the mean of removing it.","a":"46 > 38.5, so it is an outlier; removing it lowers the mean from 22.1 cm to 19.7 cm","sol":"n = 11, so the median is the 6th value, 20. n/4 = 2.75, which is not a whole number, so round up: Q₁ is the 3rd value, 16. 3n/4 = 8.25, so round up: Q₃ is the 9th value, 25 (M1). IQR = 25 − 16 = 9 and 1.5 × IQR = 13.5, so the upper boundary = 25 + 13.5 = 38.5 (M1). 46 > 38.5, so 46 is an outlier (A1). Σx = 243, so the mean of all 11 heights = 243/11 = 22.09… = 22.1 cm. Removing 46 gives Σx = 197 over 10 plants, so the new mean = 19.7 cm (M1). The mean therefore falls by about 2.4 cm (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Find the quartiles by position first — with n = 11 neither quartile position is a whole number.","Apply the stated rule to get the upper boundary, then compare, then recompute the mean with the value gone.","n/4 = 2.75, so Q₁ is the 3rd value in the list."],"codes":["2.3","2.4"]},{"q":"A cumulative frequency diagram for the annual salaries of 120 employees gives Q₁ = £21 000, median = £26 000 and Q₃ = £34 000. State, with a supporting calculation, what this tells you about the skew of the distribution.","a":"Positive skew, because Q₃ − Q₂ = £8000 is greater than Q₂ − Q₁ = £5000","sol":"Q₂ − Q₁ = 26 000 − 21 000 = £5000 and Q₃ − Q₂ = 34 000 − 26 000 = £8000 (M1). Since Q₃ − Q₂ > Q₂ − Q₁, the upper half of the middle 50% of salaries is more spread out than the lower half, so the distribution has positive skew (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Skew is judged from how the median sits between the two quartiles.","Compare the two gaps either side of the median.","Q₂ − Q₁ = 26 000 − 21 000."],"codes":["2.1"]},{"q":"The mass, m grams, of an organism t days after hatching is modelled by m = a tⁿ. Values of log₁₀ m are plotted against log₁₀ t for 2 ≤ t ≤ 20 and lie close to a straight line of gradient 1.5 with intercept 0.4 on the vertical axis. Find n and a, giving a to 3 significant figures, and use the model to estimate the mass when t = 12.","a":"n = 1.5, a = 2.51 (3 s.f.), m ≈ 104 g","sol":"m = a tⁿ, so log₁₀ m = log₁₀ a + n log₁₀ t (M1). Comparing with the plotted straight line, the gradient is n, so n = 1.5 (B1), and the intercept is log₁₀ a = 0.4, so a = 10^0.4 = 2.5118… = 2.51 to 3 s.f. (A1). t = 12 lies inside 2 ≤ t ≤ 20, so the prediction is interpolation and is reasonable (B1). log₁₀ m = 0.4 + 1.5 × log₁₀ 12 = 0.4 + 1.5 × 1.07918 = 2.01877 (M1), so m = 10^2.01877 = 104.4… ≈ 104 g (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["A power law becomes a straight line when both variables are logged.","Match the gradient and the intercept of the plotted line to the two parts of your logged equation.","Take logs of both sides: log₁₀ m = log₁₀ a + n log₁₀ t."],"codes":["2.2"]},{"q":"For a sample of 30 reaction times, x seconds, Σx = 21.0 and Σx² = 15.54. An outlier is defined as a value more than 3 standard deviations from the mean. Determine whether a recorded time of 1.55 s is an outlier.","a":"Yes: x̄ + 3σ = 1.20 s (3 s.f.) and 1.55 > 1.20","sol":"x̄ = 21.0/30 = 0.7 s (B1). Sₓₓ = Σx² − (Σx)²/n = 15.54 − 21.0²/30 = 15.54 − 14.7 = 0.84 (M1). σ = √(Sₓₓ/n) = √(0.84/30) = √0.028 = 0.16733… (A1). The upper boundary is x̄ + 3σ = 0.7 + 3 × 0.16733… = 1.2020 = 1.20 s to 3 s.f. (M1). Since 1.55 > 1.20, the recorded time is an outlier under this rule (A1). (The lower boundary, x̄ − 3σ = 0.198 s, is not the relevant one here.)","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The rule here is the mean-and-standard-deviation one, not the quartile one.","You have to build the standard deviation from the totals before you can use the rule.","x̄ = 21.0/30."],"codes":["2.3","2.4"]},{"q":"A coach records the weekly training distances of two squads. The 12 runners in squad A have mean distance 15 km with standard deviation 4 km; the 8 runners in squad B have mean 20 km with standard deviation 3 km. Find the mean and the standard deviation of the distances of all 20 runners, giving the standard deviation to 3 significant figures.","a":"Mean 17 km, standard deviation 4.38 km (3 s.f.)","sol":"Standard deviations cannot be averaged, so rebuild the totals squad by squad. Squad A: Σx = 12 × 15 = 180, and since σ² = Sₓₓ/n, Sₓₓ = 12 × 4² = 192, so Σx² = Sₓₓ + (Σx)²/n = 192 + 180²/12 = 2892 (M1 A1). Squad B: Σx = 8 × 20 = 160, Sₓₓ = 8 × 3² = 72, so Σx² = 72 + 160²/8 = 3272 (A1). For all 20 runners Σx = 180 + 160 = 340, so the mean = 340/20 = 17 km (M1). Σx² = 2892 + 3272 = 6164, so Sₓₓ = 6164 − 340²/20 = 6164 − 5780 = 384 (M1). σ = √(384/20) = √19.2 = 4.3817… = 4.38 km to 3 s.f. (A1). The combined spread is wider than either squad's because the two squad means differ.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two standard deviations cannot be averaged, so you must get behind them to something that can be added.","For each squad recover Σx from the mean and Sₓₓ from nσ², then Σx² from Sₓₓ = Σx² − (Σx)²/n; add the two squads' totals and start again.","For squad A, Σx = 12 × 15."],"codes":["2.3"]},{"q":"A histogram shows the lengths, l mm, of 150 leaves. The bar for 60 ≤ l < 80 is 2 cm wide and 3.5 cm tall and represents 42 leaves; the bar for 80 ≤ l < 90 is 1 cm wide and 6 cm tall. Given that 30 leaves have l < 60, use linear interpolation to estimate the median length, to 3 significant figures.","a":"80.8 mm (3 s.f.)","sol":"Area of the 60 ≤ l < 80 bar = 2 × 3.5 = 7 cm², representing 42 leaves, so k = 42 ÷ 7 = 6 leaves per cm² (M1 A1). Area of the 80 ≤ l < 90 bar = 1 × 6 = 6 cm², so its frequency = 6 × 6 = 36 (M1). Cumulative frequencies: 30 below 60 mm, 30 + 42 = 72 below 80 mm, 72 + 36 = 108 below 90 mm. The median is the 75th value (n/2 = 75), which lies in 80 ≤ l < 90 (M1). Median = 80 + ((75 − 72)/36) × 10 = 80 + 0.8333… = 80.83… = 80.8 mm to 3 s.f. (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The vertical scale is unknown, so start from the bar whose frequency you are told.","Convert the second bar's area into a frequency, build the cumulative frequencies, then interpolate.","Area of the first bar = 2 × 3.5 = 7 cm²."],"codes":["2.1","2.3"]},{"q":"The times, t minutes, of 120 visits to a museum are grouped: 0 ≤ t < 10, frequency 18; 10 ≤ t < 20, frequency p; 20 ≤ t < 40, frequency q; 40 ≤ t < 60, frequency 14. Linear interpolation gives the median time as 21.6 minutes. Find p and q.","a":"p = 38, q = 50","sol":"The frequencies total 120, so 18 + p + q + 14 = 120, giving p + q = 88 (B1). The median is the 60th value (n/2 = 60) and 21.6 lies in the class 20 ≤ t < 40, whose cumulative frequency starts at 18 + p, so linear interpolation gives 20 + ((60 − (18 + p))/q) × 20 = 21.6 (M1). Hence ((42 − p)/q) × 20 = 1.6, so 42 − p = 0.08q (A1). Substituting p = 88 − q gives 42 − 88 + q = 0.08q, so 0.92q = 46 (M1) and q = 50 (A1), giving p = 88 − 50 = 38 (A1). Check: the cumulative frequency before the median class is 18 + 38 = 56, and 20 + ((60 − 56)/50) × 20 = 21.6 ✓.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two unknown frequencies need two facts about them, and the median is only one of those facts.","Write the interpolation formula for the median class leaving p and q as letters, then use the total frequency to eliminate one of them.","18 + p + q + 14 = 120."],"codes":["2.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A histogram class of width 4 has frequency 30. What is the frequency density?","a":"7.5"},{"q":"A regression line is y = 8 − 1.2x, fitted from data with 3 ≤ x ≤ 15. What does the −1.2 mean?","a":"y falls by 1.2 for every increase of 1 in x"},{"q":"For n = 5 values, Σx = 30 and Σx² = 200. Find Sₓₓ.","a":"20"},{"q":"Q₁ = 14 and Q₃ = 26. Using the 1.5 × IQR rule, what is the upper outlier boundary?","a":"44"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Area of a bar in a histogram","back":"The frequency of that class. Height is frequency density, so frequency = k × area, where k is found from a class whose frequency is known.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"Frequency density","back":"Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. It is the quantity plotted on the vertical axis of a histogram.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"Five-number summary shown by a box plot","back":"Lowest value that is not an outlier, Q₁, median, Q₃, highest value that is not an outlier. Outliers are plotted separately as crosses.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"Median and quartiles from a cumulative frequency diagram of n values","back":"Read across from n/2 for the median, from n/4 for Q₁ and from 3n/4 for Q₃, then down to the horizontal axis.","code":"2.1","open":false},{"front":"Axes of a scatter diagram","back":"The explanatory (independent) variable on the horizontal axis; the response (dependent) variable on the vertical axis.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"Interpolation and extrapolation","back":"Interpolation predicts inside the range of the explanatory variable and is reliable; extrapolation predicts outside it and is unreliable, because there is no evidence the linear relationship continues.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"Correlation and causation","back":"A linear association can arise because a third variable affects both, or by chance. Only a controlled experiment can establish that one variable causes the change in the other.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"Linear form of y = axⁿ","back":"log y = log a + n log x, so plotting log y against log x gives a straight line of gradient n and intercept log a.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"Linear form of y = kbˣ","back":"log y = log k + x log b, so plotting log y against x gives a straight line of gradient log b and intercept log k.","code":"2.2","open":false},{"front":"Sₓₓ","back":"Sₓₓ = Σ(x − x̄)² = Σx² − (Σx)²/n. The second form is the one to use with summary statistics.","code":"2.3","open":false},{"front":"Standard deviation and variance from Sₓₓ","back":"Variance = Sₓₓ/n and σ = √(Sₓₓ/n). The divisor n − 1, giving s = √(Sₓₓ/(n − 1)), is also accepted at A level.","code":"2.3","open":false},{"front":"Effect of the coding y = (x − a)/b on the mean and standard deviation","back":"x̄ = bȳ + a, and the standard deviation of x is b times the standard deviation of y. Subtracting a shifts the mean but not the spread.","code":"2.3","open":false},{"front":"Linear interpolation for the median of grouped data","back":"Median = L + ((n/2 − F)/f) × w, where L is the lower class boundary, F the cumulative frequency before the class, f the class frequency and w the class width.","code":"2.3","open":false},{"front":"The two outlier rules used in exam questions","back":"Below Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR or above Q₃ + 1.5 × IQR; or more than 2 or 3 standard deviations from the mean. The question always states which rule to use.","code":"2.4","open":false},{"front":"Ways of dealing with an outlier when cleaning a data set","back":"Remove it and recalculate if it is a recording or measurement error; keep it and comment on it if it is a genuine extreme value; investigate the original record before deciding.","code":"2.4","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Mean: x̄ = Σx/n, or x̄ = Σfx/Σf from a frequency table. Not in the formulae booklet — memorise it.","Sₓₓ = Σ(x − x̄)² = Σx² − (Σx)²/n, and standard deviation = √(Sₓₓ/n). These two are printed in the Statistics section of the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet, so you may quote them; every other result on this sheet must come from memory.","Variance = Sₓₓ/n = Σx²/n − x̄². The Σx²/n − x̄² form is not printed — memorise it.","The spreadsheet form s = √(Sₓₓ/(n − 1)) is accepted as an alternative to √(Sₓₓ/n).","Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width; in a histogram, frequency = k × area of bar, with k found from a class of known frequency.","Interquartile range: IQR = Q₃ − Q₁. Interpercentile range, e.g. the 10% to 90% range = P₉₀ − P₁₀.","Quartile positions for n discrete values: Q₁ at n/4, Q₂ at n/2, Q₃ at 3n/4 — take the midpoint of that value and the next if the position is a whole number, otherwise round the position up.","Linear interpolation: median = L + ((n/2 − F)/f) × w; kth percentile = L + ((kn/100 − F)/f) × w, where L is the lower class boundary, F the cumulative frequency before the class, f the class frequency and w the class width.","Coding y = (x − a)/b: x̄ = bȳ + a, and the standard deviation of x = b × (standard deviation of y).","Outliers: below Q₁ − 1.5 × IQR or above Q₃ + 1.5 × IQR; or outside x̄ ± 2σ or x̄ ± 3σ. The rule to use is always given in the question.","Reducing to linear form: y = axⁿ gives log y = log a + n log x; y = kbˣ gives log y = log k + x log b.","Regression line y = a + bx: b is the change in y for a one-unit increase in x, and a is the predicted y at x = 0. Only predict for x inside the range of the data."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where the marks fall","items":["Write the substitution into Sₓₓ = Σx² − (Σx)²/n even when your calculator gives σ directly: the substitution is the M mark, and a bare answer scores only what the scheme allows for the answer alone.","Interpretation questions carry a mark for the numerical statement and a separate mark for saying it in the words of the context, with units — 'strong negative correlation' alone is half an answer.","When a question gives an outlier rule, quote it, work out both boundaries, and then write a sentence comparing the value with the boundary. The conclusion is a mark of its own.","For histograms with no vertical scale, always find the scaling constant k from the class you are told the frequency of before touching any other bar.","Any estimate from grouped data or from a histogram assumes the values are spread evenly within each class; say so if the question asks you to comment on the accuracy.","Comparing two distributions needs both a location comparison (medians or means) and a spread comparison (IQR, range or standard deviation), each stated in context."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:24:48.133Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Mathematically clean: all 16 questions, both 4-item check sets and all five worked examples recompute exactly in node (population divisor throughout, Pearson quartile convention, both outlier rules, both interpolations, both log-linear fits), and the three unscaled histograms are internally consistent in frequency density (0.8, 1.6 and 0.6 units per cm), so the only edits needed are the booklet claim on the formulae sheet, the one-mark tally on question 14, and the final line of example 4."}}},"S3":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"S3","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Probability is the foundation the whole of the Statistics section is built on: the binomial and normal distributions in S4, and every hypothesis test in S5, are statements about probability, so a shaky rule here costs marks on Paper 3 twice over. Examiners report the same losses year after year — adding probabilities that are not mutually exclusive, multiplying probabilities that are not independent, forgetting that without replacement changes the second draw, dividing a conditional probability the wrong way round, and asserting independence instead of testing it. The room also carries the modelling strand: a Paper 3 question will hand you a model, ask you to state the assumption behind it, judge whether it is reasonable in that context and say what a more realistic assumption would do to the answer, and those AO3 marks are given for saying so in a sentence, not for more arithmetic.","idea":[{"code":"3.1","h":"Mutually exclusive, independent, and probability as area","t":"Two events are mutually exclusive if they cannot both happen: their circles on a Venn diagram do not overlap, P(A ∩ B) = 0, and so P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B). Two events are independent if neither one changes the probability of the other, which is tested by P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B), or equivalently by P(A | B) = P(A) and P(B | A) = P(B). These are different ideas and neither implies the other: two events with non-zero probabilities that are mutually exclusive are as far from independent as they can be, because knowing one has happened makes the other impossible. Exam questions state independence to let you multiply, or give you enough probabilities that you must test it and state a conclusion; a test with no comparison line and no conclusion scores no accuracy mark. Set notation — A ∩ B, A ∪ B, A′ — is examinable in its own right, so read it and write it fluently. The same ideas carry over to a continuous distribution, where probability is the area under the graph: the total area is 1, the probability of an interval is the area over it, and a single value has no area at all, so P(X = a) = 0 and P(X ≤ a) = P(X < a). No formal work with probability density functions is required."},{"code":"3.2","h":"Conditional probability and the rules that follow from it","t":"P(A | B) is the probability of A given that B has happened: you throw away everything outside B and ask what fraction of B is also A, so P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B). Rearranged, this is the multiplication rule P(A ∩ B) = P(B) P(A | B) = P(A) P(B | A), which is exactly what a tree diagram does when you multiply along a path. The other two rules you need constantly are the complement P(A′) = 1 − P(A) and the addition rule P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B), whose subtraction stops the overlap being counted twice. Read the information into a picture before doing any algebra: a Venn diagram for overlapping events (fill the intersection first, then subtract to get the parts of A and B outside it, since P(A′ ∩ B) = P(B) − P(A ∩ B)), a tree diagram for a sequence, especially without replacement, and a two-way table when two categorical variables are counted. The hardest version reverses the tree — you are told what happened second and asked about the first — and the method is always the same: the intersection on top, and on the bottom the total probability of the event you are given, found by adding every path that produces it."},{"code":"3.3","h":"Modelling with probability and criticising the assumptions","t":"A probability model is a set of assumptions: that a coin or die is fair, that each outcome is equally likely, that trials are independent, that the probability stays the same from trial to trial. Those assumptions are what allow you to write down a number in the first place, and an AO3 question will ask you to name one, judge it and improve it. The standard evidence is relative frequency: if a die is rolled a large number of times and a six comes up on a third of them, the assumption of fairness does not survive, and the better model replaces 1/6 with the observed 1/3. The mark for the criticism is given for three things together — stating the assumption in the words of the context, saying whether it is reasonable and why, and saying what direction a more realistic assumption would push the answer in. Independence is the assumption most often broken in real contexts: train delays, rainy days and illnesses cluster, so treating repeated days as independent usually understates the chance of a run of them and overstates the chance of at least one isolated event. Say so in a sentence about the context, not in the abstract."}],"examples":[{"title":"Independent events: intersection then union","setup":"The events A and B are independent, with P(A) = 0.4 and P(B) = 0.25. Find P(A ∩ B) and P(A ∪ B).","steps":["Independence gives the multiplication rule, so P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B) = 0.4 × 0.25 = 0.1 — this is the line that earns the method mark.","For the union, the addition rule P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B) removes the overlap that would otherwise be counted twice.","Substituting all three probabilities: P(A ∪ B) = 0.4 + 0.25 − 0.1.","Working that out, P(A ∪ B) = 0.55, sensibly less than P(A) + P(B) because of the overlap."],"cues":["Which rule does the word 'independent' unlock?","Why is P(A) + P(B) on its own wrong here?","Which three probabilities does the addition rule need?","What does that arithmetic come to, and should it be bigger or smaller than P(A) + P(B)?"]},{"title":"Conditional probability, then a test for independence","setup":"The events A and B are such that P(A) = 0.6, P(B) = 0.5 and P(A ∩ B) = 0.3. Find P(A | B) and determine whether A and B are independent.","steps":["Conditioning on B throws away everything outside B, so P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) ÷ P(B) — intersection on top, the event you are given underneath.","Substituting the given values: P(A | B) = 0.3 ÷ 0.5 = 0.6.","The independence test compares that with the unconditional probability, and P(A) = 0.6 as well.","Since P(A | B) = P(A), knowing that B has happened has not changed the probability of A, so A and B are independent."],"cues":["Which formula links a conditional probability to an intersection?","Which two of the three given values does that formula use?","What would P(A | B) have to equal if B told you nothing about A?","So what is the conclusion, and what is the reason for it?"]},{"title":"Without replacement: 'at least one' by the complement","setup":"A bag contains 3 red counters and 5 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Find the probability that at least one of the counters is red.","steps":["'At least one red' covers three separate tree paths, but its complement, 'both blue', is a single path, so deal with that instead.","On the first draw there are 8 counters of which 5 are blue, giving a probability of 5/8.","One blue counter has now gone, leaving 4 blue out of 7, so P(both blue) = 5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56 = 5/14.","P(at least one red) = 1 − 5/14 = 9/14."],"cues":["Which single event is much easier to count than 'at least one red'?","How many counters are in the bag when the first one is taken?","What is different about the bag on the second draw?","How do you get from the probability of both blue to what was asked?"]},{"title":"Criticising a model: is the spinner fair?","setup":"A student models the outcome of spinning a five-sided spinner labelled 1 to 5 by assuming that each number is equally likely, and so takes P(5) = 0.2. In a trial the spinner is spun 250 times and lands on 5 on 80 of those spins. Comment on the student's assumption, and find the probability of two 5s in a row under a more realistic model.","steps":["Work out what the trial actually says: the relative frequency of a 5 is 80 ÷ 250 = 0.32.","That is well above the 0.2 the model assumes, and 250 spins is a long enough run for the gap to be evidence rather than chance, so the spinner does not look fair and the assumption of equally likely outcomes is not reasonable.","A better model takes the value the data gives, P(5) = 0.32, rather than the symmetry of the shape.","Assuming successive spins are independent of one another, P(two 5s in a row) = 0.32 × 0.32 = 0.1024, well above the 0.04 the fair model would predict."],"cues":["What does the evidence itself say about how often a 5 comes up?","How does that compare with the assumed probability, and is the trial long enough to trust?","Which number should replace 0.2 in a better model?","What further assumption do you need before you may multiply two spins together?"]},{"title":"A continuous distribution: probability as area","setup":"The continuous random variable X has a distribution whose graph is symmetrical about x = 30, and the total area under the graph is 1. You are told that P(X < 22) = 0.09. Find P(22 ≤ X ≤ 38).","steps":["For a continuous distribution the probability of an interval is the area under the graph over that interval, and all of the area together comes to 1.","The line of symmetry sits 8 above 22 and 8 below 38, so the upper tail mirrors the lower one and P(X > 38) = 0.09.","The two tails together therefore account for 0.09 + 0.09 = 0.18 of the area.","Everything else lies between, so P(22 ≤ X ≤ 38) = 1 − 0.18 = 0.82."],"cues":["What does an area under this graph represent?","Where does the region below 22 get mirrored to?","How much area lies outside the interval you want altogether?","How do you turn the area outside into the area inside?"]}],"check":[{"q":"P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.3 and P(A ∪ B) = 0.65. Find P(A ∩ B).","a":"0.15"},{"q":"Using those same values, are A and B independent? Give your reason.","a":"Yes: P(A) P(B) = 0.5 × 0.3 = 0.15 = P(A ∩ B)"},{"q":"The events A and B are mutually exclusive and P(B) > 0. Write down P(A | B).","a":"0, because A cannot happen once B has"},{"q":"X is a continuous random variable. What is P(X = 4), and why?","a":"0 — a single value has no area under the graph"}]},"room":{"facts":["P(A′) = 1 − P(A)","P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)","Mutually exclusive: P(A ∩ B) = 0, so P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B)","Independent: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B), equivalently P(A | B) = P(A) and P(B | A) = P(B)","P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B), and rearranged P(A ∩ B) = P(B) P(A | B) = P(A) P(B | A)","On a tree diagram multiply along a path and add between paths; for a continuous distribution the area under the graph is the probability, the total area is 1 and P(X = a) = 0"],"questions":[{"q":"A factory component is given two checks. It fails the visual check with probability 0.15 and, independently of that, it fails the pressure check with probability 0.08. Find the probability that the component fails at least one of the two checks.","a":"0.218","sol":"Let V be 'fails the visual check' and W be 'fails the pressure check', so P(V) = 0.15 and P(W) = 0.08. The checks are independent, so P(V ∩ W) = P(V) P(W) = 0.15 × 0.08 = 0.012 (M1 for the product). 'At least one' is the union, so P(V ∪ W) = P(V) + P(W) − P(V ∩ W) (M1 for the addition rule with their intersection subtracted). P(V ∪ W) = 0.15 + 0.08 − 0.012 = 0.218 (A1 cao). Equivalently 1 − 0.85 × 0.92 = 1 − 0.782 = 0.218.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["'Independently' fixes the probability that both checks are failed, which is the piece the union rule is missing.","Work out the probability of failing both first, then use the rule for 'one or the other or both'.","The answer is a single decimal, and it must be smaller than 0.15 + 0.08 because the overlap is only counted once."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"The events C and D are mutually exclusive, with P(C) = 0.35 and P(D) = 0.2. Find P(C′ ∩ D′).","a":"0.45","sol":"Mutually exclusive means P(C ∩ D) = 0, so the addition rule loses its subtraction: P(C ∪ D) = 0.35 + 0.2 = 0.55 (M1 for adding with nothing subtracted). C′ ∩ D′ is the region of the Venn diagram outside both circles, which is the complement of C ∪ D, so P(C′ ∩ D′) = 1 − P(C ∪ D) (M1 for 1 − their union). = 1 − 0.55 = 0.45 (A1 cao).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Mutually exclusive events cannot overlap, so one term of the addition rule is already known.","Sketch the Venn diagram and shade the part that lies in neither circle; that region is the complement of something you can work out.","The answer is the probability left over outside both events, so it is less than a half."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"Two events A and B satisfy P(A ∩ B) = 0.18 and P(B) = 0.45. Find P(A | B).","a":"0.4","sol":"P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) ÷ P(B) — the intersection on top, the event you are given underneath (M1 for the correct quotient). = 0.18 ÷ 0.45 = 0.4 (A1 cao).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Conditioning on B means only the outcomes inside B still count, so B becomes the new whole.","One of the two given probabilities goes on top and the other underneath — the one you are told has happened goes on the bottom.","Your answer is a probability between 0 and 1, and it must be larger than P(A ∩ B)."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"For two events A and B, P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4 and P(A ∪ B) = 0.75. Find P(A ∩ B).","a":"0.15","sol":"Addition rule: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B), so P(A ∩ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∪ B) (M1 for the rearrangement). = 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.75 = 0.15 (A1 cao).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The overlap is exactly the quantity the addition rule subtracts to stop double counting.","Rearrange the addition rule so that the intersection is the subject, then substitute.","As a check, the answer cannot be bigger than the smaller of the two individual probabilities."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"Three fair coins are tossed. Find the probability of getting at least one head.","a":"7/8","sol":"The complement of 'at least one head' is 'no heads at all', that is three tails. The tosses are independent, so P(TTT) = 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8 (M1 for the product of three independent probabilities). P(at least one head) = 1 − P(no heads) = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8 (A1 cao).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Listing every outcome containing a head is slow; there is only one outcome that contains none.","The tosses do not affect one another, so probabilities along a sequence multiply.","Take the result away from certainty; the answer is a fraction very close to 1."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"A bag contains 3 red counters and 5 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Find the probability that exactly one of the two counters is red.","a":"15/28","sol":"Draw a tree diagram. P(red then blue) = 3/8 × 5/7 = 15/56 (M1 for a correct product with the second denominator reduced to 7). P(blue then red) = 5/8 × 3/7 = 15/56. 'Exactly one red' covers both orders, so add the two paths: P = 15/56 + 15/56 = 30/56 (M1 for adding two correct paths). = 15/28 (A1 cao). Check: P(both red) = 3/28, P(both blue) = 10/28, and 3/28 + 10/28 + 15/28 = 1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["'Exactly one' does not tell you which of the two draws produced the red counter.","There are two different routes through the tree that end with one of each colour — work out both of them.","Combine the routes into a single fraction; it comes out a little over a half."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"At a garden centre, 40% of the customers buy compost and 25% buy plant food, and 8% of the customers buy both. A customer is chosen at random. Find the probability that the customer buys plant food, given that the customer buys compost, and hence state, with a reason, whether buying compost and buying plant food are independent.","a":"P(F | C) = 0.2, so the two events are not independent","sol":"Let C be 'buys compost' and F be 'buys plant food', so P(C) = 0.4, P(F) = 0.25 and P(C ∩ F) = 0.08. P(F | C) = P(C ∩ F) ÷ P(C) (M1 for the formula) = 0.08 ÷ 0.4 = 0.2 (A1). Independence test: the two events are independent exactly when P(F | C) = P(F) (M1 for comparing the conditional probability with P(F); equivalently comparing P(C) P(F) = 0.4 × 0.25 = 0.1 with P(C ∩ F) = 0.08). Here P(F | C) = 0.2 but P(F) = 0.25, and 0.2 ≠ 0.25, so knowing that a customer buys compost changes the probability that they buy plant food: the two events are not independent (A1 cso, with the comparison and the conclusion both seen).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The percentages are probabilities: write down P(C), P(F) and P(C ∩ F) before touching any rule.","The event you are told has happened is the one that goes underneath in the conditional probability.","Then set the conditional probability against the probability of buying plant food on its own; the reason is that comparison plus a one-line conclusion, and a bare verdict scores nothing."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"Of 200 students, 120 study Mathematics, 80 study Physics and 50 study both. A student is chosen at random. Find the probability that the student studies Physics, given that the student studies Mathematics.","a":"5/12","sol":"Put the counts into a two-way table or a Venn diagram: 50 study both, so 70 study Mathematics only, 30 study Physics only and 50 study neither (B1 for a correct structure with the overlap placed first). The word 'given' restricts the sample to the 120 students who study Mathematics (M1). P(Physics | Mathematics) = 50/120 = 5/12 (A1 cao). Equivalently P(P ∩ M) ÷ P(M) = (50/200) ÷ (120/200) = 5/12.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["You already know the student studies Mathematics, so most of the 200 are no longer in the running.","Count only within the group named after the word 'given', and ask what fraction of it also studies Physics.","The answer is a fraction of the Mathematics group alone, so it comes out below a half."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled. Event A is 'the total is 7' and event B is 'the first die shows a 3'. Determine whether A and B are independent.","a":"Independent, because P(A ∩ B) = 1/36 = P(A) × P(B)","sol":"There are 36 equally likely outcomes. A occurs for (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2) and (6,1), so P(A) = 6/36 = 1/6 (B1). The first die shows a 3 in 6 of the 36 outcomes, so P(B) = 6/36 = 1/6 (B1). For A ∩ B the first die is 3 and the total is 7, so the second must be 4 — one outcome only, and P(A ∩ B) = 1/36 (M1). P(A) P(B) = 1/6 × 1/6 = 1/36 = P(A ∩ B), so A and B are independent (A1 cso).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Independence has a test rather than an intuition, and the test compares a product with an intersection.","Work out all three probabilities by counting outcomes out of the 36 equally likely pairs.","Finish with the comparison written out and a conclusion in words, not three probabilities left on the page."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"A drawing pin is thrown 200 times and lands point up on 130 of those throws. A student's model assumes the pin is equally likely to land point up as point down, and uses that model to find the probability that two throws both land point up. Criticise the assumption and find the probability of two point-up throws under a more realistic model.","a":"The assumption is unreasonable; using P(point up) = 0.65 gives 0.4225 rather than 0.25","sol":"Relative frequency of point up = 130 ÷ 200 = 0.65 (M1). That is a long way from the assumed 0.5, and 200 throws is a large enough trial for the gap to be evidence rather than chance; a drawing pin is not symmetrical, so there is no reason to expect the two outcomes to be equally likely. The assumption is therefore not reasonable (B1 for a criticism with a reason in context). A better model takes P(point up) = 0.65 from the data, and assuming the throws are independent, P(both point up) = 0.65 × 0.65 = 0.4225 (M1 for squaring their revised probability, A1 cao). The fair model would give 0.5 × 0.5 = 0.25, so the more realistic assumption raises the probability (B1 for the comparison).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The data is given for a reason: it tells you how this pin actually behaves, whatever the model assumed.","Compare the relative frequency from the trial with the probability the model assumes, then rebuild the model on the observed value.","You need a judgement with a reason in context, and then a single multiplication using the revised probability."],"codes":["3.3"]},{"q":"The continuous random variable Y has a distribution whose graph is symmetrical about y = 12, and the total area under the graph is 1. Given that P(9 ≤ Y ≤ 15) = 0.64, find P(Y > 15).","a":"0.18","sol":"For a continuous distribution the probability of an interval is the area under the graph over that interval, and the total area is 1, so the two regions outside the interval have probability 1 − 0.64 = 0.36 (M1 for subtracting the given probability from 1). Since 15 − 12 = 12 − 9 = 3, the interval is placed symmetrically about the line of symmetry, so the region above 15 and the region below 9 are mirror images and have equal probability (M1 for halving by symmetry). P(Y > 15) = 0.36 ÷ 2 = 0.18 (A1 cao). Note that P(Y = 15) = 0, so P(Y > 15) = P(Y ≥ 15).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A probability is an area here, and all the area under the graph adds up to 1.","Account for everything outside the given interval first, then ask how that leftover area is shared between the two ends.","Compare the distance from 12 to each end of the interval before you split the leftover area; the answer is a decimal well below 0.5."],"codes":["3.1"]},{"q":"The events A and B are independent. P(A) = 0.3 and P(A ∪ B) = 0.58. Find P(B).","a":"0.4","sol":"Let P(B) = p. Independence gives P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B) = 0.3p (M1 for writing the intersection in terms of p). Addition rule: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B), so 0.58 = 0.3 + p − 0.3p (M1 for a correct equation in p). Then 0.58 − 0.3 = 0.7p, so 0.28 = 0.7p (dM1 for collecting and solving) and p = 0.4 (A1 cao). Check: 0.3 + 0.4 − 0.12 = 0.58.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["You cannot write the overlap down directly, but independence expresses it in terms of the unknown.","Call the unknown probability p and turn the addition rule into an equation in p.","What you end up solving is a linear equation, and the root has to be a probability between 0 and 1."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"A box contains 4 red sweets and 6 green sweets. Two sweets are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Given that the second sweet taken is green, find the probability that the first sweet taken was red.","a":"4/9","sol":"Let R1 be 'the first sweet is red' and G2 be 'the second sweet is green'. Along the tree, P(R1 ∩ G2) = 4/10 × 6/9 = 24/90 (M1 for a correct product with the reduced second denominator). P(G1 ∩ G2) = 6/10 × 5/9 = 30/90 (M1 for the other path that ends in green). These are the only two ways the second sweet can be green, so P(G2) = 24/90 + 30/90 = 54/90 = 3/5 (A1). Conditional formula the right way round: P(R1 | G2) = P(R1 ∩ G2) ÷ P(G2) (M1) = (24/90) ÷ (54/90) = 24/54 = 4/9 (A1 cao).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The information you are given is about the second draw but the question asks about the first, so the conditioning runs backwards along the tree.","The denominator is the probability of the event you were told happened — find it by adding every path that ends the way you were told.","Your last line is one path divided by that total, and the answer is a fraction a little under a half."],"codes":["3.2"]},{"q":"A bag contains n red counters and 4 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. The probability that both counters are red is 1/3. Show that n² − 5n − 6 = 0, and hence find the value of n.","a":"n = 6","sol":"There are n + 4 counters altogether, so P(both red) = n/(n + 4) × (n − 1)/(n + 3) (M1 for a product with both denominators correct and the numerator reduced by one). Setting this equal to the given probability: n(n − 1) / ((n + 4)(n + 3)) = 1/3 (M1). Cross-multiplying, 3n(n − 1) = (n + 4)(n + 3), so 3n² − 3n = n² + 7n + 12 (M1 for expanding both sides). Collecting, 2n² − 10n − 12 = 0, and dividing by 2 gives n² − 5n − 6 = 0 (A1 cso — the printed result must be reached with every line shown). Factorising, (n − 6)(n + 1) = 0 (M1), so n = 6 or n = −1. A number of counters cannot be negative, so n = 6 (A1 cao, rejection stated). Check: 6/10 × 5/9 = 30/90 = 1/3.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Nothing is put back, so both the number of red counters and the total in the bag change between the two draws.","Write the probability of two reds as a product in terms of n, set it equal to the value you are given and clear the fractions.","The printed result earns nothing by itself, so every line of the algebra must be seen; the 'hence' then factorises and one root has to be thrown out on common-sense grounds."],"codes":["3.1","3.2"]},{"q":"A screening test for a condition is used on a large population in which 2% of people have the condition. The test gives a positive result for 95% of people who have the condition and for 3% of people who do not. A person is chosen at random and tests positive. Find the probability that this person has the condition, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.393 (3 s.f.)","sol":"Let C be 'has the condition' and + be 'tests positive'. P(C ∩ +) = P(C) P(+ | C) = 0.02 × 0.95 = 0.019 (M1 for a correct product along the tree). P(C′ ∩ +) = 0.98 × 0.03 = 0.0294 (M1 for the false-positive path). A positive result arises in exactly those two ways, so P(+) = 0.019 + 0.0294 = 0.0484 (A1). P(C | +) = P(C ∩ +) ÷ P(+) (M1 for the conditional formula with the total of the positives underneath) = 0.019 ÷ 0.0484 = 0.392561… = 0.393 to 3 significant figures (A1 awrt 0.393).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["A positive result can arise in two quite different ways, and only one of them involves somebody who is actually ill.","Build the tree from the whole population first, then add together every path that ends in a positive test.","Divide a single path by that total and round as asked; the answer is far smaller than most people expect — well under a half."],"codes":["3.2","3.3"]},{"q":"A commuter models her train as being late on any given day with probability 0.15, independently of every other day. Find, to 3 significant figures, the probability that the train is late on at least one of the five days of a working week, and state one assumption of this model that may not hold in practice.","a":"0.556 (3 s.f.); the days are assumed independent, which fails when a single cause such as bad weather or engineering work delays several days at once","sol":"'Late on at least one day' is the complement of 'not late on any day' (M1 for using the complement rather than listing cases). Under the model the days are independent and each has probability 0.85 of no delay, so P(no late days) = 0.85⁵ (M1 for the fifth power of the correct probability) = 0.443705… (A1). P(at least one late day) = 1 − 0.443705… = 0.556294… = 0.556 to 3 significant figures (A1 awrt 0.556). Assumption (B1): the model treats the five days as independent with the same probability each day. In practice delays cluster, because one cause — bad weather, a signal failure or engineering work — affects several consecutive days, so late days are positively associated; that would make a week with no late days more likely than the model says, and the true probability of at least one late day lower than 0.556.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Five days and the words 'at least one' — counting all the ways it can happen is the slow route.","Work out the probability of the opposite thing happening right across the week first.","What is left over from certainty is the probability you want; then name the assumption that let you multiply the days together and say why real weeks are not like that."],"codes":["3.1","3.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A and B are independent, with P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 0.4. Find P(A ∩ B).","a":"0.12"},{"q":"P(A ∩ B) = 0.15 and P(B) = 0.6. Find P(A | B).","a":"0.25"},{"q":"A die is rolled 60 times and shows a six on 20 of them. A model assumes the die is fair. Is that assumption reasonable, and why?","a":"No — the relative frequency 1/3 is much larger than the 1/6 a fair die would give"},{"q":"X is a continuous random variable. What does the area under its graph between x = 2 and x = 5 represent?","a":"P(2 ≤ X ≤ 5)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The addition rule for any two events A and B","back":"P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). The subtraction stops the overlap being counted twice.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"Mutually exclusive events: what it means and what P(A ∩ B) is","back":"A and B cannot both happen, so P(A ∩ B) = 0 and the addition rule reduces to P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B).","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"The test for independence using a product","back":"A and B are independent if and only if P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B). To test, compute P(A) P(B) and compare it with P(A ∩ B).","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"Independence written as a conditional probability","back":"A and B are independent when P(A | B) = P(A) and P(B | A) = P(B): knowing that one event has happened does not change the probability of the other.","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"The conditional probability formula","back":"P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B), provided P(B) > 0. The event you are given goes underneath.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"The multiplication rule written with a conditional probability","back":"P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B | A) = P(B) P(A | B). This is what multiplying along a branch of a tree diagram does.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"The complement rule","back":"P(A′) = 1 − P(A), where A′ is the event that A does not happen. It is the fastest route into any 'at least one' question.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"The probability of A′ ∩ B in terms of P(B) and P(A ∩ B)","back":"The part of B lying outside A, so P(A′ ∩ B) = P(B) − P(A ∩ B).","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying along a path and adding between paths on a tree diagram","back":"Multiply the probabilities along a single path; add the results of the separate paths that satisfy the event.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"Tree diagram branch probabilities for a second draw without replacement","back":"The total falls by one on the second draw, and so does the count of whichever type was taken first, so the second branch probabilities differ from the first.","code":"3.2","open":false},{"front":"Area under the graph of a continuous distribution","back":"Area over an interval is the probability that X lies in that interval; the total area is 1, and P(X = a) = 0 for any single value a.","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"P(X ≤ a) and P(X < a) for a continuous random variable","back":"Because P(X = a) = 0, so P(X ≤ a) = P(X < a). For a discrete random variable the two are different.","code":"3.1","open":false},{"front":"Give one assumption that a probability model of rolling a die relies on.","back":"The die is fair, so each face has probability 1/6; the rolls are independent of one another; the die always settles on exactly one face.","code":"3.3","open":true},{"front":"State one reason why a probability model built from a small number of trials may be unreliable.","back":"A small sample gives a relative frequency that may be far from the true probability; repeating the same few trials could easily give a very different estimate, so the model is unstable; one unusual run of outcomes shifts the proportion a long way when the number of trials is small.","code":"3.3","open":true},{"front":"The three parts of a critique of a probability model","back":"State the assumption in the words of the context, say whether it is reasonable and why, and say what effect a more realistic assumption would have on the answer.","code":"3.3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A′) = 1 − P(A) — printed in the Probability section of the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet, but far too useful to look up.","P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B) — printed in the Probability section of the booklet, but you are expected to apply it without looking it up.","P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B | A) — printed in the Probability section of the booklet; it is the tree-diagram multiplication written out.","P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B) — not printed in this form (the booklet's conditional-probability entry is the Bayes form P(B | A) P(A) ÷ [P(B | A) P(A) + P(B | A′) P(A′)]); it is the booklet's product rule rearranged, so write it from memory.","Mutually exclusive events: P(A ∩ B) = 0, so P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) — not in the booklet.","Independent events: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B), equivalently P(A | B) = P(A) and P(B | A) = P(B) — all three printed in the Probability section of the booklet, under 'For independent events A and B'.","P(A′ ∩ B) = P(B) − P(A ∩ B), the part of B outside A — not in the booklet; read it off a Venn diagram.","Tree diagrams: multiply along a path, add between paths, and all the paths from one node total 1 — not in the booklet.","Continuous distribution: probability is the area under the graph, the total area is 1, and P(X = a) = 0 so P(X ≤ a) = P(X < a) — not in the booklet."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Set notation and the traps examiners set","items":["A ∩ B is 'A and B' (the overlap); A ∪ B is 'A or B or both' (everything in either circle); A′ is 'not A' (everything outside the circle).","Mutually exclusive and independent are not the same thing: if P(A) and P(B) are both non-zero and A and B are mutually exclusive, then P(A ∩ B) = 0 ≠ P(A) P(B), so they cannot be independent.","Only multiply probabilities directly when you have been told the events are independent, or when the second probability you use is already conditional on the first.","Only add probabilities without subtracting when the events are mutually exclusive.","Read 'given that' and put that event on the bottom of the fraction: P(A | B) and P(B | A) are different numbers.","'At least one' almost always means 1 − P(none).","Without replacement: the denominator drops by one on the second draw, and so does the numerator of whichever branch was just taken.","A question that asks you to determine or show independence wants the comparison written out and a conclusion in words; a verdict with no comparison scores nothing.","In a modelling question, quote the assumption in the context's own words (this die, these five days), judge it against any data given, and say which way a more realistic assumption would move the answer."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:17:59Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"I re-solved all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit tickets, the 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples from the question text alone and computed every value in Python — 0.15 + 0.08 − 0.012 = 0.218 (= 1 − 0.85 × 0.92), 1 − 0.55 = 0.45, 0.18/0.45 = 0.4, 0.15, 7/8, 15/28 (with 3/28 + 10/28 + 15/28 = 1), 0.08/0.4 = 0.2 ≠ 0.25 so not independent (0.4 × 0.25 = 0.1 ≠ 0.08), 5/12, 1/36 = (1/6)(1/6) so independent, 130/200 = 0.65 and 0.65² = 0.4225 against 0.25, (1 − 0.64)/2 = 0.18, 0.28/0.7 = 0.4, (24/90)/(54/90) = 4/9, n² − 5n − 6 = 0 with n = 6 and n = −1 rejected, 0.019/0.0484 = 0.392562 → 0.393 (3 s.f.), and 1 − 0.85⁵ = 0.556295 → 0.556 — and every answer key, every line of every sol and every mark tally matches its m field, so 'wrong' is empty. The three ideas are covered in order with nothing beyond the restrictions (no probability density functions), no hint gives an answer, the tiers now read 5/8/3 across d = 1/2/3, and the Formulae sheet's booklet claims are right against Appendix 1, which lists no probability formulae at all: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B) and P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B | A) are the booklet's two Probability entries and are the only two marked 'printed', with the careful 'not printed in this form' on P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) accurate. All four faults the first examiner raised are gone: questions[0], [6] and [10] are now genuinely new items (factory checks 0.15/0.08; garden centre 0.4/0.25/0.08; symmetry about 12 with the central probability given and a tail asked), cards[13]'s three answers all address sample size, cards[7]–[11] and cards[14] now carry named-result fronts in the style of cards[0]–[6] with only the two open cards phrased as questions, and questions[12].d is 2. What remains is presentational only, so this is publishable."}}},"S4":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"S4","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Almost every Statistics question on Paper 3 begins by choosing a distribution and ends by commenting on whether it was a fair choice. This room gives you the two models the specification names — the binomial for counting successes in a fixed number of independent trials, and the Normal for a continuous measurement clustered symmetrically about a mean — plus the discrete uniform, the Normal approximation to the binomial with its continuity correction, and the language examiners want when a model does not fit. The same distributions carry the hypothesis-testing room, so marks lost here are lost twice.","idea":[{"h":"Discrete distributions and the binomial","t":"A discrete random variable takes separate values, each with a probability, and those probabilities must add to 1 — that single fact solves every 'find k' question. If every value is equally likely the variable has a discrete uniform distribution. The binomial X ~ B(n, p) counts successes in n independent trials each with constant probability p, and P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ (1 − p)ⁿ⁻ʳ. In the exam you use the calculator: binomial PD for a single value P(X = r), binomial CD for P(X ≤ r). The mark is usually for rewriting the question first: P(X ≥ r) = 1 − P(X ≤ r − 1), and P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = P(X ≤ b) − P(X ≤ a − 1). Note that calculating the mean and variance of a general discrete random variable is not in this specification.","code":"4.1"},{"h":"The Normal distribution and approximating a binomial","t":"X ~ N(μ, σ²) is the bell curve: symmetric about μ, total area 1, asymptotic to the axis in both tails, with points of inflection at x = μ − σ and x = μ + σ. The second parameter is the variance, so N(50, 12²) has standard deviation 12 and N(40, 20) has standard deviation √20 — misreading this loses whole questions. Standardise with Z = (X − μ)/σ ~ N(0, 1); use Normal CD for a probability and inverse Normal to go from a probability back to a value. When μ or σ is unknown, each given probability turns into one equation in μ and σ, and two probabilities give simultaneous equations you solve by eliminating μ. Finally, when n is large and p is close to 0.5, B(n, p) ≈ N(np, np(1 − p)), and because you are replacing a discrete variable by a continuous one you must apply a continuity correction of half a unit.","code":"4.2"},{"h":"Choosing a model and saying when it fails","t":"Selecting the distribution is worth marks in its own right, and the reasons must be in the language of the context, not the textbook. Choose the binomial when you are counting how many out of a fixed number of trials, the trials are independent and the probability of success is the same each time. Choose the Normal for a continuous measurement that is roughly symmetric about one central value with few extremes. The binomial fails when the probability drifts between trials (sampling without replacement from a small population, a machine wearing out), when trials affect each other (people in the same household), when there are more than two outcomes, or when the number of trials is not fixed. The Normal fails for a skewed or strictly positive quantity with a long tail, and the Normal approximation itself is poor when p is far from 0.5 or n is small.","code":"4.3"}],"examples":[{"title":"A discrete uniform distribution","setup":"A fair spinner has 5 equal sectors numbered 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. The random variable X is the score obtained on one spin. Name the distribution of X, write down P(X = 3) and find P(X ≥ 4).","steps":["Every sector is equally likely and there are five of them, so X takes each value with the same probability — a discrete uniform distribution.","Five equal probabilities must total 1, so P(X = x) = 1/5 for x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and in particular P(X = 3) = 1/5.","X ≥ 4 happens when the score is 4 or when it is 5, which is two of the five equally likely outcomes.","P(X ≥ 4) = 2/5 = 0.4."],"cues":["What can you say about the chance of landing on each sector?","If five equal probabilities add to 1, what is each one worth?","Which scores does the event X ≥ 4 contain?","How do you turn a count of equally likely outcomes into a probability?"]},{"title":"Binomial: a single value and an 'at least'","setup":"A production line makes components and each component is faulty independently of the others with probability 0.12. A random sample of 18 components is taken and X is the number of faulty components in the sample. Find P(X = 2) and P(X ≥ 3), giving each answer to 4 decimal places.","steps":["Each component is either faulty or not, the number examined is fixed at 18, p = 0.12 is constant and the components are independent, so X ~ B(18, 0.12).","P(X = 2) = ¹⁸C₂ × 0.12² × 0.88¹⁶ = 153 × 0.00186245 = 0.2850 (4 d.p.), or binomial PD on the calculator.","'At least 3' is the complement of 'at most 2', so P(X ≥ 3) = 1 − P(X ≤ 2).","Binomial CD with n = 18, p = 0.12 and upper value 2 gives P(X ≤ 2) = 0.6310 (4 d.p.).","P(X ≥ 3) = 1 − 0.6310 = 0.3690 (4 d.p.)."],"cues":["Which distribution fits a count of faulty items in a fixed sample?","Which formula or calculator function gives one exact value of X?","How do you rewrite 'at least 3' so a cumulative function can be used?","Which calculator function gives the cumulative value you now need?","How do you finish once the cumulative probability is known?"]},{"title":"Normal probabilities and an inverse Normal","setup":"The time T minutes taken by a student to travel to school is modelled by T ~ N(24, 4.5²). Find the probability that a randomly chosen journey takes between 20 and 30 minutes, to 4 decimal places, and find the time that is exceeded by 5% of journeys, to 1 decimal place.","steps":["Standardise each end: z = (20 − 24)/4.5 = −0.8889 and z = (30 − 24)/4.5 = 1.3333.","P(20 < T < 30) = Φ(1.3333) − Φ(−0.8889) = 0.9088 − 0.1870 = 0.7218 (4 d.p.).","If 5% of journeys exceed the time t, then P(T < t) = 0.95, and the inverse Normal gives z = 1.6449.","t = 24 + 1.6449 × 4.5 = 31.4018, so the time exceeded by 5% of journeys is 31.4 minutes."],"cues":["What must be done to each end-point before using the standard Normal?","How do two cumulative values combine into the probability of an interval?","What probability lies below the time you are being asked for?","How do you get from a z-value back to a time in minutes?"]},{"title":"Finding μ and σ from two probabilities","setup":"The mass, in grams, of a bag of crisps is modelled by X ~ N(μ, σ²). It is given that P(X < 28) = 0.2 and P(X > 34) = 0.1. Find the values of μ and σ, each to 3 significant figures.","steps":["P(X < 28) = 0.2 gives z = −0.8416 from the inverse Normal, so (28 − μ)/σ = −0.8416.","P(X > 34) = 0.1 means P(X < 34) = 0.9, giving z = 1.2816, so (34 − μ)/σ = 1.2816.","Multiply out and subtract to eliminate μ: 6 = (1.2816 + 0.8416)σ = 2.1232σ, so σ = 2.8260.","μ = 34 − 1.2816 × 2.8260 = 30.3784, so μ = 30.4 g and σ = 2.83 g to 3 s.f."],"cues":["What does the probability 0.2 give you before μ and σ can appear?","How must a 'greater than' probability be rewritten for the inverse Normal?","How can μ be removed from the two equations you now have?","Which equation gives μ once σ is known?"]},{"title":"Normal approximation with a continuity correction","setup":"A fair coin is tossed 80 times and X is the number of heads obtained. Explain why a Normal approximation is reasonable, state the approximating distribution, and use it with a continuity correction to estimate P(X ≥ 47), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","steps":["n = 80 is large and p = 0.5 for a fair coin, so X ~ B(80, 0.5) can be approximated by N(np, np(1 − p)).","np = 40 and np(1 − p) = 80 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 20, so use Y ~ N(40, 20), with standard deviation √20 = 4.4721.","X is discrete and Y is continuous, so the continuity correction replaces P(X ≥ 47) by P(Y > 46.5).","Standardise: z = (46.5 − 40)/4.4721 = 1.4534.","P(Y > 46.5) = 1 − Φ(1.4534) = 1 − 0.9269 = 0.0731 (4 d.p.)."],"cues":["Which two features of this binomial make a Normal approximation reasonable?","What mean and variance must the approximating Normal have?","Why can the boundary 47 not be used unchanged with a continuous model?","What is the z-value of the corrected boundary?","Which tail of the Normal curve does the question want?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write P(X ≥ 6) for a binomial variable in terms of a cumulative probability.","a":"1 − P(X ≤ 5)"},{"q":"Where are the points of inflection on the curve of X ~ N(40, 3²)?","a":"At x = 37 and x = 43"},{"q":"State the Normal distribution used to approximate B(50, 0.48).","a":"N(24, 12.48)"},{"q":"Under a Normal approximation, what does P(X ≤ 30) become after the continuity correction?","a":"P(Y < 30.5)"}]},"room":{"facts":["For any discrete random variable, Σ P(X = x) = 1 over all values of x.","X ~ B(n, p): P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ (1 − p)ⁿ⁻ʳ, with mean np and variance np(1 − p).","Binomial conditions: a fixed number of trials, two outcomes per trial, a constant probability of success, and independent trials.","P(X ≥ r) = 1 − P(X ≤ r − 1) and P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = P(X ≤ b) − P(X ≤ a − 1).","X ~ N(μ, σ²) is symmetric about μ with points of inflection at x = μ − σ and x = μ + σ; standardise using Z = (X − μ)/σ ~ N(0, 1).","When n is large and p is close to 0.5, B(n, p) ≈ N(np, np(1 − p)); apply a continuity correction of 0.5."],"questions":[{"q":"The random variable X ~ B(15, 0.2). Find P(X = 4), giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.188","sol":"P(X = 4) = ¹⁵C₄ × 0.2⁴ × 0.8¹¹ (M1 for the correct binomial expression, or for using binomial PD with n = 15, p = 0.2 and x = 4). = 1365 × 0.0016 × 0.0858993 = 0.187604… = 0.188 to 3 s.f. (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["You are counting successes in a fixed number of independent trials, so one named distribution applies.","For a single value of a binomial you need the number of ways of choosing which trials succeed, times the probability of one such arrangement.","Write ¹⁵C₄ × 0.2^r × 0.8^(15 − r) with the correct power on each factor."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The random variable X ~ B(25, 0.35). Use your calculator to find P(X ≤ 8), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"0.4668","sol":"This is a cumulative binomial probability. Binomial CD with n = 25, p = 0.35 and upper value 8 gives P(X ≤ 8) = 0.466819… = 0.4668 to 4 d.p. (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The symbol ≤ tells you which of the two binomial calculator functions is wanted.","Use the cumulative binomial function rather than the single-value one.","P(X ≤ 8) is asking for every outcome from 0 up to and including 8 — check that the 8 itself is inside the range you ask for."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The discrete random variable X has probability distribution P(X = x) = kx² for x = 1, 2, 3, 4, where k is a constant. Find the exact value of k.","a":"k = 1/30","sol":"The probabilities of a discrete random variable sum to 1, so k(1² + 2² + 3² + 4²) = 1 (M1 for setting the sum of the four probabilities equal to 1). k(1 + 4 + 9 + 16) = 30k = 1, so k = 1/30 (A1). 'Exact' means the fraction must be left as 1/30; 0.0333 would lose the accuracy mark.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There is one rule every discrete probability distribution must obey.","Write down each of the four probabilities in terms of k, then add them together.","Form the equation k(1² + 2² + 3² + 4²) = 1."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The random variable X ~ N(50, 12²). Find P(X < 62), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"0.8413","sol":"Standardise: Z = (62 − 50)/12 = 1 (M1). P(Z < 1) = 0.841344… (A1). Equivalently, Normal CD with μ = 50, σ = 12 and upper value 62 gives 0.8413 to 4 d.p. Note σ = 12, not 144: the second parameter of N(μ, σ²) is the variance.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Ask how many standard deviations above the mean the given value lies.","Either standardise with Z = (X − μ)/σ, or use Normal CD with μ and σ entered directly.","Work out (62 − 50)/12 first."],"codes":["4.2"]},{"q":"A fair spinner has 8 equal sectors numbered 1 to 8. The random variable X is the score obtained on one spin. Name the distribution of X and find P(X ≥ 6).","a":"Discrete uniform; P(X ≥ 6) = 3/8 = 0.375","sol":"All eight scores are equally likely, so X has a discrete uniform distribution over 1 to 8, with P(X = x) = 1/8 for each x (B1). X ≥ 6 means X = 6, 7 or 8, which is three of the eight equally likely outcomes (M1). P(X ≥ 6) = 3/8 = 0.375 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what is true of the probability of every score on a fair spinner.","Name the distribution in which all the values are equally likely, then count the outcomes in the event.","List the scores that satisfy X ≥ 6."],"codes":["4.1","4.3"]},{"q":"State the four conditions that must hold for the number of successes in a series of trials to be modelled by a binomial distribution.","a":"A fixed number of trials; two outcomes per trial (success or failure); a constant probability of success; independent trials","sol":"There is a fixed number n of trials; each trial has exactly two outcomes, success or failure; the probability p of success is the same in every trial; the trials are independent of one another. (B1 for any two correct conditions, B2 for all four.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what must be true of the trials before you can count successes at all.","Two of the conditions describe the set of trials as a whole, and two describe what happens in a single trial.","Your answer should be four separate statements, not one long sentence — check that none of them is really two conditions merged together."],"codes":["4.3"]},{"q":"The random variable X ~ B(30, 0.45). Find P(10 ≤ X ≤ 15), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"0.6997","sol":"P(10 ≤ X ≤ 15) = P(X ≤ 15) − P(X ≤ 9) (M1 for a difference of two cumulative probabilities, A1 for the lower one being at 9 and not 10, since X is discrete). Binomial CD gives P(X ≤ 15) = 0.769091… and P(X ≤ 9) = 0.069407…. The difference is 0.699684… = 0.6997 to 4 d.p. (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Your calculator gives probabilities of the form P(X ≤ a), so build the interval from two of those.","Subtract the cumulative probability just below the interval from the cumulative probability at the top of it.","Because X is discrete, the whole number immediately below 10 is 9."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The time taken by trains on a route is modelled by T ~ N(68, 5²) minutes. Find the probability that a randomly chosen train takes between 62 and 74 minutes, giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"0.7699","sol":"Standardise both ends: z₁ = (62 − 68)/5 = −1.2 and z₂ = (74 − 68)/5 = 1.2 (M1). P(−1.2 < Z < 1.2) = Φ(1.2) − Φ(−1.2) = 0.884930 − 0.115070 (M1) = 0.769861… = 0.7699 to 4 d.p. (A1). The two limits are symmetric about the mean, so the answer is also 2Φ(1.2) − 1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Notice how far each end-point is from the mean before you calculate anything.","Standardise each end and take the difference of the two cumulative probabilities.","The first line of working is z = (x − μ)/σ written out once for each end-point."],"codes":["4.2"]},{"q":"The random variable X ~ N(μ, 6²) and P(X > 40) = 0.15. Find the value of μ, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"μ = 33.8","sol":"P(X > 40) = 0.15 so P(X < 40) = 0.85, and the inverse Normal gives z = 1.03643 (M1 for a correct z-value). Standardising, (40 − μ)/6 = 1.03643 (M1 for a correct standardised equation). 40 − μ = 6.21860, so μ = 33.7814… = 33.8 to 3 s.f. (A1). The mean is below 40, as expected since less than half the distribution lies above 40.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The inverse Normal on your calculator works from the probability below a value, not above it.","Turn the probability into a z-value, then set the standardised expression for 40 equal to it.","Rewrite P(X > 40) = 0.15 as a 'less than' probability."],"codes":["4.2"]},{"q":"The random variable X ~ N(200, σ²) and P(X < 190) = 0.25. Find the value of σ, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"σ = 14.8","sol":"P(X < 190) = 0.25 gives z = −0.67449 from the inverse Normal (M1; the z must be negative because 190 is below the mean). Standardising, (190 − 200)/σ = −0.67449 (M1). So −10/σ = −0.67449 and σ = 10/0.67449 = 14.8260… = 14.8 to 3 s.f. (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The value 190 lies below the mean, which tells you the sign of the z-value.","Find the z-value from the probability, then standardise and rearrange for σ.","Write (190 − 200)/σ equal to the z-value your calculator gives."],"codes":["4.2"]},{"q":"A fair coin is tossed 100 times and X is the number of heads obtained. Using a suitable Normal approximation with a continuity correction, estimate P(X ≥ 45), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"0.8643","sol":"X ~ B(100, 0.5). n is large and p = 0.5, so X may be approximated by Y ~ N(np, np(1 − p)) = N(50, 25), with standard deviation 5 (B1). Continuity correction: P(X ≥ 45) ≈ P(Y > 44.5) (M1). Standardise: z = (44.5 − 50)/5 = −1.1 (M1). P(Y > 44.5) = 1 − Φ(−1.1) = Φ(1.1) = 0.864334… = 0.8643 to 4 d.p. (A1). (The exact binomial value is 0.8644, so the approximation is very good here.)","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which Normal distribution shares its mean and variance with this binomial?","Write down N(np, np(1 − p)), shift the boundary by half a unit, then standardise.","Decide first in which direction the boundary 45 must move."],"codes":["4.2","4.3"]},{"q":"A gardener plants 40 seeds of a variety that germinates independently of the others with probability 0.85. Let G be the number of seeds that germinate. State a suitable model for G and find P(G ≥ 36), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.","a":"G ~ B(40, 0.85); P(G ≥ 36) = 0.2633","sol":"A fixed number of trials (40 seeds), two outcomes per seed (germinates or not), a constant probability 0.85 and independent seeds give G ~ B(40, 0.85) (B1). P(G ≥ 36) = 1 − P(G ≤ 35) (M1). Binomial CD gives P(G ≤ 35) = 0.736680… (A1). P(G ≥ 36) = 1 − 0.736680 = 0.263320… = 0.2633 to 4 d.p. (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Check the wording against the conditions for a binomial model before calculating anything.","'At least 36' must be turned into 1 minus a cumulative probability.","Decide which cumulative value you subtract from 1 — it is not P(G ≤ 36)."],"codes":["4.1","4.3"]},{"q":"The probability that a light bulb from a large batch is faulty is 0.08. An inspector takes a random sample of n bulbs. Find the smallest value of n for which the probability that the sample contains at least one faulty bulb is greater than 0.95.","a":"n = 36","sol":"Let X be the number of faulty bulbs, so X ~ B(n, 0.08). P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − P(X = 0) = 1 − 0.92ⁿ (M1). Require 1 − 0.92ⁿ > 0.95, so 0.92ⁿ < 0.05 (A1). Taking natural logarithms, n ln 0.92 < ln 0.05; since ln 0.92 is negative the inequality reverses on dividing, giving n > ln 0.05 / ln 0.92 = 35.928… (M1 for taking logs and reversing the inequality, A1 for 35.9). n must be a whole number, so n = 36 (A1). Check: 0.92³⁶ = 0.0497 < 0.05 while 0.92³⁵ = 0.0540 > 0.05.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["'At least one' is far easier through the complementary event.","Express the probability of no faulty bulbs in terms of n, form an inequality and solve it using logarithms.","P(X = 0) = 0.92ⁿ — what inequality must this expression satisfy?"],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The mass of an apple from an orchard is modelled by M ~ N(160, 15²) grams. An apple is called large if its mass is more than 180 g. A box of 12 apples is chosen at random from the orchard and L is the number of large apples in the box. Find P(L = 3), giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.0706","sol":"First find the probability that one apple is large. z = (180 − 160)/15 = 1.3333 (M1), so p = P(M > 180) = 1 − Φ(1.3333) = 1 − 0.908789 = 0.091211 (A1). The apples are chosen at random, so each is large or not independently with this constant probability and the number of trials is fixed: L ~ B(12, 0.091211) (M1 for a binomial using the probability just found). P(L = 3) = ¹²C₃ × 0.091211³ × 0.908789⁹ (M1) = 220 × 0.00075883 × 0.42283 = 0.070589 (A1) = 0.0706 to 3 s.f. (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two different models are needed here, one feeding the other.","Use the Normal model to find the probability that a single apple is large, then treat the 12 apples as binomial trials with that probability.","Standardise the boundary mass first: (180 − 160)/15."],"codes":["4.2","4.1"]},{"q":"The random variable X ~ B(20, 0.3). Find P(X ≥ 8 | X ≥ 5), giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.299","sol":"Conditional probability: P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) (M1). Every outcome with X ≥ 8 also has X ≥ 5, so the intersection is simply X ≥ 8 (B1 for this reasoning). P(X ≥ 8) = 1 − P(X ≤ 7) = 1 − 0.772272 = 0.227728 (M1, A1). P(X ≥ 5) = 1 − P(X ≤ 4) = 1 − 0.237508 = 0.762492 (A1). P(X ≥ 8 | X ≥ 5) = 0.227728/0.762492 = 0.298663… = 0.299 to 3 s.f. (A1).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Conditional probability needs the intersection of the two events — consider whether one of them contains the other.","Divide the probability of the event in the numerator by the probability of the condition, each found from a cumulative binomial value.","The first line is the conditional probability formula with these two events written into it — list the values of X in each event to decide what the intersection is."],"codes":["4.1"]},{"q":"The heights, in cm, of adult males in a population are modelled by X ~ N(μ, σ²). It is given that P(X > 180) = 0.1 and P(X < 165) = 0.2. Find μ and σ to 3 significant figures, and state the heights at which the points of inflection of the distribution curve occur, to 1 decimal place.","a":"μ = 171, σ = 7.06; points of inflection at x = 163.9 cm and x = 178.0 cm (accept x = 163.9 cm and x = 178.1 cm, the values obtained from the rounded μ = 171 and σ = 7.06)","sol":"P(X > 180) = 0.1 gives P(X < 180) = 0.9 and z = 1.28155, so (180 − μ)/σ = 1.28155 (M1, A1). P(X < 165) = 0.2 gives z = −0.84162, so (165 − μ)/σ = −0.84162 (A1). Multiplying out: 180 − μ = 1.28155σ and 165 − μ = −0.84162σ. Subtracting eliminates μ: 15 = 2.12317σ (M1), so σ = 7.06490 = 7.06 to 3 s.f. (A1). Then μ = 180 − 1.28155 × 7.06490 = 170.946 = 171 to 3 s.f. (A1). The points of inflection are at x = μ ± σ, that is 170.946 − 7.065 = 163.9 cm and 170.946 + 7.065 = 178.0 cm (B1). A student who uses the rounded values gets 171 − 7.06 = 163.94 and 171 + 7.06 = 178.06, so accept x = 163.9 cm and x = 178.1 cm for the upper point as well (B1 either way).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["Each of the two probabilities gives you one equation connecting μ and σ.","Convert both probabilities to z-values, standardise to get two simultaneous equations, then eliminate μ between them.","P(X > 180) = 0.1 means P(X < 180) = 0.9 — what z-value does that give?"],"codes":["4.2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"The random variable X ~ B(12, 0.25). Find P(X = 3) to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.258"},{"q":"A fair ten-sided die has faces numbered 1 to 10 and X is the score. Name the distribution of X and write down P(X = 4).","a":"Discrete uniform; P(X = 4) = 1/10 = 0.1"},{"q":"The random variable X ~ N(70, 6²). Find P(X < 79) to 4 decimal places.","a":"0.9332"},{"q":"State the Normal distribution that approximates B(200, 0.5), and state what P(X ≥ 96) becomes after a continuity correction.","a":"N(100, 50); P(Y > 95.5)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Notation for the binomial distribution","back":"X ~ B(n, p): X is the number of successes in n independent trials, each with constant probability p of success.","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"The binomial probability formula","back":"P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ (1 − p)ⁿ⁻ʳ for r = 0, 1, …, n. This one is printed in the formulae booklet (written with q = 1 − p).","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"The four conditions for a binomial model","back":"A fixed number of trials n; exactly two outcomes per trial; a constant probability p of success; independent trials.","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"The discrete uniform distribution","back":"A discrete random variable taking n listed values, each with probability 1/n — the score on a fair n-sided die or spinner.","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"Conditions satisfied by the probabilities of a discrete random variable","back":"Each lies between 0 and 1, and Σ P(X = x) = 1 summed over every value the variable can take.","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"Turning 'at least' and 'between' into cumulative probabilities","back":"P(X ≥ r) = 1 − P(X ≤ r − 1); P(X > r) = 1 − P(X ≤ r); P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = P(X ≤ b) − P(X ≤ a − 1).","code":"4.1","open":false},{"front":"Notation for the Normal distribution","back":"X ~ N(μ, σ²): μ is the mean and σ² the variance, so the standard deviation is σ. The second parameter is the variance, not σ.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"Standardising a Normal variable","back":"Z = (X − μ)/σ ~ N(0, 1). The booklet gives this only in the sample-mean form used for hypothesis testing, so learn this version.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"Shape and symmetry of the Normal curve","back":"Bell-shaped and symmetric about x = μ, with total area 1 and both tails approaching the horizontal axis without meeting it.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"Points of inflection of the Normal curve","back":"At x = μ − σ and x = μ + σ, one standard deviation either side of the mean.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"The 68–95–99.7 result for N(μ, σ²)","back":"About 68% of values lie within 1σ of μ, about 95% within 2σ and about 99.7% within 3σ.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"The Normal approximation to the binomial","back":"If n is large and p is close to 0.5, then B(n, p) ≈ N(np, np(1 − p)), used with a continuity correction.","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"The continuity correction","back":"P(X ≥ a) → P(Y > a − 0.5); P(X ≤ a) → P(Y < a + 0.5); P(X = a) → P(a − 0.5 < Y < a + 0.5).","code":"4.2","open":false},{"front":"Give one reason why a binomial model may not be appropriate","back":"The probability of success changes between trials, as in sampling without replacement from a small population; the trials are not independent; the number of trials is not fixed in advance.","code":"4.3","open":true},{"front":"Conditions under which a Normal model is suitable","back":"For a continuous quantity that is roughly symmetric about a single central value, with most readings near the mean and few extreme values in either tail.","code":"4.3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Discrete random variable: Σ P(X = x) = 1, summed over every value x — not in the formulae booklet, write it from memory.","Discrete uniform distribution over n values: P(X = x) = 1/n for each value — not in the booklet.","Binomial distribution X ~ B(n, p): P(X = r) = ⁿCᵣ pʳ (1 − p)ⁿ⁻ʳ — given in the booklet under 'The binomial distribution', written with q = 1 − p.","Mean of X ~ B(n, p): np — given in the booklet.","Variance of X ~ B(n, p): np(1 − p), written npq in the booklet — given in the booklet.","Cumulative binomial rewrites: P(X ≥ r) = 1 − P(X ≤ r − 1) and P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = P(X ≤ b) − P(X ≤ a − 1) — not in the booklet.","Normal distribution X ~ N(μ, σ²): the second parameter is the variance, so the standard deviation is σ — not in the booklet.","Standardising: Z = (X − μ)/σ ~ N(0, 1) — the booklet gives this only in the sample-mean form (X̄ − μ)/(σ/√n) for hypothesis testing, so learn this version.","Points of inflection of the Normal curve: x = μ − σ and x = μ + σ — not in the booklet.","Normal approximation to the binomial: when n is large and p is close to 0.5, B(n, p) ≈ N(np, np(1 − p)) — not in the booklet.","Continuity correction: P(X ≥ a) ≈ P(Y > a − 0.5), P(X ≤ a) ≈ P(Y < a + 0.5), P(X = a) ≈ P(a − 0.5 < Y < a + 0.5) — not in the booklet.","The booklet does contain the Normal table Φ(z) = P(Z ≤ z) and the percentage points of the Normal distribution, but you are expected to use the calculator's Normal CD and inverse Normal."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Choosing a distribution and criticising the model","items":["Binomial: you are counting how many out of a fixed number of trials; each trial succeeds or fails; the probability of success is the same every time; the trials do not affect one another. The answer is a whole number between 0 and n.","Discrete uniform: a small set of listed outcomes that are all equally likely, such as the score on a fair spinner or die.","Normal: a continuous measurement — height, mass, time, length, measurement error — that clusters symmetrically about one mean with few extreme values.","Why a binomial may fail: sampling without replacement from a small population makes p drift; a machine that wears out makes p drift; people in the same household are not independent; more than two outcomes; the number of trials is not fixed.","Why a Normal may fail: the data are skewed or have a long tail; the quantity cannot be negative but the model gives negative values a real probability; the data are discrete counts rather than measurements.","Why a Normal approximation to a binomial may fail: p is far from 0.5 so B(n, p) is skewed, or n is too small for the curve to smooth out the bars.","Always answer in the context given: say 'the probability that a seed germinates may not be the same for every seed', not 'p is not constant'."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:21:28.704669Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Every number in the room is right — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit items, the 4 check items and all 5 worked examples re-solve to exactly the stated values at the stated accuracy (including the Q15 inflection points from both the unrounded and the rounded μ and σ), and all seven faults in the first verdict have genuinely been repaired, so what is left is a hint-policy sweep: two third hints still hand over the substituted first line of the mark scheme and one hands over an A1 point, and two questions re-run a worked example's context and ask."}}},"M6":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"M6","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Mechanics on Paper 3 is set in the real world, and the marks attached to units are the cheapest on the paper. A question that gives a mass in grams, a length in centimetres or a speed in km h⁻¹ is checking whether you convert to S.I. units before you substitute, and a correct number written without its unit still loses the accuracy mark. This room fixes the three fundamental quantities and units, the derived units built from them (velocity, acceleration, force, weight and moment) and the conversions the specification names, so that every later mechanics room — kinematics, forces and moments — starts from figures already in metres, kilograms and seconds.","idea":[{"h":"The three fundamental quantities and their units","code":"6.1","t":"Mechanics measures everything from three fundamental quantities: length, in metres (m); time, in seconds (s); and mass, in kilograms (kg). These are the base units of the S.I. system, and every other unit in mechanics is built from them. Unless a question says otherwise, answers are expected in S.I. units, so a mass given in grams or tonnes, or a length given in centimetres or kilometres, must be converted before it is used. Examiners test this by hiding a non-S.I. unit inside an ordinary question: 400 g, 30 cm, 2.5 tonnes. Spot it in the first line of your working, not after you have an answer."},{"h":"Converting units: km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹ and back","code":"6.1","t":"The conversion the specification names by name is km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹: multiply by 1000, because 1 km = 1000 m, and divide by 3600, because 1 h = 3600 s — in other words divide by 3.6. Going the other way, from m s⁻¹ into km h⁻¹, multiply by 3.6. So 72 km h⁻¹ = 20 m s⁻¹ and 15 m s⁻¹ = 54 km h⁻¹. Use the size of the answer as a check: for the same speed, the number of km h⁻¹ is always the larger. The same one-line idea handles the others you meet: grams to kilograms (divide by 1000), centimetres to metres (divide by 100), tonnes to kilograms (multiply by 1000)."},{"h":"Derived quantities: velocity, acceleration, force, weight and moment","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","t":"Every other quantity in mechanics is derived from the base units. Velocity is a displacement divided by a time, so it is measured in m s⁻¹; acceleration is a change of velocity divided by a time, so m s⁻². Force is defined by F = ma, so the newton is 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻². Weight is the particular force of gravity on a mass, W = mg newtons, taking g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless a question gives another value — so a mass of 5 kg has weight 49 N, not 5 N. A moment is a force multiplied by a perpendicular distance, so it is measured in newton metres, N m, never N/m. Units must be consistent inside a calculation and must be written beside every numerical answer."},{"h":"Units as algebra: checking a formula and finding the units of a constant","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","t":"Units multiply and divide exactly as the quantities do, so you can do algebra with them. Two uses come up in questions. First, a check: every term added together in a correct formula must carry the same units, so if one term works out as m s⁻¹ and another as m, the formula, or a substituted value, is wrong. Second, a constant in a model has whatever units make the two sides agree: make the constant the subject, replace each remaining quantity by its units, write every newton as kg m s⁻², then collect the powers of kg, m and s using the ordinary index laws."}],"examples":[{"title":"Converting a speed from km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹","setup":"A lorry travels along a motorway at a steady 72 km h⁻¹. Express this speed in S.I. units, that is in m s⁻¹.","cues":["How many metres are there in one kilometre?","How many seconds are there in one hour?","Speed is one quantity divided by which other?","What does that division come to?"],"steps":["Since 1 km = 1000 m, the distance covered in an hour is 72 × 1000 = 72 000 m.","An hour is 60 × 60 = 3600 s.","Speed is distance ÷ time, so the speed is 72 000 m ÷ 3600 s.","72 000 ÷ 3600 = 20, so the lorry's speed is 20 m s⁻¹."]},{"title":"Weight in newtons, and the newton in base units","setup":"A suitcase has mass 5 kg. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the weight of the suitcase in newtons, and write that weight in S.I. base units.","cues":["Which quantity here is the force, the mass or the weight?","What is that product, and in which unit?","Which equation defines a force from a mass?","Written with base units only, what is a newton?"],"steps":["Weight is the force of gravity on a mass, so W = mg with m = 5 kg and g = 9.8 m s⁻².","W = 5 × 9.8 = 49, and force is measured in newtons, so W = 49 N.","The newton is defined by F = ma, so 1 N = 1 kg × 1 m s⁻².","In base units 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², so a weight of 49 N is 49 kg m s⁻²."]},{"title":"A moment when the distance is given in centimetres","setup":"A spanner is used to turn a nut. A force of 25 N is applied at right angles to the spanner at a point 40 cm from the centre of the nut. Find the moment of the force about the centre of the nut, giving the units of your answer.","cues":["Which unit must the distance be in before you multiply?","How many metres is that distance?","A moment is the product of which two quantities?","What is that product, with its unit?"],"steps":["Moments are measured in newton metres, so the distance must be changed from centimetres to metres.","40 cm = 40 ÷ 100 = 0.4 m.","Moment = force × perpendicular distance = 25 × 0.4.","25 × 0.4 = 10, so the moment about the centre of the nut is 10 N m."]},{"title":"A weight and a moment in one calculation","setup":"A tin of paint of mass 2.4 kg hangs from a horizontal shelf bracket at a point 25 cm from the wall. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the moment of the weight of the tin about the point where the bracket meets the wall, giving the units of your answer.","cues":["The moment needs a force: which force does the tin exert on the bracket?","Which unit must the distance be in for a moment in N m?","How is the moment about the wall now formed?","What is that product, with its unit?"],"steps":["The force is the weight of the tin, so W = mg = 2.4 × 9.8 = 23.52 N.","A moment in newton metres needs the distance in metres: 25 cm = 25 ÷ 100 = 0.25 m.","Moment about the wall = force × perpendicular distance = 23.52 × 0.25.","23.52 × 0.25 = 5.88, so the moment is 5.88 N m."]},{"title":"Finding the units of a constant in a model","setup":"The resistance to the motion of a boat is modelled by R = kv², where R is the resistance in newtons, v is the speed of the boat in m s⁻¹ and k is a constant. Find the S.I. base units of k.","cues":["How can the model be rearranged to make k the subject?","What is a newton in base units?","What are the units of the square of a speed?","What is left when those two sets of units are divided?"],"steps":["Rearranging the model gives k = R ÷ v², so the units of k are the units of R divided by the units of v².","R is a force, and in base units a newton is kg m s⁻².","The speed is in m s⁻¹, so its square has units m² s⁻².","Dividing, k has units kg m s⁻² ÷ m² s⁻² = kg m⁻¹."]}],"check":[{"q":"What is 90 km h⁻¹ in m s⁻¹?","a":"25 m s⁻¹"},{"q":"Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², what is the weight of a body of mass 12 kg?","a":"117.6 N"},{"q":"What is 250 g in the S.I. base unit of mass?","a":"0.25 kg"},{"q":"A force acting at a perpendicular distance of 20 cm from a pivot has a moment of 6 N m about that pivot. What is the magnitude of the force?","a":"30 N"}]},"room":{"facts":["Fundamental S.I. quantities and units in mechanics: length in metres (m), time in seconds (s), mass in kilograms (kg).","km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹: multiply by 1000 and divide by 3600 (that is, divide by 3.6). m s⁻¹ into km h⁻¹: multiply by 3.6.","Derived units: velocity m s⁻¹, acceleration m s⁻², force in newtons with 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², moment in newton metres (N m).","Weight W = mg newtons, taking g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless the question states another value.","Other conversions: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 h = 3600 s, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 kN = 1000 N, 1 cm = 0.01 m, 1 g = 0.001 kg."],"questions":[{"q":"A high-speed train travels at a constant 108 km h⁻¹. Express this speed in m s⁻¹.","a":"30 m s⁻¹","sol":"1 km = 1000 m, so in one hour the train covers 108 × 1000 = 108 000 m, and 1 h = 3600 s, so the speed is 108 000 ÷ 3600 (M1 for a complete method: multiplying by 1000 and dividing by 3600). 108 000 ÷ 3600 = 30, so the speed is 30 m s⁻¹ (A1, the unit is required).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Answers in mechanics are expected in S.I. units, so both the kilometres and the hours have to change.","Work out the distance the train covers in one hour in metres, then divide by the number of seconds in an hour.","1 km = 1000 m, so start from 108 × 1000 m travelled in one hour."],"codes":["6.1"]},{"q":"A runner moves at a constant 8 m s⁻¹. Express this speed in km h⁻¹.","a":"28.8 km h⁻¹","sol":"In one hour the runner covers 8 × 3600 = 28 800 m (M1 for multiplying by 3600 and dividing by 1000, in either order). 28 800 m = 28 800 ÷ 1000 = 28.8 km, so the speed is 28.8 km h⁻¹ (A1, the unit is required).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is the reverse of the usual conversion, so for the same speed the number of km h⁻¹ must be the larger one.","Find how far the runner travels in one hour, then rewrite that distance in kilometres.","Distance travelled in one hour = 8 × 3600 m."],"codes":["6.1"]},{"q":"A crate has mass 4.5 kg. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the weight of the crate.","a":"44.1 N","sol":"Weight is the force of gravity on the mass, so W = mg = 4.5 × 9.8 (M1). 4.5 × 9.8 = 44.1, and a force is measured in newtons, so W = 44.1 N (A1, the unit is required; an answer of 44.1 kg scores no accuracy mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Weight is a force, not a mass, so it is not measured in kilograms.","Multiply the mass in kilograms by the acceleration due to gravity.","W = mg, with m = 4.5 and g = 9.8."],"codes":["6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A moment is measured in newton metres (N m). Express the newton metre in terms of S.I. base units only.","a":"1 N m = 1 kg m² s⁻²","sol":"The unit of a moment is the unit of force multiplied by the unit of length, so unit of moment = N × m (M1). A force is mass × acceleration, so 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻² (B1). Hence 1 N m = kg m s⁻² × m = 1 kg m² s⁻² (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A moment is built from two other quantities, and one of those two is itself measured in a derived unit.","Write the unit of a moment as a product of the units of the quantities that make it, then rewrite any derived unit in that product in base units.","Unit of moment = unit of force × unit of perpendicular distance = N × m."],"codes":["6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A cyclist covers 1.8 km in 90 s at a constant speed. Find the speed of the cyclist in m s⁻¹.","a":"20 m s⁻¹","sol":"The answer is asked for in S.I. units, so the distance must be in metres: 1.8 km = 1.8 × 1000 = 1800 m (B1). Speed = distance ÷ time = 1800 ÷ 90 (M1) = 20, so the speed is 20 m s⁻¹ (A1, the unit is required).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The time is already in the base unit, but the distance is not.","Rewrite the distance in metres, then divide it by the time in seconds.","1.8 km = 1.8 × 1000 m, and speed = distance ÷ time."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A ball of mass 400 g is given an acceleration of 2.5 m s⁻². Using F = ma, find the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the ball.","a":"1 N","sol":"The mass must first be in the base unit: 400 g = 400 ÷ 1000 = 0.4 kg (B1). Using F = ma, F = 0.4 × 2.5 (M1). F = 1, so the resultant force has magnitude 1 N (A1, the unit is required). Substituting 400 instead of 0.4 gives 1000 N and can score the M mark only.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The formula only works when every quantity in it is in S.I. units — look hard at the mass.","Convert the mass into kilograms, then substitute the mass and the acceleration into the formula.","Mass = 400 ÷ 1000 kg."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A force of magnitude 12 N acts at right angles to a rod at a point 30 cm from a fixed pivot. Find the moment of the force about the pivot, stating the units of your answer.","a":"3.6 N m","sol":"A moment is measured in newton metres, so the distance must be converted: 30 cm = 30 ÷ 100 = 0.3 m (B1). Moment = force × perpendicular distance = 12 × 0.3 (M1) = 3.6, so the moment is 3.6 N m (A1, the unit is required; N/m is not accepted).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the unit a moment is measured in, then check the unit the distance is given in.","Change the distance into the base unit of length, then multiply it by the force.","30 cm = 30 ÷ 100 m."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A stone has weight 1.47 N. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the mass of the stone in grams.","a":"150 g","sol":"W = mg, so m = W ÷ g = 1.47 ÷ 9.8 (M1). m = 0.15 kg (A1). The answer is asked for in grams, so m = 0.15 × 1000 = 150 g (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Weight and mass are different quantities: one is a force in newtons, the other is measured in kilograms.","Rearrange W = mg to make the mass the subject, then change the unit to the one the question asks for.","m = 1.47 ÷ 9.8 kg."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"The speed of a train increases steadily from 36 km h⁻¹ to 90 km h⁻¹ in 15 s. Find the acceleration of the train in m s⁻².","a":"1 m s⁻²","sol":"Convert both speeds: 36 km h⁻¹ = 36 000 ÷ 3600 = 10 m s⁻¹ and 90 km h⁻¹ = 90 000 ÷ 3600 = 25 m s⁻¹ (M1 for a correct conversion method, A1 for both values). Acceleration is a change of velocity divided by a time, so a = (25 − 10) ÷ 15 = 1, and the acceleration is 1 m s⁻² (A1, the unit is required).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The answer is asked for in m s⁻², so nothing in the working can be left in kilometres or hours.","Change both speeds into m s⁻¹, then divide the change in speed by the time it takes.","36 km h⁻¹ = 36 000 ÷ 3600 m s⁻¹, and convert the other speed the same way."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A lorry has mass 2.5 tonnes, where 1 tonne = 1000 kg. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the weight of the lorry in kilonewtons.","a":"24.5 kN","sol":"2.5 tonnes = 2.5 × 1000 = 2500 kg (B1). W = mg = 2500 × 9.8 (M1) = 24 500 N. Since 1 kN = 1000 N, W = 24 500 ÷ 1000 = 24.5 kN (A1, the unit is required).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Before any mechanics formula is used the mass must be in the base unit of mass.","Convert the mass into kilograms, find the weight in newtons, then rewrite that force in the unit the question asks for.","Mass = 2.5 × 1000 kg."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A uniform plank AB has mass 60 kg and length 4 m, and its weight acts at the midpoint of the plank. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the magnitude of the moment of the weight of the plank about the end A, stating the units of your answer.","a":"1176 N m","sol":"The weight acts at the midpoint, 4 ÷ 2 = 2 m from A (B1). The weight is W = mg = 60 × 9.8 = 588 N (M1). Moment about A = force × perpendicular distance = 588 × 2 (M1) = 1176, so the moment is 1176 N m (A1, the unit is required).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A moment needs a force and a perpendicular distance, and neither is written down in the question as it stands.","Turn the mass into a weight in newtons, then multiply that weight by the distance from A to the point at which it acts.","The weight acts 4 ÷ 2 m from A, and W = mg = 60 × 9.8 N."],"codes":["6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"The power P developed by a force is given by P = Fv, where F is a force measured in newtons and v is a speed measured in m s⁻¹. Express the S.I. unit of P in base units.","a":"kg m² s⁻³","sol":"Unit of P = unit of F × unit of v = N × m s⁻¹ (M1). In base units 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻² (B1). So the unit of P = kg m s⁻² × m s⁻¹, and collecting the powers of m and of s gives kg m² s⁻³ (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Units can be multiplied and divided in exactly the same way as the quantities themselves.","Write down the product of the two units as it stands, replace the newton by its base units, then collect the powers of m and of s.","Unit of P = N × m s⁻¹."],"codes":["6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"The tension T newtons in an elastic string is modelled by T = kx, where x is the extension of the string in metres and k is a constant. Find the S.I. base units of k.","a":"kg s⁻²","sol":"The two sides of the model must carry the same units, so rearranging gives k = T ÷ x and the units of k are the units of T divided by the units of x (M1). T is a force, so in base units it has units kg m s⁻², and x has unit m (B1). Therefore k has units kg m s⁻² ÷ m = kg s⁻² (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A model can only be correct if the two sides of the equation carry the same units.","Make the constant the subject, then replace each quantity on the right-hand side by its units and simplify.","k = T ÷ x, so the units of k are the units of T divided by the unit of x."],"codes":["6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"A car is travelling at 54 km h⁻¹ when it begins to accelerate uniformly at 2 m s⁻² for 4 s. The constant-acceleration result from the kinematics topic that follows this one, s = ut + ½at², is valid only when u, a, t and s are in S.I. units. Using s = ut + ½at², find the distance the car travels in these 4 s, giving your answer in metres.","a":"76 m","sol":"The formula is only valid when every quantity in it is in S.I. units, so convert the initial speed: u = 54 × 1000 ÷ 3600 = 15 m s⁻¹ (M1 A1). The units are then consistent, since ut has units (m s⁻¹)(s) = m and ½at² has units (m s⁻²)(s²) = m. Substituting, s = 15 × 4 + ½ × 2 × 4² (M1) = 60 + 16 = 76, so the car travels 76 m (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["One of the quantities that goes into the formula is not yet in S.I. units, and the formula is only valid when they all are.","Convert the initial speed into m s⁻¹, then substitute the initial speed, the acceleration and the time into the formula.","u = 54 × 1000 ÷ 3600 m s⁻¹."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]},{"q":"The kinematics topic that follows this one uses the result v² = u² + 2as, in which u and v are speeds in m s⁻¹, a is an acceleration in m s⁻² and s is a distance in metres. Show that each of the three terms v², u² and 2as has the same S.I. base units, and state those units.","a":"m² s⁻²","sol":"v is a speed, measured in m s⁻¹, so v² has units (m s⁻¹)² = m² s⁻² (M1 for squaring the unit using the index laws). u is also a speed, so u² likewise has units m² s⁻² (A1). The 2 is a pure number and so carries no units, and as has units (m s⁻²)(m) = m² s⁻² (M1 A1). All three terms therefore have units m² s⁻² (cso — every line must be seen, since the equality of the units is what is to be shown).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Terms that are added together in a correct formula must all carry the same units, and a pure number carries none.","Take the three terms one at a time and replace each letter by its unit, using the index laws on the powers of m and of s.","Replace v by its unit m s⁻¹ in the term v², and treat the 2 in 2as as a pure number with no units."],"codes":["6.1","6.1 Derived quantities and units"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the S.I. fundamental unit of mass and the S.I. unit of acceleration.","a":"kilogram (kg); metre per second squared (m s⁻²)"},{"q":"Convert 45 km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹.","a":"12.5 m s⁻¹"},{"q":"A parcel has mass 3 kg. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², write down its weight, with the unit.","a":"29.4 N"},{"q":"A force of 8 N acts at a perpendicular distance of 50 cm from a pivot. Find the moment of the force about the pivot, with its unit.","a":"4 N m"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The fundamental S.I. quantity of length: its unit and symbol","back":"The metre, m.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"The fundamental S.I. quantity of time: its unit and symbol","back":"The second, s.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"The fundamental S.I. quantity of mass: its unit and symbol","back":"The kilogram, kg. A mass given in grams must be divided by 1000 before it is used in any mechanics formula.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"Converting km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹","back":"Multiply by 1000 and divide by 3600, that is divide by 3.6. So 72 km h⁻¹ = 20 m s⁻¹.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"Converting m s⁻¹ into km h⁻¹","back":"Multiply by 3600 and divide by 1000, that is multiply by 3.6. So 15 m s⁻¹ = 54 km h⁻¹.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"Name one conversion, other than km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹, that a mechanics question may hide in its numbers, with its factor.","back":"1 tonne = 1000 kg; 1 g = 0.001 kg (divide grams by 1000); 1 cm = 0.01 m (divide centimetres by 100); 1 kN = 1000 N.","code":"6.1","open":true},{"front":"The rule for units inside a mechanics calculation","back":"Convert every quantity into metres, kilograms and seconds before substituting, keep them consistent throughout, and write the unit beside every numerical answer.","code":"6.1","open":false},{"front":"The S.I. unit of velocity","back":"The metre per second, m s⁻¹, because velocity is a displacement divided by a time.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false},{"front":"The S.I. unit of acceleration","back":"The metre per second squared, m s⁻², because acceleration is a change of velocity divided by a time.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false},{"front":"The newton written in S.I. base units","back":"1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², which follows from F = ma. The newton itself is the S.I. unit of force.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false},{"front":"The weight of a body of mass m","back":"W = mg newtons, acting vertically downwards, with g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless a question states another value.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false},{"front":"The S.I. unit of the moment of a force","back":"The newton metre, N m: a force in newtons multiplied by a perpendicular distance in metres. It is never written N/m.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false},{"front":"The difference between mass and weight","back":"Mass is a fundamental quantity, measured in kilograms; weight is the force of gravity on that mass, a derived quantity measured in newtons, equal to mg.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false},{"front":"Name two derived quantities used in mechanics, with the S.I. unit of each.","back":"Velocity, m s⁻¹; acceleration, m s⁻²; force, the newton N; weight, also in newtons; the moment of a force, the newton metre N m.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":true},{"front":"How to find the units of a constant in a model","back":"Make the constant the subject, replace each remaining quantity by its units, write every newton as kg m s⁻², then collect the powers of kg, m and s.","code":"6.1 Derived quantities and units","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Fundamental S.I. quantities and units: length — metre (m); time — second (s); mass — kilogram (kg). From memory.","km h⁻¹ into m s⁻¹: multiply by 1000 and divide by 3600 (divide by 3.6). From memory.","m s⁻¹ into km h⁻¹: multiply by 3600 and divide by 1000 (multiply by 3.6). From memory.","Other conversions: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 h = 3600 s, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 kN = 1000 N, 1 cm = 0.01 m, 1 g = 0.001 kg. From memory.","Velocity: m s⁻¹. Acceleration: m s⁻². From memory.","Force: F = ma, giving 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻². From memory.","Weight: W = mg newtons. From memory; the value g = 9.8 m s⁻² is given in the rubric on the front of the paper, so that value alone does not have to be recalled.","Moment of a force about a point = force × perpendicular distance from the point, measured in N m. From memory.","What the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet gives for this topic: nothing — its whole Mechanics section is the five constant-acceleration formulae under Kinematics, and there is no list of units or conversions anywhere in it. Appendix 1 of the specification lists Weight = mass × g, Newton's second law F = ma, friction F ≤ μR and the variable-acceleration kinematics results (v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt = d²r/dt², r = ∫v dt, v = ∫a dt) among the formulae students are expected to know, which will NOT appear in the booklet — so every unit, conversion and definition in this room must be written from memory."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where units cost marks","items":["A mass left in grams or tonnes and substituted straight into F = ma or W = mg: divide by 1000, or multiply by 1000, in the first line of working instead.","A distance left in centimetres inside a moment: 12 N at 30 cm gives 3.6 N m, not 360 N m.","A speed left in km h⁻¹ inside a constant-acceleration formula: convert it to m s⁻¹ before substituting, or every later line is wrong.","A numerical answer written with no unit, or the wrong one: N for a force, N m for a moment (never N/m), m s⁻¹ for a velocity, m s⁻² for an acceleration.","Take g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless told otherwise, so weights rarely come out exact: where g has been used the rubric accepts an answer given to either 2 or 3 significant figures."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:24:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, every sol line and every mark annotation checks out — 15 room questions, 4 exit tickets, 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples re-solved independently — and all seven faults from the first review are genuinely fixed (the Formulae sheet now states correctly that the booklet gives nothing for mechanics and that Appendix 1 makes W = mg, F = ma, F ≤ μR and the variable-acceleration kinematics results memory items; the four questions that copied worked examples have new contexts and numbers; two open cards with several acceptable answers have been added; the two suvat/Newton questions now quote their formulae in the stem and are labelled as coming from the following topic; P = Fv is now d=3 alongside the constant-units question; the significant-figures note now says 2 or 3; and the two offending third hints are now genuine first lines of working), leaving only a minor difficulty-tier inconsistency."}}},"M7":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"M7","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Kinematics is the backbone of Paper 3 Section B: almost every mechanics paper opens with a suvat or graph question and closes with a projectile or a variable-acceleration problem worth six or seven marks. It is also where the calculus from Pure earns a physical meaning, so it carries AO1 marks for routine technique, AO2 marks for derivations such as the range formula, and AO3 marks for modelling the flight of a ball or the motion of a car. Almost all the marks lost here are lost to signs and to a positive direction that was never written down, so a room that drills the language, the graphs, the five formulae, the calculus and the projectile split is worth more than its topic weight suggests.","idea":[{"h":"The language of motion: what is a vector and what is not","t":"Position is measured from a stated origin; displacement is the change in position. Displacement, velocity and acceleration are vectors, so in a straight line they carry a sign relative to the positive direction you choose, and each may be negative. Distance travelled and speed are scalars and can never be negative: speed is the magnitude of the velocity. A particle that goes 20 m east then 8 m west has a displacement of 12 m east but has travelled 28 m, and the examiner writes the mark scheme in exactly those terms, so read whether the question wants distance or displacement before you write anything down.","code":"7.1"},{"h":"Motion graphs: gradients and areas","t":"On a displacement–time graph the gradient is the velocity, so a horizontal section means the particle is at rest and a negative gradient means it is returning. On a velocity–time graph the gradient is the acceleration and the area between the graph and the time axis is the displacement, with any area below the axis counting as negative displacement; add the areas as positive numbers instead and you get the distance travelled. Many questions are solved by geometry alone: split the region into triangles, rectangles and trapezia, and be ready to build the graph yourself from a description of the journey and read an unknown time, velocity or distance off it.","code":"7.2"},{"h":"The five constant acceleration formulae, in one and two dimensions","t":"For constant acceleration in a straight line, v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², s = ½(u + v)t, v² = u² + 2as and s = vt − ½at². Each omits one of the five quantities, so list what you know and what you want and choose the equation that misses the one you neither have nor need. They come straight from a velocity–time graph: the gradient gives v = u + at and the area of the trapezium beneath gives s = ½(u + v)t, and eliminating v or u between them gives the rest. In two dimensions the same formulae hold with vectors, v = u + at and r = ut + ½at², written in i, j or column form, so work component by component and add the initial position vector when the origin is elsewhere.","code":"7.3"},{"h":"Calculus when the acceleration varies","t":"If the acceleration is not constant the suvat formulae are illegal and calculus takes over: v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt = d²r/dt², and going back r = ∫v dt and v = ∫a dt with the constants of integration found from the initial conditions, which the question always supplies. Displacement between two times is the definite integral of v; distance travelled is not, because you must first find where v changes sign, integrate over each interval separately and add the magnitudes. Vectors are differentiated and integrated one component at a time, so r = t²i + t^(3/2)j gives ṙ = 2ti + (3/2)t^(1/2)j.","code":"7.4"},{"h":"Projectiles: two motions linked only by time","t":"Model the object as a particle moving freely under gravity, so air resistance is ignored and the only acceleration is g downwards. Resolve the initial velocity into u cos α horizontally and u sin α vertically, then treat the two directions separately: horizontally the acceleration is zero so x = (u cos α)t, vertically the acceleration is −g so y = (u sin α)t − ½gt². Time is the only quantity shared, so almost every projectile question is solved by finding t from one direction and substituting it into the other. Derivations of the time of flight 2u sin α/g, the range u² sin 2α/g, the greatest height u² sin²α/(2g) and the path y = x tan α − gx²/(2u² cos²α) are all examinable.","code":"7.5"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading acceleration and distance from a velocity–time graph","setup":"A lorry's velocity–time graph is made of three straight segments: the velocity rises steadily from 0 to 18 m s⁻¹ over the first 12 s, stays at 18 m s⁻¹ for the next 25 s, then falls steadily to rest over a further 6 s. Find the acceleration in the first stage, the deceleration in the last stage, and the total distance travelled.","steps":["Gradient of the first sloping segment = rise over run = (18 − 0)/12 = 1.5, so the acceleration is 1.5 m s⁻².","Gradient of the last segment = (0 − 18)/6 = −3, so the deceleration is 3 m s⁻².","Area of the opening triangle = ½ × 12 × 18 = 108 m.","Area of the rectangle = 25 × 18 = 450 m, and area of the closing triangle = ½ × 6 × 18 = 54 m.","Total distance = 108 + 450 + 54 = 612 m."],"cues":["What does the gradient of a velocity–time graph tell you?","Will the gradient of the final segment be positive or negative?","Which shape sits under the first stage, and what is its area?","What are the areas of the other two pieces?","How do the three areas combine to give the distance?"]},{"title":"Vertical motion under gravity from above ground level","setup":"A ball is thrown vertically upwards from a point 1.5 m above horizontal ground with speed 17.5 m s⁻¹. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻² and ignore air resistance. Find the greatest height of the ball above the ground, and the time from projection until it hits the ground.","steps":["Take upwards as positive and measure displacement from the point of projection, so u = 17.5, a = −9.8, and at the highest point v = 0.","v² = u² + 2as gives 0 = 17.5² − 19.6s, so s = 306.25/19.6 = 15.625 above the point of projection.","Greatest height above the ground = 15.625 + 1.5 = 17.125, which is 17.1 m to 3 significant figures.","The ball hits the ground when its displacement is −1.5, so −1.5 = 17.5t − 4.9t², i.e. 4.9t² − 17.5t − 1.5 = 0.","t = (17.5 + √(306.25 + 29.4))/9.8 = (17.5 + 18.321)/9.8 = 3.6552, so the ball lands after 3.66 s to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["Which direction will you call positive, and what is the velocity at the top?","Which formula links u, v, a and s without involving the time?","The ball did not start on the ground — what must you add?","What is the displacement of the ball at the moment it lands?","How do you solve that quadratic, and which root can be used?"]},{"title":"Integrating a variable acceleration twice","setup":"A particle P moves along a straight line. At time t seconds its acceleration is a = (4 − 6t) m s⁻². When t = 0, P passes through the origin O with velocity 5 m s⁻¹. Find an expression for the displacement of P from O at time t, and the value of that displacement when t = 2.","steps":["Integrating with respect to time, v = ∫(4 − 6t) dt = 4t − 3t² + c.","The velocity is 5 when t = 0, so c = 5 and v = 4t − 3t² + 5.","Integrating once more, s = ∫(4t − 3t² + 5) dt = 2t² − t³ + 5t + k.","P passes through O at the start, so s = 0 when t = 0, giving k = 0 and s = 2t² − t³ + 5t.","Substituting t = 2: s = 2(4) − 8 + 10 = 10 m."],"cues":["Which operation takes you from acceleration to velocity?","What piece of information fixes the constant of integration?","What must you do to the velocity to reach displacement?","What does 'passes through O' tell you about s at the start?","What goes into the displacement expression now?"]},{"title":"A projectile launched at an angle from level ground","setup":"A golf ball is struck from a point O on level ground with speed 28 m s⁻¹ at an angle of 35° above the horizontal. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻² and model the ball as a particle moving freely under gravity. Find the time of flight, the horizontal range and the greatest height reached.","steps":["Resolve the initial velocity: horizontally 28 cos 35° = 22.94, vertically 28 sin 35° = 16.06, both in m s⁻¹.","Vertically, with up positive, u = 16.06 and a = −9.8; the ball lands when its vertical displacement is zero, so 0 = 16.06t − 4.9t².","Factorising, t(16.06 − 4.9t) = 0, so the time of flight is 16.06/4.9 = 3.278 s.","The horizontal velocity never changes, so the range = 22.94 × 3.278 = 75.2 m to 3 significant figures.","At the greatest height the vertical velocity is zero: 0 = 16.06² − 19.6s, so s = 257.9/19.6 = 13.2 m to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["What must be done to the initial speed before any suvat is used?","What is the vertical displacement at the moment the ball lands?","Which root of that equation is the time of flight?","What happens to the horizontal velocity during the flight?","What is the vertical velocity at the top of the path?"]},{"title":"Constant acceleration in two dimensions with vectors","setup":"A boat is modelled as a particle moving in a horizontal plane with constant acceleration. At time t = 0 it passes through the point with position vector (2i + 5j) m relative to a fixed origin O, moving with velocity (6i − 2j) m s⁻¹, and its acceleration is (−1.2i + 0.8j) m s⁻². Find the velocity and the speed of the boat when t = 5, and its position vector at that time.","steps":["Use v = u + at with the vectors kept in component form, taking t = 5: v = (6i − 2j) + 5(−1.2i + 0.8j).","Collecting components, v = (6 − 6)i + (−2 + 4)j, so the velocity is 2j m s⁻¹.","The speed is the magnitude of that vector, √(0² + 2²) = 2 m s⁻¹, and the boat is momentarily moving in the direction of j only.","For the position, r = r₀ + ut + ½at² = (2i + 5j) + 5(6i − 2j) + ½ × 25 × (−1.2i + 0.8j).","Collecting components again, r = (2 + 30 − 15)i + (5 − 10 + 10)j = (17i + 5j) m."],"cues":["Which constant acceleration formula gives velocity from u, a and t?","What do the i and j components come to separately?","How do you turn a velocity vector into a speed?","Which formula gives position, and what must be added for the starting point?","What are the i and j components of that position vector?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A velocity–time graph dips below the time axis between t = 6 and t = 10. What does the area of that region represent?","a":"Negative displacement — the particle is moving in the negative direction. Count the area as positive only when you want the distance travelled."},{"q":"Taking upwards as positive, what value of a do you use for a ball thrown vertically upwards?","a":"a = −9.8 m s⁻²"},{"q":"A particle moves in a straight line with v = 2t³ − 5 m s⁻¹. What is its acceleration at time t?","a":"a = 6t² m s⁻²"},{"q":"What is the horizontal acceleration of a projectile modelled as moving freely under gravity?","a":"Zero, so the horizontal component of velocity stays constant throughout the flight."}]},"room":{"facts":["The five constant acceleration formulae: v = u + at; s = ut + ½at²; s = ½(u + v)t; v² = u² + 2as; s = vt − ½at². Each one omits a different quantity, and all five are given in the formulae booklet under Mechanics › Kinematics.","Gradient of a displacement–time graph = velocity; gradient of a velocity–time graph = acceleration; area between a velocity–time graph and the time axis = displacement, with area below the axis negative.","Variable acceleration: v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt = d²r/dt², r = ∫ v dt, v = ∫ a dt, with the constants of integration found from the initial conditions.","Constant acceleration in 2-D: v = u + at and r = ut + ½at² with u, v, a and r as vectors; add the initial position vector r₀ when position is measured from a fixed origin.","Projectile components: horizontally a = 0 and x = (u cos α)t; vertically a = −g and y = (u sin α)t − ½gt². The two motions share only the time t.","For a projectile on level ground: time of flight = 2u sin α/g, range = u² sin 2α/g, greatest height = u² sin²α/(2g), and the path is y = x tan α − gx²/(2u² cos²α)."],"questions":[{"q":"A particle moves along a straight line. It travels 12 m due east, then 7 m due west, taking 5 s in total. Taking east as the positive direction, find the displacement of the particle, the distance it has travelled, and its average speed.","a":"Displacement 5 m east; distance travelled 19 m; average speed 3.8 m s⁻¹","sol":"Displacement is the change in position, so it is +12 − 7 = 5, i.e. 5 m east (B1). Distance travelled is a scalar and adds both legs whatever the direction: 12 + 7 = 19 m (B1). Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 19 ÷ 5 = 3.8 m s⁻¹ (M1 for distance ÷ time, A1). Note that average velocity would be 5 ÷ 5 = 1 m s⁻¹ east, which is not what was asked.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["One of these three quantities is a vector and two of them are scalars — decide which before you combine the two legs of the journey.","Displacement is the change in position, so the westward leg counts against the eastward one; distance adds every part of the path whatever its direction.","Average speed is total distance divided by total time, not total displacement divided by total time."],"codes":["7.1"]},{"q":"A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly at 1.5 m s⁻² for 8 s along a straight road. Find the speed of the car at the end of the 8 s and the distance it travels in that time.","a":"12 m s⁻¹; 48 m","sol":"List the quantities: u = 0, a = 1.5, t = 8. For the speed use v = u + at = 0 + 1.5 × 8 (M1) = 12 m s⁻¹ (A1). For the distance use s = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½ × 1.5 × 8² (M1) = ½ × 1.5 × 64 = 48 m (A1). The same distance follows from s = ½(u + v)t = ½ × 12 × 8 = 48 m.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Write down which of s, u, v, a and t you have been given and which one the question wants each time.","One constant acceleration formula gives v from u, a and t; a different one gives s from u, a and t.","Starting from rest means u = 0, so the second formula reduces to s = ½at²."],"codes":["7.3"]},{"q":"A cyclist travelling in a straight line at 14 m s⁻¹ brakes and decelerates uniformly to rest in a distance of 35 m. Find the magnitude of the deceleration.","a":"2.8 m s⁻²","sol":"Taking the direction of motion as positive, u = 14, v = 0, s = 35, and t is neither known nor wanted, so use v² = u² + 2as and substitute: 0 = 14² + 2 × a × 35 (M1). Then 70a = −196, so a = −2.8 (A1). The negative sign shows the acceleration is opposite to the motion, so the magnitude of the deceleration is 2.8 m s⁻² (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["You know the starting speed, the finishing speed and the distance, but nothing about the time — that tells you which formula to reach for.","Use the constant acceleration formula that contains s, u, v and a but not t.","Substitute u = 14, v = 0 and s = 35 and solve the resulting linear equation for a, then quote its magnitude."],"codes":["7.3"]},{"q":"A particle moves along a straight line so that its velocity at time t seconds is v = (4t³ − 6t) m s⁻¹. Find the acceleration of the particle when t = 2.","a":"42 m s⁻²","sol":"Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, so a = dv/dt (M1). Differentiating term by term, a = 12t² − 6 (A1). Substituting t = 2 gives a = 12 × 4 − 6 = 48 − 6 (M1) = 42 m s⁻² (A1).","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The velocity is not constant here, so no suvat formula applies — think about what acceleration means as a rate of change.","Acceleration is dv/dt, so differentiate the expression for v with respect to t.","Differentiate 4t³ − 6t first, and only then put t = 2 into the result."],"codes":["7.4"]},{"q":"A car's velocity–time graph is a straight line from (0 s, 0 m s⁻¹) to (6 s, 15 m s⁻¹), followed by a horizontal line from (6 s, 15 m s⁻¹) to (20 s, 15 m s⁻¹). Find the acceleration during the first 6 s and the distance travelled in the first 20 s.","a":"Acceleration 2.5 m s⁻²; distance 255 m","sol":"The acceleration is the gradient of the velocity–time graph: (15 − 0)/(6 − 0) (M1) = 2.5 m s⁻² (A1). The distance is the area between the graph and the time axis. The sloping part gives a triangle of area ½ × 6 × 15 = 45 m (M1) and the level part gives a rectangle of area (20 − 6) × 15 = 14 × 15 = 210 m (M1). Total distance = 45 + 210 = 255 m (A1).","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["One of the two answers is read off as a gradient and the other as an area — decide which is which before calculating.","Acceleration is the gradient of a velocity–time graph; the area between the graph and the time axis gives the displacement.","Split the region under the graph into a triangle for the first stage and a rectangle for the second, and note the second stage lasts 20 − 6 seconds."],"codes":["7.2"]},{"q":"A stone is thrown vertically upwards from ground level with speed 21 m s⁻¹ and moves freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the greatest height reached by the stone and the total time until it returns to the ground, giving the time to 3 significant figures.","a":"Greatest height 22.5 m; total time 4.29 s","sol":"Take upwards as positive, so u = 21 and a = −9.8. At the greatest height v = 0 (B1). Using v² = u² + 2as: 0 = 21² − 19.6s (M1), so s = 441/19.6 = 22.5 m (A1). For the total time, the stone returns to the ground when s = 0, so 0 = 21t − 4.9t² (M1), giving t(21 − 4.9t) = 0 and t = 21/4.9 = 4.2857… (M1), which is 4.29 s to 3 significant figures (A1). The root t = 0 is the moment of projection and is rejected.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Choose a positive direction and write down what the velocity must be at the highest point of the flight.","Use the formula linking u, v, a and s for the height, and the formula linking s, u, a and t for the time of the whole flight.","For the total time set the displacement back to zero and factorise the resulting quadratic in t, rejecting the root at the start."],"codes":["7.3"]},{"q":"A train travelling in a straight line decelerates uniformly from 30 m s⁻¹ to 12 m s⁻¹ in 30 s, and then travels at a constant 12 m s⁻¹ for a further T seconds. The total distance covered in the whole journey is 1110 m. Find T.","a":"T = 40","sol":"First stage: the distance is the area of a trapezium under the velocity–time graph, s = ½(u + v)t = ½(30 + 12) × 30 (M1) = 21 × 30 = 630 m (A1). Second stage: the remaining distance is 1110 − 630 = 480 m (M1). At a constant 12 m s⁻¹ the time for this is T = 480/12 (M1) = 40 s (A1). A sketch of the graph shows the trapezium followed by a rectangle of height 12 and width T.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Sketch the velocity–time graph: a sloping line down to 12 m s⁻¹ followed by a horizontal line, and the total area is given.","Find the area under the first stage, subtract it from the total distance, and use the constant speed for the rest.","The first stage is a trapezium, so its area is ½(30 + 12) × 30."],"codes":["7.2","7.3"]},{"q":"A particle P moves along a straight line. At time t seconds its acceleration is a = (6t − 10) m s⁻². When t = 0, P is at the origin O and has velocity 4 m s⁻¹. Find the displacement of P from O when t = 3.","a":"−6 m (6 m from O in the negative direction)","sol":"Integrate the acceleration: v = ∫(6t − 10) dt = 3t² − 10t + c (M1 A1). When t = 0, v = 4, so c = 4 and v = 3t² − 10t + 4 (A1). Integrate again, using the fact that P is at O when t = 0 so that the second constant is zero: s = ∫(3t² − 10t + 4) dt = t³ − 5t² + 4t (M1 A1). When t = 3, s = 27 − 45 + 12 = −6, so the displacement is −6 m, i.e. 6 m from O in the negative direction (A1).","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The acceleration changes with time, so the constant acceleration formulae cannot be used at all here.","Integrate the acceleration to get velocity, then integrate the velocity to get displacement, fixing a constant at each stage.","Start with v = ∫(6t − 10) dt = 3t² − 10t + c and use the velocity at t = 0 to find c."],"codes":["7.4"]},{"q":"A particle moves along a straight line with velocity v = (t² − 5t + 6) m s⁻¹ at time t seconds, where t ≥ 0. Find the total distance travelled by the particle between t = 0 and t = 3, giving your answer as an exact fraction.","a":"29/6 m","sol":"Factorise to find where the velocity changes sign: v = (t − 2)(t − 3), so v = 0 at t = 2 and t = 3 (M1 A1). For 0 ≤ t < 2 the velocity is positive and for 2 < t < 3 it is negative, so the journey must be split at t = 2 (B1). The antiderivative is t³/3 − 5t²/2 + 6t (M1). From 0 to 2: (8/3 − 10 + 12) − 0 = 14/3 (A1). From 2 to 3: (9 − 45/2 + 18) − 14/3 = 9/2 − 14/3 = −1/6, so 1/6 m is travelled in the negative direction (A1). Total distance = 14/3 + 1/6 = 29/6 m (A1). The displacement, by contrast, is only 14/3 − 1/6 = 27/6 = 9/2 m.","m":7,"d":2,"hints":["Distance travelled and displacement are only the same while the particle keeps moving the same way, so check whether it ever turns round.","Find the times in the interval at which v = 0, integrate v over each part separately, and add the magnitudes of the results.","Factorise t² − 5t + 6 to locate the change of sign before integrating anything."],"codes":["7.4","7.1"]},{"q":"A small ball is projected horizontally with speed 15 m s⁻¹ from a point 40 m above horizontal ground and moves freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the time the ball takes to reach the ground and its horizontal distance from the point of projection when it lands, each to 3 significant figures.","a":"2.86 s; 42.9 m","sol":"Treat the two directions separately. Vertically, taking downwards as positive, u = 0, a = 9.8, s = 40, so s = ut + ½at² gives 40 = 4.9t² (M1 A1). Hence t² = 400/49 and t = 20/7 = 2.857… (M1), which is 2.86 s to 3 significant figures (A1). Horizontally there is no acceleration, so the velocity stays at 15 m s⁻¹ and x = 15t (M1) = 15 × 20/7 = 300/7 = 42.857… = 42.9 m to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["The ball is thrown horizontally, so think about what its initial vertical velocity must be.","Use the vertical motion, which has constant acceleration g, to find the time of flight, then use that time in the horizontal motion.","Vertically, with down positive, 40 = 0 × t + ½ × 9.8 × t²."],"codes":["7.5"]},{"q":"A model hovercraft is moving in a horizontal plane with constant acceleration (0.4i − 0.3j) m s⁻². At time t = 0 it passes through the origin O with velocity (−3i + 4j) m s⁻¹. Find the value of t at which the hovercraft is moving parallel to the vector i + j, and its distance from O at that time.","a":"t = 10; distance 26.9 m (exactly 5√29 m)","sol":"Velocity at time t: v = u + at = (−3i + 4j) + t(0.4i − 0.3j) (M1) = (−3 + 0.4t)i + (4 − 0.3t)j (A1). A vector pi + qj is parallel to i + j exactly when p = q, so −3 + 0.4t = 4 − 0.3t (M1). Hence 0.7t = 7 and t = 10 (A1); the velocity is then (i + j) m s⁻¹, a positive multiple of i + j, so the direction is right. Position at t = 10: r = ut + ½at² = 10(−3i + 4j) + ½ × 10² × (0.4i − 0.3j) = (−30i + 40j) + (20i − 15j) = (−10i + 25j) m (M1). Distance from O = |r| = √((−10)² + 25²) = √725 = 26.9 m to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Two vectors are parallel when one is a scalar multiple of the other, so ask what has to be true of the two components of the velocity.","Write the velocity at time t with v = u + at, form an equation in t from the parallel condition, then use r = ut + ½at² for the position and take its magnitude.","Set the i component of (−3i + 4j) + t(0.4i − 0.3j) equal to its j component and solve for t."],"codes":["7.3"]},{"q":"A particle is projected from a point O on horizontal ground with speed u at an angle α above the horizontal and moves freely under gravity. (a) Show that the horizontal range on the ground is R = u² sin 2α / g. (b) Hence find, to 3 significant figures, the two possible angles of projection that give a range of 40 m when u = 25 m s⁻¹ and g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"α = 19.4° or α = 70.6°","sol":"(a) Vertically, taking up as positive, the initial velocity component is u sin α and a = −g, so the displacement is y = (u sin α)t − ½gt² (M1). On landing y = 0, so t((u sin α) − ½gt) = 0 and the time of flight is t = 2u sin α/g (A1). Horizontally the velocity u cos α is constant, so R = (u cos α) × 2u sin α/g = 2u² sin α cos α/g (M1). Using the identity sin 2α = 2 sin α cos α gives R = u² sin 2α/g as required (A1 cso). (b) Substituting, 40 = 25² sin 2α/9.8 (M1), so sin 2α = 40 × 9.8/625 = 0.6272 (A1). Hence 2α = 38.84° or 2α = 180° − 38.84° = 141.16° (M1), so α = 19.4° or α = 70.6° to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The horizontal and vertical motions are linked only by the time, and the range is what the horizontal motion covers during the whole flight.","Get the time of flight from the vertical displacement being zero on landing, substitute it into the horizontal distance, then use a double angle identity.","In part (b) remember that an equation of the form sin X = k has a second solution X = 180° − the first, and both give a valid angle of projection."],"codes":["7.5","7.3"]},{"q":"A stone is projected from a point A with speed 20 m s⁻¹ at an angle of 40° above the horizontal. A is 5 m above horizontal ground and the stone moves freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the greatest height of the stone above the ground and the speed with which it strikes the ground, each to 3 significant figures.","a":"Greatest height 13.4 m above the ground; striking speed 22.3 m s⁻¹","sol":"Resolve: horizontal component 20 cos 40° = 15.32 m s⁻¹ (constant), vertical component 20 sin 40° = 12.856 m s⁻¹ (M1 A1). At the greatest height the vertical velocity is zero, so 0 = 12.856² − 19.6s (M1), giving s = 165.27/19.6 = 8.432 m above A (A1). Height above the ground = 8.432 + 5 = 13.4 m to 3 significant figures (A1). For the striking speed, apply v² = u² + 2as vertically over a fall of 5 m below A: v_y² = 12.856² + 2 × 9.8 × 5 = 165.27 + 98 = 263.27 (M1). The horizontal component is unchanged, so v² = 15.32² + 263.27 = 234.7 + 263.3 = 498 and v = 22.316 (M1) = 22.3 m s⁻¹ (A1). Note this equals √(20² + 2 × 9.8 × 5), since only the vertical drop changes the speed.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["The stone does not land at the height it was launched from, so the vertical displacement on landing is not zero.","Work out the greatest height above the launch point first and then adjust for the height of A, and for the final speed combine the unchanged horizontal component with the new vertical component.","Resolve the initial velocity into 20 cos 40° horizontally and 20 sin 40° vertically before using any formula."],"codes":["7.5"]},{"q":"A particle moves in a plane with acceleration a = (6t i + (12t² − 4) j) m s⁻² at time t seconds. When t = 0 its velocity is (2i − 3j) m s⁻¹. Find the speed of the particle when t = 2, giving your answer in exact form.","a":"7√13 m s⁻¹","sol":"Integrate the acceleration component by component: v = ∫a dt = 3t² i + (4t³ − 4t) j + c (M1 A1). When t = 0, v = 2i − 3j, so c = 2i − 3j and v = (3t² + 2)i + (4t³ − 4t − 3)j (A1). When t = 2, v = (12 + 2)i + (32 − 8 − 3)j = 14i + 21j (M1 A1). Speed = |v| = √(14² + 21²) = √(196 + 441) = √637 (M1), and 637 = 49 × 13, so the speed is 7√13 m s⁻¹ (A1).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["The acceleration is a vector that varies with time, so neither the suvat formulae nor a single integration will be enough on their own.","Integrate each component with respect to t, use the velocity at t = 0 to find the constant vector of integration, then take the magnitude.","The constant of integration is itself a vector, so write it as c = c₁i + c₂j (or just as a vector c) before substituting t = 0."],"codes":["7.4"]},{"q":"A car passes a stationary motorcycle at a point P, travelling at a constant 25 m s⁻¹ along a straight road. Four seconds later the motorcycle starts from rest at P and accelerates uniformly at 3 m s⁻² until it reaches 30 m s⁻¹, which it then maintains. Find the time, measured from the moment the car passed P, at which the motorcycle draws level with the car.","a":"54 s","sol":"The motorcycle reaches 30 m s⁻¹ after 30/3 = 10 s of acceleration (M1), covering ½ × 3 × 10² = 150 m (A1). That instant is 4 + 10 = 14 s after the car passed P, by which time the car has travelled 25 × 14 = 350 m (M1 A1). The motorcycle is therefore 350 − 150 = 200 m behind and is now closing at 30 − 25 = 5 m s⁻¹ (M1), so it needs a further 200/5 = 40 s (A1). Total time from P = 14 + 40 = 54 s (A1). Check: car 25 × 54 = 1350 m; motorcycle 150 + 30 × 40 = 1350 m.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["Sketch both journeys on one velocity–time graph, remembering that the motorcycle's graph starts four seconds after the car's.","The motorcycle's motion has two stages, so deal with the accelerating stage first and find how far behind it still is when it reaches its top speed.","The acceleration stage lasts 30 ÷ 3 seconds; work out how far each vehicle has gone by the end of it before considering the constant speed stage."],"codes":["7.2","7.3"]},{"q":"A particle moves in a straight line with constant acceleration a. Its velocity is u at time 0 and v at time t, and its displacement over that time is s. By considering the area under the velocity–time graph, derive the formula s = ut + ½at².","a":"s = ½(u + v)t = ½(u + (u + at))t = ut + ½at²","sol":"With constant acceleration the velocity–time graph is a straight line from (0, u) to (t, v), so the region between the graph and the time axis is a trapezium with parallel sides u and v and width t (B1). The displacement is that area, so s = ½(u + v)t (M1 A1). The gradient of the line is the acceleration, so (v − u)/t = a, giving v = u + at, and substituting for v gives s = ½(u + u + at)t = ½(2u + at)t (M1) = ut + ½at² as required (A1 cso). No step may assume the formula being derived.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["With constant acceleration the velocity–time graph is a straight line, so the region beneath it is a shape whose area you already know.","Write the area of that region in terms of u, v and t, then replace v using the gradient of the same line.","The parallel sides of the trapezium have lengths u and v and the width is t."],"codes":["7.2","7.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A particle travels 9 m due north, then 4 m due south. State its displacement and the distance it has travelled.","a":"Displacement 5 m due north; distance travelled 13 m"},{"q":"What quantity is given by the area between a velocity–time graph and the time axis?","a":"The displacement (area below the axis counting as negative)"},{"q":"A ball is projected horizontally at 8 m s⁻¹ from a point 4.9 m above horizontal ground, with g = 9.8 m s⁻². How long does it take to reach the ground?","a":"1 s"},{"q":"A particle moves in a straight line with velocity v = (6t² − 2t) m s⁻¹. Find its acceleration when t = 4.","a":"46 m s⁻²"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Displacement","back":"The change in position of a particle, measured from a stated origin. A vector, so it carries a sign or direction and may be negative.","code":"7.1","open":false},{"front":"Distance travelled","back":"The total length of the path covered, adding every leg of the journey whatever its direction. A scalar, so it is never negative.","code":"7.1","open":false},{"front":"Speed and velocity","back":"Speed is the magnitude of the velocity. Velocity is a vector and may be negative; speed is a scalar and never is.","code":"7.1","open":false},{"front":"Acceleration","back":"The rate of change of velocity with respect to time, in m s⁻². A vector: a negative value means the acceleration acts in the negative direction.","code":"7.1","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a displacement–time graph","back":"The velocity at that instant. A horizontal section means the particle is at rest; a negative gradient means it is moving in the negative direction.","code":"7.2","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a velocity–time graph","back":"The acceleration at that instant.","code":"7.2","open":false},{"front":"Area between a velocity–time graph and the time axis","back":"The displacement, with area below the axis counting as negative. Add the areas as positive numbers instead to get the distance travelled.","code":"7.2","open":false},{"front":"The five constant acceleration formulae","back":"v = u + at; s = ut + ½at²; s = ½(u + v)t; v² = u² + 2as; s = vt − ½at². Each one leaves out a different quantity.","code":"7.3","open":false},{"front":"Constant acceleration in two dimensions","back":"v = u + at and r = ut + ½at², with u, v, a and r as vectors in i, j or column form. Add r₀ when position is measured from a fixed origin.","code":"7.3","open":false},{"front":"Vertical motion under gravity (upwards positive)","back":"a = −g = −9.8 m s⁻²; at the greatest height v = 0; returning to the point of projection means s = 0.","code":"7.3","open":false},{"front":"Calculus in kinematics","back":"v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt = d²r/dt², r = ∫ v dt, v = ∫ a dt. Every integration needs a constant fixed by the initial conditions.","code":"7.4","open":false},{"front":"Differentiating a vector with respect to time","back":"Differentiate each component separately: if r = t²i + t^(3/2)j then ṙ = 2ti + (3/2)t^(1/2)j and r̈ = 2i + (3/4)t^(−1/2)j.","code":"7.4","open":false},{"front":"The two components of projectile motion","back":"Horizontally: acceleration 0, so x = (u cos α)t. Vertically: acceleration −g, so y = (u sin α)t − ½gt². The two are linked only by t.","code":"7.5","open":false},{"front":"Time of flight, range and greatest height on level ground","back":"T = 2u sin α/g, R = u² sin 2α/g, H = u² sin²α/(2g). The range is greatest when α = 45°.","code":"7.5","open":false},{"front":"Equation of the path of a projectile","back":"y = x tan α − gx²/(2u² cos²α), obtained by eliminating t between x = (u cos α)t and y = (u sin α)t − ½gt².","code":"7.5","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["The Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet gives the five constant-acceleration formulae under Mechanics › Kinematics, and nothing else for this room: every other result below is written from memory. Appendix 1 of the specification lists the variable-acceleration relations (with weight = mass × g, F ≤ μR and F = ma) among the formulae you are expected to know.","v = u + at — constant acceleration in a straight line. Given in the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet under Mechanics › Kinematics.","s = ut + ½at² — constant acceleration. Given in the booklet.","s = ½(u + v)t — constant acceleration (the area of the trapezium under the velocity–time graph). Given in the booklet.","v² = u² + 2as — constant acceleration with no t. Given in the booklet.","s = vt − ½at² — constant acceleration with no u. Given in the booklet.","v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt = d²r/dt², r = ∫ v dt, v = ∫ a dt — motion in a straight line with variable acceleration. Appendix 1 of the specification lists exactly these under Mechanics > Kinematics, so they are from memory: you must know which one to use and must find every constant of integration from the initial conditions.","v = u + at and r = ut + ½at² with u, v, a and r as vectors — constant acceleration in two dimensions. The booklet gives these only in the straight-line scalar form, so learn the vector version, and add r₀ when position is measured from a fixed origin.","Projectile components: x = (u cos α)t horizontally and y = (u sin α)t − ½gt² vertically. Not in the booklet: learn them.","Projectile results on level ground: time of flight 2u sin α/g, range u² sin 2α/g, greatest height u² sin²α/(2g). Not in the booklet, and the derivation of any of them may be asked for.","Equation of the path: y = x tan α − gx²/(2u² cos²α). Not in the booklet; derive it by eliminating t between the two component equations.","sin 2α = 2 sin α cos α — needed to reach the range formula. Appendix 1 lists sin 2A ≡ 2 sin A cos A among the trigonometric identities you are expected to know, so this one is from memory too.","Unless a question says otherwise, take g = 9.8 m s⁻² and state that you are doing so."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Signs, modelling and the marks students lose","items":["Write down the positive direction before the first line of working. Most lost marks in this room are sign errors in a, u or s rather than mistakes in the algebra.","The suvat formulae are only valid while the acceleration is constant. If the question gives a or v as a function of t, differentiate or integrate instead.","If the acceleration changes partway through a journey, split the motion into stages and apply the formulae to each stage separately, carrying the final velocity of one stage in as the initial velocity of the next.","'Distance travelled' is not 'displacement' whenever the particle turns round: find where v = 0, integrate over each interval, and add the magnitudes.","Modelling assumptions to quote when asked: the object is a particle, so its size and any spin are ignored; air resistance is ignored; g is constant; the ground is horizontal. Each one limits the model, and real ranges are shorter than the model predicts.","In two dimensions keep i and j components in separate columns of working — the mark scheme awards a method mark for a correct vector equation before any arithmetic.","Quote non-exact answers to 3 significant figures unless told otherwise, and always give the unit; an 'exact' instruction forbids a decimal, so leave surds and fractions alone."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:16:56.428629Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Mathematically clean throughout — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 check questions and the 5 worked examples re-solve exactly as keyed, the Formulae sheet now correctly tells students the booklet gives nothing for mechanics and that sin 2α = 2 sin α cos α is memory work, and the only blemish left is that the room's first question is already answered word for word in the lesson text."}}},"M8":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"M8","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Mechanics is the whole of Section B of Paper 3, and once you are past the opening kinematics question almost every mark in it is a forces mark. This room holds the one equation the section is built on, F = ma, and the discipline that turns it into marks: draw the forces, choose a positive direction, resolve, and write one equation for one body in one direction. The same routine answers a book resting on a table, a lift accelerating downwards, a car towing a trailer, a crate dragged over rough ground and a particle sliding down a slope, so time spent here pays out across every mechanics question, including the modelling and 'show that' parts that carry the AO2 and AO3 marks.","idea":[{"h":"What a force is, and Newton's first law","code":"8.1","t":"A force is a push or a pull measured in newtons, and the first thing an examiner wants to see is that you can name the ones acting and show them on a diagram: the weight mg vertically down, the normal reaction R perpendicular to the surface, the tension in a string or rod pulling the body, the thrust (compression) in a rod pushing it, and resistance or friction opposing the motion. Newton's first law says a body stays at rest, or keeps moving with constant velocity in a straight line, unless a resultant external force acts. So 'at rest', 'in equilibrium' and 'moving at constant speed in a straight line' all mean the same thing to you: the resultant force is zero in every direction. Questions exploit this by giving you a constant velocity and asking for an unknown force."},{"h":"Newton's second law, one direction at a time","code":"8.2","t":"Newton's second law says the resultant force on a body equals mass times acceleration: F = ma, with the resultant and the acceleration in the same direction. In practice you apply it along one chosen direction at a time. Choose the direction of motion as positive, add up the components of every force along it, and set the total equal to ma. Perpendicular to that direction there is usually no acceleration, so the components balance and you get the normal reaction. On an inclined plane the natural directions are along a line of greatest slope and perpendicular to the plane, and the weight has to be resolved into mg sin α and mg cos α. Forces may also be given in i, j form, in which case F = ma is a vector equation and you work component by component."},{"h":"Weight, gravity and the accuracy of your answer","code":"8.3","t":"Weight is the force of gravity on a body, W = mg newtons, always vertically downwards; mass in kilograms is not a force. Unless a question says otherwise you take g = 9.8 m s⁻², the value printed on the front of the paper, and because that value is only given to 2 significant figures every answer that depends on g is given to 2 significant figures. Some questions specify a different value, for example g = 10 m s⁻², and you must then use theirs. The inverse square law is not required: g is treated as constant through a question, but you should know that it is not a universal constant and depends on where you are. A body moving freely under gravity has acceleration g downwards, which feeds straight into the constant-acceleration formulae."},{"h":"Newton's third law, connected particles and lifts","code":"8.4","t":"Newton's third law says that if body A exerts a force on body B, then B exerts an equal and opposite force on A. That is what makes the tension the same at both ends of a light string, what lets the tow bar pull the trailer forwards and the car backwards, and what makes the floor of a lift push up on a passenger with the same force the passenger presses down. For connected particles over a smooth pulley, or a car and trailer, or a lift and its passenger, write F = ma for each body separately using the same magnitude of acceleration and the same connecting force. Adding the equations eliminates the internal force and gives the acceleration; the whole-system equation alone can never give you the tension, so you must always take one body on its own for that."},{"h":"Adding forces: resultants and motion in a plane","code":"8.5","t":"When several forces act in different directions, replace them by a single resultant. Either resolve each force into components along two perpendicular directions and add the components, or draw a vector triangle or polygon and use the cosine and sine rules. A force of magnitude F at angle θ to a chosen direction has components F cos θ along it and F sin θ perpendicular to it, and components X and Y combine into a resultant of magnitude √(X² + Y²) at angle arctan(Y/X) to the X direction. Once you have the resultant, Newton's second law applies in the plane: the acceleration has magnitude resultant ÷ mass and points along the resultant. In i, j questions the same work is done by adding vectors and dividing by the mass."},{"h":"Friction: F ≤ μR","code":"8.6","t":"A rough surface exerts friction along the surface, opposing the motion or the attempted motion. The model is F ≤ μR, where R is the normal reaction and μ the coefficient of friction. The inequality matters: in ordinary equilibrium friction takes whatever value up to μR is needed to hold the body still, so you may not write F = μR. Only when the body is moving, or is in limiting equilibrium (on the point of sliding), does friction take its maximum value F = μR. So the routine is: resolve perpendicular to the surface to find R, decide whether friction is limiting, then resolve along the surface. A pull at an angle changes R, and with it the friction, which is where most marks are lost."}],"examples":[{"title":"Driving force against resistance on a level road","setup":"A car of mass 1200 kg is pulled along a straight horizontal road by a driving force of 2000 N. The total resistance to motion is modelled as a single constant horizontal force of 800 N. Find the acceleration of the car.","steps":["Model the car as a particle and take its direction of travel as positive; vertically the weight and the normal reaction balance, so only two forces matter — the driving force forwards and the resistance backwards.","Newton's second law along the road: driving force − resistance = ma, so 2000 − 800 = 1200a.","Work out the left-hand side: the resultant force is 1200 N, so 1200 = 1200a.","a = 1200 ÷ 1200 = 1.0 m s⁻², in the direction of motion."],"cues":["Which forces act on the car, and which of them have a component along the road?","Which law connects the forces along the road with the acceleration?","What is left when the backward force is taken from the forward one?","What do you divide the resultant by, and what unit does the answer carry?"]},{"title":"Two particles over a smooth pulley","setup":"Particles A and B, of masses 3 kg and 5 kg, are connected by a light inextensible string passing over a smooth fixed pulley. Both particles hang vertically and the system is released from rest. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the acceleration of the particles and the tension in the string.","steps":["The string is light and inextensible and the pulley is smooth, so the tension T N is the same in both parts and both particles have acceleration of magnitude a m s⁻²; the heavier particle B moves down and A moves up.","For B, taking downwards as positive: 5 × 9.8 − T = 5a.","For A, taking upwards as positive: T − 3 × 9.8 = 3a.","Adding the two equations eliminates T: 2 × 9.8 = 8a, so a = 19.6 ÷ 8 = 2.45, which is 2.5 m s⁻² to 2 significant figures.","Substituting a = 2.45 into T − 29.4 = 3a gives T = 29.4 + 7.35 = 36.75, which is 37 N to 2 significant figures."],"cues":["What does the pulley being smooth and the string light and inextensible tell you about T and about the two accelerations?","Which way does the heavier particle accelerate, and what is its equation of motion?","What is the corresponding equation for the lighter particle, taking its acceleration as positive?","What happens to T if you add the two equations?","Which of the two equations now gives you the tension in one step?"]},{"title":"A pull at an angle on rough ground","setup":"A box of mass 4 kg is pulled across rough horizontal ground by a string inclined at 20° above the horizontal. The tension in the string is 20 N and the coefficient of friction between the box and the ground is 0.3. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the acceleration of the box.","steps":["Four forces act: the weight 4 × 9.8 = 39.2 N downwards, the normal reaction R N upwards, the tension at 20° above the horizontal and the friction F N backwards along the ground.","Resolve vertically, where there is no acceleration: R + 20 sin 20° = 39.2, so R = 39.2 − 6.84 = 32.36 N.","The box is moving, so friction takes its maximum value: F = μR = 0.3 × 32.36 = 9.71 N.","Resolve horizontally with the direction of motion positive: 20 cos 20° − F = 4a, so 18.794 − 9.708 = 4a.","4a = 9.086, so a = 2.27, which is 2.3 m s⁻² to 2 significant figures."],"cues":["Which four forces act on the box, and what is its weight in newtons?","Why is the normal reaction not simply equal to the weight here?","Is the friction at its maximum value, and how do you know?","Which components act along the ground, and what does Newton's second law give?","What is left to do once the two horizontal forces have been combined?"]},{"title":"The resultant of two forces at an angle","setup":"Two forces, of magnitudes 8 N and 5 N, act on a particle of mass 2 kg. The angle between the two forces is 60°. Find the magnitude and direction of the resultant force, and hence the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle.","steps":["Take axes along and perpendicular to the larger force, so the other one makes an angle of 60° with the positive x-axis.","Add the components along the x-axis: 8 + 5 cos 60° = 8 + 2.5 = 10.5 N.","Add the components along the y-axis: 5 sin 60° = 4.330 N.","Magnitude of the resultant √(10.5² + 4.330²) = √129 = 11.4 N (3 s.f.); its direction is given by tan θ = 4.330 ÷ 10.5, so θ = 22.4° from the larger force.","Newton's second law in the plane: acceleration = resultant ÷ mass = 11.358 ÷ 2 = 5.68 m s⁻² (3 s.f.), along the resultant."],"cues":["Which directions will you resolve along to make the working shortest?","What is the component of each force along the x-axis?","What is the component of each force along the y-axis?","How do two perpendicular components combine into a magnitude and an angle?","What turns the resultant force into an acceleration, and does g appear anywhere here?"]},{"title":"Sliding down a rough slope","setup":"A particle of mass 3 kg is released from rest on a rough plane inclined at 20° to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the particle and the plane is 0.15. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², find the acceleration of the particle down a line of greatest slope.","steps":["First check the particle really slides: it does if tan 20° > μ, and tan 20° = 0.364, which is bigger than 0.15.","Resolve perpendicular to the plane, where there is no acceleration: R = 3 × 9.8 × cos 20° = 27.627 N.","The particle slides, so friction is limiting and acts up the slope: F = μR = 0.15 × 27.627 = 4.144 N.","Resolve along a line of greatest slope with down the slope positive: 3 × 9.8 × sin 20° − F = 3a, so 10.055 − 4.144 = 3a.","3a = 5.911, so a = 1.970, which is 2.0 m s⁻² to 2 significant figures."],"cues":["How can you tell before calculating anything whether the particle will move at all?","Which direction has no acceleration, and what does resolving that way give you?","Is the friction limiting here, and which way along the slope does it act?","Which component of the weight drives the motion, and what is the equation along the slope?","What remains once the two forces along the slope have been combined?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A lift is moving upwards at a constant 2 m s⁻¹. What is the resultant force on a passenger standing inside it?","a":"Zero. Constant velocity means zero acceleration, so by Newton's first law the resultant force is zero and the normal reaction equals the passenger's weight."},{"q":"A particle rests on a plane inclined at α to the horizontal. Which component of its weight is mg sin α, and which is mg cos α?","a":"mg sin α acts down a line of greatest slope; mg cos α acts perpendicular to the plane, into it."},{"q":"When may you write F = μR instead of F ≤ μR?","a":"Only when the body is moving, or is in limiting equilibrium (on the point of sliding)."},{"q":"A crate is dragged along rough level ground by a rope at 30° above the horizontal, with tension T. Why is the normal reaction less than the weight?","a":"The rope has an upward component T sin 30°, so resolving vertically gives R = mg − T sin 30°, which is less than mg (and so the friction is smaller too)."}]},"room":{"facts":["Newton's first law: a body stays at rest or moves with constant velocity in a straight line unless a resultant external force acts on it — so in equilibrium, or at constant velocity, the resultant force is zero in every direction.","Newton's second law: resultant force = ma, applied along one chosen direction at a time, with the resultant and the acceleration in the same direction.","Newton's third law: if A exerts a force on B, then B exerts an equal and opposite force on A.","Weight W = mg newtons, vertically downwards; take g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless told otherwise, and give answers that depend on g to 2 significant figures.","Friction obeys F ≤ μR, with F = μR only when the body is moving or is in limiting equilibrium; R is the normal reaction and μ the coefficient of friction.","On a plane inclined at α the weight resolves into mg sin α down a line of greatest slope and mg cos α perpendicular to the plane.","Over a smooth pulley a light inextensible string has the same tension throughout, and the connected particles have accelerations of equal magnitude.","A force of magnitude F at θ to a direction has components F cos θ along it and F sin θ perpendicular to it; components X and Y give a resultant √(X² + Y²) at arctan(Y/X) to the X direction."],"questions":[{"q":"A particle of mass 5 kg is acted on by a single resultant force of magnitude 12 N. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle.","a":"2.4 m s⁻²","sol":"Apply Newton's second law along the line of the force. F = ma gives 12 = 5a (M1 for a correct use of F = ma with F = 12 and m = 5). a = 12 ÷ 5 = 2.4 m s⁻² (A1). No value of g is involved, so no rounding is needed.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only one of Newton's laws links a resultant force to an acceleration.","Work along the line the force acts in, with the mass in kilograms and the force in newtons.","First line of working: Newton's second law along the line the force acts in, resultant = ma, that is 12 = 5a."],"codes":["8.2"]},{"q":"Find, in newtons, the weight of a particle of mass 7 kg. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"69 N (2 s.f.)","sol":"Weight is the force of gravity on the particle: W = mg = 7 × 9.8 = 68.6 N (M1 for mg with m = 7 and g = 9.8). Since g is given to 2 significant figures, the answer is given to 2 significant figures: W = 69 N (A1). The weight acts vertically downwards.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Mass and weight are different quantities: one is in kilograms, the other is a force in newtons.","Multiply the mass by the gravitational acceleration the question gives you.","First line of working: W = mg, so the weight is 7g N; put g = 9.8 in after that, and round at the end."],"codes":["8.3"]},{"q":"Two forces, (5i − 2j) N and (−3i + 7j) N, act on a particle. Find the exact magnitude of the resultant force.","a":"√29 N","sol":"Add the forces component by component: (5i − 2j) + (−3i + 7j) = (5 − 3)i + (−2 + 7)j = (2i + 5j) N (M1 A1). Magnitude = √(2² + 5²) = √(4 + 25) = √29 N (A1). 'Exact' means the surd must be left as it is; √29 ≈ 5.39 N would lose the final accuracy mark on its own.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Forces add like vectors, so the i parts go together and the j parts go together.","Once you have the single resultant vector, its magnitude comes from Pythagoras.","First line of working: resultant = (5i − 2j) + (−3i + 7j), collecting the i terms and the j terms separately; the magnitude then comes from √(X² + Y²)."],"codes":["8.5"]},{"q":"A block of mass 8 kg rests on rough horizontal ground. The coefficient of friction between the block and the ground is 0.35. Find the greatest possible magnitude of the frictional force that can act on the block. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"27 N (2 s.f.)","sol":"Resolve vertically: there is no vertical acceleration, so R = 8g = 8 × 9.8 = 78.4 N (M1). Friction is greatest when it is limiting, F = μR = 0.35 × 78.4 = 27.44 N (M1). To 2 significant figures the greatest friction is 27 N (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Friction depends on how hard the surfaces are pressed together, not on the weight directly.","Resolve vertically first to find the normal reaction, then use the largest friction the model allows.","First line of working: resolve vertically, where there is no acceleration, R = 8g; the greatest friction is then F = μR with μ = 0.35."],"codes":["8.6","8.3"]},{"q":"A crate is pushed across a horizontal floor by a horizontal force of magnitude 40 N and moves in a straight line at a constant speed of 0.5 m s⁻¹. State the magnitude of the total resistance to the motion of the crate, and name the law that justifies your answer.","a":"40 N, by Newton's first law","sol":"Constant speed in a straight line means constant velocity, so the acceleration is zero and by Newton's first law the resultant force is zero (B1). Horizontally the only forces are the 40 N push forwards and the resistance backwards, so the resistance must be 40 N (B1). Note that the speed itself, 0.5 m s⁻¹, plays no part in the answer.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what the words 'constant speed in a straight line' tell you about the acceleration.","If the acceleration is zero, what must the forces along the floor do to each other?","First line of working: resolve horizontally with zero acceleration, so 40 − R = 0 where R N is the total resistance; name the law that lets you set the resultant to zero."],"codes":["8.1"]},{"q":"A woman of mass 50 kg stands on the floor of a lift. The lift is moving downwards with constant acceleration of magnitude 2 m s⁻². Find the magnitude of the normal reaction of the floor on the woman. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"390 N","sol":"Two forces act on the woman: her weight 50g N downwards and the normal reaction R N upwards. Take the direction of the acceleration, downwards, as positive. Newton's second law: 50g − R = 50 × 2 (M1 A1). So R = 50 × 9.8 − 100 = 490 − 100 = 390 N (M1 A1). The reaction is less than her weight of 490 N, which is what you expect when a lift accelerates downwards: she feels lighter.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Only two forces act on the woman herself; the lift's motion enters through her acceleration.","Take the direction of the acceleration as positive and write Newton's second law for the woman alone.","First line of working: take the direction of the acceleration as positive and write Newton's second law for the woman alone, 50g − R = 50a."],"codes":["8.4","8.3"]},{"q":"A particle of mass 4 kg is released from rest on a smooth plane inclined at 25° to the horizontal. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle down a line of greatest slope. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"4.1 m s⁻² (2 s.f.)","sol":"The plane is smooth, so there is no friction and the only force with a component along the slope is the weight. Resolve along a line of greatest slope with down the slope positive: 4g sin 25° = 4a (M1 A1). So a = 9.8 sin 25° = 9.8 × 0.42262 = 4.1417 (M1), which is 4.1 m s⁻² to 2 significant figures (A1). The mass cancels, so any particle on a smooth 25° slope has this acceleration.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["On a slope the useful directions are along the slope and perpendicular to it, not horizontal and vertical.","Only one force has a component along the slope here, because the plane is smooth; resolve the weight.","First line of working: resolve along a line of greatest slope, down the slope positive, 4g sin 25° = 4a; the plane is smooth, so no friction term appears."],"codes":["8.2","8.3"]},{"q":"A block of mass 5 kg is pulled along rough horizontal ground by a horizontal force of magnitude 30 N. The coefficient of friction between the block and the ground is 0.4. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the block. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"2.1 m s⁻² (2 s.f.)","sol":"Resolve vertically, where there is no acceleration: R = 5g = 49 N (B1). The block moves, so friction is limiting: F = μR = 0.4 × 49 = 19.6 N (M1). Resolve horizontally with the direction of motion positive: 30 − 19.6 = 5a (M1 A1). So 5a = 10.4 and a = 2.08, which is 2.1 m s⁻² to 2 significant figures (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["You cannot find the friction until you know how hard the block presses on the ground.","Resolve vertically for the normal reaction, then horizontally for the motion; the block is sliding, so friction is at its maximum.","First line of working: resolve vertically, R = 5g, then use F = μR in the horizontal equation of motion, 30 − F = 5a."],"codes":["8.6","8.2"]},{"q":"Two forces, of magnitudes 10 N and 6 N, act at a point. The angle between the two forces is 120°. Find the exact magnitude of their resultant.","a":"2√19 N (≈ 8.72 N)","sol":"Resolve along and perpendicular to the 10 N force. Along it: 10 + 6 cos 120° = 10 − 3 = 7 N (M1 A1). Perpendicular to it: 6 sin 120° = 3√3 N (A1). Magnitude of the resultant = √(7² + (3√3)²) = √(49 + 27) = √76 = 2√19 N (A1) ≈ 8.72 N. The cosine rule on the vector triangle gives the same thing: R² = 10² + 6² − 2 × 10 × 6 × cos 60° = 76.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two forces at an angle can be combined either by components or by a vector triangle.","Resolving along and perpendicular to the larger force keeps the arithmetic short; remember cos 120° is negative.","First line of working: resolve along and perpendicular to the 10 N force — along it, 10 + 6 cos 120°; perpendicular to it, 6 sin 120° — then combine the two components."],"codes":["8.5"]},{"q":"Two particles A and B, of masses 2 kg and 6 kg, are connected by a light inextensible string passing over a smooth fixed pulley. The particles hang vertically, one on each side of the pulley, and the system is released from rest. Find the tension in the string. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"29 N (2 s.f.)","sol":"The string is light and inextensible over a smooth pulley, so the tension T N is the same throughout and the particles have accelerations of equal magnitude a m s⁻², with B going down and A going up. For B, downwards positive: 6g − T = 6a (M1 A1). For A, upwards positive: T − 2g = 2a (M1 A1). Adding eliminates T: 4g = 8a, so a = 4.9 m s⁻². Substituting into the second equation: T = 2(9.8 + 4.9) = 2 × 14.7 = 29.4 N, which is 29 N to 2 significant figures (A1). Check: the tension lies between 2g = 19.6 N and 6g = 58.8 N, as it must.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The two particles are joined, so what they share is the size of the acceleration and the value of the tension.","Write Newton's second law for each particle separately, taking the direction each one actually moves as positive.","First line of working: one equation of motion for each particle, for B downwards 6g − T = 6a and for A upwards T − 2g = 2a."],"codes":["8.4","8.3"]},{"q":"A car of mass 1200 kg tows a trailer of mass 400 kg along a straight horizontal road. The engine of the car produces a driving force of 3000 N. The resistances to motion are 600 N on the car and 200 N on the trailer, and the tow bar is light and horizontal. Find the tension in the tow bar.","a":"750 N","sol":"For the whole system (mass 1600 kg), the tow-bar forces are internal and cancel: 3000 − 600 − 200 = 1600a (M1 A1), so a = 2200 ÷ 1600 = 1.375 m s⁻². Now take the trailer alone, forwards positive: T − 200 = 400 × 1.375 (M1 A1), so T = 200 + 550 = 750 N (A1). Check with the car: 3000 − 600 − 750 = 1650 = 1200 × 1.375. Note the whole-system equation on its own can never give the tension, because the tension does not appear in it.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two bodies are joined by the tow bar, so there are three equations available: one for each body and one for the system.","The quickest route is the system equation for the acceleration, then one body on its own for the connecting force.","First line of working: the system equation, in which the tow-bar forces cancel, 3000 − 600 − 200 = 1600a; then the trailer on its own, T − 200 = 400a."],"codes":["8.4","8.2"]},{"q":"A particle of mass 0.5 kg is acted on by two forces, (4i − 3j) N and (−2i + 5j) N. Find the exact magnitude of the acceleration of the particle.","a":"4√2 m s⁻² (≈ 5.66 m s⁻²)","sol":"Resultant force = (4i − 3j) + (−2i + 5j) = (2i + 2j) N (M1 A1). Newton's second law in vector form, F = ma: (2i + 2j) = 0.5a, so a = (4i + 4j) m s⁻² (M1). Magnitude = √(4² + 4²) = √32 = 4√2 m s⁻² (A1) ≈ 5.66 m s⁻². The acceleration is parallel to the resultant force, at 45° to i.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Newton's second law works exactly the same way when the forces are vectors.","Add the two force vectors first, then divide by the mass before finding any magnitude.","First line of working: resultant force = (4i − 3j) + (−2i + 5j), then F = ma in vector form gives that resultant = 0.5a."],"codes":["8.5","8.2"]},{"q":"A particle of mass 2 kg is released from rest on a rough plane inclined at 30° to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the particle and the plane is 0.2. The particle slides 5 m down a line of greatest slope. Find its speed at that moment. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"5.7 m s⁻¹ (2 s.f.)","sol":"Resolve perpendicular to the plane, where there is no acceleration: R = 2g cos 30° = 19.6 × 0.86603 = 16.97 N (M1 A1). The particle slides, so friction is limiting and acts up the slope: F = μR = 0.2 × 16.974 = 3.3948 N (M1). Resolve along a line of greatest slope, down the slope positive: 2g sin 30° − F = 2a, so 9.8 − 3.3948 = 6.4052 = 2a (M1 A1) and a = 3.2026 m s⁻². The forces are constant, so the acceleration is constant and the constant-acceleration formulae apply. With u = 0, s = 5 and a = 3.2026, v² = u² + 2as = 2 × 3.2026 × 5 = 32.03 (M1). So v = 5.659 = 5.7 m s⁻¹ to 2 significant figures (A1).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["This is two topics joined together: the forces give you something constant, and that is what the second half needs.","Work out the acceleration from the forces on the slope first, then use a constant-acceleration formula with u = 0 and s = 5.","First line of working: resolve perpendicular to the plane, R = 2g cos 30°, then along a line of greatest slope with friction up it, 2g sin 30° − μR = 2a."],"codes":["8.6","8.2","8.3"]},{"q":"A box of mass 6 kg is pulled across rough horizontal ground by a force of magnitude 30 N acting at 25° above the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the box and the ground is 0.25. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the box. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"2.6 m s⁻² (2 s.f.)","sol":"Resolve vertically, where there is no acceleration: R + 30 sin 25° = 6g, so R = 58.8 − 12.68 = 46.12 N (M1 A1). The box is moving, so friction is limiting: F = μR = 0.25 × 46.12 = 11.53 N (M1). Resolve horizontally, direction of motion positive: 30 cos 25° − F = 6a, so 27.19 − 11.53 = 6a (M1 A1). Hence 6a = 15.66 and a = 2.6098, which is 2.6 m s⁻² to 2 significant figures (A1). The common error is to take R = 6g: the upward component of the pull means the box presses less hard on the ground, so both R and the friction are smaller.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["The pull is not horizontal, so it does two jobs at once — think about what each of its components changes.","Resolve vertically for the normal reaction before you touch the friction, then resolve horizontally for the motion.","First line of working: resolve vertically, R + 30 sin 25° = 6g, then resolve horizontally along the motion, 30 cos 25° − μR = 6a."],"codes":["8.6","8.2","8.5"]},{"q":"A particle of mass m kg rests in limiting equilibrium on a rough plane inclined at an angle α to the horizontal, where 0° < α < 90°. The coefficient of friction between the particle and the plane is μ. Show that μ = tan α.","a":"μ = tan α (shown)","sol":"The particle is on the point of sliding down, so friction acts up a line of greatest slope and takes its maximum value F = μR. Resolve perpendicular to the plane: R = mg cos α (M1 A1). Resolve along a line of greatest slope, where the particle is in equilibrium: F = mg sin α, that is μR = mg sin α (M1). Substituting R: μ mg cos α = mg sin α. Dividing by mg cos α, which is not zero for 0° < α < 90°: μ = sin α ÷ cos α = tan α (A1, cso). Because this is a 'show that', every line must be seen. Note that neither m nor g appears in the result, so the angle at which a body begins to slide depends only on the coefficient of friction.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'Limiting equilibrium' is doing two jobs in this question: say what each of them tells you.","Resolve in the two directions of the slope and write the friction at its maximum value in terms of μ and R.","First line of working: resolve perpendicular to the plane, R = mg cos α, and along a line of greatest slope in equilibrium with friction limiting, μR = mg sin α."],"codes":["8.6","8.2"]},{"q":"A particle of mass 2 kg hangs from one end of a light inextensible string, the other end of which is attached to a fixed point. A horizontal force of magnitude P newtons holds the particle in equilibrium with the string making an angle of 30° with the vertical. Find P. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"11 N (2 s.f.)","sol":"Three coplanar forces act on the particle: the weight 2g N vertically down, the tension T N along the string and the horizontal force P N. The particle is in equilibrium, so the components balance in each direction. Resolve vertically: T cos 30° = 2g (M1 A1). Resolve horizontally: T sin 30° = P (M1). Dividing the second by the first: P = 2g tan 30° = 19.6 × 0.57735 = 11.32 (A1), so P = 11 N to 2 significant figures (A1). (If the tension were wanted, T = 2g ÷ cos 30° = 22.63…, which is 23 N to 2 significant figures.)","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Equilibrium under three forces means the same thing it always does, applied in two perpendicular directions.","The string is at 30° to the vertical, so resolve vertically and horizontally and treat the tension as a second unknown.","First line of working: resolve vertically, T cos 30° = 2g, and horizontally, T sin 30° = P, treating T as the second unknown."],"codes":["8.4","8.5","8.1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A resultant force of 15 N acts on a particle of mass 6 kg. Find the magnitude of the acceleration.","a":"2.5 m s⁻²"},{"q":"Find the weight, in newtons, of a particle of mass 4 kg. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"39 N (2 s.f.; 39.2 N)"},{"q":"A particle of mass 6 kg lies on a plane inclined at 30° to the horizontal. Find the component of its weight acting down a line of greatest slope. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"29 N (2 s.f.; 6 × 9.8 × sin 30° = 29.4 N)"},{"q":"A block of mass 10 kg rests on rough horizontal ground with coefficient of friction 0.2. Find the greatest possible frictional force on the block. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².","a":"20 N (2 s.f.; μR = 0.2 × 98 = 19.6 N)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Newton's first law","back":"A body stays at rest, or keeps moving with constant velocity in a straight line, unless a resultant external force acts on it.","code":"8.1","open":false},{"front":"Newton's second law","back":"Resultant force = mass × acceleration, F = ma, with F and a in the same direction. Apply it along one chosen direction at a time.","code":"8.2","open":false},{"front":"Newton's third law","back":"If body A exerts a force on body B, then B exerts a force on A equal in magnitude and opposite in direction.","code":"8.4","open":false},{"front":"Normal reaction R","back":"The contact force a surface exerts on a body, acting perpendicular to the surface and away from it.","code":"8.1","open":false},{"front":"Tension and thrust","back":"Tension acts along a string or rod, pulling the body towards the other end. Thrust (compression) acts along a rod only, pushing outwards. A string can never thrust.","code":"8.1","open":false},{"front":"Weight","back":"The force of gravity on a body: W = mg newtons, acting vertically downwards. Mass, in kilograms, is not a force.","code":"8.3","open":false},{"front":"The value of g","back":"Take g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless the question specifies another value, such as 10 m s⁻². It is treated as constant, but it is not a universal constant: it depends on location.","code":"8.3","open":false},{"front":"Accuracy when g is used","back":"g = 9.8 is given to 2 significant figures, so any answer that depends on g is given to 2 significant figures.","code":"8.3","open":false},{"front":"Condition for a particle to be in equilibrium","back":"The resultant force is zero: the components of all the forces balance in each of two perpendicular directions. The same holds at constant velocity.","code":"8.4","open":false},{"front":"Components of the weight on a plane inclined at α","back":"mg sin α down a line of greatest slope; mg cos α perpendicular to the plane, pressing into it.","code":"8.2","open":false},{"front":"The friction model","back":"F ≤ μR, where R is the normal reaction and μ the coefficient of friction. Friction acts along the surface, opposing motion or attempted motion.","code":"8.6","open":false},{"front":"Limiting friction","back":"The greatest friction the model allows, F = μR. It acts only when the body is moving or is in limiting equilibrium (on the point of sliding).","code":"8.6","open":false},{"front":"Light inextensible string over a smooth pulley","back":"The tension is the same throughout the string, and the connected particles have accelerations of equal magnitude.","code":"8.4","open":false},{"front":"Resolving a force of magnitude F at angle θ to a direction","back":"Component F cos θ along that direction and F sin θ perpendicular to it.","code":"8.5","open":false},{"front":"Resultant of perpendicular components X and Y","back":"Magnitude √(X² + Y²), at an angle arctan(Y ÷ X) to the direction of X.","code":"8.5","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Newton's second law: resultant force = ma (force in N, mass in kg, acceleration in m s⁻²) — not given in the booklet.","Weight: W = mg, vertically downwards — not given in the booklet.","Newton's third law: the forces two bodies exert on each other are equal in magnitude and opposite in direction — not given in the booklet.","Equilibrium of a particle: the sum of the components of the forces is zero in each of two perpendicular directions — not given in the booklet.","Friction: F ≤ μR, and F = μR when the body is moving or in limiting equilibrium — not given in the booklet.","Limiting equilibrium on a rough plane inclined at α: μ = tan α — not given in the booklet; you are expected to derive it.","Resolving: a force F at angle θ to a direction has components F cos θ along it and F sin θ perpendicular to it — not given in the booklet.","Weight on a plane inclined at α: mg sin α down a line of greatest slope, mg cos α perpendicular to the plane — not given in the booklet.","Resultant of perpendicular components X and Y: magnitude √(X² + Y²) at arctan(Y ÷ X) to the X direction — not given in the booklet.","Magnitude of a force or acceleration given as ai + bj: √(a² + b²) — not given in the booklet.","Connected particles over a smooth pulley: one equation F = ma per particle, with the same tension T and the same magnitude of acceleration a — not given in the booklet.","Constant acceleration, needed whenever a forces question continues into the motion: v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², v² = u² + 2as, s = ½(u + v)t — these, and s = vt − ½at², are given in the booklet under Mechanics › Kinematics.","What the Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet gives for Mechanics: only the five constant-acceleration formulae, under Mechanics › Kinematics. Appendix 1 of the specification lists Weight = mass × g, Friction: F ≤ μR and Newton's second law in the form F = ma, together with the variable-acceleration results v = dr/dt, a = dv/dt = d²r/dt², r = ∫v dt and v = ∫a dt, as formulae you are expected to know and which will not appear in the booklet. So every other result above, and those variable-acceleration results too, must be written from memory.","The rubric on the front of the paper: take g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless the question states otherwise, and give answers that depend on g to 2 or 3 significant figures (this kit uses 2 s.f. throughout, which is always accepted)."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Method and common errors","items":["Draw the force diagram first, with every force labelled and the positive direction marked. Mark only forces that act on the body you are considering — not forces it exerts on something else.","Write F = ma for one body in one direction at a time. Perpendicular to the motion there is no acceleration, so the components balance: that is how you find the normal reaction.","For two connected bodies you have three usable equations — one for each body and one for the system — but only two are independent, and the system equation never contains the connecting force.","Common error: taking R = mg when the applied force is at an angle. If it pulls upwards R = mg − F sin θ; if it pushes downwards R = mg + F sin θ.","Common error: swapping sin and cos on a slope. The component down the slope is mg sin α, and it must grow as α grows towards 90°.","Friction opposes the motion or the attempted motion, so decide which way the body tends to slide before you put friction on the diagram.","Use F = μR only when the body slides or is in limiting equilibrium. In ordinary equilibrium friction takes whatever value up to μR is needed, so all you may write is F ≤ μR.","Modelling words earn marks when asked about: a particle has no size, so the forces all act at a point and there is no turning effect; a light string has no mass, so the tension is the same throughout; a smooth pulley only changes the direction of the tension; a rod, unlike a string, can thrust.","Keep full accuracy in the calculator and round only at the end; answers that use g = 9.8 are given to 2 significant figures."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:18:17.000Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"A clean, publishable kit: all 16 question answers, the 4 exit answers and all 5 worked examples recompute exactly with g = 9.8 and are rounded to 2 s.f. wherever g was used and left exact or to 3 s.f. where it was not, idea codes 8.1–8.6 are covered in order within the specification's restrictions, the Formulae sheet now states correctly that the booklet supplies nothing for Mechanics, and every third hint is a genuine first line of working; the only thing left to tidy is the claim that the paper's rubric demands 2 significant figures when it in fact allows 2 or 3."}}},"M9":{"id":"EDX-9MA0","topic":"M9","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Moments is the one wholly new topic in Year 2 statics, and it appears on Paper 3 almost every year as a multi-part question worth six to ten marks: a beam resting on two supports, a plank about to tip, a non-uniform rod hiding its centre of mass, or a ladder leaning on a smooth wall. The marks are won by the same two or three lines every time — one moment equation about a well-chosen point, plus one or two resolving equations — so the technique can be learned in full and used with confidence. It also carries AO3 marks, because every one of these questions is a model: a rigid uniform rod, a particle, a light inextensible string, a smooth wall, rough ground. Examiners ask you to use those assumptions and then to say what they cost you.","idea":[{"h":"What a moment is and how to calculate it","t":"The moment of a force about a point is force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action of the force, measured in newton metres (N m). A moment always has a sense: clockwise or anticlockwise about that point. If a force F acts at a point P of a rod and its line of action makes an angle θ with the rod, its moment about the end A is F sin θ × AP — either resolve the force perpendicular to the rod and multiply by AP, or multiply F by the perpendicular distance AP sin θ, but never do both. A force whose line of action passes through the point has zero moment about it, and that single fact is what makes the whole topic manageable.","code":"9.1"},{"h":"Equilibrium of a rigid body, and choosing the point","t":"A rigid body in equilibrium satisfies two conditions at once: the resultant force is zero, so resolving in any two perpendicular directions gives zero in each; and the resultant moment about any point is zero, so the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment. The words 'about any point' are the useful part. Take moments about a point that lies on the line of action of an unknown force — a support, a hinge, the foot of a ladder — and that unknown vanishes from the equation, leaving one equation in one unknown. The weight of a uniform rod or beam acts at its midpoint; a non-uniform body's centre of mass is often the thing you are asked to find.","code":"9.1"},{"h":"Parallel forces: supports, non-uniform rods and tilting","t":"When every force is vertical — a beam on two supports, a plank with a child on it, a rod held by vertical strings — you have exactly two equations available: moments about one support, and resolving vertically. Take moments about a support first, because the reaction there has no moment, then resolve vertically for the other reaction. A non-uniform rod is handled by calling the distance of its centre of mass from one end x and taking moments about that end. A tilting question is the same technique with one extra idea: when the body is on the point of tilting about one support it is about to lift off the other, so the reaction at that other support is zero.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"},{"h":"Non-parallel forces: ladders and hinged rods","t":"Once the forces are not all parallel you have three equations: resolve horizontally, resolve vertically, and take moments about one point. A ladder against a smooth vertical wall on rough horizontal ground carries a horizontal reaction S at the wall (smooth means no friction there), and a normal reaction R with a friction force F at the foot. Take moments about the foot, where both R and F disappear at once. Friction obeys F ≤ μR, with F = μR when the ladder is on the point of slipping, which is how a question asking for the least possible coefficient of friction is answered. A freely hinged rod is treated in the same way: take moments about the hinge, since the force there is unknown in both magnitude and direction.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"}],"examples":[{"title":"A beam on two supports with a load on it","setup":"A uniform beam AB of length 6 m and mass 30 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports, one at A and one at C, where AC = 4 m. A block of mass 10 kg is placed on the beam at D, where AD = 5 m, and the beam stays in equilibrium. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the magnitude of the reaction at each support, giving each answer to 2 significant figures.","steps":["Take moments about A, where the unknown reaction R_A has no turning effect, using the fact that the uniform beam's weight 30g N acts at the midpoint: R_C × 4 = 30g × 3 + 10g × 5.","4R_C = 90g + 50g = 140g, so R_C = 35g = 35 × 9.8 = 343 N.","Resolve vertically for the whole beam: R_A + R_C = 30g + 10g = 40g = 392 N.","R_A = 392 − 343 = 49 N, so the reaction at A is 49 N and the reaction at C is 343 N = 340 N to 2 s.f."],"cues":["Which of the two supports should you take moments about so that the equation contains only one unknown?","What does that moment equation give for the reaction at C?","What second equation links the two reactions?","How do you now get the reaction at A?"]},{"title":"Finding the centre of mass of a non-uniform rod","setup":"A non-uniform rod AB of length 5 m and mass 12 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports, one at A and one at B. The magnitude of the reaction at A is three times the magnitude of the reaction at B. Find the distance of the centre of mass of the rod from A.","steps":["Resolve vertically for the rod: R_A + R_B = 12g, and R_A = 3R_B, so 4R_B = 12g.","R_B = 3g N, and therefore R_A = 9g N.","Let the centre of mass be x m from A and take moments about A, where R_A has no turning effect: 12g × x = R_B × 5 = 3g × 5 = 15g.","x = 15g ÷ 12g = 1.25, so the centre of mass is 1.25 m from A — nearer the end carrying the larger reaction, as it should be."],"cues":["What equation do the vertical forces give you?","What is each reaction, in terms of g?","Where do you take moments, and what letter do you introduce?","What does that equation give for x?"]},{"title":"A plank on the point of tilting","setup":"A uniform plank AB of length 8 m and mass 25 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports at C and D, where AC = 1 m and AD = 6 m. A child of mass 50 kg stands on the plank at a distance d m from A, where d > 6, and the plank is on the point of tilting about D. Find d.","steps":["On the point of tilting about D the plank is about to lift off the support at C, so the reaction at C is zero and the only forces left are the two weights and the reaction at D.","The plank is uniform, so its weight 25g N acts at the midpoint, 4 m from A, which is 2 m from D on the A side; the child is (d − 6) m from D on the B side.","Take moments about D, where the reaction at D has no turning effect: 50g × (d − 6) = 25g × 2.","50(d − 6) = 50, so d − 6 = 1 and d = 7: the child is 7 m from A, that is 1 m beyond D."],"cues":["What happens at one of the supports just as the plank starts to tip?","Where does the plank's weight act, and how far is that from D?","Which point now gives an equation with only one unknown in it?","What value of d does that equation give?"]},{"title":"A ladder on the point of slipping","setup":"A uniform ladder AB of mass 20 kg and length 6 m rests in a vertical plane with its top A against a smooth vertical wall and its foot B on rough horizontal ground. The ladder makes an angle of 60° with the ground and is on the point of slipping. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the magnitude of the reaction at the wall and the coefficient of friction between the ladder and the ground, giving each answer to 2 significant figures.","steps":["Mark the forces: the wall is smooth, so it gives only a horizontal reaction S at A; the ground gives a vertical normal reaction N and a horizontal friction force F at B acting towards the wall; the weight 20g N acts at the midpoint, 3 m from B.","Take moments about B, which removes both N and F at once: S × 6 sin 60° = 20g × 3 cos 60°.","3√3 S = 30g, so S = 10g ÷ √3 = 56.58 N = 57 N to 2 s.f.","Resolve vertically: N = 20g = 196 N. Resolve horizontally: F = S = 56.58 N.","On the point of slipping F = μN, so μ = 56.58 ÷ 196 = 0.2886… = 0.29 to 2 s.f."],"cues":["Which forces act on the ladder, and what does 'smooth wall' rule out?","Which point makes two of the three unknowns disappear?","What does that moment equation give for the reaction at the wall?","What do the two resolving equations give for N and F?","What equation links F and N when the ladder is about to slip?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Why does taking moments about a support make a beam problem easier?","a":"The reaction at that support acts through the point you are taking moments about, so its perpendicular distance is zero and it has no moment. The equation then contains only the other unknowns."},{"q":"A uniform beam of length 6 m and mass 20 kg rests horizontally on supports at its two ends. Taking g = 9.8 m s⁻², what is the reaction at each support?","a":"10g = 98 N at each support, by symmetry (or by moments about one end)."},{"q":"A force of 30 N acts at a point P of a rod, at an angle of 25° to the rod, where AP = 1.2 m. What is its moment about A, to 3 significant figures?","a":"30 sin 25° × 1.2 = 15.2 N m."},{"q":"A ladder on rough ground leans against a smooth wall and is on the point of slipping. What equation links the friction force F and the normal reaction R at the ground, and what would the relation be if it were not on the point of slipping?","a":"On the point of slipping F = μR; otherwise F ≤ μR."}]},"room":{"facts":["Moment of a force about a point = force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action, measured in N m, with a sense: clockwise or anticlockwise.","A force F acting at P at an angle θ to a rod has moment F sin θ × AP about the end A — use the perpendicular component or the perpendicular distance, never both.","A rigid body is in equilibrium when the resultant force is zero and the total moment about any point is zero, so total clockwise moment = total anticlockwise moment.","The weight of a uniform rod or beam acts at its midpoint; the centre of mass of a non-uniform rod does not, and is often the unknown.","On the point of tilting about one of two supports, the reaction at the other support is zero.","At a rough contact F ≤ μR, with F = μR in limiting equilibrium; a smooth wall or peg exerts a reaction perpendicular to its surface only.","Unless a question says otherwise, take g = 9.8 m s⁻² and give any answer that depends on g to 2 or 3 significant figures (follow the accuracy the question asks for)."],"questions":[{"q":"A force of magnitude 12 N acts vertically downwards at a point P of a rigid body. The perpendicular distance from a fixed point O to the line of action of the force is 0.4 m. Find the magnitude of the moment of the force about O.","a":"4.8 N m","sol":"The moment of a force about a point is the force multiplied by the perpendicular distance from that point to the line of action of the force. Moment = 12 × 0.4 (M1) = 4.8 N m (A1). The unit N m must be given; if the question asked for the moment rather than its magnitude, the sense (clockwise or anticlockwise about O) would also be stated.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A moment measures the turning effect of a force about a point, and it depends on how far from that point the line of action of the force passes.","You have been given the perpendicular distance already, so there is no resolving and no trigonometry to do here — just combine the two quantities the right way round.","The answer is a single number followed by the unit of a moment; keep the force in newtons and the distance in metres throughout."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"A force of magnitude 15 N acts at the point P of a light rod AB. The line of action of the force makes an angle of 40° with the rod, and AP = 0.8 m. Find the magnitude of the moment of the force about A, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.","a":"7.71 N m","sol":"Resolve the force into components along the rod and perpendicular to it. The component along the rod passes through A, so it has no moment about A. Component perpendicular to the rod = 15 sin 40° (M1). Moment about A = 15 sin 40° × 0.8 (M1) = 9.6418… × 0.8 = 7.7134… = 7.71 N m (A1, awrt 7.71). Equivalently, the perpendicular distance from A to the line of action is 0.8 sin 40° = 0.5142… m, and 15 × 0.5142… = 7.71 N m.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Only the part of the force at right angles to the rod turns it about A; the part along the rod pushes straight through A and does nothing.","Either resolve the 15 N force perpendicular to the rod and use AP, or use the whole force with the perpendicular distance from A to its line of action — one or the other, never both.","Your working is (force × sin of the angle) × distance, and the answer is a number of N m given to 3 significant figures; keep your calculator in degrees."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"A uniform beam PQ of length 5 m and mass 20 kg rests in equilibrium in a horizontal position on two supports, one at P and one at Q. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the magnitude of the reaction at each support, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"98 N at each support","sol":"The beam is uniform, so its weight 20g N acts at the midpoint, 2.5 m from each support. Moments about P, where the reaction at P has no moment: R_Q × 5 = 20g × 2.5 (M1), so R_Q = 10g = 98 N (A1). Resolving vertically: R_P + R_Q = 20g (M1), so R_P = 20g − 10g = 10g = 98 N (A1). The symmetry of the beam gives the same result at once: each support carries half the weight. Both reactions are 98 N to 2 significant figures.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["A uniform beam has its weight at one particular point, and the two supports are placed symmetrically about that point.","Take moments about one support so that its reaction disappears from the equation, then resolve vertically to get the other.","Your moment equation has the form (reaction at Q) × PQ = (weight) × (distance of the midpoint from P); work with the weight as 20g N and multiply by 9.8 only on the last line."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"A light rod AB of length 3 m rests on a pivot at the point C, where AC = 1 m. A force of magnitude 20 N acts vertically downwards at A, and a force of magnitude F N acts vertically downwards at B. The rod rests in equilibrium in a horizontal position. Find F.","a":"F = 10","sol":"The rod is light, so it has no weight of its own and only the two applied forces and the force at the pivot act on it. Take moments about C, where the force from the pivot has no moment. The 20 N force at A is 1 m from C; the F N force at B is CB = 3 − 1 = 2 m from C. Moments about C: 20 × 1 = F × 2 (M1 for a complete moment equation, A1 correct). So 2F = 20 and F = 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The rod is light, so there is no weight of the rod to include — only the two applied forces and whatever the pivot does.","Take moments about the pivot, where the pivot's own force has no turning effect, and measure every distance from there.","Your equation has the form 20 × AC = F × CB, and the two distances are not equal."],"codes":["9.1"]},{"q":"A rigid body rests in equilibrium under the action of a system of coplanar forces. State the two conditions that the forces must satisfy.","a":"The resultant force is zero, and the total moment about any point is zero","sol":"For a rigid body in equilibrium: (1) the resultant force is zero, so resolving in any two perpendicular directions gives a total of zero in each direction (B1); (2) the resultant moment about any point is zero, so about any chosen point the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment (B1). The second condition holds about every point, which is exactly why a point may be chosen so that an unwanted unknown force has no moment.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Equilibrium of a rigid body asks more than equilibrium of a particle: a body with no resultant force can still be made to spin.","One of the two conditions is about the forces themselves; the other is about their turning effects.","Two short sentences are enough, one for each condition and one mark each; each sentence has to finish by saying precisely what the total it describes comes to."],"codes":["9.1","9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform beam AB of length 8 m and mass 30 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports at C and D, where AC = 2 m and AD = 6 m. A block of mass 10 kg is placed on the beam at B and the beam remains in equilibrium. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the magnitude of the reaction at C and the magnitude of the reaction at D, giving each answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"Reaction at C = 98 N, reaction at D = 290 N","sol":"The beam is uniform, so its weight 30g N acts at the midpoint, 4 m from A; the block's weight 10g N acts at B, 8 m from A. Measuring from C: the beam's weight is 2 m from C, D is 4 m from C and B is 6 m from C. Moments about C, where the reaction at C has no moment: R_D × 4 = 30g × 2 + 10g × 6 (M1 for a complete equation, A1 correct). 4R_D = 60g + 60g = 120g, so R_D = 30g = 294 N = 290 N to 2 s.f. (A1). Resolving vertically: R_C + R_D = 30g + 10g = 40g = 392 N (M1), so R_C = 392 − 294 = 98 N (A1). Check by moments about D: R_C × 4 = 30g × 2 − 10g × 2 = 40g, giving R_C = 10g = 98 N.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Draw the beam with four forces on it: two upward reactions at the supports and two downward weights, each at a distance you can state from A.","Take moments about one support to get the reaction at the other, then resolve vertically to get the first one back.","Moments about C: (reaction at D) × CD = (weight of beam) × (distance of the midpoint from C) + (weight of block) × CB."],"codes":["9.1","9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A non-uniform rod AB of length 4 m and mass 5 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports, one at A and one at B. The magnitude of the reaction at A is 29.4 N. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the distance of the centre of mass of the rod from A.","a":"1.6 m","sol":"The weight of the rod is 5g = 49 N. Resolving vertically: R_A + R_B = 49, so R_B = 49 − 29.4 = 19.6 N (M1 A1). Let the centre of mass be x m from A. Moments about A, where R_A has no moment: 49 × x = R_B × 4 = 19.6 × 4 = 78.4 (M1 for the moment equation). x = 78.4 ÷ 49 = 1.6 (A1). The centre of mass is 1.6 m from A, which is nearer A than B — consistent with A carrying the larger of the two reactions.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Three forces act: two upward reactions and the weight. The weight of a non-uniform rod does not act at the midpoint, and where it does act is what you are looking for.","Get the second reaction by resolving vertically first, then take moments about A so that the reaction at A drops out.","Call the distance x metres; your moment equation has the form (weight of rod) × x = (reaction at B) × AB."],"codes":["9.1","9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform rod AB of length 4 m and mass 6 kg rests in a horizontal position on a small smooth support at C, where AC = 1.5 m. The end A is attached to the floor by a light vertical string, and the rod is in equilibrium. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the tension in the string, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"20 N","sol":"The rod is uniform, so its weight 6g N acts at the midpoint, 2 m from A, which is 2 − 1.5 = 0.5 m from C on the B side. A string can only pull, and to balance that weight the string at A must pull the rod downwards; A is 1.5 m from C on the other side. Moments about C, where the reaction at C has no moment: T × 1.5 = 6g × 0.5 (M1 for a complete equation, A1 correct). T = 6g × 0.5 ÷ 1.5 = 2g = 19.6 = 20 N to 2 s.f. (A1). Resolving vertically would then give the reaction at C as 6g + 2g = 8g = 78 N.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Three forces act on the rod: its weight at the midpoint, the reaction at the support, and the pull of the string — and a string can only pull, never push.","Take moments about C, where the unknown reaction has no turning effect, and measure the other two distances from C.","Moments about C: (tension) × AC = (weight) × (distance of the midpoint from C)."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform ladder AB of mass 15 kg and length 4 m rests in a vertical plane with its top A against a smooth vertical wall and its foot B on rough horizontal ground. The ladder makes an angle of 70° with the ground and rests in equilibrium. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the magnitude of the frictional force acting on the ladder at B, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"27 N","sol":"The wall is smooth, so the only force there is a horizontal reaction S at A. At B the ground gives a normal reaction N vertically upwards and a friction force F horizontally towards the wall. The weight 15g N acts at the midpoint, 2 m from B. Moments about B, where N and F both have no moment: S × 4 sin 70° = 15g × 2 cos 70° (M1 for a complete equation with both perpendicular distances, A1 correct). 3.7587…S = 100.55…, so S = 26.75… N (A1). Resolving horizontally for the ladder: F = S (M1), so F = 26.75… = 27 N to 2 s.f. (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["A smooth wall can only push at right angles to itself, so the force at A is horizontal; the rough ground supplies both a normal reaction and friction.","Take moments about the foot of the ladder, where two of the three unknowns act, then resolve horizontally to link the wall reaction to the friction.","Moments about B: (reaction at the wall) × AB sin 70° = (weight) × (half of AB) × cos 70°."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform rod AB of mass 8 kg and length 2 m is freely hinged at A to a vertical wall. The rod is held in a horizontal position by a light inextensible string joining B to a point C on the wall vertically above A, the string making an angle of 30° with the rod. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the tension in the string, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"78 N","sol":"The rod is uniform, so its weight 8g N acts at the midpoint, 1 m from A. The tension T acts along BC, at 30° to the rod, so its component perpendicular to the rod is T sin 30°. The force at the hinge is unknown in magnitude and direction, so take moments about A, where it has no moment: T sin 30° × 2 = 8g × 1 (M1 for a complete moment equation, A1 correct). T × 0.5 × 2 = 8g, so T = 8g = 78.4 = 78 N to 2 s.f. (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A freely hinged end can exert a force in any direction, so choose the point about which that force cannot matter.","Use the component of the tension at right angles to the rod, and put the weight of the uniform rod at its midpoint.","Moments about A: (T sin 30°) × AB = (weight) × (half of AB)."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"In a moments question a plank resting on two supports is modelled as a uniform rod and a child standing on it is modelled as a particle. State what modelling the child as a particle lets you assume about where the child's weight acts, and hence explain why the distance used in the moment of that weight is only approximate.","a":"The whole of the child's weight is taken to act at a single point of the plank, so its moment is weight × distance from that point; in reality the weight is spread over the area of the child's feet, whose parts are at different distances, so that single distance is only approximate","sol":"B1: a particle has no size, so the whole of the child's weight is taken to act at one single point of the plank, and the moment of that weight about any point is simply weight × horizontal distance from that point — there is no need to add up the moments of a load spread across a contact area. B1: the child's weight is in fact spread over the area of the feet, and different parts of that area are at different horizontal distances from the point moments are taken about, so the single distance used in that moment is only an approximation.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what the word 'particle' takes away from a real object.","Taking moments needs one single distance from the pivot to the line of action of each force.","Two sentences: one saying where the weight is taken to act and what that lets you write for its moment, one saying why the one distance you used is not exactly right."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform plank AB of length 5 m and mass 20 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports at C and D, where AC = 1 m and AD = 4 m. A girl of mass 40 kg stands on the plank and walks slowly from C towards B. Find how far she is from A at the moment the plank is on the point of tilting about D.","a":"4.75 m from A","sol":"On the point of tilting about D the plank is about to lift off the support at C, so the reaction at C is zero (B1 — this is the key modelling step). Let the girl be x m from A, with x > 4. The plank is uniform, so its weight 20g N acts at the midpoint, 2.5 m from A, which is 1.5 m from D on the A side; the girl is (x − 4) m from D on the B side. Moments about D, where the reaction at D has no moment: 40g(x − 4) = 20g × 1.5 (M1 for a complete equation, A1 correct). Dividing by g: 40(x − 4) = 30, so x − 4 = 0.75 (M1) and x = 4.75 (A1). She is 4.75 m from A, that is 0.75 m beyond D and 0.25 m short of B. Since g cancels, the answer does not depend on the value used for g.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Just before it tips, the plank is about to lose contact with one of the two supports.","Set the reaction at the support it is lifting off equal to zero, then take moments about the support it is turning on.","Let her be x m from A, put the reaction at C equal to zero, and take moments about D."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A non-uniform rod AB of length 4 m and mass 10 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports at C and D, where AC = 0.5 m and AD = 3 m. A particle of mass 4 kg is attached to the rod at A, and the rod is then on the point of tilting about C. Find the distance of the centre of mass of the rod from A.","a":"0.7 m","sol":"On the point of tilting about C the rod is about to lift off the support at D, so the reaction at D is zero (B1). Let the centre of mass of the rod be x m from A, so it is (x − 0.5) m from C on the B side; the particle at A is 0.5 m from C on the other side. Moments about C, where the reaction at C has no moment: 4g × 0.5 = 10g × (x − 0.5) (M1 for a complete equation, A1 correct). Dividing by g: 2 = 10x − 5 (M1), so 10x = 7 and x = 0.7 (A1). The centre of mass of the rod is 0.7 m from A. Since g cancels the answer is exact and does not depend on the value used for g, and 0.7 lies between C and D as it must.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Hanging a mass on the overhanging end at A turns the rod about the nearer support, so it lifts off at the far one.","With that far reaction equal to zero, take moments about the support the rod is turning on; the unknown is the distance of the centre of mass from A.","Put the reaction at D equal to zero, let the centre of mass be x m from A, and take moments about C."],"codes":["9.1","9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform ladder AB of mass 20 kg and length 5 m rests in a vertical plane with its top A against a smooth vertical wall and its foot B on rough horizontal ground, the ladder being inclined at 60° to the horizontal. A man of mass 80 kg stands on the ladder at the point P, where BP = 4 m, and the ladder remains in equilibrium. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻². Find the least possible value of the coefficient of friction between the ladder and the ground, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.43","sol":"Model the man as a particle at P. The smooth wall gives a horizontal reaction S at A; the ground gives a normal reaction N vertically upwards and a friction force F horizontally towards the wall at B. The ladder's weight 20g N acts at the midpoint, 2.5 m from B. Moments about B, where N and F have no moment: S × 5 sin 60° = 20g × 2.5 cos 60° + 80g × 4 cos 60° (M1 for a complete equation, A1 correct). 4.3301…S = 245 + 1568 = 1813, so S = 418.7 N (A1). Resolving vertically: N = 20g + 80g = 100g = 980 N (B1). Resolving horizontally: F = S = 418.7 N (M1). For equilibrium F ≤ μN (M1), so μ ≥ 418.7 ÷ 980 = 0.4272…, and the least possible value of the coefficient of friction is 0.43 to 2 s.f. (A1).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["The man adds one more downward force, acting at his own distance up the ladder; nothing else about the ladder model changes.","Take moments about the foot for the wall reaction, resolve vertically for the normal reaction, and remember what inequality friction obeys when you are asked for a least value.","Moments about B: (reaction at the wall) × AB sin 60° = (weight of ladder) × (half of AB) × cos 60° + (weight of man) × BP cos 60°."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform rod AB of length 2a and weight W is freely hinged at A to a vertical wall. The rod is held in a horizontal position by a light inextensible string attached to the rod at the point D, where AD = 3a/2, and attached at its other end to a point C on the wall vertically above A, the string making an angle of 45° with the rod. A particle of weight W is attached to the rod at B. (a) Show that the tension in the string is 2√2 W. (b) Hence find, in terms of W, the magnitude of the force exerted by the hinge on the rod.","a":"2W","sol":"(a) The rod is uniform, so its weight W acts at the midpoint, a from A; the particle of weight W acts at B, 2a from A. The force at the hinge is unknown in both magnitude and direction, so take moments about A, where it has no moment. The component of the tension perpendicular to the rod is T sin 45°, acting at D, which is 3a/2 from A: T sin 45° × 3a/2 = W × a + W × 2a (M1 for a complete moment equation, A1 correct). So (3√2/4)Ta = 3Wa, giving T = 12W ÷ (3√2) = 4W/√2 = 2√2 W as required (A1 cso — the printed result scores nothing, every mark is in the working above). (b) The string pulls the rod towards the wall and upwards. Resolving horizontally, the horizontal component of the force at the hinge balances T cos 45° = 2√2 W × (√2/2) = 2W, so the hinge pushes the rod away from the wall with a horizontal force of 2W (M1 A1). Resolving vertically, the tension lifts with T sin 45° = 2W while the total weight is W + W = 2W, so the vertical component of the force at the hinge is zero (B1). The force at the hinge is therefore horizontal, of magnitude 2W (A1).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["A freely hinged end can push or pull in any direction, so there is one point about which that unknown force cannot appear in the equation at all.","Only the component of the tension perpendicular to the rod turns the rod about the hinge, and there are two weights to include, acting at different distances from A.","Moments about A: (T sin 45°) × AD = W × (distance of the midpoint from A) + W × AB; for part (b) resolve horizontally and vertically and combine the two components."],"codes":["9.1","9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]},{"q":"A uniform beam AB of length 4 m and mass 50 kg rests in a horizontal position on two supports, one at A and one at B. A block of mass M kg is placed on the beam at the point E, where AE = 3 m, and the beam remains in equilibrium. Given that the magnitude of the reaction at B is twice the magnitude of the reaction at A, find M.","a":"M = 100","sol":"Let the reaction at A be R_A; the reaction at B is then 2R_A. Resolving vertically: R_A + 2R_A = 50g + Mg, so 3R_A = (50 + M)g (M1 A1). The beam is uniform, so its weight acts at the midpoint, 2 m from B, and the block at E is 4 − 3 = 1 m from B. Moments about B, where the reaction at B has no moment: R_A × 4 = 50g × 2 + Mg × 1 (M1 A1). Substituting R_A = (50 + M)g ÷ 3 and dividing through by g: 4(50 + M) ÷ 3 = 100 + M (M1), so 200 + 4M = 300 + 3M and M = 100 (A1). Check: R_A = 50g = 490 N and the reaction at B is 100g = 980 N, which is twice R_A, and moments about B give 490 × 4 = 1960 = 50g × 2 + 100g × 1.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["There are two unknowns here, the reaction at A and the mass of the block, so you will need two independent equations.","Use the given ratio to write both reactions in terms of one letter, resolve vertically, and take moments about one of the supports.","Write the reaction at B as twice the reaction at A, then take moments about B so that it disappears and the two equations can be combined."],"codes":["9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies"]}],"exit":[{"q":"State the unit of a moment and the two quantities that are multiplied together to get it.","a":"N m; force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action of the force."},{"q":"A force of 8 N acts at a perpendicular distance of 0.25 m from a point P. What is the magnitude of its moment about P?","a":"2 N m"},{"q":"A plank rests on supports at C and D and is on the point of tilting about D. What is the reaction at C?","a":"Zero"},{"q":"A ladder leans against a smooth vertical wall. In what direction does the force from the wall act on the ladder, and why?","a":"Horizontally, at right angles to the wall, because a smooth surface exerts no friction, so the force has no vertical component."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Moment of a force about a point","back":"Force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action of the force. Measured in newton metres (N m) and given a sense: clockwise or anticlockwise.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"The unit of a moment","back":"The newton metre, N m. It is never written as a joule, even though the units multiply out the same way.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"Moment about A of a force F acting at P, at an angle θ to the rod","back":"F sin θ × AP, which is the same as F × AP sin θ. Use the perpendicular component or the perpendicular distance — never both.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"A force whose line of action passes through the point you take moments about","back":"Has zero moment about that point, because its perpendicular distance is zero. This is why a well-chosen point removes an unknown force.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"Where the weight of a uniform rod or beam acts","back":"At its midpoint, which is its centre of mass.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"The two conditions for a rigid body to be in equilibrium","back":"The resultant force is zero, so resolving in two perpendicular directions gives zero in each; and the total moment about any point is zero.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":false},{"front":"Which point to take moments about","back":"A point on the line of action of an unknown force — a support, a hinge, the foot of a ladder — so that the unknown drops out of the equation.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"Centre of mass of a non-uniform rod","back":"Not at the midpoint. Call its distance from one end x, take moments about that end and solve for x.","code":"9.1","open":false},{"front":"On the point of tilting about a support","back":"The body is about to lift off the other support, so the reaction there is zero. Take moments about the support it is turning on.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":false},{"front":"Force exerted by a smooth vertical wall on a ladder","back":"A horizontal normal reaction only. A smooth surface exerts no friction, so there is no vertical component at the wall.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":false},{"front":"Friction at a rough contact","back":"F ≤ μR, where R is the normal reaction. In limiting equilibrium — on the point of slipping — F = μR.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":false},{"front":"The standard first move in a ladder problem","back":"Take moments about the foot of the ladder: the normal reaction and the friction force both act there, so both disappear at once.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":false},{"front":"Moments about the foot for a ladder of length 2a and weight W at θ to the horizontal, smooth wall","back":"S × 2a sin θ = W × a cos θ, so the wall reaction is S = (W/2) cot θ.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":false},{"front":"Two modelling assumptions usually made in a moments question","back":"The plank is a rigid uniform rod, so its weight acts at the midpoint; a person or load is a particle, so its weight acts at a single point; a string is light and inextensible, so its tension is the same throughout.","code":"9.1 Equilibrium of rigid bodies","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Moment of a force about a point = force × perpendicular distance from the point to the line of action, in N m, with a sense (clockwise or anticlockwise). Not in the formulae booklet — learn it.","Force F acting at P at an angle θ to a rod: moment about A = F sin θ × AP = F × AP sin θ. Not in the formulae booklet.","Equilibrium of a rigid body: resultant force = 0 (resolve in two perpendicular directions) and total moment about any point = 0, so total clockwise moment = total anticlockwise moment. Not in the formulae booklet.","Weight of a body of mass m kg: W = mg N, with g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless the question states otherwise. Not in the formulae booklet; an answer that uses g is given to 2 or 3 significant figures (follow the accuracy the question asks for).","Uniform rod or beam of length 2a: the weight acts at the midpoint, a from each end. Not in the formulae booklet.","On the point of tilting about one of two supports: the reaction at the other support = 0. Not in the formulae booklet.","Friction at a rough contact: F ≤ μR, and F = μR in limiting equilibrium (on the point of slipping). Not in the formulae booklet.","Ladder of length 2a and weight W at θ to the horizontal, top on a smooth wall and foot on rough ground: moments about the foot give S × 2a sin θ = W × a cos θ, so S = (W/2) cot θ. Not in the formulae booklet.","The Mathematical Formulae and Statistical Tables booklet contains no result for moments at all: every line on this sheet has to be written from memory."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Method: choosing the point to take moments about","items":["Draw the diagram first: every force with an arrow at the point where it acts, and every distance marked from one named end.","Take moments about a point on the line of action of the force you do not want in the equation: a support, a hinge, or the foot of a ladder.","A problem with parallel forces only (a beam on two supports) needs one moment equation and one vertical resolving equation — two equations, two unknowns.","A problem with non-parallel forces (a ladder, a hinged rod held by a string) needs one moment equation and two resolving equations, horizontally and vertically.","If a force acts at an angle to the rod, use either its component perpendicular to the rod or the perpendicular distance from the point to its line of action — never both.","If the body is on the point of tilting, put the reaction at the support it is lifting off equal to zero before you write anything else.","Check the answer by taking moments about a second point: every equation you can write must agree.","Use g = 9.8 m s⁻² unless told otherwise and give answers that depend on g to 2 or 3 significant figures, following the accuracy the question asks for; where g cancels, give the exact value."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T16:23:56Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Second, independent pass: I re-solved all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit tickets, the 4 lesson checks and all 4 worked examples in node with g = 9.8 and every key is right (4.8 N m; 7.7134 -> 7.71; 98 N; F = 10; R_D = 294 -> 290 and R_C = 98; x = 1.6; T = 2g = 19.6 -> 20; F = S = 26.75 -> 27; T = 8g = 78.4 -> 78; x = 4.75; x = 0.7; S = 418.69, N = 980, mu = 0.4272 -> 0.43; T = 2√2 W with hinge force 2W horizontal and zero vertical; M = 100; examples give R_C = 343 -> 340 with R_A = 49, x = 1.25, d = 7, S = 56.58 -> 57 with mu = 0.2887 -> 0.29), every sol’s M/A/B marks now total exactly its m in all 16 questions, and the Formulae sheet is right that the booklet prints nothing for moments — Appendix 1 lists W = mg, F ≤ µR, F = ma and the variable-acceleration results, so all of mechanics is from memory. All seven faults in the first verdict are gone: the 2-or-3-s.f. wording is in facts[6], extras[0].items[3] and extras[1].items[7]; questions[4].hints[2] no longer supplies the sentence openings or ‘any point’; the m fields of [5], [7], [9], [13] and [14] match their schemes; examples[0].cues[0] no longer names A; lesson.why says ‘the one wholly new topic in Year 2 statics’; questions[10] is pinned to one checkable answer; and questions[14] is a genuinely new hinged-rod-and-string configuration. Publishable as it stands; the three remaining items are cosmetic."}}}} };
